1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:08,356
This course from Harvard University explores the concepts and algorithms at the foundation of modern artificial intelligence,

2
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:18,052
diving into the ideas that give rise to technologies like gameplaying engines, handwriting recognition, and machine translation.

3
00:00:18,542 --> 00:00:26,852
You'll gain exposure to the theory behind graph search algorithms, classification, optimization, reinforcement learning,

4
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:30,724
and other topics in artificial intelligence and machine learning.

5
00:00:31,310 --> 00:00:33,600
Brian Yu teaches this course.

6
00:00:51,930 --> 00:00:59,090
Hello, world. This is CS50, and this is an introduction to artificial intelligence with Python with CS50's own Brian Yu.

7
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:06,320
This course picks up where CS50 itself leaves off and explores the concepts and algorithms at the foundation of modern AI.

8
00:01:06,606 --> 00:01:09,956
We'll start with a look at how AI can search for solutions to problems.

9
00:01:10,222 --> 00:01:14,820
Whether those problems are learning how to play a game or trying to find driving directions to a destination.

10
00:01:15,246 --> 00:01:23,364
We'll then look at how AI can represent information, both knowledge that our AI is certain about, but also information and events about which our AI might be uncertain,

11
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:29,956
learning how to represent that information, but more importantly, how to use that information to draw inferences and new conclusions as well.

12
00:01:30,446 --> 00:01:38,340
We'll explore how AI can solve various types of optimization problems, trying to maximize profits or minimize cost or satisfy some other constraints,

13
00:01:38,606 --> 00:01:44,640
before turning our attention to the fast-growing field of machine learning, where we won't tell our AI exactly how to solve a problem,

14
00:01:44,814 --> 00:01:51,524
but instead give our AI access to data and experiences so that our AI can learn on its own how to perform these tasks.

15
00:01:52,142 --> 00:01:59,840
In particular, we'll look at neural networks, one of the most popular tools in modern machine learning, inspired by the way that human brains learn and reason as well,

16
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,680
before finally taking a look at the world of natural language processing, so that it's not just us humans learning to learn how artificial intelligence is able to speak,

17
00:02:07,854 --> 00:02:11,716
but also AI learning how to understand and interpret human language as well.

18
00:02:12,078 --> 00:02:21,600
We'll explore these ideas and algorithms, and along the way, give you the opportunity to build your own AI programs to implement all of this and more. This is CS50.

19
00:02:44,174 --> 00:02:47,920
All right, welcome, everyone, to an introduction to artificial intelligence with Python.

20
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:49,180
My name is Brian Yu.

21
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:55,940
And in this class, we'll explore some of the ideas and techniques and algorithms that are at the foundation of artificial intelligence.

22
00:02:56,398 --> 00:02:59,844
Now, artificial intelligence covers a wide variety of types of techniques.

23
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:09,840
Anytime you see a computer do something that appears to be intelligent or rational in some way, like recognizing someone's face in a photo, or being able to play a game better than people can,

24
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:19,588
or being able to understand human language when we talk to our phones and they understand what we mean and are able to respond back to us. These are all examples of AI or artificial intelligence.

25
00:03:19,950 --> 00:03:24,228
And in this class, we'll explore some of the ideas that make that AI possible.

26
00:03:24,622 --> 00:03:33,600
So we'll begin our conversations with search, the problem of we have an AI, and we would like the AI to be able to search for solutions to some kind of problem, no matter what that problem might be,

27
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:43,140
whether it's trying to get driving directions from point A to point B, or trying to figure out how to play a game, given a tic-tac-toe game, for example, figuring out what move it ought to make.

28
00:03:43,918 --> 00:03:46,080
After that, we'll take a look at knowledge.

29
00:03:46,286 --> 00:03:54,400
Ideally, we want our AI to be able to know information, to be able to represent that information, and more importantly, to be able to draw inferences from that information,

30
00:03:54,638 --> 00:03:58,308
to be able to use the information it knows and draw additional conclusions.

31
00:03:58,670 --> 00:04:02,596
So we'll talk about how AI can be programmed in order to do just that.

32
00:04:03,118 --> 00:04:11,680
Then we'll explore the topic of uncertainty, talking about ideas of what happens if a computer isn't sure about a fact, but maybe is only sure with a certain probability.

33
00:04:11,982 --> 00:04:20,480
So we'll talk about some of the ideas behind probability and how computers can begin to deal with uncertain events in order to be a little bit more intelligent in that sense

34
00:04:20,686 --> 00:04:20,996
as well.

35
00:04:21,838 --> 00:04:27,908
After that, we'll turn our attention to optimization, problems of when the computer is trying to optimize for some sort of goal.

36
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:36,720
Especially in a situation where there might be multiple ways that a computer might solve a problem, but we're looking for a better way, or potentially the best way, if that's at all possible.

37
00:04:37,582 --> 00:04:42,464
Then we'll take a look at machine learning, or learning more generally, and looking at how when we have access to data,

38
00:04:42,624 --> 00:04:51,396
our computers can be programmed to be quite intelligent by learning from data and learning from experience, being able to perform a task better and better based on greater access to data.

39
00:04:51,694 --> 00:04:57,988
So, your email, for example, where your email inbox somehow knows which of your emails are good emails and which of your emails are spam.

40
00:04:58,224 --> 00:05:03,748
These are all examples of computers being able to learn from past experiences and past data.

41
00:05:04,366 --> 00:05:13,424
We'll take a look too at how computers are able to draw inspiration from human intelligence, looking at the structure of the human brain and how neural networks can be a computer analog

42
00:05:13,584 --> 00:05:22,340
to that sort of idea, and how by taking advantage of a certain type of structure of a computer program, we can write neural networks that are able to perform tasks very, very effectively.

43
00:05:22,926 --> 00:05:28,864
And then finally, we'll turn our attention to language, not programming languages, but human languages that we speak every day.

44
00:05:29,166 --> 00:05:36,548
And taking a look at the challenges that come about as a computer tries to understand natural language and how it is that some of the natural language processing that

45
00:05:36,864 --> 00:05:40,100
occurs in modern artificial intelligence can actually work.

46
00:05:40,974 --> 00:05:43,492
But today we'll begin our conversation with search.

47
00:05:43,664 --> 00:05:51,652
This problem of trying to figure out what to do when we have some sort of situation that the computer is in, some sort of environment that an agent is in, so to speak,

48
00:05:51,854 --> 00:05:56,388
and we would like for that agent to be able to somehow look for a solution to that problem.

49
00:05:57,070 --> 00:05:59,812
Now, these problems can come in any number of different types of formats.

50
00:06:00,238 --> 00:06:05,412
One example, for instance, might be something like this classic 15 puzzle with the sliding tiles that you might have seen,

51
00:06:05,582 --> 00:06:09,504
where you're trying to slide the tiles in order to make sure that all the numbers line up in order.

52
00:06:09,744 --> 00:06:12,384
This is an example of what you might call a search problem.

53
00:06:12,686 --> 00:06:21,060
The 15 puzzle begins in an initially mixed-up state, and we need some way of finding moves to make in order to return the puzzle to its solved state.

54
00:06:21,614 --> 00:06:23,972
But there are similar problems that you can frame in other ways.

55
00:06:24,174 --> 00:06:27,812
Trying to find your way through a maze, for example, is another example of a search problem.

56
00:06:28,144 --> 00:06:36,740
You begin in one place, you have some goal of where you're trying to get to, and you need to figure out the correct sequence of actions that will take you from that initial state to the goal.

57
00:06:37,390 --> 00:06:45,540
And while this is a little bit abstract, anytime we talk about maze solving in this class, you can translate it to something a little more real world, something like driving directions.

58
00:06:45,774 --> 00:06:54,624
If you ever wonder how Google Maps is able to figure out what is the best way for you to get from point A to point B and what turns to make at what time, depending on traffic, for example,

59
00:06:54,784 --> 00:06:56,996
it's often some sort of search algorithm.

60
00:06:57,264 --> 00:07:03,652
You have an AI that is trying to get from an initial position to some sort of goal by taking some sequence of actions.

61
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:13,860
So we'll start our conversations today by thinking about these types of search problems and what goes in to solving a search problem like this in order for an AI to be able to find a good solution.

62
00:07:14,734 --> 00:07:19,268
In order to do so though, we're going to need to introduce a little bit of terminology, some of which I've already used.

63
00:07:19,584 --> 00:07:22,212
But the first term we'll need to think about is an agent.

64
00:07:22,606 --> 00:07:30,340
An agent is just some entity that perceives its environment, it somehow is able to perceive the things around it and act on that environment in some way.

65
00:07:30,702 --> 00:07:38,464
So in the case of the driving directions, your agent might be some representation of a car that is trying to figure out what actions to take in order to arrive at a destination.

66
00:07:38,704 --> 00:07:47,104
In the case of the 15 puzzle with the sliding tiles, the agent might be the AI or the person that is trying to solve that puzzle, to try and figure out what tiles to move

67
00:07:47,278 --> 00:07:49,060
in order to get to that solution.

68
00:07:49,966 --> 00:07:52,100
Next, we introduced the idea of a state.

69
00:07:52,654 --> 00:07:56,612
A state is just some configuration of the agent in its environment.

70
00:07:57,102 --> 00:08:01,316
So in the 15 puzzle, for example, any state might be any one of these three, for example.

71
00:08:01,584 --> 00:08:08,612
A state is just some configuration of the tiles, and each of these states is different and is going to require a slightly different solution.

72
00:08:08,784 --> 00:08:16,676
A different sequence of actions will be needed in each one of these in order to get from this initial state to the goal, which is where we're trying to get.

73
00:08:17,422 --> 00:08:18,384
So the initial state then.

74
00:08:18,464 --> 00:08:18,944
What is that?

75
00:08:19,214 --> 00:08:21,984
The initial state is just the state where the agent begins.

76
00:08:22,144 --> 00:08:24,612
It is one such state where we're going to start from.

77
00:08:24,846 --> 00:08:28,420
And this is going to be the starting point for our search algorithm, so to speak.

78
00:08:28,686 --> 00:08:35,332
We're going to begin with this initial state and then start to reason about it, to think about what actions might we apply to that initial state

79
00:08:35,662 --> 00:08:41,904
in order to figure out how to get from the beginning to the end, from the initial position to whatever our goal happens to be.

80
00:08:42,830 --> 00:08:45,456
And how do we make our way from that initial position to the goal?

81
00:08:45,678 --> 00:08:47,748
Well, ultimately, it's via taking actions.

82
00:08:48,078 --> 00:08:51,044
Actions are just choices that we can make in any given state.

83
00:08:51,534 --> 00:08:59,216
And in AI, we're always going to try to formalize these ideas a little bit more precisely, such that we could program them a little bit more mathematically, so to speak.

84
00:08:59,502 --> 00:09:00,772
So this will be a recurring theme.

85
00:09:01,006 --> 00:09:04,196
And we can more precisely define actions as a function.

86
00:09:04,782 --> 00:09:13,188
We're going to effectively define a function called actions that takes an input S, where S is going to be some state that exists inside of our environment.

87
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:22,020
And actions of S is going to take this state as input and return as output the set of all actions that can be executed in that state.

88
00:09:22,510 --> 00:09:27,332
And so it's possible that some actions are only valid in certain states and not in other states.

89
00:09:27,630 --> 00:09:29,572
And we'll see examples of that soon too.

90
00:09:30,382 --> 00:09:36,496
So in the case of the 15 puzzle, for example, there are generally going to be four possible actions that we can do most of the time.

91
00:09:36,750 --> 00:09:42,016
We can slide a tile to the right, slide a tile to the left, slide a tile up, or slide a tile down, for example.

92
00:09:42,254 --> 00:09:45,216
And those are going to be the actions that are available to us.

93
00:09:45,870 --> 00:09:53,700
So somehow our AI, our program, needs some encoding of the state, which is often going to be in some numerical format, and some encoding of these actions,

94
00:09:54,094 --> 00:09:56,868
but it also needs some encoding of the relationship between these things.

95
00:09:57,136 --> 00:10:00,036
How do the states and actions relate to one another?

96
00:10:00,590 --> 00:10:10,692
And in order to do that, we'll introduce to our AI a transition model, which will be a description of what state we get after we perform some available action in some other state.

97
00:10:11,278 --> 00:10:17,284
And again, we can be a little bit more precise about this, define this transition model a little bit more formally, again, as a function.

98
00:10:17,742 --> 00:10:21,636
The function is going to be a function called result that this time takes two inputs.

99
00:10:22,158 --> 00:10:27,652
Input number one is S, some state, and input number two is A, some action.

100
00:10:28,238 --> 00:10:35,908
And the output of this function result is it is going to give us the state that we get after we perform action A in state S.

101
00:10:36,942 --> 00:10:40,176
So let's take a look at an example to see more precisely what this actually means.

102
00:10:40,494 --> 00:10:43,616
Here's an example of a state of the 15 puzzle, for example.

103
00:10:43,886 --> 00:10:46,884
And here's an example of an action, sliding a tile to the right.

104
00:10:47,470 --> 00:10:50,436
What happens if we pass these as inputs to the result function?

105
00:10:50,734 --> 00:10:57,412
Again, the result function takes this board, this state, as its first input, and it takes an action as a second input.

106
00:10:57,678 --> 00:11:02,656
And of course, here I'm describing things visually so that you can see visually what the state is and what the action is.

107
00:11:02,990 --> 00:11:10,976
In a computer, you might represent one of these actions as just some number that represents the action, or if you're familiar with enums that allow you to enumerate multiple possibilities,

108
00:11:11,056 --> 00:11:12,176
it might be something like that.

109
00:11:12,430 --> 00:11:18,096
And this state might just be represented as an array or two-dimensional array of all of these numbers that exist.

110
00:11:18,318 --> 00:11:20,612
But here we're going to show it visually just so you can see it.

111
00:11:21,454 --> 00:11:26,948
But when we take this state and this action, pass it into the result function, the output is a new state.

112
00:11:27,310 --> 00:11:32,004
The state we get after we take a tile and slide it to the right, and this is the state we get as a result.

113
00:11:32,462 --> 00:11:38,980
If we had a different action and a different state, for example, and pass that into the result function, we'd get a different answer altogether.

114
00:11:39,470 --> 00:11:45,796
So the result function needs to take care of figuring out how to take a state and take an action and get what results,

115
00:11:46,096 --> 00:11:52,452
and this is going to be our transition model that describes how it is that states and actions are related to each other.

116
00:11:53,326 --> 00:12:00,420
If we take this transition model and think about it more generally and across the entire problem, we can form what we might call a state space,

117
00:12:00,846 --> 00:12:09,156
the set of all of the states we can get from the initial state via any sequence of actions, by taking zero or one or two or more actions in addition to that.

118
00:12:09,518 --> 00:12:15,044
So we could draw a diagram that looks something like this, where every state is represented here by a game board.

119
00:12:15,598 --> 00:12:20,484
And there are arrows that connect every state to every other state we can get to from that state.

120
00:12:20,718 --> 00:12:23,616
And the state space is much larger than what you see just here.

121
00:12:23,790 --> 00:12:27,332
This is just a sample of what the state space might actually look like.

122
00:12:28,110 --> 00:12:36,260
And in general, across many search problems, whether they're this particular 15 puzzle or driving directions or something else, the state space is going to look something like this.

123
00:12:36,590 --> 00:12:40,388
We have individual states and arrows that are connecting them.

124
00:12:40,974 --> 00:12:48,292
And oftentimes, just for simplicity, we'll simplify our representation of this entire thing as a graph, some sequence of nodes

125
00:12:48,942 --> 00:12:50,336
and edges that connect nodes.

126
00:12:50,574 --> 00:12:54,336
But you can think of this more abstract representation as the exact same idea.

127
00:12:54,638 --> 00:12:59,616
Each of these little circles or nodes is going to represent one of the states inside of our problem.

128
00:12:59,854 --> 00:13:07,972
And the arrows here represent the actions that we can take in any particular state, taking us from one particular state to another state,

129
00:13:08,302 --> 00:13:08,644
for example.

130
00:13:10,220 --> 00:13:10,480
All right.

131
00:13:10,798 --> 00:13:16,576
so now we have this idea of nodes that are representing these states, actions that can take us from one state to another,

132
00:13:16,750 --> 00:13:20,836
and a transition model that defines what happens after we take a particular action.

133
00:13:21,358 --> 00:13:26,256
So the next step we need to figure out is how we know when the AI is done solving the problem.

134
00:13:26,574 --> 00:13:30,404
The AI needs some way to know when it gets to the goal that it's found the goal.

135
00:13:30,926 --> 00:13:34,884
So the next thing we'll need to encode into our artificial intelligence is a goal test.

136
00:13:35,470 --> 00:13:38,596
Some way to determine whether a given state is a goal state.

137
00:13:39,342 --> 00:13:42,496
In the case of something like driving directions, it might be pretty easy.

138
00:13:42,702 --> 00:13:48,964
If you're in a state that corresponds to whatever the user typed in as their intended destination, well then you know you're in a goal state.

139
00:13:49,166 --> 00:13:52,816
In the 15 puzzle, it might be checking the numbers to make sure they're all in ascending order.

140
00:13:53,038 --> 00:13:57,924
But the AI needs some way to encode whether or not any state they happen to be in is a goal.

141
00:13:58,286 --> 00:14:04,096
And some problems might have one goal, like a maze where you have one initial position and one ending position, and that's the goal.

142
00:14:04,366 --> 00:14:13,616
In other, more complex problems, you might imagine that there are multiple possible goals, that there are multiple ways to solve a problem, and we might not care which one the computer finds

143
00:14:13,776 --> 00:14:16,260
as long as it does find a particular goal.

144
00:14:17,326 --> 00:14:23,588
However, sometimes a computer doesn't just care about finding a goal, but finding a goal well, or one with a low cost.

145
00:14:24,046 --> 00:14:30,116
And it's for that reason that the last piece of terminology that we'll use to define these search problems is something called a path cost.

146
00:14:30,670 --> 00:14:37,376
You might imagine that in the case of driving directions, it would be pretty annoying if I said I wanted directions from point A to point B,

147
00:14:37,582 --> 00:14:45,092
and the route the Google Maps gave me was a long route with lots of detours that were unnecessary that took longer than it should have for me to get to that destination.

148
00:14:45,518 --> 00:14:51,620
And it's for that reason that when we're formulating search problems, we'll often give every path some sort of numerical cost,

149
00:14:52,110 --> 00:15:00,336
some number telling us how expensive it is to take this particular option, and then tell our AI that instead of just finding a solution,

150
00:15:00,558 --> 00:15:11,748
some way of getting from the initial state to the goal, we'd really like to find one that minimizes this path cost, that is less expensive or takes less time or minimizes some other numerical value.

151
00:15:12,494 --> 00:15:21,188
We can represent this graphically if we take a look at this graph again, and imagine that each of these arrows, each of these actions that we can take from one state to another state,

152
00:15:21,550 --> 00:15:23,524
has some sort of number associated with it.

153
00:15:23,776 --> 00:15:32,336
That number being the path cost of this particular action, where some of the costs for any particular action might be more expensive than the cost for some other action,

154
00:15:32,496 --> 00:15:33,060
for example.

155
00:15:33,518 --> 00:15:35,716
Although this will only happen in some sorts of problems.

156
00:15:36,110 --> 00:15:42,308
In other problems, we can simplify the diagram and just assume that the cost of any particular action is the same.

157
00:15:42,606 --> 00:15:49,764
And this is probably the case in something like the 15 puzzle, for example, where it doesn't really make a difference whether I'm moving right or moving left.

158
00:15:49,936 --> 00:15:58,736
The only thing that matters is the total number of steps that I have to take to get from point A to point B, and each of those steps is of equal cost.

159
00:15:58,896 --> 00:16:02,084
We can just assume it's of some constant cost like one.

160
00:16:03,246 --> 00:16:07,428
And so this now forms the basis for what we might consider to be a search problem.

161
00:16:07,696 --> 00:16:15,812
A search problem has some sort of initial state, some place where we begin, some sort of action that we can take, or multiple actions that we can take in any given state,

162
00:16:16,270 --> 00:16:24,548
and it has a transition model, some way of defining what happens when we go from one state and take one action, what state do we end up with as a result?

163
00:16:25,134 --> 00:16:29,376
In addition to that, we need some goal test to know whether or not we've reached a goal.

164
00:16:29,614 --> 00:16:37,536
And then we need a path cost function that tells us for any particular path, by following some sequence of actions, how expensive is that path.

165
00:16:37,696 --> 00:16:43,556
What is its cost in terms of money or time or some other resource that we are trying to minimize our usage of?

166
00:16:44,238 --> 00:16:46,736
And the goal ultimately is to find a solution.

167
00:16:47,054 --> 00:16:52,932
Where a solution in this case is just some sequence of actions that will take us from the initial state to the goal state.

168
00:16:53,166 --> 00:17:02,596
And ideally, we'd like to find not just any solution, but the optimal solution, which is a solution that has the lowest path cost among all of the possible solutions.

169
00:17:02,926 --> 00:17:10,976
And in some cases there might be multiple optimal solutions, but an optimal solution just means that there is no way that we could have done better in terms of finding that solution.

170
00:17:12,366 --> 00:17:13,668
So now we've defined the problem.

171
00:17:13,870 --> 00:17:18,500
And now we need to begin to figure out how it is that we're going to solve this kind of search problem.

172
00:17:18,926 --> 00:17:26,016
And in order to do so, you'll probably imagine that our computer is going to need to represent a whole bunch of data about this particular problem.

173
00:17:26,176 --> 00:17:31,940
We need to represent data about where we are in the problem, and we might need to be considering multiple different options at once.

174
00:17:32,462 --> 00:17:40,324
And oftentimes when we're trying to package a whole bunch of data related to a state together, we'll do so using a data structure that we're going to call a node.

175
00:17:40,718 --> 00:17:44,896
A node is a data structure that is just going to keep track of a variety of different values.

176
00:17:45,070 --> 00:17:50,116
And specifically in the case of a search problem, it's going to keep track of these four values in particular.

177
00:17:50,606 --> 00:17:54,052
Every node is going to keep track of a state, the state we're currently on.

178
00:17:54,542 --> 00:17:57,060
And every node is also going to keep track of a parent.

179
00:17:57,518 --> 00:18:03,108
A parent being the state before us, or the node that we used in order to get to this current state.

180
00:18:03,598 --> 00:18:12,708
And this is going to be relevant because eventually, once we reach the goal node, once we get to the end, we want to know what sequence of actions we use in order to get to that goal.

181
00:18:13,070 --> 00:18:21,376
And the way we'll know that is by looking at these parents to keep track of what led us to the goal and what led us to that state and what led us to the state before that,

182
00:18:21,646 --> 00:18:29,700
so on and so forth, backtracking our way to the beginning so that we know the entire sequence of actions we needed in order to get from the beginning to the end.

183
00:18:30,734 --> 00:18:35,748
The node is also going to keep track of what action we took in order to get from the parent to the current state.

184
00:18:36,078 --> 00:18:39,108
And the node is also going to keep track of a path cost.

185
00:18:39,566 --> 00:18:47,856
In other words, it's going to keep track of the number that represents how long it took to get from the initial state to the state that we currently happen to be at.

186
00:18:48,142 --> 00:18:54,276
And we'll see why this is relevant as we start to talk about some of the optimizations that we can make in terms of these search problems more generally.

187
00:18:55,566 --> 00:18:58,576
So this is the data structure that we're going to use in order to solve the problem.

188
00:18:58,862 --> 00:19:00,336
And now let's talk about the approach.

189
00:19:00,590 --> 00:19:03,428
How might we actually begin to solve the problem?

190
00:19:03,950 --> 00:19:10,336
Well, as you might imagine, what we're going to do is we're going to start at one particular state and we're just going to explore from there.

191
00:19:10,702 --> 00:19:16,260
The intuition is that from a given state, we have multiple options that we could take, and we're going to explore those options.

192
00:19:16,782 --> 00:19:21,828
And once we explore those options, we'll find that more options than that are going to make themselves available.

193
00:19:22,286 --> 00:19:28,868
And we're going to consider all of the available options to be stored inside of a single data structure that we'll call the frontier.

194
00:19:29,294 --> 00:19:36,036
The frontier is going to represent all of the things that we could explore next that we haven't yet explored or visited.

195
00:19:36,782 --> 00:19:42,596
So in our approach, we're going to begin the search algorithm by starting with a frontier that just contains one state.

196
00:19:43,022 --> 00:19:47,856
The frontier is going to contain the initial state, because at the beginning, that's the only state we know about.

197
00:19:48,016 --> 00:19:49,348
That is the only state that exists.

198
00:19:50,318 --> 00:19:53,508
And then our search algorithm is effectively going to follow a loop.

199
00:19:53,774 --> 00:19:56,708
We're going to repeat some process again and again and again.

200
00:19:57,358 --> 00:20:02,256
The first thing we're going to do is if the frontier is empty, then there's no solution.

201
00:20:02,478 --> 00:20:05,136
And we can report that there is no way to get to the goal.

202
00:20:05,326 --> 00:20:06,336
And that's certainly possible.

203
00:20:06,574 --> 00:20:12,576
There are certain types of problems that an AI might try to explore and realize that there is no way to solve that problem.

204
00:20:12,814 --> 00:20:15,172
And that's useful information for humans to know as well.

205
00:20:15,566 --> 00:20:19,268
So if ever the frontier is empty, that means there's nothing left to explore.

206
00:20:19,502 --> 00:20:24,260
And we haven't yet found a solution, so there is no solution there's nothing left to explore.

207
00:20:25,102 --> 00:20:28,612
Otherwise, what we'll do is we'll remove a node from the frontier.

208
00:20:28,974 --> 00:20:33,508
So right now at the beginning, the frontier just contains one node representing the initial state.

209
00:20:33,902 --> 00:20:36,772
But over time, the frontier might grow. It might contain multiple states.

210
00:20:37,056 --> 00:20:40,612
And so here we're just going to remove a single node from that frontier.

211
00:20:41,710 --> 00:20:44,612
If that node happens to be a goal, then we found a solution.

212
00:20:45,006 --> 00:20:48,256
So we remove a node from the frontier and ask ourselves, is this the goal?

213
00:20:48,366 --> 00:20:56,676
And we do that by applying the goal test that we talked about earlier, asking if we're at the destination, or asking if all the numbers of the 15 puzzle happen to be in order.

214
00:20:57,166 --> 00:21:00,868
So if the node contains the goal, we found a solution, great, we're done.

215
00:21:01,856 --> 00:21:06,416
And otherwise, what we'll need to do is we'll need to expand the node.

216
00:21:06,670 --> 00:21:08,772
And this is a term of art in artificial intelligence.

217
00:21:08,976 --> 00:21:12,736
To expand the node just means to look at all of the neighbors of that node.

218
00:21:12,896 --> 00:21:20,836
In other words, consider all of the possible actions that I could take from the state that this node is representing, and what nodes could I get to from there.

219
00:21:21,294 --> 00:21:27,556
We're going to take all of those nodes, the next nodes that I can get to from this current one I'm looking at, and add those to the frontier.

220
00:21:28,270 --> 00:21:29,508
And then we'll repeat this process.

221
00:21:30,382 --> 00:21:37,796
So at a very high level, the idea is: we start with a frontier that contains the initial state, and we're constantly removing a node from the frontier,

222
00:21:38,158 --> 00:21:41,796
looking at where we can get to next, and adding those nodes to the frontier.

223
00:21:42,126 --> 00:21:47,456
repeating this process over and over until either we remove a node from the frontier, and it contains a goal.

224
00:21:47,630 --> 00:21:54,372
meaning we've solved the problem, or we run into a situation where the frontier is empty, at which point we're left with no solution.

225
00:21:55,534 --> 00:22:01,636
So let's actually try and take the pseudocode, put it into practice by taking a look at an example of a sample search problem.

226
00:22:02,318 --> 00:22:04,112
So right here, I have a sample graph.

227
00:22:04,272 --> 00:22:10,212
A is connected to B via this action, B is connected to node C and D, C is connected to E, D is connected to F.

228
00:22:10,862 --> 00:22:16,164
And what I'd like to do is have my AI find a path from A to E.

229
00:22:16,558 --> 00:22:20,100
We want to get from this initial state to this goal state.

230
00:22:21,006 --> 00:22:22,272
So how are we going to do that?

231
00:22:22,542 --> 00:22:27,140
Well, we're going to start with the frontier that contains the initial state. This is going to represent our frontier.

232
00:22:27,598 --> 00:22:31,972
So our frontier initially will just contain A, that initial state where we're going to begin.

233
00:22:32,622 --> 00:22:33,988
And now we'll repeat this process.

234
00:22:34,446 --> 00:22:36,192
If the frontier is empty, no solution.

235
00:22:36,430 --> 00:22:38,404
That's not a problem, because the frontier is not empty.

236
00:22:38,958 --> 00:22:42,468
So we'll remove a node from the frontier as the one to consider next.

237
00:22:43,086 --> 00:22:46,564
There's only one node in the frontier, so we'll go ahead and remove it from the frontier.

238
00:22:46,862 --> 00:22:50,692
But now A, this initial node, this is the node we're currently considering.

239
00:22:51,470 --> 00:22:54,912
We follow the next step, we ask ourselves, is this node the goal?

240
00:22:55,246 --> 00:22:55,712
No, it's not.

241
00:22:55,952 --> 00:22:57,572
A is not the goal, E is the goal.

242
00:22:58,126 --> 00:22:59,364
So we don't return the solution.

243
00:22:59,790 --> 00:23:05,156
So instead we go to this last step, expand the node, and add the resulting nodes to the frontier.

244
00:23:05,934 --> 00:23:06,592
What does that mean?

245
00:23:06,958 --> 00:23:10,500
Well, it means take this state A and consider where we could get to next.

246
00:23:11,022 --> 00:23:13,732
And after A, what we could get to next is only B.

247
00:23:14,286 --> 00:23:21,828
So that's what we get when we expand A, we find B, and we add B to the frontier, and now B is in the frontier, and we repeat the process again.

248
00:23:22,318 --> 00:23:25,956
We say, all right, the frontier is not empty, so let's remove B from the frontier.

249
00:23:26,478 --> 00:23:28,032
B is now the node that we're considering.

250
00:23:28,270 --> 00:23:29,952
We ask ourselves, is B the goal?

251
00:23:30,126 --> 00:23:30,788
No, it's not.

252
00:23:31,182 --> 00:23:35,172
So we go ahead and expand B and add its resulting nodes to the frontier.

253
00:23:35,982 --> 00:23:37,472
What happens when we expand B?

254
00:23:37,646 --> 00:23:40,324
In other words, what nodes can we get to from B?

255
00:23:40,750 --> 00:23:44,804
Well, we can get to C and D, so we'll go ahead and add C and D from the frontier.

256
00:23:45,070 --> 00:23:48,772
And now we have two nodes in the frontier, C and D, and we repeat the process again.

257
00:23:49,166 --> 00:23:50,592
We remove a node from the frontier.

258
00:23:50,830 --> 00:23:53,060
For now, I'll do so arbitrarily just by picking C.

259
00:23:53,230 --> 00:23:58,500
We'll see why later, how choosing which node you remove from the frontier is actually quite an important part of the algorithm.

260
00:23:58,830 --> 00:24:04,912
But for now I'll arbitrarily remove C, say it's not the goal, so we'll add E, the next one, to the frontier.

261
00:24:05,262 --> 00:24:09,872
Then let's say I remove E from the frontier, and now I check I'm currently looking at state E.

262
00:24:10,318 --> 00:24:11,428
Is it a goal state?

263
00:24:11,632 --> 00:24:15,428
It is, because I'm trying to find a path from A to E, so I would return the goal.

264
00:24:15,726 --> 00:24:17,872
And that now would be the solution.

265
00:24:18,126 --> 00:24:21,956
That I'm now able to return the solution, and I have found a path from A to E.

266
00:24:23,310 --> 00:24:31,844
So this is the general idea, the general approach of this search algorithm, to follow these steps, constantly removing nodes from the frontier, until we're able to find a solution.

267
00:24:32,462 --> 00:24:36,352
So the next question you might reasonably ask is what could go wrong here?

268
00:24:36,590 --> 00:24:39,524
What are the potential problems with an approach like this?

269
00:24:40,046 --> 00:24:43,620
And here's one example of a problem that could arise from this sort of approach.

270
00:24:43,952 --> 00:24:47,552
Imagine this same graph, same as before, with one change.

271
00:24:47,950 --> 00:24:54,692
The change being now, instead of just an arrow from A to B, we also have an arrow from B to A, meaning we can go in both directions.

272
00:24:55,118 --> 00:25:02,832
And this is true in something like the 15 puzzle, where when I slide a tile to the right, I could then slide a tile to the left to get back to the original position.

273
00:25:02,992 --> 00:25:07,392
I could go back and forth between A and B, and that's what these double arrows symbolize.

274
00:25:07,552 --> 00:25:10,916
The idea that from one state I can get to another, and then I can get back.

275
00:25:11,310 --> 00:25:12,676
And that's true in many search problems.

276
00:25:13,550 --> 00:25:16,672
What's going to happen if I try to apply the same approach now?

277
00:25:16,974 --> 00:25:23,556
Well, I'll begin with A, same as before, and I'll remove A from the frontier, and then I'll consider where I can get to from A.

278
00:25:23,886 --> 00:25:28,036
And after A, the only place I can get to is B, so B goes into the frontier.

279
00:25:28,718 --> 00:25:30,212
Then I'll say, all right, let's take a look at B.

280
00:25:30,352 --> 00:25:31,908
That's the only thing left in the frontier.

281
00:25:32,142 --> 00:25:33,540
Where can I get to from B?

282
00:25:34,126 --> 00:25:40,868
Before it was just C and D, but now because of that reverse arrow, I can get to A or C or D.

283
00:25:41,102 --> 00:25:44,992
So all three, A, C, and D, all of those now go into the frontier.

284
00:25:45,072 --> 00:25:47,300
They are places I can get to from B.

285
00:25:47,790 --> 00:25:51,620
And now I remove one from the frontier, and maybe I'm unlucky, and maybe I pick A.

286
00:25:52,078 --> 00:25:53,252
And now I'm looking at A again.

287
00:25:53,806 --> 00:25:56,964
And I consider where can I get to from A, and from A, well, I can get to B.

288
00:25:57,454 --> 00:26:02,992
And now we start to see the problem. That if I'm not careful, I go from A to B and then back to A and then to B again.

289
00:26:03,150 --> 00:26:10,672
And I could be going in this infinite loop where I never make any progress, because I'm constantly just going back and forth between two states that I've already

290
00:26:10,672 --> 00:26:12,612
seen. So what is the solution to this?

291
00:26:12,752 --> 00:26:14,752
We need some way to deal with this problem.

292
00:26:15,054 --> 00:26:20,228
And the way that we can deal with this problem is by somehow keeping track of what we've already explored.

293
00:26:20,590 --> 00:26:25,392
And the logic is going to be well, if we've already explored the state, there's no reason to go back to it.

294
00:26:25,614 --> 00:26:30,596
Once we've explored a state, don't go back to it, don't bother adding it to the frontier, there's no need to.

295
00:26:31,566 --> 00:26:36,068
So here is going to be our revised approach, a better way to approach this sort of search problem.

296
00:26:36,430 --> 00:26:39,044
And it's going to look very similar, just with a couple of modifications.

297
00:26:39,982 --> 00:26:43,620
We'll start with a frontier that contains the initial state, same as before.

298
00:26:44,142 --> 00:26:49,936
But now we'll start with another data structure, which will just be a set of nodes that we've already explored.

299
00:26:50,318 --> 00:26:51,696
So what are the states we've explored?

300
00:26:51,856 --> 00:26:52,900
Initially, it's empty.

301
00:26:53,198 --> 00:26:54,948
We have an empty explored set.

302
00:26:55,694 --> 00:26:56,484
And now we repeat.

303
00:26:57,230 --> 00:26:59,844
If the frontier is empty, no solution, same as before.

304
00:27:00,366 --> 00:27:06,148
We remove a node from the frontier, we check to see if it's a goal state, return the solution. None of this is any different so far.

305
00:27:06,702 --> 00:27:11,108
But now what we're going to do is we're going to add the node to the explored state.

306
00:27:11,822 --> 00:27:20,176
So if it happens to be the case that we remove a node from the frontier and it's not the goal, we'll add it to the explored set so that we know we've already explored it.

307
00:27:20,256 --> 00:27:23,136
We don't need to go back to it again if it happens to come up

308
00:27:23,136 --> 00:27:28,676
later. And then the final step, we expand the node and we add the resulting nodes to the frontier.

309
00:27:29,198 --> 00:27:33,956
But before we just always added the resulting nodes to the frontier. We're going to be a little clever about it this time.

310
00:27:34,256 --> 00:27:42,148
We're only going to add the nodes to the frontier if they aren't already in the frontier, and if they aren't already in the explored set.

311
00:27:42,862 --> 00:27:51,556
So we'll check both the frontier and the explored set, make sure that the node isn't already in one of those two, and so long as it isn't, then we'll go ahead and add it to the frontier,

312
00:27:51,856 --> 00:27:52,740
but not otherwise.

313
00:27:53,582 --> 00:27:59,204
And so that revised approach is ultimately what's going to help make sure that we don't go back and forth between two nodes.

314
00:28:00,430 --> 00:28:06,564
Now, the one point that I've kind of glossed over here so far is this step here, removing a node from the frontier.

315
00:28:06,766 --> 00:28:10,276
Before I just chose arbitrarily, like let's just remove a node and that's it.

316
00:28:10,702 --> 00:28:17,316
But it turns out it's actually quite important how we decide to structure our frontier, how we add and how we remove our nodes.

317
00:28:17,806 --> 00:28:23,044
The frontier is a data structure, and we need to make a choice about in what order are we going to be removing elements.

318
00:28:24,046 --> 00:28:28,772
And one of the simplest data structures for adding and removing elements is something called a stack.

319
00:28:29,166 --> 00:28:40,484
And a stack is a data structure that is a last in, first out data type, which means the last thing that I add to the frontier is going to be the first thing that I remove from the frontier.

320
00:28:40,814 --> 00:28:46,884
So the most recent thing to go into the stack or the frontier in this case is going to be the node that I explore.

321
00:28:47,694 --> 00:28:55,236
So let's see what happens if I apply this stack-based approach to something like this problem: finding a path from A to E.

322
00:28:55,854 --> 00:28:56,576
What's going to happen?

323
00:28:56,782 --> 00:29:00,548
Well, again, we'll start with A, and we'll say, all right, let's go ahead and look at A first.

324
00:29:00,910 --> 00:29:04,736
And then notice this time, we've added A to the explored set.

325
00:29:05,006 --> 00:29:06,404
A is something we've now explored.

326
00:29:06,576 --> 00:29:08,548
We have this data structure that's keeping track.

327
00:29:09,294 --> 00:29:11,396
We then say from A, we can get to B.

328
00:29:12,014 --> 00:29:18,016
And all right, from B, what can we do? Well, from B, we can explore B and get to both C and D.

329
00:29:18,222 --> 00:29:20,100
So we added C and then D.

330
00:29:20,718 --> 00:29:26,148
So now, when we explore a node, we're going to treat the frontier as a stack, last in, first out.

331
00:29:26,510 --> 00:29:29,696
D was the last one to come in, so we'll go ahead and explore that

332
00:29:29,696 --> 00:29:32,256
next and say, all right, where can we get to from D?

333
00:29:32,430 --> 00:29:33,316
Well, we can get to F.

334
00:29:34,158 --> 00:29:36,580
And so, all right, we'll explore it, put F into the frontier.

335
00:29:37,166 --> 00:29:42,084
And now, because the frontier is a stack, F is the most recent thing that's gone in the stack.

336
00:29:42,542 --> 00:29:43,908
So F is what we'll explore next.

337
00:29:44,016 --> 00:29:47,044
We'll explore F and say, all right, where can we get to from F?

338
00:29:47,598 --> 00:29:50,116
Well, we can't get anywhere, so nothing gets added to the frontier.

339
00:29:50,798 --> 00:29:53,508
So now what was the new most recent thing added to the frontier?

340
00:29:53,838 --> 00:29:56,068
Well, it's now C, the only thing left in the frontier.

341
00:29:56,462 --> 00:30:04,868
We'll explore that, from which we can see, all right, from C we can get to E, so E goes into the frontier, and then we say, all right, let's look at E, and E is now the solution,

342
00:30:05,070 --> 00:30:06,788
and now we've solved the problem.

343
00:30:07,662 --> 00:30:13,380
So when we treat the frontier like a stack, a last in, first out data structure, that's the result we get.

344
00:30:13,646 --> 00:30:16,100
We go from A to B to D to F,

345
00:30:16,814 --> 00:30:23,216
and then we sort of backed up and went down to C and then E. And it's important to get a visual sense for how this algorithm is working.

346
00:30:23,376 --> 00:30:33,284
We went very deep in this search tree, so to speak, all the way until the bottom, where we hit a dead end. And then we effectively backed up and explored this other route that we didn't try before.

347
00:30:33,678 --> 00:30:42,020
And it's this going very deep in the search tree idea, this way the algorithm ends up working when we use a stack, that we call this version of the algorithm

348
00:30:42,350 --> 00:30:43,776
depth first search.

349
00:30:44,206 --> 00:30:49,776
Depth first search is the search algorithm where we always explore the deepest node in the frontier.

350
00:30:49,998 --> 00:30:56,452
We keep going deeper and deeper through our search tree, and then if we hit a dead end, we back up and we try something else instead.

351
00:30:57,710 --> 00:31:01,252
But depth for search is just one of the possible search options that we could use.

352
00:31:01,742 --> 00:31:08,676
It turns out that there's another algorithm called breadth first search, which behaves very similarly to depth for search, with one difference.

353
00:31:09,040 --> 00:31:18,532
Instead of always exploring the deepest node in the search tree, the way the depth for search does, breadth for search is always going to explore the shallowest node in the frontier.

354
00:31:19,310 --> 00:31:20,000
So what does that mean?

355
00:31:20,238 --> 00:31:28,800
Well, it means that instead of using a stack, which depth for search or DFS used, where the most recent item added to the frontier is the one we'll explore.

356
00:31:28,800 --> 00:31:40,640
next, in breadth for search, or BFS, we'll instead use a queue, where a queue is a first in, first out data type, where the very first thing we add to the frontier is the first one.

357
00:31:40,640 --> 00:31:48,132
we'll explore, and they effectively form a line or a queue, where the earlier you arrive in the frontier, the earlier you get explored.

358
00:31:49,198 --> 00:31:53,060
So what would that mean for the same exact problem, finding a path from A to E?

359
00:31:53,902 --> 00:31:55,524
Well, we start with A, same as before.

360
00:31:56,334 --> 00:31:59,200
Then we'll go ahead and have explored A and say, where can we get to from A?

361
00:31:59,374 --> 00:32:01,760
Well, from A we can get to B, same as before.

362
00:32:02,094 --> 00:32:06,404
From B, same as before, we can get to C and D, so C and D get added to the frontier.

363
00:32:06,958 --> 00:32:11,908
This time though, we added C to the frontier before D, so we'll explore C first.

364
00:32:12,718 --> 00:32:13,828
So C gets explored.

365
00:32:14,318 --> 00:32:15,908
And from C, where can we get to?

366
00:32:16,174 --> 00:32:17,092
Well, we can get to E.

367
00:32:17,518 --> 00:32:23,556
So E gets added to the frontier, but because D was explored before E, we'll look at D next.

368
00:32:24,302 --> 00:32:27,268
So we'll explore D and say, where can we get to from D? We can get to F.

369
00:32:27,758 --> 00:32:30,788
And only then will we say, all right, now we can get to E.

370
00:32:31,598 --> 00:32:39,440
And so what breadth for search or BFS did is we started here, we looked at both C and D, and then we looked at E.

371
00:32:39,630 --> 00:32:48,160
Effectively, we're looking at things one away from the initial state, then two away from the initial state, and only then things that are three away from the initial state.

372
00:32:48,366 --> 00:32:56,100
Unlike depth for search, which just went as deep as possible into the search tree until it hit a dead end, and then ultimately had to back up.

373
00:32:56,846 --> 00:33:01,668
So these now are two different search algorithms that we could apply in order to try and solve a problem.

374
00:33:01,934 --> 00:33:07,492
And let's take a look at how these would actually work in practice with something like maze solving, for example.

375
00:33:07,822 --> 00:33:09,200
So here's an example of a maze.

376
00:33:09,360 --> 00:33:12,388
These empty cells represent places where our agent can move.

377
00:33:12,590 --> 00:33:16,880
These darkened gray cells represent walls that the agent can't pass through.

378
00:33:17,040 --> 00:33:26,532
And ultimately, our agent, our AI, is going to try to find a way to get from position A to position B via some sequence of actions, where those actions are left,

379
00:33:26,880 --> 00:33:28,132
right, up, and down.

380
00:33:28,942 --> 00:33:30,916
What will depth first search do in this case?

381
00:33:31,406 --> 00:33:33,732
Well, depth first search will just follow one path.

382
00:33:34,222 --> 00:33:39,920
If it reaches a fork in the road where it has multiple different options, depth first search is just in this case going to choose one.

383
00:33:40,174 --> 00:33:41,252
It hasn't a real preference.

384
00:33:41,614 --> 00:33:44,548
But it's going to keep following one until it hits a dead

385
00:33:45,230 --> 00:33:54,212
end. And when it hits a dead end, depth first search effectively goes back to the last decision point and tries the other path, fully exhausting this entire path.

386
00:33:54,478 --> 00:33:58,468
And when it realizes that, okay, the goal is not here, then it turns its attention to this path.

387
00:33:58,734 --> 00:34:00,240
It goes as deep as possible.

388
00:34:00,590 --> 00:34:04,228
When it hits a dead end, it backs up and then tries this other path.

389
00:34:05,070 --> 00:34:10,724
Keeps going as deep as possible down one particular path, and when it realizes that that's a dead end, then it'll back up

390
00:34:11,406 --> 00:34:13,636
and then ultimately find its way to the goal.

391
00:34:14,094 --> 00:34:20,560
And maybe you got lucky and maybe you made a different choice earlier on, but ultimately this is how depth first search is going to work.

392
00:34:20,686 --> 00:34:26,468
It's going to keep following until it hits a dead end, and when it hits a dead end, it backs up and looks for a different solution.

393
00:34:26,990 --> 00:34:30,880
And so one thing you might reasonably ask is, is this algorithm always going to work?

394
00:34:31,040 --> 00:34:34,788
Will it always actually find a way to get from the initial state to the goal?

395
00:34:35,150 --> 00:34:44,548
And it turns out that as long as our maze is finite, as long as there are only finitely many spaces where we can travel, then yes, depth first search is going to find a solution.

396
00:34:44,814 --> 00:34:46,948
Because eventually it'll just explore everything.

397
00:34:47,246 --> 00:34:54,000
If the maze happens to be infinite and there's an infinite state space, which does exist in certain types of problems, then it's a slightly different story.

398
00:34:54,254 --> 00:34:58,084
But as long as our maze has finitely many squares, we're going to find a solution.

399
00:34:59,214 --> 00:35:02,820
The next question, though, that we want to ask is: is it going to be a good solution?

400
00:35:03,022 --> 00:35:05,156
Is it the optimal solution that we can find?

401
00:35:05,710 --> 00:35:07,908
And the answer there is not necessarily.

402
00:35:08,206 --> 00:35:09,792
And let's take a look at an example of that.

403
00:35:10,032 --> 00:35:14,436
In this maze, for example, we're again trying to find our way from A to B.

404
00:35:14,862 --> 00:35:17,392
And you notice here there are multiple possible solutions.

405
00:35:17,552 --> 00:35:21,828
We could go this way or we could go up in order to make our way from A to B.

406
00:35:22,222 --> 00:35:25,892
Now, if we're lucky, depth first search will choose this way and get to B.

407
00:35:26,126 --> 00:35:31,312
But there's no reason necessarily why depth first search would choose between going up or going to the right.

408
00:35:31,438 --> 00:35:35,620
It's sort of an arbitrary decision point because both are going to be added to the frontier.

409
00:35:36,366 --> 00:35:43,140
And ultimately, if we get unlucky, depth first search might choose to explore this path first, because it's just a random choice at this point.

410
00:35:43,406 --> 00:35:50,992
It'll explore, explore, explore, and it'll eventually find the goal, this particular path, when in actuality there was a better path.

411
00:35:51,072 --> 00:35:59,332
There was a more optimal solution that used fewer steps, assuming we're measuring the cost of a solution based on the number of steps that we need to take.

412
00:35:59,854 --> 00:36:06,468
So, depth first search, if we're unlucky, might end up not finding the best solution when a better solution is available.

413
00:36:07,822 --> 00:36:12,992
So if that's DFS, depth first search, how does BFS or breadth first search compare?

414
00:36:13,294 --> 00:36:15,108
How would it work in this particular situation?

415
00:36:15,534 --> 00:36:20,292
Well, the algorithm is going to look very different visually in terms of how BFS explores.

416
00:36:20,782 --> 00:36:29,892
Because BFS looks at shallower nodes first, the idea is going to be BFS will first look at all of the nodes that are one away from the initial state.

417
00:36:30,190 --> 00:36:35,684
Look here and look here, for example, just at the two nodes that are immediately next to this initial state.

418
00:36:36,526 --> 00:36:40,672
Then it'll explore nodes that are two away, looking at this state and that state, for example.

419
00:36:41,038 --> 00:36:43,908
Then it'll explore nodes that are three away, this state and that state.

420
00:36:44,142 --> 00:36:47,812
Whereas depth first search just picked one path and kept following it.

421
00:36:48,238 --> 00:36:57,872
Breadth first search, on the other hand, is taking the option of exploring all of the possible paths kind of at the same time, bouncing back between them, looking deeper and deeper at each one,

422
00:36:58,032 --> 00:37:03,556
but making sure to explore the shallower ones, or the ones that are closer to the initial state, earlier.

423
00:37:04,398 --> 00:37:13,540
So we'll keep following this pattern, looking at things that are four away, looking at things that are five away, looking at things that are six away, until eventually we make our way to the goal.

424
00:37:14,222 --> 00:37:22,112
And in this case, it's true we had to explore some states that ultimately didn't lead us anywhere, but the path that we found to the goal was the optimal path.

425
00:37:22,272 --> 00:37:24,644
This is the shortest way that we could get to the goal.

426
00:37:25,742 --> 00:37:29,028
And so, what might happen then in a larger maze?

427
00:37:29,422 --> 00:37:32,512
Well, let's take a look at something like this and how breadth first search is going to behave.

428
00:37:32,846 --> 00:37:38,916
Well, breadth first search, again, will just keep following the states until it receives a decision point. It could go either left or right.

429
00:37:39,406 --> 00:37:47,172
And while DFS just picked one and kept following that until it hit a dead end, BFS, on the other hand, will explore both.

430
00:37:47,534 --> 00:37:53,028
It'll say, look at this node, then this node, and it'll look at this node, then that node, so on and so forth.

431
00:37:53,390 --> 00:38:02,112
And when it hits a decision point here, rather than pick one left or two right and explore that path, it'll again explore both, alternating between them,

432
00:38:02,350 --> 00:38:07,300
going deeper and deeper. We'll explore here, and then maybe here and here, and then keep going.

433
00:38:07,552 --> 00:38:12,580
Explore here and slowly make our way, you can visually see, further and further out.

434
00:38:12,782 --> 00:38:17,444
Once we get to this decision point, we'll explore both up and down, until ultimately

435
00:38:18,638 --> 00:38:20,388
we make our way to the goal.

436
00:38:21,550 --> 00:38:31,952
And what you'll notice is, yes, breadth-first search did find our way from A to B by following this particular path, but it needed to explore a lot of states in order to do so.

437
00:38:32,302 --> 00:38:35,364
And so we see some trade-offs here between DFS and BFS.

438
00:38:35,472 --> 00:38:43,492
That in DFS, there may be some cases where there is some memory savings as compared to a breadth-first approach, where breadth-first search in this case

439
00:38:43,792 --> 00:38:47,332
had to explore a lot of states, but maybe that won't always be the

