01:06:16,206 --> 01:06:22,436
And that helps us to be able to make better decisions and to eliminate having to search through entire parts of this state space.

755
01:06:23,118 --> 01:06:28,292
But we would like to modify the algorithm so that we can achieve optimality, so that it can be optimal.

756
01:06:28,654 --> 01:06:29,936
And what is the way to do this?

757
01:06:30,096 --> 01:06:31,108
What is the intuition here?

758
01:06:31,886 --> 01:06:33,732
Well, let's take a look at this problem.

759
01:06:34,414 --> 01:06:39,844
In this initial problem, Greedy Best First Search found us this solution here, this long path.

760
01:06:40,174 --> 01:06:50,724
And the reason why it wasn't great is because yes, the heuristic numbers went down pretty low, but later on they started to build back up. They built back 8, 9, 10, 11, all the way up to 12 in this

761
01:06:50,816 --> 01:06:54,980
case. And so, how might we go about trying to improve this algorithm?

762
01:06:55,406 --> 01:06:57,412
Well, one thing that we might realize is that

763
01:06:58,256 --> 01:07:07,812
if we go all the way through this algorithm, through this path, and we end up going to the 12, and we've had to take this many steps, —like, who knows how many steps that is, —just to get to this 12,

764
01:07:08,750 --> 01:07:15,908
we could have also, as an alternative, taken much fewer steps, just six steps, and ended up at this 13 here.

765
01:07:16,270 --> 01:07:21,316
And yes, 13 is more than 12, so it looks like it's not as good, but it required far fewer steps.

766
01:07:22,016 --> 01:07:26,692
It only took six steps to get to this 13 versus many more steps to get to this 12.

767
01:07:27,086 --> 01:07:36,736
And while greedy best-first search says, oh, well, 12 is better than 13, and so pick the 12, we might more intelligently say, I'd rather be somewhere that heuristically

768
01:07:36,910 --> 01:07:41,284
looks like it takes slightly longer, if I can get there much more quickly.

769
01:07:42,062 --> 01:07:48,656
And we're going to encode that idea, this general idea, into a more formal algorithm known as A star search.

770
01:07:49,102 --> 01:07:58,212
A star search is going to solve this problem by instead of just considering the heuristic, also considering how long it took us to get to any particular state.

771
01:07:58,766 --> 01:08:08,676
So the distinction is greedy best for search. If I am in a state right now, the only thing I care about is what is the estimated distance, the heuristic value between me and the goal.

772
01:08:09,134 --> 01:08:13,028
Whereas A star search will take into consideration two pieces of information.

773
01:08:13,390 --> 01:08:20,836
It'll take into consideration how far do I estimate I am from the goal, but also how far did I have to travel in order to get here?

774
01:08:21,216 --> 01:08:22,756
Because that is relevant too.

775
01:08:23,534 --> 01:08:29,636
So we'll search algorithms by expanding the node with the lowest value of g of n plus h of n.

776
01:08:30,126 --> 01:08:35,204
H of n is that same heuristic that we were talking about a moment ago that's going to vary based on the problem.

777
01:08:35,726 --> 01:08:44,292
But g of n is going to be the cost to reach the node, how many steps I had to take in this case to get to my current position.

778
01:08:45,454 --> 01:08:47,780
So, what does that search algorithm look like in practice?

779
01:08:48,174 --> 01:08:48,996
Well, let's take a look.

780
01:08:49,710 --> 01:08:53,732
Again, we've got the same maze, and again, I've labeled them with their Manhattan distance.

781
01:08:54,144 --> 01:09:01,028
This value is the h of n value, the heuristic estimate of how far each of these squares is away from the goal.

782
01:09:02,382 --> 01:09:11,344
But now as we begin to explore states, we care not just about this heuristic value, but also about g of n, the number of steps I had to take in order to get there.

783
01:09:11,630 --> 01:09:13,572
And I care about summing those two numbers together.

784
01:09:14,190 --> 01:09:15,044
So what does that look like?

785
01:09:15,310 --> 01:09:18,212
On this very first step, I have taken one step,

786
01:09:18,958 --> 01:09:24,484
and now I am estimated to be 16 steps away from the goal, so the total value here is 17.

787
01:09:25,358 --> 01:09:26,304
Then I take one more step.

788
01:09:26,384 --> 01:09:32,356
I've now taken two steps and I estimate myself to be 15 away from the goal, again a total value of 17.

789
01:09:32,750 --> 01:09:37,380
Now I've taken three steps and I'm estimated to be 14 away from the goal, so on and so forth.

790
01:09:37,646 --> 01:09:43,300
Four steps and estimate of 13, 5 steps, estimate of 12, and now here's a decision point.

791
01:09:44,046 --> 01:09:54,788
I could either be six steps away from the goal with a heuristic of 13 for a total of 19, or I could be six steps away from the goal with a heuristic of 11

792
01:09:55,214 --> 01:09:57,252
with an estimate of 17 for the total.

793
01:09:57,838 --> 01:10:02,756
So between 19 and 17, I'd rather take the 17, the six plus eleven.

794
01:10:03,214 --> 01:10:05,184
So so far, no different than what we saw before.

795
01:10:05,264 --> 01:10:08,064
We're still taking this option because it appears to be better.

796
01:10:08,270 --> 01:10:11,104
And I keep taking this option because it appears to be better.

797
01:10:11,310 --> 01:10:14,660
But it's right about here that things get a little bit different.

798
01:10:15,726 --> 01:10:21,316
Now I could be 15 steps away from the goal with an estimated distance of six.

799
01:10:21,774 --> 01:10:24,100
So 15 plus 6, total value of 21.

800
01:10:24,814 --> 01:10:33,028
Alternatively, I could be six steps away from the goal, because this is five steps away, so this is six steps away, with a total value of 13 as my estimate.

801
01:10:33,518 --> 01:10:35,410
So six plus 13, that's 19.

802
01:10:36,334 --> 01:10:41,250
So here we would evaluate g of n plus h of n to be 19, six plus 13.

803
01:10:41,710 --> 01:10:45,670
Whereas here, we would be 15 plus six or 21.

804
01:10:46,510 --> 01:10:49,476
And so the intuition is 19 less than 21, pick here.

805
01:10:49,838 --> 01:11:01,284
But the idea is ultimately I'd rather be having taken fewer steps get to a 13 than having taken 15 steps and be at a six, because it means I've had to take more steps in order to get there.

806
01:11:01,584 --> 01:11:03,716
Maybe there's a better path this way.

807
01:11:04,654 --> 01:11:06,304
So instead, we'll explore this

808
01:11:06,304 --> 01:11:10,624
route. Now if we go one more, this is seven steps plus 14 is twenty-one.

809
01:11:11,054 --> 01:11:14,756
So between those two, it's sort of a toss-up. We might end up exploring that one anyways.

810
01:11:15,118 --> 01:11:25,024
But after that, as these numbers start to get bigger in the heuristic values, and these heuristic values start to get smaller, you'll find that we'll actually keep exploring down this path.

811
01:11:25,198 --> 01:11:34,756
And you can do the math to see that at every decision point, A star search is going to make a choice based on the sum of how many steps it took me to get to my current position,

812
01:11:35,150 --> 01:11:38,436
and then how far I estimate I am from the goal.

813
01:11:39,246 --> 01:11:46,384
So while we did have to explore some of these states, the ultimate solution we found was in fact an optimal solution.

814
01:11:46,574 --> 01:11:51,364
It did find us the quickest possible way to get from the initial state to the goal.

815
01:11:51,918 --> 01:11:56,580
And it turns out that A star is an optimal search algorithm under certain conditions.

816
01:11:57,422 --> 01:12:01,700
So the conditions are H of n, my heuristic, needs to be admissible.

817
01:12:02,126 --> 01:12:03,824
What does it mean for a heuristic to be admissible?

818
01:12:04,078 --> 01:12:08,452
Well, a heuristic is admissible if it never overestimates the true cost.

819
01:12:08,782 --> 01:12:15,876
H of n always needs to either get it exactly right in terms of how far away I am, or it needs to underestimate.

820
01:12:16,558 --> 01:12:22,276
So we saw an example from before where the heuristic value was much smaller than the actual cost it would take.

821
01:12:22,544 --> 01:12:26,116
That's totally fine, but the heuristic value should never overestimate.

822
01:12:26,224 --> 01:12:29,796
It should never think that I'm further away from the goal than I actually am.

823
01:12:30,638 --> 01:12:35,460
And meanwhile, to make a stronger statement, h of n also needs to be consistent.

824
01:12:36,078 --> 01:12:37,696
And what does it mean for it to be consistent?

825
01:12:37,936 --> 01:12:46,736
Mathematically, it means that for every node, which we'll call n, and successor, the node after me, that I'll call n prime, where it takes a cost of c

826
01:12:47,150 --> 01:12:48,004
to make that step,

827
01:12:48,878 --> 01:12:54,692
the heuristic value of n needs to be less than or equal to the heuristic value of n prime plus the cost.

828
01:12:55,246 --> 01:13:00,356
So it's a lot of math, but in words, what that ultimately means is that if I am here at this state right now,

829
01:13:00,942 --> 01:13:09,696
the heuristic value from me to the goal shouldn't be more than the heuristic value of my successor, the next place I could go to, plus however

830
01:13:10,016 --> 01:13:14,084
much it would cost me to just make that step from one step to the next step.

831
01:13:14,574 --> 01:13:19,780
And so this is just making sure that my heuristic is consistent between all of these steps that I might take.

832
01:13:20,142 --> 01:13:25,216
So as long as this is true, then A star search is going to find me an optimal solution.

833
01:13:25,582 --> 01:13:29,696
And this is where much of the challenge of solving these search problems can sometimes come in.

834
01:13:29,934 --> 01:13:37,136
That A star search is an algorithm that is known and you could write the code fairly easily, but it's choosing the heuristic that can be the interesting challenge.

835
01:13:37,390 --> 01:13:42,692
The better the heuristic is, the better I'll be able to solve the problem in the fewer states that I'll have to explore.

836
01:13:42,958 --> 01:13:47,652
And I need to make sure that the heuristic satisfies these particular constraints.

837
01:13:48,654 --> 01:13:54,756
So, all in all, these are some of the examples of search algorithms that might work, and certainly there are many more than just this.

838
01:13:55,150 --> 01:14:04,576
A star, for example, does have a tendency to use quite a bit of memory, so there are alternative approaches to A star that ultimately use less memory than this version of A star

839
01:14:04,816 --> 01:14:05,444
happens to use.

840
01:14:06,190 --> 01:14:10,212
And there are other search algorithms that are optimized for other cases as well.

841
01:14:11,598 --> 01:14:16,656
But now so far we've only been looking at search algorithms where there's one agent.

842
01:14:16,816 --> 01:14:19,456
Like I am trying to find a solution to a problem.

843
01:14:19,696 --> 01:14:27,460
I am trying to navigate my way through a maze, I am trying to solve a 15 puzzle, I am trying to find driving directions from point A to point B.

844
01:14:28,366 --> 01:14:35,856
Sometimes in search situations, though, we'll enter an adversarial situation where I am an agent trying to make intelligent decisions,

845
01:14:35,950 --> 01:14:40,576
and there's someone else who is fighting against me, so to speak, that has opposite objectives.

846
01:14:40,736 --> 01:14:44,804
Someone where I am trying to succeed, someone else that wants me to fail.

847
01:14:45,262 --> 01:14:47,876
And this is most popular in something like a game.

848
01:14:48,206 --> 01:14:56,016
A game like tic-tac-toe, where we've got this three by three grid, and X and O take turns, either writing an X or an O in any one of these squares,

849
01:14:56,238 --> 01:15:01,924
and the goal is to get three X's in a row if you're the X player, or three O's in a row if you're the O player.

850
01:15:02,670 --> 01:15:07,972
And computers have gotten quite good at playing games, tic-tac-toe very easily, but even more complex games.

851
01:15:08,462 --> 01:15:12,868
And so you might imagine what does an intelligent decision in a game look like?

852
01:15:13,454 --> 01:15:16,896
So maybe X makes an initial move in the middle, and O plays up here.

853
01:15:17,070 --> 01:15:20,096
Like what does an intelligent move for X now become?

854
01:15:20,302 --> 01:15:21,700
Like where should you move if you were X?

855
01:15:22,510 --> 01:15:29,540
And it turns out there are a couple of possibilities, but if an AI is playing this game optimally, then the AI might play somewhere like the upper right.

856
01:15:30,158 --> 01:15:33,796
Where in this situation, O has the opposite objective of X.

857
01:15:34,176 --> 01:15:43,268
X is trying to win the game to get three in a row diagonally here, and O is trying to stop that objective, opposite of the objective, and so O is going to place here to try to block.

858
01:15:44,016 --> 01:15:46,256
But now X has a pretty clever move.

859
01:15:46,574 --> 01:15:48,036
X can make a move like this.

860
01:15:49,038 --> 01:15:51,936
Where now X has two possible ways that X can win the game.

861
01:15:52,176 --> 01:15:58,244
X could win the game by getting three in a row across here, or X could win the game by getting three in a row vertically this way.

862
01:15:58,542 --> 01:16:00,496
So it doesn't matter where O makes their next move.

863
01:16:00,656 --> 01:16:08,452
O could play here, for example, blocking the three in a row horizontally, but then X is going to win the game by getting a three in a row vertically.

864
01:16:09,294 --> 01:16:13,988
And so there's a fair amount of reasoning that's going on here in order for the computer to be able to solve a problem.

865
01:16:14,336 --> 01:16:22,756
And it's similar in spirit to the problems we've looked at so far, right? There are actions, there's some sort of state of the board and some transition from one action to the next.

866
01:16:23,310 --> 01:16:29,348
But it's different in the sense that this is now not just a classical search problem, but an adversarial search problem.

867
01:16:29,456 --> 01:16:36,004
That I am the X player trying to find the best moves to make, but I know that there is some adversary that is trying to stop

868
01:16:36,718 --> 01:16:41,988
me. So we need some sort of algorithm to deal with these adversarial type of search situations.

869
01:16:42,702 --> 01:16:50,816
And the algorithm we're going to take a look at is an algorithm called Minimax, which works very well for these deterministic games where there are two players.

870
01:16:50,976 --> 01:16:58,016
It can work for other types of games as well, but we'll look right now at games where I make a move, then my opponent makes a move, and I am trying to win,

871
01:16:58,096 --> 01:17:03,108
and my opponent is trying to win also, or in other words, my opponent is trying to get me to lose.

872
01:17:04,110 --> 01:17:06,784
And so what do we need in order to make this algorithm work?

873
01:17:07,024 --> 01:17:16,064
Well, anytime we try and translate this human concept of playing a game, winning and losing, to a computer, we want to translate it in terms that the computer can understand.

874
01:17:16,302 --> 01:17:19,300
And ultimately, the computer really just understands numbers.

875
01:17:19,822 --> 01:17:26,464
And so we want some way of translating a game of X's and O's on a grid to something numerical, something the computer can understand.

876
01:17:26,624 --> 01:17:33,764
The computer doesn't normally understand notions of win or lose, but it does understand the concept of bigger and smaller.

877
01:17:34,510 --> 01:17:42,948
And so what we might do is we might take each of the possible ways that a tic-tac-toe game can unfold and assign a value or a utility

878
01:17:43,406 --> 01:17:44,836
to each one of those possible ways.

879
01:17:45,166 --> 01:17:49,380
And in a tic-tac-toe game, and in many types of games, there are three possible outcomes.

880
01:17:49,902 --> 01:17:54,020
The outcomes are O wins, X wins, or nobody wins.

881
01:17:54,382 --> 01:17:58,064
So player one wins, player two wins, or nobody wins.

882
01:17:58,510 --> 01:18:03,556
And for now, let's go ahead and assign each of these possible outcomes a different value.

883
01:18:03,982 --> 01:18:06,596
We'll say O winning, that'll have a value of negative one.

884
01:18:07,342 --> 01:18:09,284
Nobody winning, that'll have a value of zero.

885
01:18:09,710 --> 01:18:12,304
And X winning, that will have a value of one.

886
01:18:13,006 --> 01:18:16,420
So we've just assigned numbers to each of these three possible outcomes.

887
01:18:16,942 --> 01:18:19,236
And now we have two players.

888
01:18:19,470 --> 01:18:21,668
We have the X player and the O player.

889
01:18:22,254 --> 01:18:28,612
And we're going to go ahead and call the X player the max player. And we'll call the O player the min player.

890
01:18:29,102 --> 01:18:37,024
And the reason why is because in the Minimax algorithm, the max player, which in this case is X, is aiming to maximize the score.

891
01:18:37,350 --> 01:18:42,564
right? These are the possible options for the score: negative one, zero, and one, X wants to maximize the score.

892
01:18:42,926 --> 01:18:49,060
Meaning, if at all possible, X would like this situation, where X wins the game and we give it a score of one.

893
01:18:49,742 --> 01:18:57,700
But if this isn't possible, if X needs to choose between these two options, negative one meaning O winning, or zero meaning nobody winning,

894
01:18:58,064 --> 01:19:03,424
X would rather that nobody wins, score of zero, than a score of negative one, O

895
01:19:03,424 --> 01:19:12,004
winning. So this notion of winning and losing and tying has been reduced mathematically to just this idea of try and maximize the score.

896
01:19:12,224 --> 01:19:20,624
The X player always wants the score to be bigger, and on the flip side, the min player, in this case O, is aiming to minimize the score.

897
01:19:20,718 --> 01:19:24,484
The O player wants the score to be as small as possible.

898
01:19:25,422 --> 01:19:30,624
So now we've taken this game of X's and O's and winning and losing and turned it into something mathematical.

899
01:19:30,784 --> 01:19:35,108
Something where X is trying to maximize the score, O is trying to minimize the score.

900
01:19:35,566 --> 01:19:43,428
Let's now look at all of the parts of the game that we need in order to encode it in an AI so that an AI can play a game like tic-tac-toe.

901
01:19:44,878 --> 01:19:46,624
So the game is going to need a couple of things.

902
01:19:46,894 --> 01:19:54,340
We'll need some sort of initial state that we'll in this case call S0, which is how the game begins, like an empty tic-tac-toe board, for example.

903
01:19:54,864 --> 01:20:03,556
we'll also need a function called player, where the player function is going to take as input a state here represented by S,

904
01:20:04,206 --> 01:20:08,996
and the output of the player function is going to be which player's turn is it.

905
01:20:09,390 --> 01:20:15,524
Right? We need to be able to give a tic-tac-toe board to the computer, run it through a function, and that function tells us whose turn it is.

906
01:20:16,526 --> 01:20:18,864
We'll need some notion of actions that we can take.

907
01:20:19,024 --> 01:20:20,612
We'll see examples of that in just a moment.

908
01:20:20,974 --> 01:20:23,824
We need some notion of a transition model, same as before.

909
01:20:24,014 --> 01:20:28,516
If I have a state and I take an action, I need to know what results as a consequence of it.

910
01:20:29,134 --> 01:20:31,524
I need some way of knowing when the game is over.

911
01:20:31,950 --> 01:20:35,268
So this is equivalent to kind of like a goal test, but I need some terminal test.

912
01:20:35,726 --> 01:20:41,028
Some way to check to see if a state is a terminal state, where a terminal state means the game is over.

913
01:20:41,390 --> 01:20:49,732
In a classic game of tic-tac-toe, a terminal state means either someone has gotten three in a row, or all of the squares of the tic-tac-toe board are filled.

914
01:20:50,144 --> 01:20:52,580
Either of those conditions make it a terminal state.

915
01:20:52,878 --> 01:20:59,492
In a game of chess, it might be something like when there is checkmate, or if checkmate is no longer possible, that that becomes a terminal state.

916
01:21:00,366 --> 01:21:07,264
And then finally, we'll need a utility function, a function that takes a state and gives us a numerical value for that terminal state.

917
01:21:07,406 --> 01:21:10,224
Some way of saying if x wins the game, that has a value of one.

918
01:21:10,638 --> 01:21:12,784
If O has won the game, that has a value of negative one.

919
01:21:13,166 --> 01:21:15,584
If nobody has won the game, that has a value of

920
01:21:15,584 --> 01:21:17,984
zero. So let's take a look at each of these in

921
01:21:17,984 --> 01:21:26,864
turn. The initial state we can just represent in tic-tac-toe as the empty game board. This is where we begin, it's the place from which we begin this search.

922
01:21:27,118 --> 01:21:35,044
And again, I'll be representing these things visually, but you can imagine this really just being like an array or a two dimensional array of all of these possible squares.

923
01:21:36,270 --> 01:21:40,836
Then we need the player function, that again takes a state and tells us whose turn it is.

924
01:21:41,326 --> 01:21:46,980
Assuming X makes the first move, if I have an empty game board, then my player function is going to return X.

925
01:21:47,630 --> 01:21:51,984
And if I have a game board where X has made a move, then my player function is going to return O.

926
01:21:52,494 --> 01:21:56,452
The player function takes a tic tac toe game board and tells us whose turn it is.

927
01:21:58,286 --> 01:22:00,548
Next up, we'll consider the actions function.

928
01:22:00,974 --> 01:22:09,668
The actions function, much like it did in classical search, takes a state and gives us the set of all of the possible actions we can take in that state.

929
01:22:10,446 --> 01:22:11,332
So let's imagine

930
01:22:12,110 --> 01:22:15,012
it's O is turned to move in a game board that looks like this.

931
01:22:15,470 --> 01:22:17,888
What happens when we pass it into the actions function?

932
01:22:18,286 --> 01:22:24,516
So the actions function takes this state of the game as input, and the output is a set of possible actions.

933
01:22:24,974 --> 01:22:29,220
It's a set of I could move in the upper left or I could move in the bottom middle.

934
01:22:29,710 --> 01:22:34,820
Those are the two possible action choices that I have when I begin in this particular state.

935
01:22:36,302 --> 01:22:44,900
Now, just as before, when we had states and actions, we need some sort of transition model to tell us when we take this action in the state, what is the new state that we get.

936
01:22:45,486 --> 01:22:50,884
And here we define that using the result function that takes a state as input as well as an action.

937
01:22:51,566 --> 01:23:01,732
And when we apply the result function to this state, saying that let's let O move in this upper left corner, the new state we get is this resulting state, where O is in the upper left corner.

938
01:23:02,030 --> 01:23:04,608
And now this seems obvious to someone who knows how to play tic-tac-toe.

939
01:23:04,718 --> 01:23:11,248
Like of course, you play in the upper left corner, that's the board you get, but all of this information needs to be encoded into the AI.

940
01:23:11,408 --> 01:23:16,900
The AI doesn't know how to play tic-tac-toe until you tell the AI how the rules of tic-tac-toe work.

941
01:23:17,262 --> 01:23:26,372
And this function, defining this function here, allows us to tell the AI how this game actually works and how actions actually affect the outcome of the game.

942
01:23:27,246 --> 01:23:36,036
So the AI needs to know how the game works, the AI also needs to know when the game is over, as by defining a function called terminal that takes as input, a state S,

943
01:23:36,686 --> 01:23:43,140
such that if we take a game that is not yet over, pass it into the terminal function, the output is false, the game is not over.

944
01:23:43,662 --> 01:23:53,668
But if we take a game that is over, because x has gotten three in a row along that diagonal, pass that into the terminal function, then the output is going to be true, because the game now is in fact

945
01:23:54,048 --> 01:23:54,368
over.

946
01:23:54,990 --> 01:24:01,060
And finally, we've told the AI how the game works in terms of what moves can be made and what happens when you make those moves.

947
01:24:01,294 --> 01:24:03,044
We've told the AI when the game is over.

948
01:24:03,310 --> 01:24:12,768
Now we need to tell the AI what the value of each of those states is, and we do that by defining this utility function that takes a state S and tells us the score

949
01:24:13,006 --> 01:24:14,564
or the utility of that state.

950
01:24:14,894 --> 01:24:22,756
So again, we said that if X wins the game, that utility is a value of one, whereas if O wins the game, then the utility of that is negative one.

951
01:24:23,438 --> 01:24:29,924
And the AI needs to know for each of these terminal states where the game is over, what is the utility of that state.

952
01:24:30,862 --> 01:24:38,468
So if I give you a game board like this where the game is in fact over, and I ask the AI to tell me what the value of that state is, it could do so.

953
01:24:38,830 --> 01:24:40,580
The value of the state is one.

954
01:24:41,966 --> 01:24:45,764
Where things get interesting though is if the game is not yet over.

955
01:24:46,350 --> 01:24:47,716
Let's imagine a game board like this.

956
01:24:47,950 --> 01:24:51,396
We're in the middle of the game, it's O's turn to make a move.

957
01:24:52,014 --> 01:24:53,968
So how do we know it's O's turn to make a move?

958
01:24:54,128 --> 01:24:56,208
We can calculate that using the player function.

959
01:24:56,462 --> 01:25:01,636
We can say player of S, pass in the state, O is the answer, so we know it's O's turn to move.

960
01:25:02,222 --> 01:25:06,532
And now, what is the value of this board and what action should O take?

961
01:25:07,086 --> 01:25:12,868
Well, that's going to depend. We have to do some calculation here, and this is where the minimax algorithm really comes in.

962
01:25:13,550 --> 01:25:19,332
Recall that X is trying to maximize the score, which means that O is trying to minimize the score.

963
01:25:19,790 --> 01:25:24,452
O would like to minimize the total value that we get at the end of the game.

964
01:25:24,942 --> 01:25:30,768
And because this game isn't over yet, we don't really know just yet what the value of this game board is.

965
01:25:30,928 --> 01:25:33,476
We have to do some calculation in order to figure that out.

966
01:25:34,254 --> 01:25:36,100
And so how do we do that kind of calculation?

967
01:25:36,558 --> 01:25:45,508
Well, in order to do so, we're going to consider, just as we might in a classical search situation, what actions could happen next and what states will that take us to.

968
01:25:46,158 --> 01:25:53,700
And it turns out that in this position, there are only two open squares, which means there are only two open places where O can make a move.

969
01:25:54,734 --> 01:25:56,260
O could either make a move in the upper

970
01:25:56,288 --> 01:25:59,204
left, or O could make a move in the bottom middle.

971
01:26:00,238 --> 01:26:05,444
And Minimax doesn't know right out of the box which of those moves is going to be better, so it's going to consider both.

972
01:26:06,638 --> 01:26:11,056
But now we sort of run into the same situation. Now I have two more game boards, neither of which is over.

973
01:26:11,296 --> 01:26:12,036
What happens next?

974
01:26:12,942 --> 01:26:16,484
And now it's in this sense that Minimax is what we'll call a recursive algorithm.

975
01:26:16,816 --> 01:26:23,332
It's going to now repeat the exact same process, although now considering it from the opposite perspective.

976
01:26:23,758 --> 01:26:32,256
It's as if I am now going to put myself, if I am the O player, I am going to put myself in my opponent's shoes, my opponent as the X player, and consider

977
01:26:32,654 --> 01:26:35,588
what would my opponent do if they were in this position?

978
01:26:36,142 --> 01:26:39,428
What would my opponent do, the X player, if they were in that position?

979
01:26:40,174 --> 01:26:40,996
And what would then happen?

980
01:26:41,582 --> 01:26:45,988
Well, the other player, my opponent, the X player, is trying to maximize the score.

981
01:26:46,256 --> 01:26:49,156
Whereas I am trying to minimize the score as the O player.

982
01:26:49,422 --> 01:26:52,772
So X is trying to find the maximum possible value that they can get.

983
01:26:53,646 --> 01:26:54,756
And so what's going to happen?

984
01:26:55,470 --> 01:26:58,372
Well, from this board position, X only has one choice.

985
01:26:58,862 --> 01:27:01,412
X is going to play here, and they're going to get three in a row.

986
01:27:01,696 --> 01:27:05,124
And we know that that board, X winning, that has a value of one.

987
01:27:05,646 --> 01:27:08,580
If X wins the game, the value of that game board is one.

988
01:27:09,296 --> 01:27:10,948
And so from this position,

989
01:27:11,758 --> 01:27:23,140
if this state can only ever lead to this state, it's the only possible option, and this state has a value of one, then the maximum possible value that the X player can get from this game board

990
01:27:23,376 --> 01:27:24,016
is also one.

991
01:27:24,590 --> 01:27:30,212
From here, the only place we can get is to a game with a value of one, so this game board also has a value of one.

992
01:27:31,374 --> 01:27:33,296
Now we consider this one over here.

993
01:27:33,646 --> 01:27:34,656
What's going to happen now?

994
01:27:34,958 --> 01:27:36,176
Well X needs to make a move.

995
01:27:36,462 --> 01:27:41,092
The only move X can make is in the upper left, so X will go there. And in this game, no one wins the game.

996
01:27:41,376 --> 01:27:45,252
Nobody has three in a row, so the value of that game board is zero.

997
01:27:45,710 --> 01:27:46,372
Nobody's one.

998
01:27:46,990 --> 01:27:56,196
And so again, by the same logic, if from this board position, the only place we can get to is a board where the value is zero, then this state must also have a value of zero.

999
01:27:57,358 --> 01:28:00,932
And now here comes the choice part, the idea of trying to minimize.

1000
01:28:01,454 --> 01:28:10,468
I, as the O player, now know that if I make this choice, moving in the upper left, that is going to result in a game with a value of one, assuming everyone plays optimally.

1001
01:28:11,342 --> 01:28:17,284
And if I instead play in the lower middle, choose this fork in the road, that is going to result in a game board with a value of zero.

1002
01:28:17,616 --> 01:28:18,576
I have two options.

1003
01:28:18,736 --> 01:28:21,828
I have a one and a zero to choose from, and I need to pick.

1004
01:28:22,350 --> 01:28:26,532
And as the min player, I would rather choose the option with the minimum value.

1005
01:28:26,990 --> 01:28:34,656
So whenever a player has multiple choices, the min player will choose the option with the smallest value, the max player will choose the option with the largest value.

1006
01:28:34,830 --> 01:28:40,292
Between the one and the zero, the zero is smaller, meaning I'd rather tie the game than lose the game.

1007
01:28:40,718 --> 01:28:45,892
And so this game board will say also has a value of zero, because if I am playing optimally,

1008
01:28:46,606 --> 01:28:47,972
I will pick this fork in the road.

1009
01:28:48,366 --> 01:28:55,332
I'll place my O here to block X's three in a row, X will move in the upper left, and the game will be over, and no one will have won the game.

1010
01:28:56,366 --> 01:29:05,220
So this is now the logic of Minimax, to consider all of the possible options that I can take, all of the actions that I can take, and then to put myself in my opponent's shoes.

1011
01:29:05,536 --> 01:29:10,656
I decide what move I'm going to make now by considering what move my opponent will make on the next turn.

1012
01:29:10,958 --> 01:29:20,740
And to do that, I consider what move I would make on the turn after that, so on and so forth, until I get all the way down to the end of the game, to one of these so-called terminal states.

1013
01:29:21,136 --> 01:29:31,456
In fact, this very decision point where I am trying to decide as the O player what to make a decision about might have just been a part of the logic that the X player, my opponent, was using

1014
01:29:31,630 --> 01:29:32,416
the move before me.

1015
01:29:32,576 --> 01:29:42,148
This might be part of some larger tree where X is trying to make a move in this situation and needs to pick between three different options in order to make a decision about what to happen.

1016
01:29:42,542 --> 01:29:47,044
And the further and further away we are from the end of the game, the deeper this tree has to go.

1017
01:29:47,246 --> 01:29:51,616
Because every level in this tree is going to correspond to one move.

1018
01:29:51,776 --> 01:29:57,700
One move or action that I take, one move or action that my opponent takes in order to decide what happens.

1019
01:29:58,350 --> 01:30:08,016
And in fact, it turns out that if I am the X player in this position and I recursively do the logic and see I have a choice, three choices in fact, one of which leads to a value of zero.

1020
01:30:08,206 --> 01:30:11,588
If I play here, then if everyone plays optimally, the game will be a tie.

1021
01:30:12,014 --> 01:30:16,772
If I play here, then O is going to win and I'll lose playing optimally.

1022
01:30:17,102 --> 01:30:26,916
Or here, where I the X player can win, well, between a score of zero and negative one and one, I'd rather pick the board with a value of one, because that's the maximum value I can get.

1023
01:30:27,278 --> 01:30:30,884
And so this board would also have a maximum value of one.

1024
01:30:31,662 --> 01:30:36,736
And so this tree can get very, very deep, especially as the game starts to have more and more

1025
01:30:36,736 --> 01:30:46,148
moves. And this logic works not just for tic-tac-toe, but any of these sorts of games, where I make a move, my opponent makes a move, and ultimately we have these adversarial objectives.

1026
01:30:46,542 --> 01:30:49,828
And we can simplify the diagram into a diagram that looks like this.

1027
01:30:50,350 --> 01:30:57,764
This is a more abstract version of the Minimax tree, where these are each states, but I'm no longer representing them as exactly like tic tac-toe boards.

1028
01:30:58,016 --> 01:31:03,428
This is just representing some generic game that might be tic tac-toe, might be some other game altogether.

1029
01:31:04,142 --> 01:31:08,512
Any of these green arrows that are pointing up, that represents a maximizing state.

1030
01:31:08,686 --> 01:31:10,852
I would like the score to be as big as possible.

1031
01:31:11,406 --> 01:31:19,332
And any of these red arrows pointing down, those are minimizing states, where the player is the min player, and they are trying to make the score as small as possible.

1032
01:31:20,110 --> 01:31:26,020
So if you imagine in this situation, I am the maximizing player, this player here, and I have three choices.

1033
01:31:26,574 --> 01:31:31,940
One choice gives me a score of five, one choice gives me a score of three, and one choice gives me a score of nine.

1034
01:31:32,334 --> 01:31:41,792
Well then between those three choices, my best option is to choose this nine over here, the score that maximizes my options out of all the three options.

1035
01:31:42,030 --> 01:31:44,612
And so I can give this state a value of nine.

1036
01:31:44,974 --> 01:31:49,124
Because among my three options, that is the best choice that I have available to me.

1037
01:31:50,478 --> 01:31:51,632
So that's my decision now.

1038
01:31:51,918 --> 01:31:54,820
You imagine it's like one move away from the end of the game.

1039
01:31:55,918 --> 01:32:01,028
But then you could also ask a reasonable question: what might my opponent do two moves away from the end of the game?

1040
01:32:01,422 --> 01:32:03,140
My opponent is the minimizing player.

1041
01:32:03,232 --> 01:32:05,540
They are trying to make the score as small as possible.

1042
01:32:05,806 --> 01:32:09,252
Imagine what would have happened if they had to pick which choice to make.

1043
01:32:09,870 --> 01:32:16,164
One choice leads us to this state where I, the maximizing player, am going to opt for nine, the biggest score that I can get.

1044
01:32:16,910 --> 01:32:17,472
And one

1045
01:32:18,478 --> 01:32:24,548
leads to this state, where I, the maximizing player, would choose eight, which is then the largest score that I can get.

1046
01:32:24,974 --> 01:32:32,708
Now the minimizing player, forced to choose between a nine or an eight, is going to choose the smallest possible score, which in this case is an eight.

1047
01:32:33,198 --> 01:32:35,312
And that is then how this process would unfold.

1048
01:32:35,472 --> 01:32:42,436
That the minimizing player in this case considers both of their options and then all of the options that would happen as a result of that.

1049
01:32:43,662 --> 01:32:47,172
So this now is a general picture of what the Minimax algorithm looks like.

1050
01:32:47,502 --> 01:32:50,276
Let's now try to formalize it using a little bit of pseudocode.

1051
01:32:50,766 --> 01:32:53,412
So what exactly is happening in the Minimax algorithm?

1052
01:32:53,838 --> 01:32:56,868
Well, given a state S, we need to decide what to happen.

1053
01:32:57,358 --> 01:33:04,740
Uh the max player, if it's Max's player's turn, then Max is going to pick an action A in actions of S.

1054
01:33:05,326 --> 01:33:10,980
Recall that actions is a function that takes a state and gives me back all of the possible actions that I can take.

1055
01:33:11,182 --> 01:33:13,828
It tells me all of the moves that are possible.

1056
01:33:14,990 --> 01:33:23,392
The max player is going to specifically pick an action A in the set of actions that gives me the highest value of min

1057
01:33:23,632 --> 01:33:25,892
value of result of SNA.

1058
01:33:26,382 --> 01:33:27,152
So what does that mean?

1059
01:33:27,438 --> 01:33:33,392
Well, it means that I want to make the option that gives me the highest score of all of the actions A.

1060
01:33:33,998 --> 01:33:35,392
But what score is that going to have?

1061
01:33:35,790 --> 01:33:43,364
To calculate that, I need to know what my opponent, the min player, is going to do if they try to minimize the value of the state that results.

1062
01:33:44,366 --> 01:33:53,060
So we say, what state results after I take this action, and what happens when the min player tries to minimize the value of that state?

1063
01:33:53,646 --> 01:33:56,036
I consider that for all of my possible options.

1064
01:33:56,430 --> 01:34:01,316
And after I've considered that for all of my possible options, I pick the action A that has the highest value.

1065
01:34:02,766 --> 01:34:05,636
Likewise, the min player is going to do the same thing but backwards.

1066
01:34:06,222 --> 01:34:10,500
They're also going to consider what are all of the possible actions they can take if it's their turn.

1067
01:34:10,894 --> 01:34:15,812
And they're going to pick the action A that has the smallest possible value of all the options.

1068
01:34:16,334 --> 01:34:27,072
And the way they know what the smallest possible value of all the options is is by considering what the max player is going to do. By saying, what's the result of applying this action to the current

1069
01:34:27,072 --> 01:34:33,380
state? And then what would the max player try to do? What value would the max player calculate for that particular state?

1070
01:34:34,062 --> 01:34:38,884
So everyone makes their decision based on trying to estimate what the other person would do.

1071
01:34:39,854 --> 01:34:44,324
And now we need to turn our attention to these two functions, max value and min value.

1072
01:34:44,672 --> 01:34:49,632
How do you actually calculate the value of a state if you're trying to maximize its value?

1073
01:34:49,934 --> 01:34:53,348
And how do you calculate the value of a state if you're trying to minimize the value?

1074
01:34:53,774 --> 01:34:58,020
If you can do that, then we have an entire implementation of this minimax algorithm.

1075
01:34:58,862 --> 01:34:59,552
So let's try it.

1076
01:34:59,632 --> 01:35:08,964
Let's try and implement this max value function that takes a state and returns as output the value of that state if I'm trying to maximize the value of the state.

1077
01:35:09,966 --> 01:35:12,452
Well, the first thing I can check for is to see if the game is over.

1078
01:35:12,974 --> 01:35:17,824
Because if the game is over, in other words, if the state is a terminal state, then this is easy.

1079
01:35:18,064 --> 01:35:22,052
I already have this utility function that tells me what the value of the board is.

1080
01:35:22,382 --> 01:35:26,020
If the game is over, I just check, like, did X win, did O win? Is it a tie?

1081
01:35:26,254 --> 01:35:29,476
And this utility function just knows what the value of the state is.

1082
01:35:30,286 --> 01:35:37,988
What's trickier is if the game isn't over, because then I need to do this recursive reasoning about thinking what is my opponent going to do on the next move.

1083
01:35:38,958 --> 01:35:44,420
And I want to calculate the value of this state, and I want the value of the state to be as high as possible.

1084
01:35:45,070 --> 01:35:47,620
And I'll keep track of that value in a variable called V.

1085
01:35:48,270 --> 01:35:52,932
And if I want the value to be as high as possible, I need to give V an initial value.

1086
01:35:53,424 --> 01:36:00,324
And initially, I'll just go ahead and set it to be as low as possible, because I don't know what options are available to me yet.

1087
01:36:00,814 --> 01:36:09,572
So initially, I'll set V equal to negative infinity, which seems a little bit strange, but the idea here is I want the value initially to be as low as possible,

1088
01:36:09,824 --> 01:36:14,304
because as I consider my actions, I'm always going to try and do better than v.

1089
01:36:14,606 --> 01:36:17,828
And if I set v to negative infinity, I know I can always do better than that.

1090
01:36:18,926 --> 01:36:20,900
So now I consider my actions.

1091
01:36:21,230 --> 01:36:27,652
And this is going to be some kind of loop where for every action in actions of state, recall actions as a function,

1092
01:36:27,854 --> 01:36:32,132
that takes my state and gives me all the possible actions that I can use in that state.

1093
01:36:32,750 --> 01:36:36,164
So for each one of those actions, I want to compare it to V

1094
01:36:36,814 --> 01:36:41,344
and say, all right, V is going to be equal to the maximum of V

1095
01:36:42,304 --> 01:36:43,876
and this expression.

1096
01:36:44,366 --> 01:36:45,412
So what is this expression?

1097
01:36:46,094 --> 01:36:49,824
Well, first it is get the result of taking the action in this

1098
01:36:49,824 --> 01:36:53,892
state, and then get the min value of that.

1099
01:36:54,254 --> 01:36:55,524
In other words, let's say

1100
01:36:56,430 --> 01:37:02,180
I want to find out from that state what is the best that the min player can do, because they're going to try and minimize the score.

1101
01:37:02,574 --> 01:37:12,196
So whatever the resulting score is of the min value of that state, compare it to my current best value, and just pick the maximum of those two, because I am trying to maximize the value.

1102
01:37:12,590 --> 01:37:23,364
In short, what these three lines of code are doing are going through all of my possible actions and asking the question, how do I maximize the score given what my opponent is going to try to do?

1103
01:37:23,982 --> 01:37:29,284
After this entire loop, I can just return V, and that is now the value of that particular state.

1104
01:37:30,222 --> 01:37:32,704
And for the min player, it's the exact opposite of this.

1105
01:37:32,974 --> 01:37:34,660
The same logic, just backwards.

1106
01:37:35,150 --> 01:37:38,624
To calculate the minimum value of a state, first we check if it's a terminal state.

1107
01:37:38,862 --> 01:37:40,516
If it is, we return its utility.

1108
01:37:41,038 --> 01:37:47,104
Otherwise, we're going to now try to minimize the value of the state given all of my possible actions.

1109
01:37:47,438 --> 01:37:53,316
So I need an initial value for v, the value of the state, and initially I'll set it to infinity.

1110
01:37:53,550 --> 01:37:56,144
Because I know I can always get something less than infinity.

1111
01:37:56,462 --> 01:38:02,884
So by starting with v equals infinity, I make sure that the very first action I find, that will be less than this value of v.

1112
01:38:03,630 --> 01:38:04,612
And then I do the same thing.

1113
01:38:04,910 --> 01:38:14,820
Loop over all of my possible actions, and for each of the results that we could get, when the max player makes their decision, let's take the minimum of that and the current value of v.

1114
01:38:15,310 --> 01:38:21,508
So after all is said and done, I get the smallest possible value of V that I then return back to the user.

1115
01:38:22,446 --> 01:38:25,264
So that in effect is the pseudocode for Minimax.

1116
01:38:25,358 --> 01:38:34,064
That is how we take a game and figure out what the best move to make is by recursively using these max value and min value functions, where max value calls min value,

1117
01:38:34,384 --> 01:38:43,684
min value calls max value, back and forth all the way until we reach a terminal state, at which point our algorithm can simply return the utility of that particular state.

1118
01:38:45,070 --> 01:38:54,016
But what you might imagine is that this is going to start to be a long process, especially as games start to get more complex as we start to add more moves and more possible options

1119
01:38:54,096 --> 01:38:56,292
and games that might last quite a bit longer.

1120
01:38:56,750 --> 01:39:07,172
So the next question to ask is what sort of optimizations can we make here? How can we do better in order to use less space or take less time to be able to solve this kind of problem?

1121
01:39:08,078 --> 01:39:10,468
And we'll take a look at a couple of possible optimizations.

1122
01:39:10,894 --> 01:39:12,900
But for one, we'll take a look at this example.

1123
01:39:13,326 --> 01:39:15,652
Again, returning to these up arrows and down arrows.

1124
01:39:15,856 --> 01:39:20,004
Let's imagine that I now am the max player, this green arrow.

1125
01:39:20,176 --> 01:39:22,916
I am trying to make this score as high as possible.

1126
01:39:23,246 --> 01:39:25,860
And this is an easy game where there are just two moves.

1127
01:39:26,446 --> 01:39:35,332
I make a move, one of these three options, and then my opponent makes a move, one of these three options, based on what move I make, and as a result, we get some value.

1128
01:39:36,302 --> 01:39:44,100
Let's look at the order in which I do these calculations and figure out if there are any optimizations I might be able to make to this calculation process.

1129
01:39:44,846 --> 01:39:46,852
I'm going to have to look at these states one at a time.

1130
01:39:47,278 --> 01:39:53,764
So let's say I start here on the left and say, all right, now I'm going to consider what will the min player, my opponent, try to do here.

1131
01:39:54,382 --> 01:40:01,216
Well the min player is going to look at all three of their possible actions and look at their value, because these are terminal states, they're the end of the game.

1132
01:40:01,518 --> 01:40:05,796
And so they'll see, all right, this node is a value of four, value of eight, value of five.

1133
01:40:06,510 --> 01:40:13,828
And the min player is going to say, well, all right, between these three options, four, eight, and five, I'll take the smallest one. I'll take the four.

1134
01:40:14,222 --> 01:40:15,748
So this state now has a value of four.

1135
01:40:16,718 --> 01:40:21,216
Then I, as the max player, say, all right, if I take this action, it will have a value of four.

1136
01:40:21,376 --> 01:40:24,772
That's the best that I can do, because min player is going to try and minimize my score.

1137
01:40:25,966 --> 01:40:27,136
So now what if I take this option?

1138
01:40:27,374 --> 01:40:28,292
We'll explore this next.

1139
01:40:28,718 --> 01:40:31,876
And now explore what the min player would do if I choose this action.

1140
01:40:32,334 --> 01:40:34,976
And the min player is going to say, all right, what are the three options?

1141
01:40:35,374 --> 01:40:39,076
The min player has options between nine, three, and seven.

1142
01:40:39,598 --> 01:40:42,340
And so three is the smallest among nine, three, and seven.

1143
01:40:42,670 --> 01:40:44,896
So we'll go ahead and say this state has a value of

1144
01:40:44,896 --> 01:40:49,124
three. So now I, as the max player, I have now explored two of my three options.

1145
01:40:49,454 --> 01:40:55,908
I know that one of my options will guarantee me a score of four at least, and one of my options will guarantee me a score of three.

1146
01:40:57,198 --> 01:41:00,004
And now I consider my third option and say, all right, what happens here?

1147
01:41:00,366 --> 01:41:07,684
Same exact logic. The min player is going to look at these three states, two, four, and six, say the minimum possible option is two, so the min player wants the two.

1148
01:41:08,558 --> 01:41:17,056
Now I, as the max player, have calculated all of the information by looking two layers deep, by looking at all of these nodes, and I can now say, between the four,

1149
01:41:17,230 --> 01:41:18,736
the three, and the two, you know what?

1150
01:41:18,862 --> 01:41:20,516
I'd rather take the four.

1151
01:41:20,846 --> 01:41:25,956
Because if I choose this option, if my opponent plays optimally, they will try and get me to the four.

1152
01:41:26,350 --> 01:41:27,524
But that's the best I can do.

1153
01:41:27,696 --> 01:41:33,536
I can't guarantee a higher score, because if I pick either of these two options, I might get a three or I might get a two.

1154
01:41:33,856 --> 01:41:38,532
And it's true that, down here is a nine, and that's the highest score out of any of the scores.

1155
01:41:38,798 --> 01:41:42,820
So I might be tempted to say, you know what, maybe I should take this option, because I might get the nine.

1156
01:41:43,534 --> 01:41:52,256
But if the min player is playing intelligently, if they're making the best moves at each possible option they have when they get to make a choice, I'll be left with a three,

1157
01:41:52,462 --> 01:41:56,388
whereas I could better, playing optimally, have guaranteed that I would get the four.

1158
01:41:58,030 --> 01:42:04,260
So that is in effect the logic that I would use as a Minimax player trying to maximize my score from that node there.

1159
01:42:05,070 --> 01:42:11,536
But it turns out it took quite a bit of computation for me to figure that out. I had to reason through all of these nodes in order to draw this conclusion.

1160
01:42:11,696 --> 01:42:17,380
And this is for a pretty simple game where I have three choices, my opponent has three choices, and then the game's over.

1161
01:42:18,318 --> 01:42:21,124
So, what I'd like to do is come up with some way to optimize this.

1162
01:42:21,296 --> 01:42:29,124
Maybe I don't need to do all of this calculation to still reach the conclusion that you know what, this action to the left, that's the best that I could do.

1163
01:42:29,902 --> 01:42:34,788
Let's go ahead and try again and try and be a little more intelligent about how I go about doing this.

1164
01:42:36,110 --> 01:42:38,212
So first, I start the exact same way.

1165
01:42:38,510 --> 01:42:44,548
I don't know what to do initially, so I just have to consider one of the options and consider what the min player might do.

1166
01:42:45,038 --> 01:42:47,364
Min has three options: four, eight, and five.

1167
01:42:47,662 --> 01:42:53,444
And between those three options, min says four is the best they can do, because they want to try to minimize the score.

1168
01:42:54,446 --> 01:43:02,404
Now I, the max player, will consider my second option, making this move here, and considering what my opponent would do in response.

1169
01:43:02,830 --> 01:43:03,908
What will the min player do?

1170
01:43:04,558 --> 01:43:10,020
Well, the min player is going to, from that state, look at their options, and I would say, all right, nine is an option,

1171
01:43:10,478 --> 01:43:19,364
three is an option, and if I am doing the math from this initial state, doing all this calculation, when I see a three, that should immediately be a red flag for me.

1172
01:43:20,334 --> 01:43:27,236
Because when I see a three down here at this state, I know that the value of this state is going to be at most three.

1173
01:43:27,950 --> 01:43:30,436
It's going to be three or something less than three.

1174
01:43:30,734 --> 01:43:36,324
Even though I haven't yet looked at this last action or even further actions, if there were more actions that could be taken here.

1175
01:43:36,974 --> 01:43:37,664
How do I know that?

1176
01:43:37,902 --> 01:43:41,380
Well, I know that the min player is going to try to minimize my score.

1177
01:43:42,062 --> 01:43:49,956
And if they see a three, the only way this could be something other than a three is if this remaining thing that I haven't yet looked at is less than three.

1178
01:43:50,638 --> 01:43:59,780
Which means there's no way for this value to be anything more than three, because the min player can already guarantee a three, and they are trying to minimize my score.

1179
01:44:01,102 --> 01:44:01,860
So what does that tell me?

1180
01:44:02,382 --> 01:44:08,516
Well, it tells me that if I choose this action, my score is going to be three or maybe even less than three if I'm unlucky.

1181
01:44:09,422 --> 01:44:12,932
But I already know that this action will guarantee me a four.

1182
01:44:13,678 --> 01:44:22,020
And so given that I know that this action guarantees me a score of four, and this action means I can't do better than three, if I'm trying to maximize my options,

1183
01:44:22,304 --> 01:44:25,344
there is no need for me to consider this triangle here.

1184
01:44:25,504 --> 01:44:30,784
There is no value, no number that could go here that would change my mind between these two options.

1185
01:44:30,944 --> 01:44:38,660
I'm always going to opt for this path that gets me a four, as opposed to this path where the best I can do is a three if my opponent plays optimally.

1186
01:44:39,854 --> 01:44:42,784
And this is going to be true for all the future states that I look at too.

1187
01:44:43,054 --> 01:44:53,264
That if I look over here at what min player might do over here, if I see that this state is a two, I know that this state is at most a two, because the only way this value could be something

1188
01:44:53,584 --> 01:45:00,068
other than two is if one of these remaining states is less than a two, and so the min player would opt for that instead.

1189
01:45:00,654 --> 01:45:05,188
So even without looking at these remaining states, I as the maximizing player

1190
01:45:05,870 --> 01:45:15,748
can know that choosing this path to the left is going to be better than choosing either of those two paths to the right, because this one can't be better than three.

1191
01:45:16,046 --> 01:45:17,424
this one can't be better than two.

1192
01:45:17,934 --> 01:45:20,772
and so four in this case is the best that I can do.

1193
01:45:21,742 --> 01:45:24,740
So in order to do this and I can say now that this state has a value of four.

1194
01:45:25,710 --> 01:45:34,464
So in order to do this type of calculation, I was doing a little bit more bookkeeping, keeping track of things, keeping track all the time of what is the best that I can do?

1195
01:45:34,670 --> 01:45:37,924
what is the worst that I can do? and for each of these states saying, "All, right?

1196
01:45:38,144 --> 01:45:45,636
well, if I already know that I can get a four, then if the best I can do at this state is a three, no reason for me to consider it.

1197
01:45:45,904 --> 01:45:50,532
I can effectively prune this leaf and anything below it from the tree.

1198
01:45:51,118 --> 01:45:56,324
And it's for that reason this approach, this optimization to Minimax, is called alpha beta pruning.

1199
01:45:56,590 --> 01:46:02,404
Alpha and beta stand for these two values that you'll have to keep track of of the best you can do so far and the worst you can do so far.

1200
01:46:02,670 --> 01:46:06,384
And pruning is the idea of if I have a big, long, deep search

1201
01:46:06,384 --> 01:46:11,024
tree, I might be able to search it more efficiently if I don't need to search through everything.

1202
01:46:11,184 --> 01:46:17,092
If I can remove some of the nodes to try and optimize the way that I look through this entire search space.

1203
01:46:18,318 --> 01:46:25,156
So alpha beta pruning can definitely save us a lot of time as we go about the search process by making our searches more efficient.

1204
01:46:25,600 --> 01:46:29,316
But even then, it's still not great as games get more complex.

1205
01:46:29,870 --> 01:46:35,040
Tic-tac-toe, fortunately, is a relatively simple game, and we might reasonably ask a question like

1206
01:46:35,840 --> 01:46:39,300
how many total possible tic-tac-toe games are there?

1207
01:46:39,982 --> 01:46:40,560
You can think about it.

1208
01:46:40,686 --> 01:46:45,284
You can try and estimate, how many moves are there at any given point, how many moves long can the game last.

1209
01:46:45,600 --> 01:46:46,404
It turns out

1210
01:46:47,150 --> 01:46:52,900
there are about 255,000 possible tic-tac-toe games that can be played.

1211
01:46:53,902 --> 01:46:57,956
But compare that to a more complex game, something like a game of chess, for example.

1212
01:46:58,254 --> 01:47:01,476
Far more pieces, far more moves, games that last much longer.

1213
01:47:01,934 --> 01:47:04,260
How many total possible chess games could there be?

1214
01:47:05,006 --> 01:47:14,628
It turns out that after just four moves each, four moves by the white player, four moves by the black player, that there are 288 billion possible chess games that can result

1215
01:47:14,926 --> 01:47:17,120
from that situation, after just four moves each.

1216
01:47:17,390 --> 01:47:27,460
And going even further, if you look at entire chess games and how many possible chess games there could be as a result there, there are more than 10 to the 29,000 possible chess games,

1217
01:47:27,680 --> 01:47:30,020
far more chess games than could ever be considered.

1218
01:47:30,478 --> 01:47:40,036
And this is a pretty big problem for the Minimax algorithm, because the Minimax algorithm starts with an initial state, considers all the possible actions, and all the possible actions after that,

1219
01:47:40,686 --> 01:47:44,000
all the way until we get to the end of the

1220
01:47:44,000 --> 01:47:53,828
game. And that's going to be a problem if the computer is going to need to look through this many states, which is far more than any computer could ever do in any reasonable amount of time.

1221
01:47:54,734 --> 01:47:56,720
So, what do we do in order to solve this problem?

1222
01:47:57,006 --> 01:48:02,160
Instead of looking through all these states, which is totally intractable for a computer, we need some better approach.

1223
01:48:02,510 --> 01:48:07,684
And it turns out that better approach generally takes the form of something called depth-limited Minimax.

1224
01:48:08,110 --> 01:48:10,596
Where normally Minimax is depth unlimited.

1225
01:48:10,800 --> 01:48:14,640
We just keep going layer after layer, move after move, until we get to the end of the game.

1226
01:48:15,118 --> 01:48:24,452
Depth limited Minimax is instead going to say, you know what, after a certain number of moves, maybe I'll look 10 moves ahead, maybe I'll look 12 moves ahead, but after that point, I'm going to stop

1227
01:48:24,720 --> 01:48:32,868
and not consider additional moves that might come after that, just because it would be computationally intractable to consider all of those possible options.

1228
01:48:34,094 --> 01:48:39,680
But what do we do after we get 10 or 12 moves deep and we arrive at a situation where the game's not over?

1229
01:48:40,046 --> 01:48:46,052
Minimax still needs a way to assign a score to that game board or game state to figure out what its current value is,

1230
01:48:46,318 --> 01:48:50,916
which is easy to do if the game is over, but not so easy to do if the game is not yet over.

1231
01:48:51,694 --> 01:48:57,124
So in order to do that, we need to add one additional feature to depth limited Minimax called an evaluation function,

1232
01:48:57,518 --> 01:49:03,492
which is just some function that is going to estimate the expected utility of a game from a given state.

1233
01:49:04,174 --> 01:49:14,960
So in a game like chess, if you imagine that a game value of one means white wins, negative one means black wins, zero means it's a draw, then you might imagine that a score of like 0.8

1234
01:49:15,374 --> 01:49:18,788
means white is very likely to win, though certainly not guaranteed.

1235
01:49:19,086 --> 01:49:25,060
And you would have an evaluation function that estimates how good the game state happens to be.

1236
01:49:25,600 --> 01:49:32,068
And depending on how good that evaluation function is, that is ultimately what's going to constrain how good the AI is.

1237
01:49:32,240 --> 01:49:40,400
The better the AI is at estimating how good or how bad any particular game state is, the better the AI is going to be able to play that game.

1238
01:49:40,750 --> 01:49:47,364
If the evaluation function is worse and not as good as estimating what the expected utility is, then it's going to be a whole lot harder.

1239
01:49:47,758 --> 01:49:50,852
And you can imagine trying to come up with these evaluation functions.

1240
01:49:51,214 --> 01:49:59,360
In chess, for example, you might write an evaluation function based on how many pieces you have as compared to how many pieces your opponent has, because each one has a value.

1241
01:49:59,566 --> 01:50:07,680
And your evaluation function probably needs to be a little bit more complicated than that to consider other possible situations that might arise as well.

1242
01:50:08,078 --> 01:50:14,640
And there are many other variants on Minimax that add additional features in order to help it perform better under these larger,

1243
01:50:14,800 --> 01:50:20,080
more computationally untractable situations where we couldn't possibly explore all of the possible moves.

1244
01:50:20,750 --> 01:50:27,716
So we need to figure out how to use evaluation functions and other techniques to be able to play these games ultimately better.

1245
01:50:28,494 --> 01:50:37,028
But this now was a look at this kind of adversarial search, these search problems where we have situations where I am trying to play against some sort of opponent.

1246
01:50:37,454 --> 01:50:41,412
And these search problems show up all over the place throughout artificial intelligence.

1247
01:50:41,678 --> 01:50:47,908
We've been talking a lot today about more classical search problems like trying to find directions from one location to another.

1248
01:50:48,112 --> 01:50:56,032
But any time an AI is faced with trying to make a decision, like what do I do now in order to do something that is rational or do something that is intelligent,

1249
01:50:56,270 --> 01:51:01,380
or trying to play a game, like figuring out what move to make, these sort of algorithms can really come in handy.

1250
01:51:01,678 --> 01:51:05,472
It turns out that for tic-tac-toe, the solution is pretty simple, because it's a small game.

1251
01:51:05,710 --> 01:51:13,956
XKCD has famously put together a webcomic where he will tell you exactly what move to make as the optimal move to make, no matter what your opponent happens to do.

1252
01:51:14,414 --> 01:51:19,472
This type of thing is not quite as possible for a much larger game like checkers or chess, for example,

1253
01:51:19,694 --> 01:51:25,092
where chess is totally computationally untractable for most computers to be able to explore all the possible states.

1254
01:51:25,486 --> 01:51:31,712
So we really need our AI to be far more intelligent about how they go about trying to deal with these problems

1255
01:51:31,872 --> 01:51:38,276
and how they go about taking this environment that they find themselves in and ultimately searching for one of these solutions.

1256
01:51:38,894 --> 01:51:41,668
So this then was a look at search in artificial intelligence.

1257
01:51:41,872 --> 01:51:49,392
Next time, we'll take a look at knowledge, thinking about how it is that our AIs are able to know information, reason about that information, and draw conclusions,

1258
01:51:49,632 --> 01:51:52,548
all in our look at AI and the principles behind it.

1259
01:51:52,846 --> 01:51:53,604
We'll see you next time.

1260
01:52:13,830 --> 01:52:17,630
All right, welcome back, everyone, to an introduction to artificial intelligence with Python.

1261
01:52:18,170 --> 01:52:26,596
Last time, we took a look at search problems in particular, where we have AI agents that are trying to solve some sort of problem by taking actions in some sort of environment,

1262
01:52:26,672 --> 01:52:34,592
Whether that environment is trying to take actions by playing moves in a game, or whether those actions are something like trying to figure out where to make turns in order to

1263
01:52:34,592 --> 01:52:37,156
get driving directions from point A to point B.

1264
01:52:37,870 --> 01:52:41,764
This time we're going to turn our attention more generally to just this idea of knowledge.

1265
01:52:42,158 --> 01:52:46,788
The idea that a lot of intelligence is based on knowledge, especially if we think about human intelligence.

1266
01:52:47,232 --> 01:52:54,084
People know information, we know facts about the world, and using that information that we know, we're able to draw conclusions,

1267
01:52:54,352 --> 01:53:02,352
reason about the information that we know in order to figure out how to do something or figure out some other piece of information that we conclude based on the

1268
01:53:02,352 --> 01:53:04,324
information we already have available to us.

1269
01:53:05,038 --> 01:53:13,828
What we'd like to focus on now is the ability to take this idea of knowledge and being able to reason based on knowledge and apply those ideas to artificial intelligence.

1270
01:53:14,254 --> 01:53:18,244
In particular, we're going to be building what are known as knowledge-based agents.

1271
01:53:18,672 --> 01:53:22,752
Agents that are able to reason and act by representing knowledge internally.

1272
01:53:23,054 --> 01:53:27,632
Somehow inside of our AI, they have some understanding of what it means to know something.

1273
01:53:27,918 --> 01:53:35,712
And ideally, they have some algorithms or some techniques they can use based on that knowledge that they know in order to figure out the solution to a problem

1274
01:53:35,822 --> 01:53:39,972
or figure out some additional piece of information that can be helpful in some sense.

1275
01:53:40,718 --> 01:53:44,352
So, what do we mean by reasoning based on knowledge, to be able to draw conclusions?

1276
01:53:44,622 --> 01:53:47,396
Well, let's look at a simple example drawn from the world of Harry Potter.

1277
01:53:47,952 --> 01:53:50,432
we take one sentence that we know to be true.

1278
01:53:50,592 --> 01:53:54,628
Imagine, if it didn't rain, then Harry visited Hagrid today.

1279
01:53:55,086 --> 01:53:57,188
So one fact that we might know about the world.

1280
01:53:57,806 --> 01:53:58,820
And then we take another fact.

1281
01:53:59,150 --> 01:54:02,340
Harry visited Hagrid or Dumbledore today, but not both.

1282
01:54:02,958 --> 01:54:09,152
So it tells us something about the world, that Harry either visited Hagrid but not Dumbledore, or Harry visited Dumbledore but not Hagrid.

1283
01:54:09,582 --> 01:54:14,084
And now we have a third piece of information about the world that Harry visited Dumbledore today.

1284
01:54:14,766 --> 01:54:21,284
So we now have three pieces of information now, three facts inside of a knowledge base, so to speak, information that we know.

1285
01:54:21,550 --> 01:54:28,740
And now we as humans can try and reason about this and figure out, based on this information, what additional information can we begin to conclude?

1286
01:54:29,230 --> 01:54:37,572
And well, looking at these last two statements, Harry either visited Hagrid or Dumbledore, but not both, and we know that Harry visited Dumbledore today,

1287
01:54:37,998 --> 01:54:43,492
well then it's pretty reasonable that we could draw the conclusion that you know what, Harry must not have visited Hagrid today.

1288
01:54:43,872 --> 01:54:51,872
Because based on a combination of these two statements, we can draw this inference, so to speak, a conclusion that Harry did not visit Hagrid today.

1289
01:54:52,590 --> 01:54:58,816
But it turns out we can even do a little bit better than that, get some more information, by taking a look at this first statement and reasoning about that.

1290
01:54:59,150 --> 01:55:03,524
This first statement says, if it didn't rain, then Harry visited Hagrid today.

1291
01:55:04,238 --> 01:55:04,736
So what does that mean?

1292
01:55:05,056 --> 01:55:08,496
In all cases where it didn't rain, then we know that Harry visited Hagrid.

1293
01:55:09,070 --> 01:55:16,388
But if we also know now that Harry did not visit Hagrid, then that tells us something about our initial premise that we were thinking about.

1294
01:55:16,590 --> 01:55:28,004
In particular, it tells us that it did rain today. Because we can reason, if it didn't rain, then Harry would have visited Hagrid, but we know for a fact that Harry did not visit Hagrid today.

1295
01:55:28,878 --> 01:55:37,696
So it's this kind of reasoning, this sort of logical reasoning, where we use logic based on the information that we know in order to take information and reach conclusions

1296
01:55:37,902 --> 01:55:40,612
that is going to be the focus of what we're going to be talking about today.

1297
01:55:40,896 --> 01:55:49,956
How can we make our artificial intelligence logical so that they can perform the same kinds of deduction, the same kinds of reasoning that we've been doing so far?

1298
01:55:50,606 --> 01:55:56,896
Of course, humans reason about logic, generally in terms of human language that I just now was speaking in English,

1299
01:55:57,056 --> 01:56:02,404
talking in English about these sentences and trying to reason through how it is that they relate to one another.

1300
01:56:02,576 --> 01:56:12,132
We're going to need to be a little bit more formal when we turn our attention to computers and being able to encode this notion of logic and truthhood and falsehood inside of a machine.

1301
01:56:12,654 --> 01:56:19,748
So we're going to need to introduce a few more terms and a few symbols that will help us reason through this idea of logic inside of an artificial intelligence.

1302
01:56:20,462 --> 01:56:22,340
And we'll begin with the idea of a sentence.

1303
01:56:22,798 --> 01:56:27,696
Now, a sentence in a natural language like English is just something that I'm saying, like what I'm saying right now.

1304
01:56:28,014 --> 01:56:36,548
In the context of AI, though, a sentence is just an assertion about the world in what we're going to call a knowledge representation language,

1305
01:56:36,896 --> 01:56:40,516
some way of representing knowledge inside of our computers.

1306
01:56:41,230 --> 01:56:47,856
And the way that we're going to spend most of today reasoning about knowledge is through a type of logic known as propositional logic.

1307
01:56:48,078 --> 01:56:56,336
There are a number of different types of logic, some of which we'll touch on, but propositional logic is based on a logic of propositions, or just statements about the world.

1308
01:56:56,654 --> 01:57:00,452
And so we begin in propositional logic with a notion of propositional symbols.

1309
01:57:01,070 --> 01:57:11,616
We will have certain symbols that are oftentimes just letters, something like P or Q or R, where each of those symbols is going to represent some fact or sentence about the world.

1310
01:57:11,918 --> 01:57:15,588
So P, for example, might represent the fact that it is raining.

1311
01:57:15,822 --> 01:57:19,056
And so P is going to be a symbol that represents that idea.

1312
01:57:19,216 --> 01:57:22,660
And Q, for example, might represent Harry visited Hagrid today.

1313
01:57:22,990 --> 01:57:28,196
Each of these propositional symbols represents some sentence or some fact about the world.

1314
01:57:29,390 --> 01:57:37,056
But in addition to just having individual facts about the world, we want some way to connect these propositional symbols together in order to reason

1315
01:57:37,070 --> 01:57:41,828
more complexly about other facts that might exist inside of the world in which we're reasoning.

1316
01:57:42,286 --> 01:57:47,172
So in order to do that, we'll need to introduce some additional symbols that are known as logical connectives.

1317
01:57:47,598 --> 01:57:56,132
Now there are a number of these logical connectives, but five of the most important, and the ones we're going to focus on today, are these five up here, each represented by a logical symbol.

1318
01:57:56,576 --> 01:58:07,332
Not is represented by this symbol here, and is represented as sort of an upside-down V, or is represented by a V shape, implication, and we'll talk about what that means in just a moment,

1319
01:58:07,696 --> 01:58:14,244
is represented by an arrow, and biconditional, again, we'll talk about what that means in a moment, is represented by these double arrows.

1320
01:58:14,606 --> 01:58:22,404
But these five logical connectives are the main ones we're going to be focusing on in terms of thinking about how it is that a computer can reason about

1321
01:58:22,416 --> 01:58:25,732
facts and draw conclusions based on the facts that it knows.

1322
01:58:26,606 --> 01:58:33,636
But in order to get there, we need to take a look at each of these logical connectives and build up an understanding for what it is that they actually mean.

1323
01:58:34,094 --> 01:58:36,420
So let's go ahead and begin with the not symbol.

1324
01:58:36,718 --> 01:58:37,892
So this not symbol here.

1325
01:58:38,222 --> 01:58:43,012
And what we're going to show for each of these logical connectives is what we're going to call a truth table.

1326
01:58:43,310 --> 01:58:52,324
A table that demonstrates what this word not means when we attach it to a propositional symbol or any sentence inside of our logical language.

1327
01:58:52,590 --> 01:58:56,388
And so the truth table for not is shown right here.

1328
01:58:56,846 --> 01:59:00,964
If P, some propositional symbol or some other sentence even, is false,

1329
01:59:01,696 --> 01:59:04,004
then not P is true.

1330
01:59:04,590 --> 01:59:08,036
And if P is true, then not P is false.

1331
01:59:09,038 --> 01:59:15,716
So you can imagine that placing this not symbol in front of some sentence of propositional logic just says the opposite of that.

1332
01:59:16,174 --> 01:59:23,396
So if, for example, P represented it is raining, then not P would represent the idea that it is not raining.

1333
01:59:23,886 --> 01:59:29,028
And as you might expect, if P is false, meaning if the sentence it is raining is false,

1334
01:59:29,806 --> 01:59:32,656
well then the sentence not P must be true.

1335
01:59:32,896 --> 01:59:35,716
The sentence that it is not raining is therefore true.

1336
01:59:36,270 --> 01:59:39,856
So not you can imagine just takes whatever is in P and it inverts it.

1337
01:59:40,096 --> 01:59:43,076
Turns false into true and true into false.

1338
01:59:43,536 --> 01:59:49,892
Much analogously to what the English word not means, just taking whatever comes after it and inverting it to mean the opposite.

1339
01:59:51,182 --> 01:59:57,508
Next up, and also very English-like, is this idea of and represented by this upside-down V shape or this point shape.

1340
01:59:58,126 --> 02:00:08,708
And, as opposed to just taking a single argument, the way not does, we have P and we have not P, AND is going to combine two different sentences in propositional logic together.

1341
02:00:09,166 --> 02:00:15,936
So I might have one sentence P and another sentence Q, and I want to combine them together to say P and Q.

1342
02:00:16,782 --> 02:00:22,372
And the general logic for what P and Q means is it means that both of its operands are true.

1343
02:00:22,656 --> 02:00:25,924
P is true and also Q is true.

1344
02:00:26,574 --> 02:00:28,740
And so here's what that truth table looks like.

1345
02:00:29,166 --> 02:00:37,776
This time we have two variables, P and Q, and when we have two variables, each of which can be in two possible states, true or false, that leads to two

1346
02:00:38,030 --> 02:00:42,212
squared or four possible combinations of truth and falsehood.

1347
02:00:42,606 --> 02:00:50,896
So we have P is false and Q is false, we have P is false and Q is true, P is true and Q is false, and then P and Q both are true.

1348
02:00:51,086 --> 02:00:54,820
And those are the only four possibilities for what P and Q could mean.

1349
02:00:55,502 --> 02:01:03,536
And in each of those situations, this third column here, P and Q, is telling us a little bit about what it actually means for P and Q to be true.

1350
02:01:03,758 --> 02:01:12,656
And we see that the only case where P and Q is true is in this fourth row here, where P happens to be true, Q also happens to be true,

1351
02:01:12,846 --> 02:01:17,412
and in all other situations, P and Q is going to evaluate to false.

1352
02:01:18,030 --> 02:01:21,456
So this again is much in line with what our intuition of and might mean.

1353
02:01:21,616 --> 02:01:28,068
If I say P and Q, I probably mean that I expect both P and Q to be true.

1354
02:01:29,294 --> 02:01:37,156
Next up, also potentially consistent with what we mean, is this word or represented by this V shape, sort of an upside-down and symbol.

1355
02:01:37,678 --> 02:01:41,988
And OR, as the name might suggest, is true if either of its arguments are true.

1356
02:01:42,222 --> 02:01:46,788
As long as P is true or Q is true, then P or Q is going to be true.

1357
02:01:47,342 --> 02:01:52,996
Which means the only time that P or Q is false is if both of its operands are false.

1358
02:01:53,390 --> 02:01:58,372
If P is false and Q is false, then P or Q is going to be false.

1359
02:01:58,766 --> 02:02:02,724
But in all other cases, at least one of the operands is true.

1360
02:02:03,086 --> 02:02:07,780
Maybe they're both true, in which case P or Q is going to evaluate to true.

1361
02:02:08,526 --> 02:02:15,296
Now, this is mostly consistent with the way that most people might use the word or in the sense of speaking the word or in normal English.

1362
02:02:15,534 --> 02:02:23,204
Though there is sometimes when we might say or where we mean P or Q, but not both, or we mean sort of it can only be one or the other.

1363
02:02:23,456 --> 02:02:32,816
It's important to note that this symbol here, this OR, means P or Q or both, that those are totally okay, as long as either or both of them are true,

1364
02:02:33,070 --> 02:02:36,176
then the OR is going to evaluate to be true as well.

1365
02:02:36,462 --> 02:02:42,852
It's only in the case where all of the operands are false that P or Q ultimately evaluates to false as well.

1366
02:02:43,246 --> 02:02:52,736
In logic, there's another symbol known as the exclusive or, which encodes this idea of exclusivity of one or the other, but not both, but we're not going to be focusing on that today.

1367
02:02:52,910 --> 02:03:00,036
Whenever we talk about or, we're always talking about either or both in this case, as represented by this truth table here.

1368
02:03:01,518 --> 02:03:04,256
So that now is not an and or.

1369
02:03:04,782 --> 02:03:09,028
And next up is what we might call implication, as denoted by this arrow symbol.

1370
02:03:09,294 --> 02:03:15,492
So we have P and Q, and this sentence here will generally read as P implies Q.

1371
02:03:16,206 --> 02:03:22,948
And what P implies Q means is that if P is true, then Q is also true.

1372
02:03:23,406 --> 02:03:27,428
So I might say something like, if it is raining, then I will be indoors.

1373
02:03:27,776 --> 02:03:34,052
Meaning it is raining implies I will be indoors, as the logical sentence that I'm saying there.

1374
02:03:34,574 --> 02:03:37,380
And the truth table for this can sometimes be a little bit tricky.

1375
02:03:37,646 --> 02:03:44,036
So obviously if P is true and Q is true, then P implies Q, that's true.

1376
02:03:44,256 --> 02:03:45,316
That definitely makes sense.

1377
02:03:45,936 --> 02:03:52,452
and it should also stand to reason that when P is true and Q is false, then P implies Q is false.

1378
02:03:52,816 --> 02:04:04,336
Because if I said to you, if it is raining, then I will be out indoors, and it is raining, but I'm not indoors, well then it would seem to be that my original statement was not true.

1379
02:04:04,718 --> 02:04:11,812
P implies Q means that if P is true, then Q also needs to be true, and if it's not, well then the statement is false.

1380
02:04:13,166 --> 02:04:16,772
What's also worth noting though is what happens when P is false.

1381
02:04:17,518 --> 02:04:21,700
When P is false, the implication makes no claim at all.

1382
02:04:22,222 --> 02:04:33,476
If I say something like, if it is raining, then I will be indoors, and it turns out it's not raining, then in that case I am not making any statement as to whether or not I will be indoors or not.

1383
02:04:33,838 --> 02:04:37,316
P implies Q, just means that if P is true, Q must be true.

1384
02:04:37,582 --> 02:04:42,628
But if P is not true, then we make no claim about whether or not Q is true at all.

1385
02:04:43,054 --> 02:04:50,404
So in either case, if P is false, it doesn't matter what Q is, whether it's false or true, we're not making any claim about Q whatsoever.

1386
02:04:50,608 --> 02:04:53,188
We can still evaluate the implication to true.

1387
02:04:53,648 --> 02:05:02,436
The only way that the implication is ever false is if our premise P is true, but the conclusion that we're drawing Q happens to be false.

1388
02:05:02,990 --> 02:05:07,300
So in that case we would say P does not imply Q in that case.

1389
02:05:09,454 --> 02:05:12,772
Finally, the last connective that we'll discuss is this biconditional.

1390
02:05:13,198 --> 02:05:16,996
You can think of a biconditional as a condition that goes in both directions.

1391
02:05:17,518 --> 02:05:24,768
So originally when I said something like, if it is raining, then I will be indoors, I didn't say what would happen if it wasn't raining.

1392
02:05:25,008 --> 02:05:26,884
Maybe I'll be indoors, maybe I'll be outdoors.

1393
02:05:27,374 --> 02:05:30,980
This biconditional you can read as an if and only if.

1394
02:05:31,502 --> 02:05:32,208
So I can say,

1395
02:05:33,408 --> 02:05:36,612
I will be indoors if and only if it is raining.

1396
02:05:36,974 --> 02:05:43,076
Meaning if it is raining, then I will be indoors, and if I am indoors, it's reasonable to conclude that it is also raining.

1397
02:05:43,598 --> 02:05:47,972
So this biconditional is only true when P and Q are the same.

1398
02:05:48,590 --> 02:05:53,168
So if P is true and Q is true, then this biconditional is also true.

1399
02:05:53,488 --> 02:05:55,968
P implies Q, but also the reverse is true.

1400
02:05:56,208 --> 02:05:57,956
Q also implies P.

1401
02:05:58,510 --> 02:06:02,288
so if P and Q both happen to be false, we would still say it's true.

1402
02:06:02,478 --> 02:06:08,356
But in any of these other two situations, this P if and only if Q is going to ultimately evaluate to false.

1403
02:06:08,974 --> 02:06:16,804
So a lot of trues and falses going on there, but these five basic logical connectives are going to form the core of the language of propositional logic,

1404
02:06:17,088 --> 02:06:25,476
the language that we're going to use in order to describe ideas, and the language that we're going to use in order to reason about those ideas in order to draw conclusions.

1405
02:06:26,574 --> 02:06:33,568
So let's now take a look at some of the additional terms that we'll need to know about in order to go about trying to form this language of propositional logic

1406
02:06:33,742 --> 02:06:37,316
and writing AI that's actually able to understand this sort of logic.

1407
02:06:37,614 --> 02:06:42,308
The next thing we're going to need is the notion of what is actually true about the world.

1408
02:06:42,528 --> 02:06:52,292
We have a whole bunch of propositional symbols, P and Q and R and maybe others, but we need some way of knowing what actually is true in the world. Is P true or false? Is Q true or false?

1409
02:06:52,688 --> 02:06:53,860
So on and so forth.

1410
02:06:54,350 --> 02:06:56,932
And to do that, we'll introduce the notion of a model.

1411
02:06:57,422 --> 02:07:05,188
A model just assigns a truth value, where a truth value is either true or false, to every propositional symbol.

1412
02:07:05,678 --> 02:07:08,708
In other words, it's creating what we might call a possible world.

1413
02:07:09,358 --> 02:07:10,500
So let me give an example.

1414
02:07:10,830 --> 02:07:22,436
If, for example, I have two propositional symbols, P is it is raining, and Q is it is a Tuesday, a model just takes each of these two symbols and assigns a truth value to them,

1415
02:07:22,688 --> 02:07:24,164
either true or false.

1416
02:07:24,654 --> 02:07:25,828
So here's a sample model.

1417
02:07:26,048 --> 02:07:35,556
In this model, in other words, in this possible world, it is possible that P is true, meaning it is raining, and Q is false, meaning it is not a Tuesday.

1418
02:07:35,982 --> 02:07:39,008
But there are other possible worlds or other models as well.

1419
02:07:39,214 --> 02:07:43,908
There is some model where both of these variables are true, some model where both of these variables are false.

1420
02:07:44,208 --> 02:07:52,900
In fact, if there are n variables that are propositional symbols like this that are either true or false, then the number of possible models is 2 to the n,

1421
02:07:53,248 --> 02:08:02,276
because each of these possible models, uh, possible variables within my model could be set to either true or false if I don't know any information about it.

1422
02:08:03,758 --> 02:08:04,512
So now that I have

1423
02:08:05,472 --> 02:08:12,932
the symbols and the connectives that I'm going to need in order to construct these parts of knowledge, we need some way to represent that knowledge.

1424
02:08:13,358 --> 02:08:17,796
And to do so, we're going to allow our AI access to what we'll call a knowledge base.

1425
02:08:18,382 --> 02:08:23,940
And a knowledge base is really just a set of sentences that our AI knows to be true.

1426
02:08:24,238 --> 02:08:30,020
Some set of sentences in propositional logic that are things that our AI knows about the world.

1427
02:08:30,894 --> 02:08:39,588
And so we might tell our AI some information, information about a situation that it finds itself in, or a situation about a problem that it happens to be trying to solve.

1428
02:08:39,854 --> 02:08:44,196
And we would give that information to the AI that the AI would store inside of its knowledge base.

1429
02:08:44,846 --> 02:08:52,740
And what happens next is the AI would like to use that information in the knowledge base to be able to draw conclusions about the rest of the world.

1430
02:08:53,358 --> 02:08:54,916
And what do those conclusions look like?

1431
02:08:55,438 --> 02:09:01,636
Well, to understand those conclusions, we'll need to introduce one more idea, one more symbol, and that is the notion of entailment.

1432
02:09:02,606 --> 02:09:11,620
So this sentence here, with this double turnstile in these Greek letters, this is the Greek letter alpha and the Greek letter beta, and we read this as alpha entails

1433
02:09:11,950 --> 02:09:12,228
beta.

1434
02:09:12,846 --> 02:09:16,484
And alpha and beta here are just sentences in propositional logic.

1435
02:09:17,230 --> 02:09:26,756
And what this means is that alpha entails beta means that in every model, in other words, in every possible world in which sentence A is true,

1436
02:09:27,246 --> 02:09:31,140
or sentence alpha is true, then sentence beta is also true.

1437
02:09:31,502 --> 02:09:40,932
So if something entails something else, if alpha entails beta, it means that if I know alpha to be true, then beta must therefore also be true.

1438
02:09:41,262 --> 02:09:50,432
So if my alpha is something like, I know that it is a Tuesday in January, then a reasonable beta might be something like

1439
02:09:50,672 --> 02:09:52,196
I know that it is January.

1440
02:09:52,592 --> 02:09:58,884
Because in all worlds where it is a Tuesday in January, I know for sure that it must be January, just by definition.

1441
02:09:59,182 --> 02:10:03,712
This first statement or sentence about the world entails the second statement.

1442
02:10:03,822 --> 02:10:11,620
And we can reasonably use deduction based on that first sentence to figure out that the second sentence is in fact true as well.

1443
02:10:12,270 --> 02:10:17,152
And ultimately, it's this idea of entailment that we're going to try and encode into our computer.

1444
02:10:17,312 --> 02:10:21,872
We want our AI agent to be able to figure out what the possible entailments are.

1445
02:10:22,032 --> 02:10:31,012
We want our AI to be able to take these three sentences, sentences like, if it didn't rain, Harry visited Hagrid, that Harry visited Hagrid or Dumbledore, but not both,

1446
02:10:31,406 --> 02:10:32,900
and that Harry visited Dumbledore.

1447
02:10:33,152 --> 02:10:41,796
And just using that information, we'd like our AI to be able to infer or figure out that using these three sentences inside of a knowledge base,

1448
02:10:42,192 --> 02:10:43,684
we can draw some conclusions.

1449
02:10:44,046 --> 02:10:52,512
In particular, we can draw the conclusions here that one, Harry did not visit Hagrid today, and we can draw the entailment to that it did, in fact,

1450
02:10:52,846 --> 02:10:53,380
rain today.

1451
02:10:53,806 --> 02:10:56,228
And this process is known as inference.

1452
02:10:56,494 --> 02:11:01,668
And that's what we're going to be focusing on today, this process of deriving new sentences from old ones.

1453
02:11:01,952 --> 02:11:09,860
That I give you these three sentences, you put them in the knowledge base in, say, the AI, and the AI is able to use some sort of inference algorithm to

1454
02:11:09,872 --> 02:11:13,316
figure out that these two sentences must also be true.

1455
02:11:13,998 --> 02:11:15,684
And that is how we define inference.

1456
02:11:16,622 --> 02:11:22,308
So let's take a look at an inference example to see how we might actually go about inferring things in a human sense

1457
02:11:22,542 --> 02:11:30,116
before we take a more algorithmic approach to see how we could encode this idea of inference in AI. And we'll see there are a number of ways that we can actually achieve

1458
02:11:30,958 --> 02:11:33,476
this. So again, we'll deal with a couple of propositional symbols.

1459
02:11:33,792 --> 02:11:35,492
we'll deal with P, Q, and R.

1460
02:11:35,790 --> 02:11:40,996
P is it is a Tuesday, Q is it is raining, and R is Harry will go for a run.

1461
02:11:41,232 --> 02:11:44,432
Three propositional symbols that we are just defining to mean this.

1462
02:11:44,592 --> 02:11:49,092
We're not saying anything yet about whether they're true or false, we're just defining what they are.

1463
02:11:50,190 --> 02:11:57,188
Now we'll give ourselves or an AI access to a knowledge base, abbreviated to KB, the knowledge that we know about the world.

1464
02:11:57,616 --> 02:11:59,204
We know this statement.

1465
02:11:59,638 --> 02:12:00,736
All right, so let's try to parse it.

1466
02:12:00,896 --> 02:12:04,868
The parentheses here are just used for precedent, so we can see what associates with what.

1467
02:12:05,262 --> 02:12:10,816
But you would read this as P and not Q implies R.

1468
02:12:11,982 --> 02:12:14,148
All right, so what does that mean? Let's put it piece by piece.

1469
02:12:14,576 --> 02:12:16,260
P is it is a Tuesday.

1470
02:12:16,878 --> 02:12:20,836
Q is it is raining, so not Q is it is not raining.

1471
02:12:21,550 --> 02:12:29,284
And implies R is Harry will go for a run. So the way to read this entire sentence in human natural language at least, is

1472
02:12:30,030 --> 02:12:34,500
if it is a Tuesday and it is not raining, then Harry will go for a run.

1473
02:12:35,502 --> 02:12:41,220
So if it is a Tuesday and it is not raining, then Harry will go for a run, and that is now inside of our knowledge base.

1474
02:12:41,774 --> 02:12:45,284
And let's now imagine that our knowledge base has two other pieces of information as well.

1475
02:12:45,486 --> 02:12:48,868
It has information that P is true, that it is a Tuesday,

1476
02:12:49,646 --> 02:12:56,324
and we also have the information not Q, that it is not raining, that this sentence Q, it is raining, happens to be false.

1477
02:12:57,070 --> 02:12:59,204
And those are the three sentences that we have access to.

1478
02:12:59,854 --> 02:13:03,364
P and not Q implies R, P and not Q.

1479
02:13:04,366 --> 02:13:07,204
Using that information, we should be able to draw some inferences.

1480
02:13:07,982 --> 02:13:13,508
P and not Q is only true if both P and not Q are true.

1481
02:13:14,062 --> 02:13:20,196
Well, alright, we know that P is true, and we know that not Q is true, so we know that this whole expression is true.

1482
02:13:20,558 --> 02:13:26,816
And the definition of implication is if this whole thing on the left is true, then this thing on the right must also be true.

1483
02:13:27,022 --> 02:13:31,216
So if we know that P and not Q is true, then R must be true as well.

1484
