02:46:03,982 --> 02:46:06,336
This is what a classic logic puzzle might look like.

1859
02:46:06,542 --> 02:46:14,756
Something like Gilderoy, Minerva, Pomona, and Horace each belong to a different one of the four houses, Gryffindor, Hufflepuff, Ravenclaw, and Slytherin.

1860
02:46:15,056 --> 02:46:16,256
And then we have some information.

1861
02:46:16,622 --> 02:46:24,256
That Gilderoy belongs to Gryffindor or Ravenclaw, Pomona does not belong in Slytherin, and Minerva does belong to Gryffindor.

1862
02:46:24,398 --> 02:46:32,548
So we have a couple pieces of information, and using that information, we need to be able to draw some conclusions about which person should be assigned to which house.

1863
02:46:33,230 --> 02:46:36,900
And again, we can use the exact same idea to try and implement this notion.

1864
02:46:37,550 --> 02:46:46,244
So we need some propositional symbols. And in this case, the propositional symbols are going to get a little more complex, although we'll see ways to make this a little bit cleaner later on.

1865
02:46:46,510 --> 02:46:49,060
But we'll need 16 propositional symbols.

1866
02:46:49,376 --> 02:46:51,460
One for each person and house.

1867
02:46:52,014 --> 02:46:56,132
So we need to say, remember, every propositional symbol is either true or false.

1868
02:46:56,558 --> 02:46:59,136
So Gilderoy Gryffindor is either true or false.

1869
02:46:59,296 --> 02:47:01,136
Either he's in Gryffindor or he is not.

1870
02:47:01,486 --> 02:47:03,620
Likewise, Gilderoy Hufflepuff also true or false.

1871
02:47:03,856 --> 02:47:05,572
Either it is true or it's false.

1872
02:47:05,870 --> 02:47:10,564
And that's true for every combination of person and house that we could come up with.

1873
02:47:10,862 --> 02:47:13,732
We have some sort of propositional symbol for each one of those.

1874
02:47:14,926 --> 02:47:22,176
Using this type of knowledge, we can then begin to think about what types of logical sentences we can say about the puzzle.

1875
02:47:22,414 --> 02:47:30,656
That if we know before we even think about the information we were given, we can think about the premise of the problem, that every person is assigned to a different

1876
02:47:30,656 --> 02:47:32,388
house. So what does that tell us?

1877
02:47:32,750 --> 02:47:34,336
Well, it tells us sentences like this.

1878
02:47:34,416 --> 02:47:39,300
It tells us like Pomona Slytherin implies not Pomona Hufflepuff.

1879
02:47:39,918 --> 02:47:44,388
Something like, if Pomona is in Slytherin, then we know that Pomona is not in Hufflepuff.

1880
02:47:44,736 --> 02:47:53,124
And we know this for all four people and for all combinations of houses, that no matter what person you pick, if they're in one house, then they're not in some other house.

1881
02:47:53,646 --> 02:47:56,976
So I'll probably have a whole bunch of knowledge statements that are of this form.

1882
02:47:57,262 --> 02:48:00,964
That if we know Pomona's in Slytherin, then we know Pomona is not in Hufflepuff.

1883
02:48:01,710 --> 02:48:05,316
We were also given the information that each person is in a different house.

1884
02:48:05,838 --> 02:48:08,164
So I also have pieces of knowledge that look something like this.

1885
02:48:08,526 --> 02:48:12,676
Minerva Ravenclaw implies not Gilderoy Ravenclaw.

1886
02:48:13,136 --> 02:48:19,776
If they're all in different houses, then if Minerva is in Ravenclaw, then we know that Gilderoy is not in Ravenclaw as well.

1887
02:48:19,982 --> 02:48:26,628
And I have a whole bunch of similar sentences like this that are expressing that idea for other people and other houses as well.

1888
02:48:27,406 --> 02:48:31,812
And so in addition to sentences of these form, I also have the knowledge that was given to me.

1889
02:48:32,176 --> 02:48:40,356
Information like Gilderoy was in Gryffindor or in Ravenclaw that would be represented like this, Gilderoy Gryffindor or Gilderoy Ravenclaw,

1890
02:48:40,656 --> 02:48:46,052
and then using these sorts of sentences, I can begin to draw some conclusions about the world.

1891
02:48:46,734 --> 02:48:47,716
So let's see an example of this.

1892
02:48:48,078 --> 02:48:52,452
We'll go ahead and actually try and implement this logic puzzle to see if we can figure out what the answer is.

1893
02:48:53,294 --> 02:48:58,436
I'll go ahead and open up puzzle.py, where I've already started to implement this sort of idea.

1894
02:48:58,798 --> 02:49:06,576
I've defined a list of people and a list of houses, and I've so far created one symbol for every person and for every house.

1895
02:49:06,736 --> 02:49:12,612
That's what this double for loop is doing, looping over all people, looping over all houses, creating a new symbol for each of them.

1896
02:49:13,390 --> 02:49:15,684
And then I've added some information.

1897
02:49:16,174 --> 02:49:21,764
I know that every person belongs to a house, so I've added the information for every person

1898
02:49:22,510 --> 02:49:30,656
that person Gryffindor, or person Hufflepuff, or person Ravenclaw, or person Slytherin, that one of those four things must be true.

1899
02:49:30,896 --> 02:49:32,452
Every person belongs to a house.

1900
02:49:33,198 --> 02:49:34,576
What other information do I know?

1901
02:49:34,830 --> 02:49:37,856
I also know that only one house per person.

1902
02:49:38,030 --> 02:49:40,836
So no person belongs to multiple houses.

1903
02:49:41,710 --> 02:49:42,436
So how does this work?

1904
02:49:42,830 --> 02:49:46,276
Well, this is going to be true for all people, so I'll loop over every person,

1905
02:49:47,022 --> 02:49:50,816
and then I need to loop over all different pairs of houses.

1906
02:49:51,086 --> 02:49:57,028
The idea is I want to encode the idea that if Minerva is in Gryffindor, then Minerva can't be in Ravenclaw.

1907
02:49:57,486 --> 02:50:05,540
So I'll loop over all houses, H1, and I'll loop over all houses again, H2, and as long as they're different, h1 not equal to h2,

1908
02:50:06,190 --> 02:50:08,772
then I'll add to my knowledge base this piece of information.

1909
02:50:09,198 --> 02:50:17,456
That implication, in other words, an if then, if the person is in h1, then I know that they are not in house h2.

1910
02:50:18,574 --> 02:50:25,380
So these lines here are encoding the notion that for every person, if they belong to house one, then they are not in house two.

1911
02:50:26,126 --> 02:50:30,544
And the other piece of logic we need to encode is the idea that every house can only have one person.

1912
02:50:30,862 --> 02:50:37,732
In other words, if Pomona is in Hufflepuff, then nobody else is allowed to be in Hufflepuff either. And that's the same logic but sort of backwards.

1913
02:50:37,984 --> 02:50:48,964
I loop over all of the houses and loop over all different pairs of people. So I loop over people once, loop over people again, and only do this when the people are different, P1 not equal to P2.

1914
02:50:50,062 --> 02:50:54,212
And I add the knowledge that if, as given by the implication,

1915
02:50:54,944 --> 02:50:57,316
if person one belongs to the house,

1916
02:50:58,030 --> 02:51:03,012
then it is not the case that person two belongs to the same house.

1917
02:51:03,886 --> 02:51:07,812
So here I'm just encoding the knowledge that represents the problem's constraints.

1918
02:51:07,904 --> 02:51:09,540
I know that everyone's in a different house.

1919
02:51:09,744 --> 02:51:18,212
I know that any person can only belong to one house, and I can now take my knowledge and try and print out the information that I happen to know.

1920
02:51:18,638 --> 02:51:23,264
So I'll go ahead and print out knowledge.formula just to see this in action.

1921
02:51:23,598 --> 02:51:26,148
And I'll go ahead and skip this for now, but we'll come back to this in a second.

1922
02:51:26,798 --> 02:51:31,424
Let's print out the knowledge that I know by running python puzzle.py.

1923
02:51:31,822 --> 02:51:36,624
It's a lot of information, a lot that I have to scroll through, because there's 16 different variables all going on.

1924
02:51:36,942 --> 02:51:40,624
But the basic idea if we scroll up to the very top is I see my initial information.

1925
02:51:41,038 --> 02:51:50,532
Gilderoy is either in Gryffindor, or Gilderoy is in Hufflepuff, or Gilderoy is in Ravenclaw, or Gilderoy is in Slytherin, and then way more information as well.

1926
02:51:50,830 --> 02:51:57,984
So this is quite messy, more than we really want to be looking at, and soon too we'll see ways of representing this a little bit more nicely using logic.

1927
02:51:58,158 --> 02:52:01,092
But for now, we can just say these are the variables that we're dealing with.

1928
02:52:01,454 --> 02:52:03,844
And now we'd like to add some information.

1929
02:52:05,486 --> 02:52:12,004
So the information we're going to add is Gilderoy is in Gryffindor or he is in Ravenclaw, so that knowledge was given to us.

1930
02:52:12,590 --> 02:52:17,184
So I'll go ahead and say knowledge.add, and I know that either or

1931
02:52:18,414 --> 02:52:19,428
Gilderoy Gryffindor

1932
02:52:21,904 --> 02:52:22,224
or

1933
02:52:24,078 --> 02:52:25,796
Gilderoy Ravenclaw.

1934
02:52:26,384 --> 02:52:28,388
One of those two things must be true.

1935
02:52:29,230 --> 02:52:38,116
I also know that Pomona was not in Slytherin, so I can say knowledge.add not this symbol, not the Pomona Slytherin symbol.

1936
02:52:38,670 --> 02:52:44,196
And then I can add the knowledge that Minerva is in Gryffindor by adding the symbol Minerva

1937
02:52:44,846 --> 02:52:45,156
Gryffindor.

1938
02:52:46,766 --> 02:52:48,772
So those are the pieces of knowledge that I know.

1939
02:52:49,166 --> 02:52:57,664
And this loop here at the bottom just loops over all of my symbols, checks to see if the knowledge entails that symbol by calling this model check function

1940
02:52:57,664 --> 02:53:02,308
again. And if it does, if we know the symbol is true, we print out the symbol.

1941
02:53:03,566 --> 02:53:08,784
So now I can run Python, puzzle.py, and Python is going to solve this puzzle for me.

1942
02:53:08,878 --> 02:53:17,984
We're able to conclude that Gilderoy belongs to Ravenclaw, Pomona belongs to Hufflepuff, Minerva to Gryffindor, and Horace to Slytherin just by encoding this knowledge inside the computer,

1943
02:53:18,064 --> 02:53:24,004
although it was quite tedious to do in this case, and as a result, we were able to get the conclusion from that as well.

1944
02:53:24,846 --> 02:53:28,864
And you can imagine this being applied to many sorts of different deductive situations.

1945
02:53:29,070 --> 02:53:37,344
So, not only these situations where we're trying to deal with Harry Potter characters in this puzzle, but if you've ever played games like Mastermind, where you're trying to figure out, which

1946
02:53:37,504 --> 02:53:45,664
order different colors, go in and trying to make predictions about it, I could tell you, for example, let's play a simplified version of Mastermind, where there are four colors:

1947
02:53:45,824 --> 02:53:50,692
red, blue, green, and yellow, and they're in some order, but I'm not telling you what order.

1948
02:53:51,024 --> 02:53:56,932
You just have to make a guess, and I'll tell you of red, blue, green, and yellow how many of the four you got in the right position.

1949
02:53:57,262 --> 02:53:59,024
So a simplified version of this game.

1950
02:53:59,310 --> 02:54:07,744
You might make a guess like red, blue, green, yellow, and I would tell you something like two of those four are in the correct position, but the other two are not.

1951
02:54:08,622 --> 02:54:12,768
Then you could reasonably make a guess and say, all right, let's try this: blue, red, green, yellow.

1952
02:54:13,006 --> 02:54:14,308
Try switching two of them around.

1953
02:54:14,638 --> 02:54:18,788
And this time, maybe I tell you, you know what? None of those are in the correct position.

1954
02:54:19,438 --> 02:54:23,812
And the question then is, all right, what is the correct order of these four colors?

1955
02:54:24,302 --> 02:54:26,128
And we as humans could begin to reason this through.

1956
02:54:26,318 --> 02:54:35,300
All right, well, if none of these were correct, but two of these were correct, well, it must have been because I switched the red and the blue, which means red and blue here must be correct,

1957
02:54:35,566 --> 02:54:40,128
which means green and yellow are probably not correct. You can begin to do this sort of deductive reasoning.

1958
02:54:40,368 --> 02:54:49,088
We can also equivalently try and take this and encode it inside of our computer as well. And it's going to be very similar to the logic puzzle that we just did a moment ago.

1959
02:54:49,486 --> 02:54:52,132
So I won't spend too much time on this code because it is fairly similar.

1960
02:54:52,430 --> 02:54:58,528
But again, we have a whole bunch of colors and four different positions in which those colors can be. And

1961
02:54:58,542 --> 02:55:04,608
then we have some additional knowledge. And I encode all of that knowledge, and you can take a look at this code on your own time.

1962
02:55:04,848 --> 02:55:08,868
but I just want to demonstrate that when we run this code, ru run python

1963
02:55:09,518 --> 02:55:14,148
mastermind.py, and run and see what we get, we ultimately are able to compute

1964
02:55:14,862 --> 02:55:23,652
red zero in the zero position, blue in the one position, yellow in the two position, and green in the three position as the ordering of those symbols.

1965
02:55:24,110 --> 02:55:28,208
Now ultimately what you might have noticed is this process was taking quite a long time.

1966
02:55:28,526 --> 02:55:36,528
And in fact, model checking is not a particularly efficient algorithm, What I need to do in order to model check is take all of my possible different variables

1967
02:55:36,750 --> 02:55:39,300
and enumerate all of the possibilities that they could be in.

1968
02:55:39,488 --> 02:55:47,888
If I have n variables, I have two to the n possible worlds that I need to be looking through in order to perform this model checking algorithm.

1969
02:55:48,048 --> 02:55:56,036
And this is probably not tractable, especially as we start to get to much larger and larger sets of data where you have many, many more variables that are at play.

1970
02:55:56,270 --> 02:56:01,284
Right here, we only have a relatively small number of variables, so this sort of approach can actually work.

1971
02:56:01,550 --> 02:56:08,964
But as the number of variables increases, model checking becomes less and less good of a way of trying to solve these sorts of problems.

1972
02:56:09,870 --> 02:56:16,368
So while it might have been okay for something like Mastermind to conclude that this is indeed the correct sequence where all four are in the correct position,

1973
02:56:16,622 --> 02:56:23,876
what we'd like to do is come up with some better ways to be able to make inferences rather than just enumerate all of the possibilities.

1974
02:56:24,558 --> 02:56:32,772
And to do so, what we'll transition to next is the idea of inference rules, some sort of rules that we can apply to take knowledge that already exists

1975
02:56:33,168 --> 02:56:35,648
and translate it into new forms of knowledge.

1976
02:56:35,982 --> 02:56:40,292
And the general way we'll structure an inference rule is by having a horizontal line here.

1977
02:56:40,782 --> 02:56:45,220
Anything above the line is going to represent a premise, something that we know to be true.

1978
02:56:45,902 --> 02:56:54,276
And then anything below the line will be the conclusion that we can arrive at after we apply the logic from the inference rule that we're going to demonstrate.

1979
02:56:54,638 --> 02:57:00,708
So we'll do some of these inference rules by demonstrating them in English first, but then translating them into the world of propositional logic

1980
02:57:00,942 --> 02:57:03,812
so you can see what those inference rules actually look like.

1981
02:57:04,622 --> 02:57:08,448
So for example, let's imagine that I have access to two pieces of information.

1982
02:57:08,608 --> 02:57:13,476
I know, for example, that if it is raining, then Harry is inside, for example.

1983
02:57:14,094 --> 02:57:16,260
And let's say I also know it is raining.

1984
02:57:17,134 --> 02:57:22,788
Then most of us could reasonably then look at this information and conclude that, all right, Harry must be inside.

1985
02:57:23,886 --> 02:57:29,412
This inference rule is known as modus ponens, and it's phrased more formally in logic as this.

1986
02:57:29,888 --> 02:57:37,248
If we know that alpha implies beta, in other words, if alpha, then beta, and we also know that alpha is

1987
02:57:37,248 --> 02:57:41,220
true, then we should be able to conclude that beta is also true.

1988
02:57:41,518 --> 02:57:47,172
We can apply this inference rule to take these two pieces of information and generate this new piece of information.

1989
02:57:47,854 --> 02:57:56,228
Notice that this is a totally different approach from the model checking approach, where the approach was look at all of the possible worlds and see what's true in each of these worlds.

1990
02:57:56,750 --> 02:57:59,008
Here, we're not dealing with any specific world.

1991
02:57:59,214 --> 02:58:04,368
We're just dealing with the knowledge that we know and what conclusions we can arrive at based on that knowledge.

1992
02:58:04,528 --> 02:58:09,508
That I know that A implies B and I know A, and the conclusion is B.

1993
02:58:09,968 --> 02:58:18,848
And this should seem like a relatively obvious rule that of course, if alpha, then beta, and we know alpha, then we should be able to conclude that beta is also true.

1994
02:58:19,022 --> 02:58:23,488
And that's going to be true for many, but maybe even all, of the inference rules that we'll take a look at.

1995
02:58:23,568 --> 02:58:26,980
You should be able to look at them and say, yeah, of course that's going to be true.

1996
02:58:27,342 --> 02:58:35,488
But it's putting these all together, figuring out the right combination of inference rules that can be applied that ultimately is going to allow us to generate

1997
02:58:35,648 --> 02:58:37,668
interesting knowledge inside of our AI.

1998
02:58:38,446 --> 02:58:41,280
So that's modus ponens this application of implication.

1999
02:58:41,422 --> 02:58:46,372
That if we know alpha and we know that alpha implies beta, then we can conclude beta.

2000
02:58:47,246 --> 02:58:48,388
Let's take a look at another example.

2001
02:58:48,782 --> 02:58:51,920
Fairly straightforward, something like Harry is friends with Ron and Hermione.

2002
02:58:52,558 --> 02:58:56,740
Based on that information, we can reasonably conclude Harry is friends with Hermione.

2003
02:58:57,040 --> 02:58:58,148
That must also be true.

2004
02:58:58,734 --> 02:59:01,316
And this inference rule is known as and elimination.

2005
02:59:01,838 --> 02:59:10,916
And what and elimination says is that if we have a situation where alpha and beta are both true, I have information alpha and beta,

2006
02:59:11,566 --> 02:59:12,228
well then

2007
02:59:13,006 --> 02:59:13,988
just alpha is true.

2008
02:59:14,382 --> 02:59:16,240
Or likewise, just beta is true.

2009
02:59:16,558 --> 02:59:20,612
That if I know that both parts are true, then one of those parts must also be true.

2010
02:59:21,006 --> 02:59:26,960
Again, something obvious from the point of view of human intuition, but a computer needs to be told this kind of information.

2011
02:59:27,278 --> 02:59:32,000
To be able to apply the inference rule, we need to tell the computer that this is an inference rule that you can apply,

2012
02:59:32,238 --> 02:59:39,280
so the computer has access to it and is able to use it in order to translate information from one form to another.

2013
02:59:40,142 --> 02:59:43,140
In addition to that, let's take a look at another example of an inference rule.

2014
02:59:43,534 --> 02:59:48,036
Something like, it is not true that Harry did not pass the test.

2015
02:59:48,910 --> 02:59:50,692
Bit of a tricky sentence to parse, read it again.

2016
02:59:50,960 --> 02:59:54,660
It is not true, or it is false that Harry did not pass the test.

2017
02:59:55,040 --> 03:00:01,892
Well, if it is false that Harry did not pass the test, then the only reasonable conclusion is that Harry did pass the test.

2018
03:00:02,830 --> 03:00:07,440
And so this instead of being and elimination is what we call double negation elimination.

2019
03:00:07,598 --> 03:00:12,000
That if we have two negatives inside of our premise, then we can just remove them altogether.

2020
03:00:12,080 --> 03:00:13,120
They cancel each other out.

2021
03:00:13,200 --> 03:00:17,120
One turns true to false, and the other one turns false back into true.

2022
03:00:17,518 --> 03:00:25,700
Phrased a little bit more formally, we say that if the premise is not, not alpha, then the conclusion we can draw is just alpha.

2023
03:00:25,840 --> 03:00:27,460
We can say that alpha is true.

2024
03:00:28,718 --> 03:00:30,000
We'll take a look at a couple more of these.

2025
03:00:30,254 --> 03:00:33,188
If I have it is raining, then Harry is inside.

2026
03:00:33,998 --> 03:00:35,492
How do I reframe this?

2027
03:00:35,918 --> 03:00:43,460
Well, this one is a little bit trickier, but if I know if it is raining, then Harry is inside, then I conclude one of two things must be true.

2028
03:00:43,950 --> 03:00:47,844
Either it is not raining or Harry is inside.

2029
03:00:48,462 --> 03:00:50,308
Now this one's trickier, so let's think about it a little bit.

2030
03:00:50,734 --> 03:00:58,372
This first premise here, if it is raining, then Harry is inside, is saying that if I know that it is raining, then Harry must be inside.

2031
03:00:59,278 --> 03:01:01,412
So what is the other possible case?

2032
03:01:01,838 --> 03:01:06,276
Well, if Harry is not inside, then I know that it must not be raining.

2033
03:01:06,830 --> 03:01:09,252
So one of those two situations must be true.

2034
03:01:09,646 --> 03:01:14,244
Either it's not raining, or it is raining, in which case Harry is inside.

2035
03:01:14,830 --> 03:01:17,604
So the conclusion I can draw is either it is not raining,

2036
03:01:18,320 --> 03:01:22,340
or it is raining, so therefore Harry is inside.

2037
03:01:22,926 --> 03:01:27,428
And so this is a way to translate if-then statements into or statements.

2038
03:01:28,014 --> 03:01:30,340
And this is known as implication elimination.

2039
03:01:30,958 --> 03:01:37,796
And this is similar to what we actually did in the beginning when we were first looking at those very first sentences about Harry and Hagrid and Dumbledore.

2040
03:01:38,000 --> 03:01:47,268
And phrased a little bit more formally, this says that if I have the implication alpha implies beta, that I can draw the conclusion that either not alpha

2041
03:01:47,760 --> 03:01:48,708
or beta.

2042
03:01:49,166 --> 03:01:53,508
Because there are only two possibilities, either alpha is true or alpha is not true.

2043
03:01:53,998 --> 03:01:56,676
So one of those possibilities is alpha is not true.

2044
03:01:57,262 --> 03:02:01,284
But if alpha is true, well then we can draw the conclusion that beta must be true.

2045
03:02:01,614 --> 03:02:11,920
So either alpha is not true, or alpha is true, in which case beta is also true. So this is one way to turn an implication into just a statement about or.

2046
03:02:12,430 --> 03:02:17,200
In addition to eliminating implications, we can also eliminate biconditionals as well.

2047
03:02:17,582 --> 03:02:23,300
So let's take an English example, something like it is raining if and only if Harry is inside.

2048
03:02:23,598 --> 03:02:30,692
And this if and only if really sounds like that biconditional, that double arrow sign that we saw in propositional logic not too long ago.

2049
03:02:31,182 --> 03:02:33,412
And what does this actually mean if we were to translate this?

2050
03:02:33,806 --> 03:02:40,160
Well, this means that if it is raining, then Harry is inside, and if Harry is inside, then it is raining.

2051
03:02:40,240 --> 03:02:42,468
That this implication goes both ways.

2052
03:02:43,022 --> 03:02:45,840
And this is what we would call biconditional elimination.

2053
03:02:46,000 --> 03:02:51,520
That I can take a biconditional, a if and only if B, and translate that into something like this.

2054
03:02:51,840 --> 03:02:55,652
A implies B, and B implies A.

2055
03:02:56,430 --> 03:03:05,040
So many of these inference rules are taking logic that uses certain symbols and turning them into different symbols, taking an implication and turning it into an OR,

2056
03:03:05,200 --> 03:03:08,260
or taking a biconditional and turning it into implication.

2057
03:03:08,622 --> 03:03:10,852
And another example of it would be something like this

2058
03:03:11,598 --> 03:03:15,684
it is not true that both Harry and Ron passed the test.

2059
03:03:16,558 --> 03:03:18,628
Well, all right, how do we translate that? What does that mean?

2060
03:03:18,816 --> 03:03:22,116
Well, if it is not true that both of them passed the

2061
03:03:22,766 --> 03:03:27,936
test, well then the reasonable conclusion we might draw is that at least one of them didn't pass the test.

2062
03:03:28,078 --> 03:03:32,900
So the conclusion is either Harry did not pass the test or Ron did not pass the test.

2063
03:03:33,136 --> 03:03:34,976
Or both, this is not an exclusive or.

2064
03:03:35,296 --> 03:03:44,356
But if it is true that it is not true that both Harry and Ron passed the test, well then either Harry didn't pass the test or Ron didn't pass the test.

2065
03:03:45,198 --> 03:03:52,016
And this type of law is one of DeMorgan's laws, quite famous in logic, where the idea is that we can turn an AND into an OR.

2066
03:03:52,256 --> 03:03:59,696
We can say we can take this AND that both Harry and Ron passed the test and turn it into an OR by moving the knots around.

2067
03:03:59,886 --> 03:04:08,420
So if it is not true that Harry and Ron passed the test, well then either Harry did not pass the test or Ron did not pass the test either.

2068
03:04:08,878 --> 03:04:15,172
And the way we frame that more formally using logic is to say this. If it is not true that alpha and beta,

2069
03:04:15,950 --> 03:04:19,620
well then either not alpha or not beta.

2070
03:04:20,622 --> 03:04:27,776
The way I like to think about this is that if you have a negation in front of an AND expression, you move the negation inwards, so to speak,

2071
03:04:27,936 --> 03:04:34,052
moving the negation into each of these individual sentences, and then flip the AND into an or.

2072
03:04:34,766 --> 03:04:42,980
So the negation moves inwards and the and flips into an or, so I go from not a and b to not a or not b.

2073
03:04:43,790 --> 03:04:47,844
And there's actually a reverse of DeMorgan's law that goes in the other direction for something like this.

2074
03:04:48,270 --> 03:04:53,572
If I say, it is not true that Harry or Ron passed the test, meaning neither of them passed the test,

2075
03:04:54,254 --> 03:05:00,356
well then the conclusion I can draw is that Harry did not pass the test, and Ron did not pass the test.

2076
03:05:01,070 --> 03:05:06,016
So in this case, instead of turning an and into an or, we're turning an or into an and.

2077
03:05:06,542 --> 03:05:07,616
But the idea is the same.

2078
03:05:07,696 --> 03:05:10,404
And this again is another example of DeMorgan's laws.

2079
03:05:10,830 --> 03:05:22,016
And the way that works is that if I have not a or b this time, the same logic is going to apply. I'm going to move the negation inwards, and I'm going to flip this time, flip the or into an and.

2080
03:05:22,638 --> 03:05:30,976
So if not A or B, meaning it is not true that A or B or alpha or beta, then I can say not alpha and

2081
03:05:31,374 --> 03:05:31,812
not beta.

2082
03:05:32,686 --> 03:05:35,620
Moving the negation inwards in order to make that conclusion.

2083
03:05:36,558 --> 03:05:40,228
So those are DeMorgan's laws and a couple other inference rules that are worth just taking a look at.

2084
03:05:40,656 --> 03:05:43,056
one is the distributive law that works this way.

2085
03:05:43,310 --> 03:05:47,332
So if I have alpha and beta or gamma,

2086
03:05:48,014 --> 03:05:55,140
well, then much in the same way that you can use in math, use distributive laws to distribute operands, like addition and multiplication.

2087
03:05:55,406 --> 03:06:03,908
I can do a similar thing here, where I can say: if alpha and beta or gamma, then I can say something like alpha and beta

2088
03:06:04,496 --> 03:06:10,692
or alpha and gamma, that I've been able to distribute this and sign throughout this expression.

2089
03:06:11,118 --> 03:06:20,228
So this is an example of the distributive property or the distributive law as applied to logic in much the same way that you would distribute like a multiplication over the addition of something,

2090
03:06:20,416 --> 03:06:20,932
for example.

2091
03:06:22,016 --> 03:06:23,428
this works the other way too.

2092
03:06:23,694 --> 03:06:34,148
So if, for example, I have alpha or beta and gamma, I can distribute the or throughout the expression. I can say alpha or beta and alpha or gamma.

2093
03:06:34,446 --> 03:06:36,256
So the distributive law works in that way too.

2094
03:06:36,462 --> 03:06:40,016
And it's helpful if I want to take an or and move it into the expression.

2095
03:06:40,302 --> 03:06:45,060
And we'll see an example soon of why it is that we might actually care to do something like that.

2096
03:06:46,382 --> 03:06:49,252
All right, so now we've seen a lot of different inference rules.

2097
03:06:49,582 --> 03:06:56,836
And the question now is how can we use those inference rules to actually try and draw some conclusions, to actually try and prove something about entailment,

2098
03:06:57,166 --> 03:07:03,492
proving that given some initial knowledge base, we would like to find some way to prove that a query is true.

2099
03:07:04,462 --> 03:07:09,924
Well, one way to think about it is actually to think back to what we talked about last time when we talked about search problems.

2100
03:07:10,446 --> 03:07:13,092
Recall again that search problems have some sort of initial state.

2101
03:07:13,376 --> 03:07:19,908
They have actions that you can take from one state to another, as defined by a transition model that tells you how to get from one state to another.

2102
03:07:20,206 --> 03:07:30,052
We talked about testing to see if you were at a goal, and then some path cost function to see how many steps did you have to take, or how costly was the solution that you found.

2103
03:07:31,022 --> 03:07:39,972
Now that we have these inference rules that take some set of sentences in propositional logic and get us some new set of sentences in propositional logic,

2104
03:07:40,384 --> 03:07:46,788
we can actually treat those sentences, or those sets of sentences, as states inside of a search problem.

2105
03:07:47,214 --> 03:07:55,460
So if we want to prove that some query is true, prove that some logical theorem is true, we can treat theorem proving as a form of a search problem.

2106
03:07:55,824 --> 03:08:04,784
I can say that we begin in some initial state, where that initial state is the knowledge base that I begin with, the set of all of the sentences that I know to be

2107
03:08:04,784 --> 03:08:07,104
true. What actions are available to me?

2108
03:08:07,264 --> 03:08:11,460
Well, the actions are any of the inference rules that I can apply at any given time.

2109
03:08:12,078 --> 03:08:20,356
The transition model just tells me after I apply the inference rule, here is the new set of all of the knowledge that I have, which will be the old set of knowledge,

2110
03:08:20,558 --> 03:08:28,420
plus some additional inference that I've been able to draw, much as in the same way we saw what we got when we applied those inference rules and got some sort of conclusion.

2111
03:08:28,704 --> 03:08:33,508
That conclusion gets added to our knowledge base, and our transition model will encode that.

2112
03:08:34,222 --> 03:08:35,140
What is the goal test?

2113
03:08:35,406 --> 03:08:44,068
Well, our goal test is checking to see if we have proved the statement we're trying to prove, if the thing we're trying to prove is inside of our knowledge base.

2114
03:08:44,814 --> 03:08:52,708
And the path cost function, the thing we're trying to minimize, is maybe the number of inference rules that we needed to use, the number of steps, so to speak,

2115
03:08:53,024 --> 03:08:53,860
inside of our proof.

2116
03:08:54,798 --> 03:09:02,436
And so here we've been able to apply the same types of ideas that we saw last time with search problems to something like trying to prove something about knowledge

2117
03:09:02,544 --> 03:09:10,624
by taking our knowledge and framing it in terms that we can understand as a search problem with an initial state, with actions, with a transition model.

2118
03:09:10,958 --> 03:09:12,452
So this shows a couple of things.

2119
03:09:12,686 --> 03:09:22,372
One being how versatile search problems are, that they can be the same types of algorithms that we use to solve a maze or figure out how to get from point A to point B inside of driving directions,

2120
03:09:22,464 --> 03:09:30,884
for example, can also be used as a theorem-proving method of taking some sort of starting knowledge base and trying to prove something about that knowledge.

2121
03:09:31,918 --> 03:09:37,604
So this yet again is a second way, in addition to model checking, to try and prove that certain statements are true.

2122
03:09:38,734 --> 03:09:45,924
But it turns out there's yet another way that we can try and apply inference, and we'll talk about this now, which is not the only way, but certainly one of the most common,

2123
03:09:46,318 --> 03:09:48,100
which is known as resolution.

2124
03:09:48,526 --> 03:09:58,052
And resolution is based on another inference rule that we'll take a look at now, quite a powerful inference rule that will let us prove anything that can be proven about a knowledge base.

2125
03:09:58,766 --> 03:10:00,836
And it's based on this basic idea.

2126
03:10:01,294 --> 03:10:06,852
Let's say I know that either Ron is in the Great Hall or Hermione is in the library.

2127
03:10:07,982 --> 03:10:11,268
And let's say I also know that Ron is not in the Great Hall.

2128
03:10:12,494 --> 03:10:15,492
Based on those two pieces of information, what can I conclude?

2129
03:10:16,142 --> 03:10:19,556
Well, I could pretty reasonably conclude that Hermione must be in the library.

2130
03:10:20,142 --> 03:10:20,932
How do I know that?

2131
03:10:21,134 --> 03:10:29,988
Well, it's because these two statements, these two what we'll call complementary literals, literals that complement each other, they're opposites of each other,

2132
03:10:30,766 --> 03:10:31,940
seem to conflict with each other.

2133
03:10:32,590 --> 03:10:36,868
This sentence tells us that either Ron is in the Great Hall or Hermione is in the library.

2134
03:10:37,614 --> 03:10:44,644
So if we know that Ron is not in the great hall, that conflicts with this one, which means Hermione must be in the library.

2135
03:10:45,646 --> 03:10:53,584
And this we can frame as a more general rule known as the unit resolution rule, a rule that says that if we have P or Q,

2136
03:10:54,286 --> 03:10:56,224
and we also know not P,

2137
03:10:57,198 --> 03:10:59,812
well then from that we can reasonably conclude Q.

2138
03:11:00,366 --> 03:11:07,076
That if P or Q are true, and we know that P is not true, the only possibility is for Q to then be true.

2139
03:11:07,822 --> 03:11:14,704
And this, it turns out, is quite a powerful inference rule in terms of what it can do, in part because we can quickly start to generalize this rule.

2140
03:11:14,926 --> 03:11:18,864
This Q right here doesn't need to just be a single propositional symbol.

2141
03:11:19,024 --> 03:11:22,980
It could be multiple, all chained together in a single clause, as we'll call it.

2142
03:11:23,310 --> 03:11:31,908
So if I had something like P or Q1 or Q2 or Q3, so on and so forth, up until Qn, so I had n different other variables,

2143
03:11:32,366 --> 03:11:33,572
and I have not P.

2144
03:11:34,286 --> 03:11:45,540
well, then what happens when these two complement each other is that these two clauses resolve, so to speak, to produce a new clause that is just Q1 or Q2 all the way up to Qn.

2145
03:11:46,222 --> 03:11:51,876
And in an OR, the order of the arguments in the OR doesn't actually matter. The P doesn't need to be the first thing, it could have been in the middle.

2146
03:11:52,144 --> 03:12:00,484
But the idea here is that if I have P in one clause and not P in the other clause, well then I know that one of these remaining things must be true.

2147
03:12:00,750 --> 03:12:03,984
I've resolved them in order to produce a new clause.

2148
03:12:04,622 --> 03:12:12,164
But it turns out we can generalize this idea even further, in fact, and display even more power that we can have with this resolution rule.

2149
03:12:12,558 --> 03:12:13,700
So let's take another example.

2150
03:12:14,478 --> 03:12:20,676
Let's say, for instance, that I know the same piece of information that either Ron is in the Great Hall or Hermione is in the library.

2151
03:12:21,390 --> 03:12:28,516
And the second piece of information I know is that Ron is not in the Great Hall or Harry is sleeping.

2152
03:12:29,390 --> 03:12:31,332
So it's not just a single piece of information.

2153
03:12:31,600 --> 03:12:36,292
I have two different clauses, and we'll define clauses more precisely in just a

2154
03:12:36,480 --> 03:12:38,116
moment. What do I know here?

2155
03:12:38,574 --> 03:12:43,652
Well, again, for any propositional symbol, like Ron is in the Great Hall, there are only two possibilities.

2156
03:12:44,238 --> 03:12:50,116
Either Ron is in the Great Hall, in which case, based on resolution, we know that Harry must be sleeping.

2157
03:12:50,960 --> 03:12:58,148
Or Ron is not in the Great Hall, in which case we know based on the same rule that Hermione must be in the library.

2158
03:12:59,278 --> 03:13:07,492
Based on those two things in combination, I can say, based on these two premises, that I can conclude that either Hermione is in the library or

2159
03:13:07,886 --> 03:13:09,200
Harry is sleeping.

2160
03:13:10,446 --> 03:13:15,652
So again, because these two conflict with each other, I know that one of these two must be true.

2161
03:13:16,078 --> 03:13:21,988
And you can take a closer look and try and reason through that logic, make sure you convince yourself that you believe this conclusion.

2162
03:13:22,478 --> 03:13:27,972
Stated more generally, we can name this resolution rule by saying that if we know P or Q is true,

2163
03:13:28,654 --> 03:13:36,880
and we also know that not P or R is true, we resolve these two clauses together to get a new clause, Q

2164
03:13:37,166 --> 03:13:40,480
or R, that either Q or R must be

2165
03:13:40,480 --> 03:13:47,652
true. And again, much as in the last case, Q and R don't need to just be single propositional symbols. It could be multiple symbols.

2166
03:13:48,174 --> 03:13:55,140
So if I had a rule that had P or Q1 or Q2 or Q3, so on and so forth up until Qn, where N is just some number.

2167
03:13:55,662 --> 03:13:56,420
And likewise,

2168
03:13:57,198 --> 03:14:04,932
I had not P or R1 or R2, so on and so forth, up until Rm, where M again is just some other number,

2169
03:14:05,294 --> 03:14:14,480
I can resolve these two clauses together to get one of these must be true, Q1 or Q2 up until Qn, or R1 or R2 up until RM.

2170
03:14:14,670 --> 03:14:18,500
And this is just a generalization of that same rule we saw before.

2171
03:14:19,470 --> 03:14:27,332
Each of these things here are what we're going to call a clause, where a clause is formally defined as a disjunction of literals,

2172
03:14:27,694 --> 03:14:31,200
where a disjunction means it's a bunch of things that are connected with or.

2173
03:14:31,694 --> 03:14:36,640
Disjunction means things connected with or, conjunction, meanwhile, is things connected with and.

2174
03:14:37,326 --> 03:14:42,052
And a literal is either a propositional symbol or the opposite of a propositional symbol.

2175
03:14:42,318 --> 03:14:51,844
So it's something like P or Q or not P or not Q, those are all propositional symbols or not of the propositional symbols, and we call those literals.

2176
03:14:52,910 --> 03:14:56,228
So a clause is just something like this, P or Q or R, for example.

2177
03:14:57,902 --> 03:15:07,428
Meanwhile, what this gives us an ability to do is it gives us an ability to turn logic, any logical sentence, into something called conjunctive normal form.

2178
03:15:07,918 --> 03:15:13,732
A conjunctive normal form sentence is a logical sentence that is a conjunction of clauses.

2179
03:15:14,222 --> 03:15:22,000
Recall again, conjunction means things are connected to one another using and, and so a conjunction of clauses means that it is an and

2180
03:15:22,510 --> 03:15:25,028
of individual clauses, each of which has ors in it.

2181
03:15:25,422 --> 03:15:31,840
So something like this A or B or C, and D or not E, and F or G.

2182
03:15:32,174 --> 03:15:34,948
Everything in parentheses is one clause.

2183
03:15:35,374 --> 03:15:42,116
All of the clauses are connected to each other using an AND, and everything in the clause is separated using an or.

2184
03:15:42,990 --> 03:15:49,924
And this is just a standard form that we can translate a logical sentence into that just makes it easy to work with and easy to manipulate.

2185
03:15:50,382 --> 03:15:59,588
And it turns out that we can take any sentence in logic and turn it into conjunctive normal form just by applying some inference rules and transformations to it.

2186
03:15:59,950 --> 03:16:02,180
So we'll take a look at how we can actually do that.

2187
03:16:03,118 --> 03:16:09,892
So, what is the process for taking a logical formula and converting it into conjunctive normal form, otherwise known as CNF?

2188
03:16:10,446 --> 03:16:12,132
Well, the process looks a little something like this.

2189
03:16:12,480 --> 03:16:18,564
We need to take all of the symbols that are not part of conjunctive normal form, the biconditionals and the implications and so forth,

2190
03:16:18,880 --> 03:16:28,548
and turn them into something that is more closely like conjunctive normal form. So the first step will be to eliminate biconditionals, those if and only if double arrows.

2191
03:16:29,102 --> 03:16:33,892
And we know how to eliminate biconditionals because we saw there was an inference rule to do just that.

2192
03:16:34,126 --> 03:16:45,700
Anytime I have an expression like alpha if and only if beta, I can turn that into alpha implies beta and beta implies alpha, based on that inference rule we saw before.

2193
03:16:46,414 --> 03:16:52,324
Likewise, in addition to eliminating biconditionals, I can eliminate implications as well, the if then arrows.

2194
03:16:52,654 --> 03:16:55,780
And I can do that using the same inference rule we saw before too.

2195
03:16:56,110 --> 03:17:05,124
Taking alpha implies beta and turning that into not alpha or beta, because that is logically equivalent to this first thing here.

2196
03:17:06,446 --> 03:17:15,404
Then we can move knots inwards because we don't want knots on the outsides of our expressions. Conjunctive normal form requires that it's just clause and clause and clause and clause.

2197
03:17:15,776 --> 03:17:19,204
Any knots need to be immediately next to propositional symbols.

2198
03:17:19,534 --> 03:17:27,856
But we can move those knots around using DeMorgan's laws by taking something like not a and b and turn it into not a or

2199
03:17:28,174 --> 03:17:31,428
not b, for example, using DeMorgan's laws to manipulate that.

2200
03:17:31,758 --> 03:17:35,696
And after that, all we'll be left with are ands and ors, and those are easy to deal with.

2201
03:17:35,856 --> 03:17:44,708
We can use the distributive law to distribute the ors so that the ors end up on the inside of the expression, so to speak, and the ands end up on the outside.

2202
03:17:45,326 --> 03:17:49,764
So this is the general pattern for how we'll take a formula and convert it into conjunctive normal form.

2203
03:17:50,126 --> 03:17:56,580
And let's now take a look at an example of how we would do this and explore then why it is that we would want to do something like this.

2204
03:17:57,454 --> 03:17:58,308
Here's how we can do it.

2205
03:17:58,574 --> 03:18:00,228
Let's take this formula, for example.

2206
03:18:00,590 --> 03:18:11,684
P or Q implies R, and I'd like to convert this into conjunctive normal form, where it's all ands of clauses, and every clause is a disjunctive clause. It's ors together.

2207
03:18:12,430 --> 03:18:13,764
So what's the first thing I need to do?

2208
03:18:13,998 --> 03:18:17,700
Well, this is an implication, so let me go ahead and remove that implication.

2209
03:18:18,158 --> 03:18:26,176
Using the implication inference rule, I can turn P or Q into P or Q implies R into not

2210
03:18:26,574 --> 03:18:28,196
P or Q or R.

2211
03:18:29,038 --> 03:18:29,776
So that's the first step.

2212
03:18:29,856 --> 03:18:31,300
I've gotten rid of the implication.

2213
03:18:32,046 --> 03:18:39,588
And next, I can get rid of the not on the outside of this expression too. I can move the knots inwards so they're closer to the literals

2214
03:18:40,398 --> 03:18:42,692
themselves by using DeMorgan's laws.

2215
03:18:43,022 --> 03:18:50,052
And DeMorgan's law says that not P or Q is equivalent to not P and not Q.

2216
03:18:50,416 --> 03:18:55,776
Again, here just applying the inference rules that we've already seen in order to translate these

2217
03:18:55,776 --> 03:19:02,656
statements. And now I have two things that are separated by an OR, where this thing on the inside is an AND.

2218
03:19:03,086 --> 03:19:11,364
What I'd really like is to move the ORs so the ORs are on the inside, because conjunctive normal form means I need clause and clause and clause and clause.

2219
03:19:11,662 --> 03:19:13,956
And so to do that, I can use the distributive law.

2220
03:19:14,222 --> 03:19:22,736
If I have not P and not Q or R, I can distribute the OR r to both of these, to get not P or R

2221
03:19:23,216 --> 03:19:30,244
and NOT Q or R using the distributive law. And this now here at the bottom is in conjunctive normal form.

2222
03:19:30,496 --> 03:19:37,604
It is a conjunction and and of disjunctions, of clauses that just are separated by ORs.

2223
03:19:38,190 --> 03:19:48,548
And so this process can be used by any formula to take a logical sentence and turn it into this conjunctive normal form where I have clause and clause and clause and clause and clause and so on.

2224
03:19:49,838 --> 03:19:54,404
So why is this helpful? Why do we even care about taking all these sentences and converting them into this form?

2225
03:19:54,656 --> 03:20:03,216
It's because once they're in this form where we have these clauses, these clauses are the inputs to the resolution inference rule that we saw a moment ago.

2226
03:20:03,376 --> 03:20:12,772
That if I have two clauses where there's something that conflicts or something complementary between those two clauses, I can resolve them to get a new clause, to draw a new conclusion.

2227
03:20:13,134 --> 03:20:19,204
And we call this process inference by resolution, using the resolution rule to draw some sort of inference.

2228
03:20:19,598 --> 03:20:29,216
And it's based on the same idea, that if I have P or Q, this clause, and I have not P or R, that I can resolve these two clauses together to get Q

2229
03:20:29,550 --> 03:20:34,532
or R as the resulting clause, a new piece of information that I didn't have before.

2230
03:20:35,342 --> 03:20:39,268
Now a couple of key points that are worth noting about this before we talk about the actual algorithm.

2231
03:20:39,694 --> 03:20:46,628
One thing is that let's imagine we have P or Q or S, and I also have not P or R or S.

2232
03:20:47,246 --> 03:20:56,196
The resolution rule says that because this P conflicts with this not P, we would resolve to put everything else together, to get Q or S or R or S.

2233
03:20:57,006 --> 03:21:01,296
But it turns out that this double S is redundant, or S here and or S there.

2234
03:21:01,456 --> 03:21:03,376
It doesn't change the meaning of the sentence.

2235
03:21:03,726 --> 03:21:12,228
So in resolution, when we do this resolution process, we'll usually also do a process known as factoring, where we take any duplicate variables that show up and just eliminate them.

2236
03:21:12,494 --> 03:21:16,676
So q or s or r or s just becomes q or r or s.

2237
03:21:17,006 --> 03:21:20,612
The s only needs to appear once, no need to include it multiple times.

2238
03:21:21,966 --> 03:21:28,388
Now, one final question worth considering is what happens if I try to resolve P and not P together?

2239
03:21:28,974 --> 03:21:36,580
If I know that P is true and I know that not P is true, well resolution says I can merge these clauses together and look at everything else.

2240
03:21:37,134 --> 03:21:41,796
Well, in this case, there is nothing else, so I'm left with what we might call the empty clause.

2241
03:21:42,224 --> 03:21:43,300
I'm left with nothing.

2242
03:21:43,790 --> 03:21:46,340
And the empty clause is always false.

2243
03:21:46,926 --> 03:21:49,380
The empty clause is equivalent to just being false.

2244
03:21:49,870 --> 03:21:57,104
And that's pretty reasonable because it's impossible for both P and not P to both hold at the same time.

2245
03:21:57,424 --> 03:22:02,500
P is either true or it's not true, which means that if P is true, then this must be false.

2246
03:22:02,830 --> 03:22:04,944
And if this is true, then this must be false.

2247
03:22:05,104 --> 03:22:07,460
There's no way for both of these to hold at the same time.

2248
03:22:07,822 --> 03:22:16,388
So if ever I try and resolve these two, it's a contradiction, and I'll end up getting this empty clause where the empty clause I can call equivalent to false.

2249
03:22:17,390 --> 03:22:22,564
And this idea that if I resolve these two contradictory terms, I get the empty clause.

2250
03:22:23,022 --> 03:22:26,372
This is the basis for our inference by resolution algorithm.

2251
03:22:26,894 --> 03:22:30,276
Here's how we're going to perform inference by resolution at a very high level.

2252
03:22:30,990 --> 03:22:40,740
We want to prove that our knowledge base entails some query alpha, that based on the knowledge we have, we can prove conclusively that alpha is going to be true.

2253
03:22:41,550 --> 03:22:42,660
How are we going to do that?

2254
03:22:43,118 --> 03:22:51,044
Well, in order to do that, we're going to try to prove that if we know the knowledge and not alpha, that that would be a contradiction.

2255
03:22:51,662 --> 03:22:57,092
And this is a common technique in computer science more generally, this idea of proving something by contradiction.

2256
03:22:57,390 --> 03:23:05,764
If I want to prove that something is true, I can do so by first assuming that it is false and showing that it would be contradictory,

2257
03:23:06,126 --> 03:23:07,812
showing that it leads to some contradiction.

2258
03:23:08,334 --> 03:23:14,276
And if the thing I'm trying to prove, if when I assume it's false, leads to a contradiction, then it must be true.

2259
03:23:14,734 --> 03:23:18,372
And that's the logical approach or the idea behind a proof by contradiction.

2260
03:23:18,510 --> 03:23:19,780
And that's what we're going to do here.

2261
03:23:20,064 --> 03:23:25,732
We want to prove that this query alpha is true, so we're going to assume that it's not true.

2262
03:23:25,998 --> 03:23:29,924
We're going to assume not alpha, and we're going to try and prove that it's a contradiction.

2263
03:23:30,606 --> 03:23:35,844
If we do get a contradiction, well then we know that our knowledge entails the query alpha.

2264
03:23:36,366 --> 03:23:38,724
If we don't get a contradiction, there is no entailment.

2265
03:23:39,024 --> 03:23:48,644
This is this idea of a proof by contradiction of assuming the opposite of what you're trying to prove, and if you can demonstrate that that's a contradiction, then what you're proving must be true.

2266
03:23:49,838 --> 03:23:51,584
But more formally, how do we actually do this?

2267
03:23:51,664 --> 03:23:57,664
How do we check that knowledge base and not alpha is going to lead to a contradiction?

2268
03:23:57,998 --> 03:24:00,452
Well, here is where resolution comes into play.

2269
03:24:01,358 --> 03:24:09,764
To determine if our knowledge base entails some query alpha, we're going to convert knowledge base and not alpha to conjunctive normal form,

2270
03:24:09,984 --> 03:24:13,508
that form where we have a whole bunch of clauses that are all anded together.

2271
03:24:14,350 --> 03:24:23,012
And when we have these individual clauses, is now we can keep checking to see if we can use resolution to produce a new clause.

2272
03:24:23,584 --> 03:24:31,984
We can take any pair of clauses and check, Is there some literal that is the opposite of each other or complementary to each other in both of them?

2273
03:24:32,174 --> 03:24:39,108
For example, I have a P in one clause and a not P in another clause, or an R in one clause and a not R in another clause.

2274
03:24:39,438 --> 03:24:48,452
If ever I have that situation where once I convert to conjunctive normal form and I have a whole bunch of clauses, I see two clauses that I can resolve to produce a new clause,

2275
03:24:48,814 --> 03:24:49,424
then I'll do so.

2276
03:24:49,678 --> 03:24:59,332
This process occurs in a loop. I'm going to keep checking to see if I can use resolution to produce a new clause and keep using those new clauses to try to generate more new clauses after that.

2277
03:25:00,494 --> 03:25:06,768
Now it just so may happen that eventually we may produce the empty clause, the clause we were talking about before.

2278
03:25:06,848 --> 03:25:17,568
If I resolve P and not P together, that produces the empty clause, and the empty clause we know to be false, because we know that there's no way for both P and not

2279
03:25:17,808 --> 03:25:20,356
P to both simultaneously be true.

2280
03:25:21,230 --> 03:25:24,868
So if ever we produce the empty clause, then we have a contradiction.

2281
03:25:25,390 --> 03:25:29,568
And if we have a contradiction, that's exactly what we were trying to do in a proof by contradiction.

2282
03:25:29,742 --> 03:25:36,708
If we have a contradiction, then we know that our knowledge base must entail this query alpha. We know that alpha must be true.

2283
03:25:37,550 --> 03:25:45,188
And it turns out, and we won't go into the proof here, but you can show that otherwise, if you don't produce the empty clause, then there is no entailment.

2284
03:25:45,408 --> 03:25:53,092
If we run into a situation where there are no more new clauses to add, we've done all the resolution that we can do, and yet we still haven't produced the empty clause,

2285
03:25:53,358 --> 03:25:55,396
then there is no entailment in this case.

2286
03:25:56,334 --> 03:25:58,404
And this now is the resolution algorithm.

2287
03:25:58,688 --> 03:26:03,108
And it's very abstract looking, especially this idea of like what does it even mean to have the empty clause?

2288
03:26:03,566 --> 03:26:10,212
So let's take a look at an example, actually try and prove some entailment by using this inference by resolution process.

2289
03:26:11,246 --> 03:26:12,260
So here's our question.

2290
03:26:12,654 --> 03:26:14,020
We have this knowledge base.

2291
03:26:14,264 --> 03:26:24,004
Here is the knowledge that we know: A or B, and not B or C, and not C, and we want to know if all of this entails

2292
03:26:25,870 --> 03:26:32,036
So this is our knowledge base here, this whole log thing, and our query alpha is just this propositional symbol A.

2293
03:26:33,166 --> 03:26:33,860
So what do we do?

2294
03:26:34,190 --> 03:26:36,208
Well, first we want to prove by contradiction.

2295
03:26:36,462 --> 03:26:41,700
So we want to first assume that A is false and see if that leads to some sort of contradiction.

2296
03:26:42,094 --> 03:26:47,876
So here is what we're going to start with A or B and not B or C and not C. This is our knowledge base.

2297
03:26:48,590 --> 03:26:50,756
And we're going to assume not A.

2298
03:26:50,928 --> 03:26:55,524
We're going to assume that the thing we're trying to prove is in fact false.

2299
03:26:56,718 --> 03:27:00,996
And so this is now in conjunctive normal form, and I have four different clauses.

2300
03:27:01,408 --> 03:27:07,908
I have A or B, I have not B or C, I have not C, and I have not A.

2301
03:27:08,814 --> 03:27:14,852
And now I can begin to just pick two clauses that I can resolve and apply the resolution rule to them.

2302
03:27:15,790 --> 03:27:17,956
And so looking at these four clauses, I see, all right,

2303
03:27:18,798 --> 03:27:21,248
these two clauses are ones I can resolve.

2304
03:27:21,408 --> 03:27:25,764
I can resolve them because there are complementary literals that show up in them.

2305
03:27:25,968 --> 03:27:33,540
There's a C here and a not C here. So just looking at these two clauses, if I know that not B or C is true,

2306
03:27:34,190 --> 03:27:41,088
and I know that C is not true, well then I can resolve these two clauses to say, all right, not B, that must be true.

2307
03:27:41,248 --> 03:27:46,788
I can generate this new clause as a new piece of information that I now know to be true.

2308
03:27:47,982 --> 03:27:50,608
And all right, now I can repeat this process, do the process again.

2309
03:27:50,798 --> 03:27:53,728
Can I use resolution again to get some new conclusion?

2310
03:27:54,126 --> 03:27:58,212
Well, it turns out I can. I can use that new clause I just generated along with this one here.

2311
03:27:58,734 --> 03:28:00,292
There are complementary literals.

2312
03:28:00,688 --> 03:28:05,408
This B is complementary to or conflicts with this not B over

2313
03:28:05,408 --> 03:28:08,708
here. And so if I know that A or B is true,

2314
03:28:09,408 --> 03:28:14,916
and I know that B is not true, well then the only remaining possibility is that A must be true.

2315
03:28:15,502 --> 03:28:16,196
So now we have A.

2316
03:28:16,654 --> 03:28:18,532
That is a new clause that I've been able to generate.

2317
03:28:19,726 --> 03:28:23,168
And now I can do this one more time. I'm looking for two clauses that can be resolved.

2318
03:28:23,342 --> 03:28:29,888
And you might programmatically do this by just looping over all possible pairs of clauses and checking for complementary literals in each.

2319
03:28:30,190 --> 03:28:35,652
And here I can say, all right, I found two clauses, not a and a, that conflict with each other.

2320
03:28:36,288 --> 03:28:41,700
And when I resolve these two together, well, this is the same as when we were resolving P and not P from before.

2321
03:28:42,030 --> 03:28:47,812
When I resolve these two clauses together, I get rid of the A's and I'm left with the empty clause.

2322
03:28:48,206 --> 03:28:56,208
And the empty clause we know to be false, which means we have a contradiction, which means we can safely say that this whole knowledge base does entail A.

2323
03:28:56,368 --> 03:29:01,092
That if this sentence is true, that we know that A for sure is also true.

2324
03:29:01,998 --> 03:29:10,048
So, this now, using inference by resolution, is an entirely different way to take some statement and try and prove that it is in fact true.

2325
03:29:10,208 --> 03:29:18,436
Instead of enumerating all of the possible worlds that we might be in in order to try to figure out in which cases is the knowledge base true and in which cases are query true.

2326
03:29:18,768 --> 03:29:26,848
Instead, we use this resolution algorithm to say, let's keep trying to figure out what conclusions we can draw and see if we reach a contradiction.

2327
03:29:27,214 --> 03:29:33,284
And if we reach a contradiction, then that tells us something about whether our knowledge actually entails the query or not.

2328
03:29:33,774 --> 03:29:37,316
And it turns out there are many different algorithms that can be used for inference.

2329
03:29:37,518 --> 03:29:39,556
What we've just looked at here are just a couple of them.

2330
03:29:39,790 --> 03:29:43,716
And in fact, all of this is just based on one particular type of logic.

2331
03:29:44,046 --> 03:29:52,164
It's based on propositional logic, where we have these individual symbols and we connect them using and and or and not and implies and biconditionals.

2332
03:29:52,654 --> 03:29:56,356
But propositional logic is not the only kind of logic that exists.

2333
03:29:56,718 --> 03:30:00,996
And in fact, we see that there are limitations that exist in propositional logic.

2334
03:30:01,294 --> 03:30:10,128
Especially as we saw in examples, like with the mastermind example, or with the example with the logic puzzle, where we had different Hogwarts,

2335
03:30:10,288 --> 03:30:14,788
house people that belonged to different houses, and we were trying to figure out who belonged to which houses.

2336
03:30:15,054 --> 03:30:21,028
there were a lot of different propositional symbols that we needed in order to represent some fairly basic ideas.

2337
03:30:21,678 --> 03:30:30,116
So now, as a final topic that we'll take a look at just before we end class today, is one final type of logic different from propositional logic known as first order logic,

2338
03:30:30,288 --> 03:30:36,548
which is a little bit more powerful than propositional logic, and is going to make it easier for us to express certain types of ideas.

2339
03:30:37,198 --> 03:30:45,888
In propositional logic, if we think back to that puzzle with the people in the Hogwarts houses, we had a whole bunch of symbols, and every symbol could only be true or false.

2340
03:30:46,124 --> 03:30:55,168
right? We had a symbol for Minerva Gryffindor, which was either true if Minerva was in Gryffindor and false otherwise, and likewise for Minerva Hufflepuff and Minerva Ravenclaw and Minerva Slytherin,

2341
03:30:55,248 --> 03:30:55,748
and so forth.

2342
03:30:56,846 --> 03:31:05,488
But this was starting to get quite redundant, that we wanted some way to be able to express that there is a relationship between these propositional symbols, that Minerva shows up in all of them.

2343
03:31:05,678 --> 03:31:12,548
And also, I would have liked to have not have had so many different symbols to represent what really was a fairly straightforward problem.

2344
03:31:13,390 --> 03:31:19,012
So first order logic will give us a different way of trying to deal with this idea by giving us two different types of symbols.

2345
03:31:19,470 --> 03:31:26,084
We're going to have constant symbols that are going to represent objects like people or houses, and then predicate symbols,

2346
03:31:27,022 --> 03:31:32,644
which you can think of as relations or functions, that take an input and evaluate them to true or false;

2347
03:31:32,768 --> 03:31:40,356
for example, that tell us whether or not some property of some constant or some pair of constants or multiple constants actually holds.

2348
03:31:41,102 --> 03:31:42,820
So we'll see an example of that in just a moment.

2349
03:31:43,008 --> 03:31:49,088
But for now, in this same problem, our constant symbols might be objects, things like people or houses.

2350
03:31:49,364 --> 03:31:57,252
some Minerva, Pomona, Horace, Gilderoy, those are all constant symbols, as are my four houses: Gryffindor, Hufflepuff, Ravenclaw, and Slytherin.

2351
03:31:58,062 --> 03:32:05,700
Predicates, meanwhile, these predicate symbols are going to be properties that might hold true or false of these individual constants.

2352
03:32:06,208 --> 03:32:11,876
So person might hold true of Minerva, but it would be false for Gryffindor, because Gryffindor is not a person.

2353
03:32:12,270 --> 03:32:19,012
And house is going to hold true for Ravenclaw, but it's not going to hold true for Horace, for example, because Horace is a person.

2354
03:32:19,726 --> 03:32:26,048
And belongs to, meanwhile, is going to be some relation that is going to relate people to their houses.

2355
03:32:26,414 --> 03:32:29,636
And it's going to only tell me when someone belongs to a house or does not.

2356
03:32:30,478 --> 03:32:35,972
So let's take a look at some examples of what a sentence in first order logic might actually look like.

2357
03:32:36,430 --> 03:32:45,284
A sentence might look like something like this person Minerva, with Minerva in parentheses, and person being a predicate symbol, Minerva being a constant symbol.

2358
03:32:45,902 --> 03:32:54,052
This sentence in first order logic effectively means Minerva is a person, or the person property applies to the Minerva object.

2359
03:32:54,478 --> 03:32:59,940
So if I want to say something like Minerva is a person, here is how I express that idea using first-order logic.

2360
03:33:00,750 --> 03:33:06,788
Meanwhile, I can say something like house Gryffindor to likewise express the idea that Gryffindor is a house.

2361
03:33:07,278 --> 03:33:08,164
I can do that this way.

2362
03:33:08,750 --> 03:33:16,448
And all of the same logical connectives that we saw in propositional logic, those are going to work here too. So and or implication, biconditional, not.

2363
03:33:16,718 --> 03:33:20,484
In fact, I can use not to say something like not house Minerva.

2364
03:33:20,878 --> 03:33:25,956
And this sentence in first order logic means something like Minerva is not a house.

2365
03:33:26,128 --> 03:33:30,404
It is not true that the house property applies to Minerva.

2366
03:33:31,630 --> 03:33:41,732
Meanwhile, in addition to some of these predicate symbols that just take a single argument, some of our predicate symbols are going to express binary relations, relations between two of its arguments.

2367
03:33:42,126 --> 03:33:51,108
So I could say something like belongs to, and then two inputs, Minerva and Gryffindor, to express the idea that Minerva belongs to Gryffindor.

2368
03:33:51,886 --> 03:33:56,580
And so now here's the key difference or one of the key differences between this and propositional logic.

2369
03:33:56,878 --> 03:34:05,860
In propositional logic, I needed one symbol for Minerva Gryffindor, and one symbol for Minerva Hufflepuff, and one symbol for all the other people's Gryffindor and Hufflepuff variables.

2370
03:34:06,318 --> 03:34:12,420
In this case, I just need one symbol for each of my people and one symbol for each of my houses,

2371
03:34:13,134 --> 03:34:22,980
and then I can express as a predicate something like belongs to, and say belongs to Minerva Gryffindor, to express the idea that Minerva belongs to Gryffindor house.

2372
03:34:23,438 --> 03:34:33,796
So already we can see that first-order logic is quite expressive in being able to express these sorts of sentences using the existing constant symbols and predicates that already exist

2373
03:34:34,126 --> 03:34:36,992
while minimizing the number of new symbols that I need to create.

2374
03:34:37,198 --> 03:34:45,156
I can just use eight symbols for people, for houses, instead of 16 symbols for every possible combination of each.

2375
03:34:46,062 --> 03:34:51,716
But first order logic gives us a couple of additional features that we can use to express even more complex ideas.

2376
03:34:51,982 --> 03:34:55,716
And these additional features are generally known as quantifiers.

2377
03:34:56,110 --> 03:35:01,060
And there are two main quantifiers in first order logic, the first of which is universal quantification.

2378
03:35:01,614 --> 03:35:08,548
Universal quantification lets me express an idea like something is going to be true for all values of a variable.

2379
03:35:09,006 --> 03:35:12,644
Like for all values of x, some statement is going to hold true.

2380
03:35:13,486 --> 03:35:16,036
So what might a sentence in universal quantification look like?

2381
03:35:16,590 --> 03:35:20,452
Well, we're going to use this upside down a to mean for all.

2382
03:35:21,102 --> 03:35:26,020
So upside down ax means for all values of x, where x is any object,

2383
03:35:26,734 --> 03:35:27,972
this is going to hold true.

2384
03:35:28,846 --> 03:35:36,004
Belongs to x Gryffindor implies not belongs to x Hufflepuff.

2385
03:35:36,846 --> 03:35:37,956
So let's try and parse this out.

2386
03:35:38,192 --> 03:35:43,972
This means that for all values of X, if this holds true, if X belongs to Gryffindor,

2387
03:35:44,672 --> 03:35:46,564
then this does not hold true.

2388
03:35:46,992 --> 03:35:48,996
X does not belong to Hufflepuff.

2389
03:35:50,254 --> 03:36:00,356
So translated into English, this sentence is saying something like, for all objects X, if X belongs to Gryffindor, then X does not belong to Hufflepuff, for example.

2390
03:36:00,750 --> 03:36:05,124
Or phrased even more simply, anyone in Gryffindor is not in Hufflepuff.

2391
03:36:05,712 --> 03:36:07,332
Simplified way of saying the same

2392
03:36:07,982 --> 03:36:15,236
thing. So this universal quantification lets us express an idea like something is going to hold true for all values of a particular variable.

2393
03:36:16,398 --> 03:36:20,836
In addition to universal quantification, though, we also have existential quantification.

2394
03:36:21,902 --> 03:36:29,712
Whereas universal quantification said that something is going to be true for all values of a variable, existential quantification says that

2395
03:36:29,742 --> 03:36:36,068
some expression is going to be true for some value of a variable, at least one value of the variable.

2396
03:36:36,622 --> 03:36:40,132
So let's take a look at a sample sentence using existential quantification.

2397
03:36:40,558 --> 03:36:41,892
One such sentence looks like this.

2398
03:36:42,478 --> 03:36:52,672
There exists an x, this backwards e stands for exists, and here we're saying there exists an x such that house x and belongs to Minerva

2399
03:36:53,358 --> 03:36:59,972
In other words, there exists some object X where X is a house, and Minerva belongs to X.

2400
03:37:00,462 --> 03:37:04,996
Or phrased a little more succinctly in English, I'm here just saying Minerva belongs to a house.

2401
03:37:05,392 --> 03:37:09,668
There's some object that is a house and Minerva belongs to a house.

2402
03:37:10,254 --> 03:37:18,980
And combining this universal and existential quantification, we can create far more sophisticated logical statements than we were able to just using propositional logic.

2403
03:37:19,312 --> 03:37:21,220
I could combine these to say something like this.

2404
03:37:21,934 --> 03:37:30,788
For all x, person x implies there exists a y such that house y and belongs to xy.

2405
03:37:31,224 --> 03:37:33,312
All right. so a lot of stuff going on there, a lot of symbols.

2406
03:37:33,582 --> 03:37:35,972
Let's try and parse it out and just understand what it's saying.

2407
03:37:36,366 --> 03:37:42,308
Here we're saying that for all values of x, if x is a person, then this is true.

2408
03:37:43,022 --> 03:37:48,580
So in other words, I'm saying for all people, and we call that person x, this statement is going to be true.

2409
03:37:48,974 --> 03:37:50,404
What statement is true of all people?

2410
03:37:50,766 --> 03:37:53,284
Well, there exists a y that is a house.

2411
03:37:53,518 --> 03:37:54,756
So there exists some house,

2412
03:37:55,712 --> 03:37:57,860
and x belongs to y.

2413
03:37:58,670 --> 03:38:06,532
In other words, I'm saying that for all people out there, there exists some house such that x, the person, belongs to y, the house.

2414
03:38:07,534 --> 03:38:11,204
So phrased more succinctly, I'm saying that every person belongs to a house.

2415
03:38:11,534 --> 03:38:16,580
That for all X, if X is a person, then there exists a house that X belongs to.

2416
03:38:17,134 --> 03:38:21,668
And so we can now express a lot more powerful ideas using this idea now, of first-order logic.

2417
03:38:21,902 --> 03:38:30,528
And it turns out there are many other kinds of logic out there. There's second-order logic, and other higher order logic, each of which allows us to express more and more complex ideas.

2418
03:38:30,702 --> 03:38:35,908
But all of it, in this case, is really in pursuit of the same goal, which is the representation of knowledge.

2419
03:38:36,288 --> 03:38:45,168
We want our AI agents to be able to know information, to represent that information, whether that's using propositional logic or first-order logic or some other logic,

2420
03:38:45,422 --> 03:38:52,484
and then be able to reason based on that, to be able to draw conclusions, make inferences, figure out whether there's some sort of entailment relationship,

2421
03:38:52,768 --> 03:38:58,528
as by using some sort of inference algorithm, something like inference by resolution or model checking,

2422
03:38:58,608 --> 03:39:05,796
or any number of these other algorithms that we can use in order to take information that we know and translate it to additional conclusions.

2423
03:39:06,094 --> 03:39:12,836
So all of this has helped us to create AI that is able to represent information about what it knows and what it doesn't know.

2424
03:39:13,582 --> 03:39:21,488
Next time though, we'll take a look at how we can make our AI even more powerful by not just encoding information that we know for sure to be true and not to be true,

2425
03:39:21,648 --> 03:39:31,268
but also to take a look at uncertainty, to look at what happens if AI thinks that something might be probable or maybe not very probable or somewhere in between those two extremes,

2426
03:39:31,488 --> 03:39:36,516
all in the pursuit of trying to build our intelligent systems to be even more intelligent.

2427
03:39:36,846 --> 03:39:37,572
We'll see you next time.

2428
03:40:29,262 --> 03:40:35,648
But in reality, when we think about computers and we think about AI, very rarely are our machines going to be able to know things for sure.

2429
03:40:35,888 --> 03:40:43,888
Oftentimes there's going to be some amount of uncertainty in the information that our AIs or our computers are dealing with, where it might believe something with some probability,

2430
03:40:44,078 --> 03:40:48,608
as we'll soon discuss what probability is all about and what it means, but not entirely for certain.

2431
03:40:48,814 --> 03:40:56,804
And we want to use the information that it has some knowledge about, even if it doesn't have perfect knowledge, to still be able to make inferences, still be able to draw conclusions.

2432
03:40:57,198 --> 03:41:06,020
So you might imagine, for example, in the context of a robot that has some sensors and is exploring some environment, it might not know exactly where it is or exactly what's around it,

2433
03:41:06,222 --> 03:41:10,688
but it does have access to some data that can allow it to draw inferences with some probability.

2434
03:41:10,848 --> 03:41:13,408
There's some likelihood that one thing is true or another.

2435
03:41:13,646 --> 03:41:18,688
Or you can imagine in contexts where there is a little bit more randomness and uncertainty, something like predicting the weather,

2436
03:41:18,848 --> 03:41:27,808
where you might not be able to know for sure what tomorrow's weather is with 100% certainty, but you can probably infer with some probability what tomorrow's weather is going to be

2437
03:41:28,014 --> 03:41:33,248
based on maybe today's weather and yesterday's weather and other data that you might have access to as well.

2438
03:41:33,550 --> 03:41:39,568
And so oftentimes we can distill this in terms of just possible events that might happen and what the likelihood of those events are.

2439
03:41:39,728 --> 03:41:43,908
This comes a lot in games, for example, where there's an element of chance inside of those games.

2440
03:41:44,270 --> 03:41:52,688
So you imagine rolling a dice, you're not sure exactly what the die roll is going to be, but you know it's going to be one of these possibilities from one to six, for example.

2441
03:41:53,454 --> 03:41:56,448
And so here now we introduce the idea of probability theory.

2442
03:41:56,686 --> 03:42:05,124
And what we'll take a look at today is beginning by looking at the mathematical foundations of probability theory, getting an understanding for some of the key concepts within probability,

2443
03:42:05,326 --> 03:42:13,892
and then diving into how we can use probability and the ideas that we look at mathematically to represent some ideas in terms of models that we can put into our computers

2444
03:42:14,126 --> 03:42:24,132
in order to program an AI that is able to use information about probability to draw inferences, to make some judgments about the world with some probability or likelihood of being true.

2445
03:42:24,942 --> 03:42:32,528
So probability ultimately boils down to this idea that there are possible worlds that we're here representing using this little Greek letter omega.

2446
03:42:32,848 --> 03:42:38,768
And the idea of a possible world is that, when I roll a die, there are six possible worlds that could result from it.

2447
03:42:38,862 --> 03:42:44,612
I could roll a one or a two or a three or a four or a five or a six, and each of those are a possible world.

2448
03:42:44,974 --> 03:42:52,900
And each of those possible worlds has some probability of being true, the probability that I do roll a one or a two or a three or something else.

2449
03:42:53,358 --> 03:43:00,228
And we represent that probability like this, using the capital letter P, and then in parentheses, what it is that we want the probability of.

2450
03:43:00,558 --> 03:43:06,308
So this right here would be the probability of some possible world as represented by the little letter omega.

2451
03:43:06,990 --> 03:43:13,828
Now there are a couple of basic axioms of probability that become relevant as we consider how we deal with probability and how we think about it.

2452
03:43:14,222 --> 03:43:20,128
First and foremost, every probability value must range between zero and one inclusive.

2453
03:43:20,208 --> 03:43:25,540
So the smallest value any probability can have is the number zero, which is an impossible event.

2454
03:43:25,838 --> 03:43:35,556
Something like I roll a die and the die is a seven is the roll that I get. If the die only has numbers one through six, the event that I roll a seven is impossible, so it would have probability zero.

2455
03:43:36,206 --> 03:43:42,528
And on the other end of the spectrum, probability can range all the way up to the positive number one, meaning an event is certain to happen.

2456
03:43:42,798 --> 03:43:45,968
That I roll a die and the number is less than ten, for example.

2457
03:43:46,158 --> 03:43:51,332
That is an event that is guaranteed to happen if the only sides on my die are one through six, for instance.

2458
03:43:51,726 --> 03:43:54,916
And then there can range through any real number in between these two values.

2459
03:43:55,182 --> 03:44:04,644
Where generally speaking, a higher value for the probability means an event is more likely to take place, and a lower value for the probability means the event is less likely to take place.

2460
03:44:05,646 --> 03:44:08,388
And the other key rule for probability looks a little bit like this.

2461
03:44:08,928 --> 03:44:16,128
This sigma notation, if you haven't seen it before, refers to summation, the idea that we're going to be adding up a whole sequence of values.

2462
03:44:16,366 --> 03:44:20,608
And this sigma notation is going to come up a couple of times today, because as we deal with probability,

2463
03:44:20,782 --> 03:44:25,968
oftentimes we're adding up a whole bunch of individual values or individual probabilities to get some other value.

2464
03:44:26,254 --> 03:44:27,812
So we'll see this come up a couple of times.

2465
03:44:28,174 --> 03:44:37,508
But what this notation means is that if I sum up all of the possible worlds omega that are in big omega, which represents the set of all the possible worlds.

2466
03:44:38,254 --> 03:44:44,004
Meaning I take for all of the worlds in the set of possible worlds and add up all of their probabilities,

2467
03:44:44,686 --> 03:44:46,528
what I ultimately get is the number one.

2468
03:44:47,022 --> 03:44:54,848
So if I take all the possible worlds, add up what each of their probabilities is, I should get the number one at the end, meaning all probabilities just need to sum to one.

2469
03:44:55,214 --> 03:45:01,168
So for example, if I take dice, for example, if you imagine I have a fair die with numbers one through six and I roll the die,

2470
03:45:01,488 --> 03:45:07,556
each one of these rolls has an equal probability of taking place, and the probability is one over six, for example.

2471
03:45:07,854 --> 03:45:13,316
So each of these probabilities is between zero and one, zero meaning impossible and one meaning for certain.

2472
03:45:13,550 --> 03:45:18,212
And if you add up all of these probabilities for all of the possible worlds, you get the number one.

2473
03:45:18,926 --> 03:45:21,764
And we can represent any one of those probabilities like this.

2474
03:45:22,158 --> 03:45:26,980
The probability that we roll the number two, for example, is just one over six.

2475
03:45:27,374 --> 03:45:33,008
Every six times we roll the die, we'd expect that one time, for instance, the die might come up as a two.

2476
03:45:33,248 --> 03:45:37,028
Its probability is not certain, but it's a little more than nothing, for instance.

2477
03:45:38,062 --> 03:45:40,580
And so this is all fairly straightforward for just a single die.

2478
03:45:40,878 --> 03:45:44,292
But things get more interesting as our models of the world get a little bit more complex.

2479
03:45:44,814 --> 03:45:49,408
Let's imagine now that we're not just dealing with a single die, but we have two dice, for example.

2480
03:45:49,646 --> 03:45:56,768
I have a red die here and a blue die there, and I care not just about what the individual roll is, but I care about the sum of the two rolls.

2481
03:45:56,928 --> 03:45:59,652
In this case, the sum of the two rolls is the number three.

2482
03:46:00,270 --> 03:46:06,788
How do I begin to now reason about what does the probability look like if instead of having one die, I now have two dice?

2483
03:46:07,534 --> 03:46:12,036
Well, what we might imagine is that we could first consider what are all of the possible worlds.

2484
03:46:12,462 --> 03:46:18,016
And in this case, all of the possible worlds are just every combination of the red and blue die that I could come up with.

2485
03:46:18,176 --> 03:46:22,244
For the red die, it could be a one or a two or a three or a four or a five or a six.

2486
03:46:22,606 --> 03:46:29,348
And for each of those possibilities, the blue die likewise could also be either one or two or three or four or five or six.

2487
03:46:30,286 --> 03:46:36,004
And it just so happens that in this particular case, each of these possible combinations is equally likely.

2488
03:46:36,176 --> 03:46:39,204
Equally likely are all of these various different possible worlds.

2489
03:46:39,376 --> 03:46:40,816
That's not always going to be the case.

2490
03:46:40,976 --> 03:46:46,176
As you imagine more complex models that we could try to build and things that we could try to represent in the real world,

2491
03:46:46,382 --> 03:46:50,656
it's probably not going to be the case that every single possible world is always equally likely.

2492
03:46:50,926 --> 03:47:00,548
But in the case of fair dice, where in any given die roll any one number has just as good a chance of coming up as any other number, we can consider all of these possible worlds to be equally likely.

2493
03:47:01,390 --> 03:47:07,056
But even though all of the possible worlds are equally likely, that doesn't necessarily mean that their sums are equally likely.

2494
03:47:07,246 --> 03:47:16,740
So if we consider what the sum is of all of these two. So one plus one, that's a two, two plus one is a three, and consider for each of these possible pairs of numbers what their sum ultimately is,

2495
03:47:17,006 --> 03:47:22,916
we can notice that there are some patterns here where it's not entirely the case that every number comes up equally likely.

2496
03:47:23,182 --> 03:47:28,420
If you consider seven, for example, what's the probability that when I roll two dice their sum is seven?

2497
03:47:28,686 --> 03:47:38,148
There are several ways this can happen. There are six possible worlds where the sum is seven. It could be a one and a six or a two and a five or a three and a four, a four and a three, and so forth.

2498
03:47:38,926 --> 03:47:45,348
But if you instead consider, you know, what's the probability that I roll two dice and the sum of those two die rolls is twelve, for example.

2499
03:47:45,870 --> 03:47:50,256
Well, looking at this diagram, there's only one possible world in which that can happen.

2500
03:47:50,350 --> 03:47:57,668
And that's the possible world where both the red die and the blue die both come up as sixes to give us a sum total of twelve.

2501
03:47:58,414 --> 03:48:02,788
So based on just taking a look at this diagram, we see that some of these probabilities are likely different.

2502
03:48:03,022 --> 03:48:08,036
The probability that the sum is a seven must be greater than the probability that the sum is a twelve.

2503
03:48:08,398 --> 03:48:14,816
And we can represent that even more formally by saying, okay, the probability that we sum to 12 is one out of 36.

2504
03:48:15,278 --> 03:48:23,140
Out of the 36 equally likely possible worlds, six squared, because we have six options for the red die and six options for the blue die,

2505
03:48:23,376 --> 03:48:27,012
out of those 36 options, only one of them sums to 12.

2506
03:48:27,822 --> 03:48:35,204
Whereas on the other hand, the probability that if we take two dice rolls and they sum up to the number seven, well, out of those 36 possible worlds,

2507
03:48:35,376 --> 03:48:42,660
there were six worlds where the sum was seven, and so we get six over 36, which we can simplify as a fraction to just one over six.

2508
03:48:43,630 --> 03:48:52,068
So here now we're able to represent these different ideas of probability, representing some events that might be more likely and then other events that are less likely as well.

2509
03:48:52,654 --> 03:49:01,252
And these sorts of judgments, where we're figuring out just in the abstract, what is the probability that this thing takes place, are generally known as unconditional probabilities.

2510
03:49:01,710 --> 03:49:09,296
Some degree of belief we have in some proposition, some fact about the world, in the absence of any other evidence, without knowing any additional information.

2511
03:49:09,456 --> 03:49:12,016
If I roll a die, what's the chance it comes up as a two?

2512
03:49:12,256 --> 03:49:16,324
Or if I roll two dice, what's the chance that the sum of those two die rolls is a seven?

2513
03:49:17,102 --> 03:49:25,136
But usually when we're thinking about probability, especially when we're thinking about training an AI to intelligently be able to know something about the world and make predictions

2514
03:49:25,136 --> 03:49:31,936
based on that information, it's not unconditional probability that our AI is dealing with, but rather conditional probability.

2515
03:49:32,176 --> 03:49:38,820
Probability where rather than having no original knowledge, we have some initial knowledge about the world and how the world actually works.

2516
03:49:39,342 --> 03:49:46,756
So conditional probability is the degree of belief in a proposition given some evidence that has already been revealed to us.

2517
03:49:47,854 --> 03:49:48,708
So what does this look like?

2518
03:49:49,006 --> 03:49:51,428
Well, it looks like this in terms of notation.

2519
03:49:51,694 --> 03:49:58,596
We're going to represent conditional probability as probability of A and then this vertical bar and then B.

2520
03:49:59,022 --> 03:50:04,580
And the way to read this is the thing on the left-hand side of the vertical bar is what we want the probability of.

2521
03:50:04,974 --> 03:50:11,300
Here now I want the probability that A is true, that it is the real world, that it is the event that actually does take place.

2522
03:50:11,950 --> 03:50:18,052
And then on the right side of the vertical bar is our evidence, the information that we already know for certain about the world.

2523
03:50:18,446 --> 03:50:20,816
For example, that B is true.

2524
03:50:21,230 --> 03:50:26,336
So the way to read this entire expression is what is the probability of A given B?

2525
03:50:26,496 --> 03:50:31,044
The probability that A is true given that we already know that B is true.

2526
03:50:31,438 --> 03:50:36,656
And this type of judgment, conditional probability, the probability of one thing given some other fact.

2527
03:50:37,184 --> 03:50:42,144
Comes up quite a lot when we think about the types of calculations we might want our AI to be able to do.

2528
03:50:42,304 --> 03:50:47,460
For example, we might care about the probability of rain today given that we know that it rained yesterday.

2529
03:50:47,694 --> 03:50:50,864
We could think about the probability of rain today just in the abstract.

2530
03:50:51,024 --> 03:50:52,644
What is the chance that today it rains?

2531
03:50:52,974 --> 03:50:54,820
But usually we have some additional evidence.

2532
03:50:55,104 --> 03:51:03,024
I know for certain that it rained yesterday, and so I would like to calculate the probability that it rains today given that I know that it rained yesterday.

2533
03:51:03,214 --> 03:51:09,824
Or you might imagine that I want to know the probability that my optimal route to my destination changes given the current traffic conditions.

2534
03:51:09,934 --> 03:51:15,984
So whether or not traffic conditions change, that might change the probability that this route is actually the optimal route.

2535
03:51:16,144 --> 03:51:25,476
Or you might imagine in a medical context, I want to know the probability that a patient has a particular disease given some results of some tests that have been performed on that patient.

2536
03:51:25,584 --> 03:51:32,516
And I have some evidence, the results of that test, and I would like to know the probability that a patient has a particular disease.

2537
03:51:33,070 --> 03:51:38,064
So this notion of conditional probability comes up everywhere as we begin to think about what we would like to reason about,

2538
03:51:38,318 --> 03:51:43,584
but being able to reason a little more intelligently by taking into account evidence that we already have.

2539
03:51:43,744 --> 03:51:50,500
We're more able to get an accurate result for what is the likelihood that someone has this disease if we know this evidence, the results of the test,

2540
03:51:50,926 --> 03:52:00,384
as opposed to if we were just calculating the unconditional probability of saying what is the probability they have the disease without any evidence to try and back up our result

2541
03:52:00,718 --> 03:52:02,020
one way or the other.

2542
03:52:03,406 --> 03:52:10,064
So now that we've got this idea of what conditional probability is, the next question we have to ask is, all right, how do we calculate conditional probability?

2543
03:52:10,224 --> 03:52:15,172
How do we figure out mathematically, if I have an expression like this, how do I get a number from that?

2544
03:52:15,264 --> 03:52:17,264
What does conditional probability actually mean?

2545
03:52:17,582 --> 03:52:20,708
Well, the formula for conditional probability looks a little something like this.

2546
03:52:21,134 --> 03:52:28,996
The probability of A given B, the probability that A is true, given that we know that B is true, is equal to this fraction,

2547
03:52:29,358 --> 03:52:35,268
the probability that A and B are true, divided by just the probability that B is true.

2548
03:52:35,566 --> 03:52:45,220
And the way to intuitively try to think about this is if I want to know the probability that A is true, given that B is true, well, I want to consider all the ways they could both be true

2549
03:52:45,710 --> 03:52:55,504
out of the only worlds that I care about are the worlds where B is already true. I can sort of ignore all the cases where B isn't true, because those aren't relevant to my ultimate computation.

2550
03:52:55,598 --> 03:52:58,980
They're not relevant to what it is that I want to get information about.

2551
03:52:59,918 --> 03:53:06,404
So let's take a look at an example. Let's go back to that example of rolling two dice and the idea that those two dice might sum up to the number 12.

2552
03:53:06,862 --> 03:53:12,944
We discussed earlier that the unconditional probability that if I roll two dice and they sum to 12 is one out of 36.

2553
03:53:13,184 --> 03:53:19,236
Because out of the 36 possible worlds that I might care about, in only one of them is the sum of those two dice 12.

2554
03:53:19,344 --> 03:53:22,084
It's only when red is six and blue is also six.

2555
03:53:22,862 --> 03:53:25,092
But let's say now that I have some additional information.

2556
03:53:25,358 --> 03:53:32,864
I now want to know what is the probability that the two dice sum to twelve, given that I know that the red die was a

2557
03:53:32,864 --> 03:53:35,184
six. So I already have some evidence.

2558
03:53:35,344 --> 03:53:40,868
I already know the red die is a six. I don't know what the blue die is. That information isn't given to me in this expression.

2559
03:53:41,230 --> 03:53:46,212
But given the fact that I know that the red die rolled a six, what is the probability that we sum to twelve?

2560
03:53:47,054 --> 03:53:49,744
And so we can begin to do the math using that expression from before.

2561
03:53:50,030 --> 03:53:57,892
Here again are all of the possibilities, all of the possible combinations of red die being one through six and blue die being one through six.

2562
03:53:58,830 --> 03:54:06,724
And I might consider first, all right, what is the probability of my evidence, my b variable, where I want to know what is the probability that the red die is a six.

2563
03:54:07,342 --> 03:54:10,820
Well, the probability that the red die is a six is just one out of six.

2564
03:54:11,246 --> 03:54:16,032
So these one out of six options are really the only worlds that I care about here now.

2565
03:54:16,192 --> 03:54:25,476
All the rest of them are irrelevant to my calculation because I already have this evidence that the red die was a six, so I don't need to care about all of the other possibilities that could result.

2566
03:54:26,286 --> 03:54:35,332
So now in addition to the fact that the red dye rolled as a six and the probability of that, the other piece of information I need to know in order to calculate this conditional probability

2567
03:54:35,632 --> 03:54:44,068
is the probability that both of my variables A and B are true. The probability that both the red die is a six and they all sum to 12.

2568
03:54:44,398 --> 03:54:46,788
So what is the probability that both of these things happen?

2569
03:54:46,992 --> 03:54:55,232
Well, it only happens in one possible case, in one out of these 36 cases, and it's the case where both the red and the blue die are equal to six.

2570
03:54:55,534 --> 03:54:57,508
This is an informate piece of information that we already knew.

2571
03:54:57,742 --> 03:55:01,024
And so this probability is equal to one over 36.

2572
03:55:01,806 --> 03:55:10,832
And so to get the conditional probability that the sum is twelve, given that I know that the red dice is equal to six, well I just divide these two values together, and one

2573
03:55:10,992 --> 03:55:16,132
over 36 divided by one over six gives us this probability of one over six.

2574
03:55:16,558 --> 03:55:24,868
Given that I know that the red die rolled a value of six, the probability that the sum of the two dice is twelve is also one over six.

2575
03:55:25,326 --> 03:55:32,612
And that probably makes intuitive sense to you too, because if the red die is a six, the only way for me to get to a twelve is if the blue die also rolls a six,

2576
03:55:32,942 --> 03:55:36,992
and we know that the probability of the blue die rolling a six is one over six.

2577
03:55:37,072 --> 03:55:47,632
So in this case, the conditional probability seems fairly straightforward, but this idea of calculating a conditional probability by looking at the probability that both of these events take place

2578
03:55:47,854 --> 03:55:49,792
is an idea that's going to come up again and again.

2579
03:55:49,952 --> 03:55:58,912
This is the definition now of conditional probability, and we're going to use that definition as we think about probability more generally to be able to draw conclusions about the world.

2580
03:55:59,152 --> 03:56:00,592
This again is that formula.

2581
03:56:00,752 --> 03:56:08,512
The probability of A given B is equal to the probability that a and b take place divided by the probability of B.

2582
03:56:08,814 --> 03:56:11,552
And you'll see this formula sometimes written in a couple of different ways.

2583
03:56:11,758 --> 03:56:19,908
You could imagine algebraically multiplying both sides of this equation by probability of b to get rid of the fraction, and you'll get an expression like this.

2584
03:56:20,272 --> 03:56:28,352
The probability of a and b, which is this expression over here, is just the probability of b times the probability of a given b.

2585
03:56:28,512 --> 03:56:35,232
Or you could represent this equivalently, since a and b in this expression are interchangeable. A and b is the same thing as b and a.

2586
03:56:35,470 --> 03:56:44,836
You could imagine also representing the probability of a and b as the probability of a times the probability of b given a, just switching all of the A's and Bs.

2587
03:56:45,152 --> 03:56:49,632
These three are all equivalent ways of trying to represent what joint probability means.

2588
03:56:49,742 --> 03:56:59,108
And so you'll sometimes see all of these equations, and they might be useful to you as you begin to reason about probability and to think about what values might be taking place in the real world.

2589
03:57:00,046 --> 03:57:06,432
Now, sometimes when we deal with probability, we don't just care about a Boolean event, like did this happen or did this not happen.

2590
03:57:06,766 --> 03:57:15,556
Sometimes we might want the ability to represent variable values in a probability space where some variable might take on multiple different possible values.

2591
03:57:16,046 --> 03:57:20,740
And in probability, we call a variable in probability theory a random variable.

2592
03:57:20,974 --> 03:57:28,228
A random variable in probability is just some variable in probability theory that has some domain of values that it can take on.

2593
03:57:28,814 --> 03:57:29,632
So what do I mean by this?

2594
03:57:30,032 --> 03:57:35,844
what I mean is I might have a random variable that is just called roll, for example, that has six possible values.

2595
03:57:36,112 --> 03:57:43,072
Roll is my variable, and the possible values, the domain of values that it can take on, are one, two, three, four, five, and six.

2596
03:57:43,152 --> 03:57:45,232
And I might like to know the probability of each.

2597
03:57:45,486 --> 03:57:47,232
In this case, they happen to all be the same.

2598
03:57:47,374 --> 03:57:50,276
But in other random variables, that might not be the case.

2599
03:57:50,574 --> 03:57:59,492
For example, I might have a random variable to represent the weather, for example, where the domain of values it could take on are things like sun or cloudy or rainy or windy or snowy,

2600
03:57:59,632 --> 03:58:07,876
and each of those might have a different probability. And I care about knowing what is the probability that the weather equals sun or that the weather equals clouds, for instance.

2601
03:58:08,526 --> 03:58:12,260
And I might like to do some mathematical calculations based on that information.

2602
03:58:12,686 --> 03:58:15,072
Other random variables might be something like traffic.

2603
03:58:15,278 --> 03:58:18,592
What are the odds that there is no traffic or light traffic or heavy traffic?

2604
03:58:18,832 --> 03:58:26,500
Traffic in this case is my random variable, and the values that that random variable can take on are here. It's either none or light or heavy.

2605
03:58:26,702 --> 03:58:36,192
And I, the person doing these calculations, I, the person encoding these random variables into my computer, need to make the decision as to what these possible values actually are.

2606
03:58:36,558 --> 03:58:43,712
You might imagine, for example, for a flight, if I care about whether or not I make it into a flight on time, my flight has a couple of possible values that it could take on.

2607
03:58:43,792 --> 03:58:47,632
My flight could be on time, my flight could be delayed, my flight could be canceled.

2608
03:58:47,822 --> 03:58:52,432
So flight in this case is my random variable, and these are the values.

2609
03:58:52,992 --> 03:59:00,420
That it can take on, and often I'll want to know something about the probability that my random variable takes on each of those possible values.

2610
03:59:00,846 --> 03:59:04,004
And this is what we then call a probability distribution.

2611
03:59:04,302 --> 03:59:11,172
A probability distribution takes a random variable and gives me the probability for each of the possible values in its domain.

2612
03:59:11,854 --> 03:59:16,548
So in the case of this flight, for example, my probability distribution might look something like this.

2613
03:59:16,910 --> 03:59:25,284
My probability distribution says the probability that the random variable flight is equal to the value on time is 0.6.

2614
03:59:25,806 --> 03:59:31,684
Or otherwise put into more English human-friendly terms, the likelihood that my flight is on time is 60%, for example.

2615
03:59:32,014 --> 03:59:39,460
And in this case, the probability that my flight is delayed is 30%, the probability that my flight is canceled is 10% or 0.1.

2616
03:59:39,712 --> 03:59:43,552
And if you sum up all of these possible values, the sum is going to be 1.

2617
03:59:43,872 --> 03:59:52,912
If you take all of the possible worlds, here are my three possible worlds for the value of the random variable flight, add them all up together, the result needs to be the number one

2618
03:59:53,230 --> 03:59:56,324
per that axiom of probability theory that we've discussed before.

2619
03:59:57,070 --> 04:00:03,044
So this now is one way of representing this probability distribution for the random variable flight.

2620
04:00:03,566 --> 04:00:10,432
Sometimes you'll see it represented a little bit more concisely, that this is pretty verbose for really just trying to express three possible values.

2621
04:00:10,670 --> 04:00:14,912
And so often you'll instead see the same notation representing using a vector.

2622
04:00:15,054 --> 04:00:19,392
And all a vector is, is a sequence of values, as opposed to just a single value.

2623
04:00:19,598 --> 04:00:20,912
I might have multiple values.

2624
04:00:21,102 --> 04:00:24,708
And so I could extend instead represent this idea this way.

2625
04:00:25,294 --> 04:00:35,236
Bold P, so a larger p, generally meaning the probability distribution of this variable flight is equal to this vector represented in angle brackets.

2626
04:00:35,566 --> 04:00:44,192
The probability distribution is 0.6, 0.3, and 0.1, and I would just have to know that this probability distribution is in order of on

2627
04:00:44,352 --> 04:00:52,228
time or delayed and canceled, to know how to interpret this vector. To mean the first value in the vector is the probability that my flight is on time.

2628
04:00:52,462 --> 04:00:59,780
The second value in the vector is the probability that my flight is delayed, and the third value in the vector is the probability that my flight is canceled.

2629
04:01:00,398 --> 04:01:10,112
And so this is just an alternate way of representing this idea a little more verbosely, but oftentimes you'll see us just talk about a probability distribution over a random variable.

2630
04:01:10,350 --> 04:01:17,700
And whenever we talk about that, what we're really doing is trying to figure out the probabilities of each of the possible values that that random variable can take on,

2631
04:01:17,872 --> 04:01:24,272
but this notation is just a little bit more succinct, even though it can sometimes be a little confusing depending on the context in which you see it.

2632
04:01:24,398 --> 04:01:32,068
So we'll start to look at examples where we use this sort of notation to describe probability and to describe events that might take place.

2633
04:01:33,422 --> 04:01:36,836
A couple of other important ideas to know with regards to probability theory.

2634
04:01:37,072 --> 04:01:39,076
One is this idea of independence.

2635
04:01:39,470 --> 04:01:46,052
And independence refers to the idea that the knowledge of one event doesn't influence the probability of another event.

2636
04:01:46,414 --> 04:01:54,672
So, for example, in the context of my two dice rolls, where I had the red die and the blue die, the probability that I roll the red die and the blue die, uh, those two events,

2637
04:01:54,832 --> 04:01:56,992
red die and blue die, are independent.

2638
04:01:57,232 --> 04:02:07,044
Knowing the result of the red die doesn't change the probabilities for the blue die. It doesn't give me any additional information about what the value of the blue die is ultimately going to be.

2639
04:02:07,374 --> 04:02:15,072
But that's not always going to be the case. You might imagine that in the case of weather, something like clouds and rain, those are probably not independent.

2640
04:02:15,232 --> 04:02:24,388
That if it is cloudy, that might increase the probability that later in the day it's going to rain. So some information informs some other event or some other random variable.

2641
04:02:24,686 --> 04:02:33,316
So independence refers to the idea that one event doesn't influence the other. And if they're not independent, then there might be some relationship.

2642
04:02:34,254 --> 04:02:36,996
So mathematically, formally, what does independence actually mean?

2643
04:02:37,390 --> 04:02:46,032
Well, recall this formula from before: that the probability of A and B is the probability of A times the probability of B given A.

2644
04:02:46,222 --> 04:02:53,956
And the more intuitive way to think about this is that to know how likely it is that A and B happen, well, let's first figure out the likelihood that A happens.

2645
04:02:54,446 --> 04:03:00,900
And then given that we know that A happens, let's figure out the likelihood that B happens and multiply those two things together.

2646
04:03:01,582 --> 04:03:09,060
But if A and B were independent, Meaning knowing A doesn't change anything about the likelihood that B is true.

2647
04:03:09,422 --> 04:03:16,164
Well, then the probability of B given A, meaning the probability that B is true, given that I know A is true,

2648
04:03:16,558 --> 04:03:20,320
well, that I know A is true shouldn't really make a difference if these two things are independent.

2649
04:03:20,480 --> 04:03:22,640
That A shouldn't influence B at all.

2650
04:03:22,894 --> 04:03:30,436
So the probability of B given A is really just the probability of B if it is true that A and B are independent.

2651
04:03:30,766 --> 04:03:36,320
And so this right here is one example of a definition for what it means for A and B to be independent.

2652
04:03:36,462 --> 04:03:41,604
That the probability of A and B is just the probability of A times the probability of B.

2653
04:03:42,094 --> 04:03:48,708
Anytime you find two events A and B where this relationship holds, then you can say that A and B are independent.

2654
04:03:49,486 --> 04:03:53,200
So an example of that might be the dice that we were taking a look at before.

2655
04:03:53,614 --> 04:04:03,300
Here, if I wanted the probability of red being a six and blue being a six, well that's just the probability that red is a six multiplied by the probability that blue is a six.

2656
04:04:03,598 --> 04:04:05,644
It's both equal to one over 36.

2657
04:04:05,840 --> 04:04:09,380
So I can say that these two events are independent.

2658
04:04:10,286 --> 04:04:17,284
What wouldn't be independent, for example, would be an example, so this, for example, has a probability of one over 36, as we talked about before.

2659
04:04:17,614 --> 04:04:25,508
But what wouldn't be independent would be a case like this: the probability that the red die rolls a six and the red die rolls a four.

2660
04:04:26,318 --> 04:04:33,200
If you just naively took, okay, red die six, red die four, well, if I'm only rolling the die once, you might imagine the naive approach is to say,

2661
04:04:33,422 --> 04:04:38,884
well, each of these has a probability of one over six, so multiply them together, and the probability is one over 36.

2662
04:04:39,470 --> 04:04:47,716
But of course, if you're only rolling the red die once, there's no way you could get two different values for the red die. It couldn't both be a six and a four.

2663
04:04:48,014 --> 04:04:56,904
So the probability should be zero. But if you were to multiply probability of red six times probability of red four, well that would equal one over 36.

2664
04:04:57,422 --> 04:05:05,988
but of course that's not true, because we know that there is no way, probability zero, that when we roll the red die once, we get both a six and a four,

2665
04:05:06,160 --> 04:05:09,828
because only one of those possibilities can actually be the result.

2666
04:05:10,606 --> 04:05:18,212
And so we can say that the event that red roll is six and the event that red roll is four, those two events are not independent.

2667
04:05:18,414 --> 04:05:23,760
If I know that the red roll is a six, I know that the red roll cannot possibly be a four.

2668
04:05:23,982 --> 04:05:25,760
So these things are not independent.

2669
04:05:25,840 --> 04:05:35,684
And instead, if I wanted to calculate the probability, I would need to use this conditional probability as is the regular definition of the probability of two events taking place.

2670
04:05:36,142 --> 04:05:40,932
And the probability of this now, well, the probability of the red roll being a six, that's one over six.

2671
04:05:41,326 --> 04:05:45,252
But what's the probability that the roll is a four given that the roll is a six?

2672
04:05:45,966 --> 04:05:53,156
Well, this is just zero, because there's no way for the red roll to be a four, given that we already know the red roll is a six.

2673
04:05:53,518 --> 04:05:58,084
And so the value, if we do add all that multiplication, is we get the number zero.

2674
04:05:59,342 --> 04:06:06,800
So this idea of conditional probability is going to come up again and again, especially as we begin to reason about multiple different random variables that might be

2675
04:06:06,800 --> 04:06:08,480
interacting with each other in some way.

2676
04:06:08,718 --> 04:06:13,988
And this gets us to one of the most important rules in probability theory, which is known as Bayes' rule.

2677
04:06:14,350 --> 04:06:22,320
And it turns out that just using the information we've already learned about probability and just applying a little bit of algebra, we can actually derive Bayes'

2678
04:06:22,480 --> 04:06:23,360
rule for ourselves.

2679
04:06:23,470 --> 04:06:29,920
But it's a very important rule when it comes to inference and thinking about probability in the context of what it is that a computer can do

2680
04:06:30,190 --> 04:06:34,372
or what a mathematician could do by having access to information about probability.

2681
04:06:34,926 --> 04:06:38,980
So let's go back to these equations to be able to derive Bayes' rule ourselves.

2682
04:06:39,374 --> 04:06:48,900
We know the probability of A and B, the likelihood that A and B take place, is the likelihood of B and then the likelihood of A given that we know that B is already true.

2683
04:06:49,614 --> 04:06:58,080
And likewise, the probability of A and B is the probability of A times the probability of B given that we know that A is already true.

2684
04:06:58,240 --> 04:07:03,716
This is sort of a symmetric relationship where it doesn't matter the order of A and B and B and A mean the same thing.

2685
04:07:03,840 --> 04:07:09,440
And so in these equations, we can just swap out A and B to be able to represent the exact same idea.

2686
04:07:09,742 --> 04:07:11,920
So we know that these two equations are already true.

2687
04:07:12,142 --> 04:07:12,960
We've seen that already.

2688
04:07:13,390 --> 04:07:16,912
And now let's just do a little bit of algebraic manipulation of this stuff.

2689
04:07:17,072 --> 04:07:22,596
Both of these expressions on the right hand side are equal to the probability of A and B.

2690
04:07:22,990 --> 04:07:28,196
So what I can do is take these two expressions on the right hand side and just set them equal to each other.

2691
04:07:28,752 --> 04:07:34,148
If they're both equal to the probability of A and B, then they both must be equal to each other.

2692
04:07:34,638 --> 04:07:42,592
So probability of A times probability of B given A is equal to the probability of B times the probability of A

2693
04:07:42,926 --> 04:07:43,556
given B.

2694
04:07:44,302 --> 04:07:46,980
And now all we're going to do is do a little bit of division.

2695
04:07:47,406 --> 04:07:53,444
I'm going to divide both sides by P of A, and now I get what is Bayes' rule?

2696
04:07:53,632 --> 04:08:02,992
The probability of B given A is equal to the probability of B times the probability of A given B divided by the probability of A.

2697
04:08:03,182 --> 04:08:06,272
And sometimes in Bayes rule you'll see the order of these two arguments switched.

2698
04:08:06,352 --> 04:08:10,752
So instead of B times A given B, it'll be A given B times B.

2699
04:08:10,990 --> 04:08:16,740
That ultimately doesn't matter, because in multiplication, you can switch the order of the two things you're multiplying, and it doesn't change the result.

2700
04:08:17,072 --> 04:08:21,072
But this here, right now, is the most common formulation of Bayes' rule.

2701
04:08:21,262 --> 04:08:30,500
The probability of B given A is equal to the probability of A given B times the probability of B divided by the probability of A.

2702
04:08:31,182 --> 04:08:39,396
And this rule, it turns out, is really important when it comes to trying to infer things about the world because it means you can express one conditional probability.

2703
04:08:39,726 --> 04:08:42,340
The conditional probability of B given A

2704
04:08:43,086 --> 04:08:49,392
using knowledge about the probability of A given B, using the reverse of that conditional probability.

2705
04:08:49,710 --> 04:08:56,388
So let's first do a little bit of an example with this just to see how we might use it, and then explore what this means a little bit more generally.

2706
04:08:56,750 --> 04:08:59,332
So we're going to construct a situation where I have some information.

2707
04:08:59,630 --> 04:09:01,572
Uh there are two events that I care about.

2708
04:09:01,872 --> 04:09:10,112
The idea that it's cloudy in the morning, and the idea that it is rainy in the afternoon. Those are two different possible events that could take place.

2709
04:09:10,272 --> 04:09:13,232
Cloudy in the morning or the AM, rainy in the P.M.

2710
04:09:13,678 --> 04:09:19,712
And what I care about is given clouds in the morning, what is the probability of rain in the afternoon?

2711
04:09:19,872 --> 04:09:21,152
A reasonable question I might ask.

2712
04:09:21,312 --> 04:09:27,108
In the morning, I look outside or an AI's camera looks outside and sees that there are clouds in the morning,

2713
04:09:27,470 --> 04:09:33,152
and we want to conclude, we want to figure out what is the probability that in the afternoon there is going to be

2714
04:09:33,152 --> 04:09:40,192
rain. Of course, in the abstract, we don't have access to this kind of information, but we can use data to begin to try and figure this out.

2715
04:09:40,398 --> 04:09:44,292
So let's imagine now that I have access to some pieces of information.

2716
04:09:44,592 --> 04:09:50,276
I have access to the idea that 80% of rainy afternoons start out with a cloudy morning.

2717
04:09:50,766 --> 04:09:58,084
And you might imagine that I could have gathered this data just by looking at data over a sequence of time, that I know that 80% of the time, when it's raining in the afternoon,

2718
04:09:58,350 --> 04:09:59,908
it was cloudy that morning.

2719
04:10:01,326 --> 04:10:03,620
I also know that 40% of days have cloudy mornings.

2720
04:10:04,718 --> 04:10:07,780
And I also know that 10% of days have rainy afternoons.

2721
04:10:08,686 --> 04:10:11,460
And now using this information, I would like to figure out,

2722
04:10:12,302 --> 04:10:16,672
given clouds in the morning, what is the probability that it rains in the afternoon?

2723
04:10:16,832 --> 04:10:25,392
I want to know the probability of afternoon rain given morning clouds, and I can do that in particular using this fact, the probability of

2724
04:10:26,032 --> 04:10:34,020
so if I know that 80% of rainy afternoons start with cloudy mornings, then I know the probability of cloudy mornings given rainy afternoons.

2725
04:10:34,062 --> 04:10:37,540
So using sort of the reverse conditional probability, I can figure that out.

2726
04:10:38,030 --> 04:10:40,868
Expressed in terms of Bayes' rule, this is what that would look like.

2727
04:10:41,232 --> 04:10:49,872
Probability of rain given clouds is the probability of clouds given rain times the probability of rain divided by the probability of clouds.

2728
04:10:50,032 --> 04:10:54,992
Here I'm just substituting in for the values of A and B from that equation of Bayes' rule from before.

2729
04:10:55,214 --> 04:11:02,980
And then I can just do the math. I have this information. I know that 80% of the time, if it was raining, then there were clouds in the morning. So 0.8 here.

2730
04:11:03,278 --> 04:11:08,272
The probability of rain is 0.1, because 10% of days were rainy and 40% of days were cloudy.

2731
04:11:08,430 --> 04:11:11,232
I do the math and I can figure out the answer is 0.2.

2732
04:11:11,566 --> 04:11:18,756
So the probability that it rains in the afternoon, given that it was cloudy in the morning, is 0.2 in this case.

2733
04:11:19,662 --> 04:11:27,268
And this now is an application of Bayes' rule, the idea that using one conditional probability, we can get the reverse conditional probability.

2734
04:11:27,662 --> 04:11:36,192
And this is often useful when one of the conditional probabilities might be easier for us to know about or easier for us to have data about, and using that information,

2735
04:11:36,398 --> 04:11:39,140
we can calculate the other conditional probability.

2736
04:11:39,406 --> 04:11:40,272
So what does this look like?

2737
04:11:40,526 --> 04:11:49,764
Well, it means that knowing the probability of cloudy mornings given rainy afternoons, we can calculate the probability of rainy afternoons given cloudy mornings.

2738
04:11:50,112 --> 04:11:58,020
Or for example, more generally, if we know the probability of some visible effect, some effect that we can see and observe

2739
04:11:58,542 --> 04:12:01,380
given some unknown cause that we're not sure about.

2740
04:12:01,966 --> 04:12:07,748
Well, then we can calculate the probability of that unknown cause given the visible effect.

2741
04:12:08,430 --> 04:12:09,380
So what might that look like?

2742
04:12:09,998 --> 04:12:16,928
Well, in the context of medicine, for example, I might know the probability of some medical test result given a disease.

2743
04:12:17,088 --> 04:12:23,524
Like I know that if someone has a disease, then X percent of the time, the medical test result will show up as this, for instance.

2744
04:12:23,918 --> 04:12:32,740
And using that information, then I can calculate, all right, what is the probability that given I know the medical test result, what is the likelihood that someone has the disease?

2745
04:12:33,102 --> 04:12:38,468
This is the piece of information that is usually easier to know, easier to immediately have access to data for.

2746
04:12:38,734 --> 04:12:41,956
And this is the information that I actually want to calculate.

2747
04:12:42,286 --> 04:12:45,088
Or I might want to know, for example, if I know that

2748
04:12:45,888 --> 04:12:53,328
some probability of counterfeit bills have blurry text around the edges, because counterfeit printers aren't nearly as good at printing text precisely.

2749
04:12:53,582 --> 04:13:00,836
So I have some information about given that something is a counterfeit bill, like X percent of counterfeit bills have blurry text, for example.

2750
04:13:01,102 --> 04:13:05,604
And using that information, then I can calculate some piece of information that I might want to know.

2751
04:13:05,888 --> 04:13:11,972
Like, given that I know there's blurry text on a bill, what is the probability that that bill is counterfeit?

2752
04:13:12,238 --> 04:13:18,116
So given one conditional probability, I can calculate the other conditional probability as well.

2753
04:13:19,310 --> 04:13:22,448
And so now we've taken a look at a couple of different types of probability.

2754
04:13:22,608 --> 04:13:30,596
We've looked at unconditional probability, where I just look at what is the probability of this event occurring, given no additional evidence that I might have access to.

2755
04:13:30,990 --> 04:13:40,036
And we've also looked at conditional probability, where I have some sort of evidence, and I would like to, using that evidence, be able to calculate some other probability as well.

2756
04:13:40,782 --> 04:13:44,932
The other kind of probability that will be important for us to think about is joint probability.

2757
04:13:45,230 --> 04:13:50,244
And this is when we're considering the likelihood of multiple different events simultaneously.

2758
04:13:50,766 --> 04:13:51,780
And so what do we mean by this?

2759
04:13:52,078 --> 04:13:56,676
Well, for example, I might have probability distributions that look a little something like this.

2760
04:13:56,846 --> 04:14:02,020
Like, oh, I want to know the probability distribution of clouds in the morning, and that distribution looks like this.

2761
04:14:02,478 --> 04:14:10,020
40% of the time, C, which is my random variable here, is equal to it's cloudy, and 60% of the time it's not cloudy.

2762
04:14:10,606 --> 04:14:16,196
So here is just a simple probability distribution that is effectively telling me that 40% of the time it's cloudy.

2763
04:14:17,230 --> 04:14:27,888
I might also have a probability distribution for rain in the afternoon, where 10% of the time, or with probability 0.1, it is raining in the afternoon, and with probability 0.9,

2764
04:14:28,208 --> 04:14:30,180
it is not raining in the afternoon.

2765
04:14:30,768 --> 04:14:39,140
And using just these two pieces of information, I don't actually have a whole lot of information about how these two variables relate to each other.

2766
04:14:39,534 --> 04:14:47,568
But I could if I had access to their joint probability, meaning for every combination of these two things, meaning morning cloudy and afternoon rain,

2767
04:14:47,822 --> 04:14:57,060
morning cloudy and afternoon not rain, morning not cloudy and afternoon rain, and morning not cloudy and afternoon not raining, if I had access to values for each of those four,

2768
04:14:57,294 --> 04:14:58,528
I'd have more information.

2769
04:14:58,830 --> 04:15:07,488
So information that'd be organized in a table like this, and this, rather than just a probability distribution, is a joint probability distribution.

2770
04:15:07,694 --> 04:15:15,108
It tells me the probability distribution of each of the possible combinations of values that these random variables can take on.

2771
04:15:15,822 --> 04:15:22,368
So if I want to know what is the probability that on any given day it is both cloudy and rainy, well I would say, all right,

2772
04:15:22,448 --> 04:15:30,820
we're looking at cases where it is cloudy and cases where it is raining, and the intersection of those two, that row and that column, is 0.08.

2773
04:15:30,990 --> 04:15:35,716
So that is the probability that it is both cloudy and rainy using that information.

2774
04:15:36,654 --> 04:15:46,768
And using this conditional probability table, or using this joint probability table, I can begin to draw other pieces of information about things like conditional probability.

2775
04:15:47,054 --> 04:15:53,508
So I might ask a question like what is the probability distribution of clouds given that I know that it is raining?

2776
04:15:53,806 --> 04:15:55,972
Meaning I know for sure that it's raining.

2777
04:15:56,334 --> 04:16:05,008
Tell me the probability distribution over whether it's cloudy or not, given that I know already that it is in fact raining. And here I'm using C to stand for that random variable.

2778
04:16:05,088 --> 04:16:13,328
I'm looking for a distribution, meaning the answer to this is not going to be a single value, it's going to be two values, a vector of two values, where the first value is

2779
04:16:13,488 --> 04:16:19,728
probability of clouds, the second value is probability that it is not cloudy, but the sum of those two values is going to be one.

2780
04:16:19,982 --> 04:16:25,860
Because when you add up the probabilities of all of the possible worlds, the result that you get must be the number one.

2781
04:16:26,670 --> 04:16:29,860
And well, what do we know about how to calculate a conditional probability?

2782
04:16:30,318 --> 04:16:38,052
Well, we know that the probability of A given B is the probability of A and B divided by the probability of B.

2783
04:16:39,022 --> 04:16:39,844
So what does this mean?

2784
04:16:40,238 --> 04:16:44,368
Well, it means that I can calculate the probability of clouds given that it's raining.

2785
04:16:45,232 --> 04:16:50,660
As the probability of clouds and raining divided by the probability of rain.

2786
04:16:50,832 --> 04:16:57,072
And this comma here, for the probability distribution of clouds and rain, this comma sort of stands in for the word and.

2787
04:16:57,312 --> 04:17:00,992
You'll sort of see in the logical operator and and the comma used interchangeably.

2788
04:17:01,072 --> 04:17:08,324
This means the probability distribution over the clouds and knowing the fact that it is raining divided by the probability of rain.

2789
04:17:09,454 --> 04:17:16,324
And the interesting thing to note here, and what we'll often do in order to simplify our mathematics, is that dividing by the probability of rain,

2790
04:17:16,750 --> 04:17:19,632
the probability of rain here is just some numerical constant.

2791
04:17:19,726 --> 04:17:20,512
It is some number.

2792
04:17:20,672 --> 04:17:27,300
Dividing by probability of rain is just dividing by some constant, or in other words, multiplying by the inverse of that constant.

2793
04:17:27,712 --> 04:17:35,792
And it turns out that oftentimes we can just not worry about what the exact value of this is, and just know that it is in fact a constant value.

2794
04:17:35,982 --> 04:17:36,912
And we'll see why in a moment.

2795
04:17:37,166 --> 04:17:44,900
So instead of expressing this as this joint probability divided by the probability of rain, sometimes we'll just represent it as alpha

2796
04:17:45,392 --> 04:17:52,580
times the numerator here, the probability distribution of C, this variable, and that we know that it is raining, for instance.

2797
04:17:52,910 --> 04:18:02,500
So all we've done here is said this value of one over the probability of rain, that's really just a constant that we're going to divide by or equivalently multiply by the inverse of at the end.

2798
04:18:02,734 --> 04:18:05,700
We'll just call it alpha for now and deal with it a little bit later.

2799
04:18:06,382 --> 04:18:15,312
But the key idea here now, and this is an idea that's going to come up again, is that the conditional distribution of C given rain is proportional to,

2800
04:18:15,502 --> 04:18:21,732
meaning just some factor multiplied by, the joint probability of C and rain being true.

2801
04:18:22,190 --> 04:18:23,312
And so how do we figure this out?

2802
04:18:23,534 --> 04:18:32,612
Well, this is going to be the probability that it is cloudy given that it's rainy, which is 0.08, and the probability that it's not cloudy given that it's rainy, which is 0.02.

2803
04:18:32,942 --> 04:18:37,324
And so we get alpha times here now is that probability distribution.

2804
04:18:37,684 --> 04:18:42,884
0.08 is clouds and rain.02 is not cloudy and rain.

2805
04:18:43,854 --> 04:18:47,472
But of course, 0.08 and 0.02 don't sum up to the number one.

2806
04:18:47,854 --> 04:18:54,912
And we know that in a probability distribution, if you consider all of the possible values, they must sum up to a probability of one.

2807
04:18:55,342 --> 04:19:05,792
And so we know that we just need to figure out some constant to normalize, so to speak, these values, something we can multiply or divide by to get it so that all these probabilities sum up to one,

2808
04:19:06,094 --> 04:19:11,437
and it turns out that if we multiply both numbers by 10, then we can get that result of 0.8 and 0.2.

2809
04:19:11,872 --> 04:19:17,284
the proportions are still equivalent, but now 0.8 plus 0.2, those sum up to the number one.

2810
04:19:18,222 --> 04:19:23,268
So take a look at this and see if you can understand step by step how it is we're getting from one point to another.

2811
04:19:23,502 --> 04:19:33,312
But the key idea here is that by using the joint probabilities, these probabilities that it is both cloudy and rainy, and that it is not cloudy and rainy,

2812
04:19:33,582 --> 04:19:39,232
I can take that information and figure out the conditional probability, given that it's raining.

2813
04:19:39,406 --> 04:19:46,112
What is the chance that it's cloudy versus not cloudy, just by multiplying by some normalization constant, so to speak.

2814
04:19:46,192 --> 04:19:52,512
And this is what a computer can begin to use to be able to interact with these various different types of probabilities.

2815
04:19:52,846 --> 04:20:02,272
And it turns out there are a number of other probability rules that are going to be useful to us as we begin to explore how we can actually use this information to encode into our computers

2816
04:20:02,510 --> 04:20:10,192
some more complex analysis that we might want to do about probability and distributions and random variables that we might be interacting with.

2817
04:20:10,478 --> 04:20:12,592
So here are a couple of those important probability rules.

2818
04:20:12,832 --> 04:20:15,268
One of the simplest rules is just this negation rule.

2819
04:20:15,552 --> 04:20:18,912
What is the probability of not event a?

2820
04:20:19,086 --> 04:20:25,152
So a is an event that has some probability, and I would like to know what is the probability that a does not occur.

2821
04:20:25,422 --> 04:20:34,564
And it turns out it's just one minus p of a, which makes sense, because if those are the two possible cases, either A happens or A doesn't happen,

2822
04:20:34,894 --> 04:20:38,340
then when you add up those two cases, you must get one, which means

2823
04:20:38,990 --> 04:20:39,712
P of not A.

2824
04:20:40,432 --> 04:20:46,352
Must just be one minus P of A, because P of A and P of not A must sum up to the number one.

2825
04:20:46,592 --> 04:20:48,324
They must include all of the possible cases.

2826
04:20:49,646 --> 04:20:53,232
We've seen an expression for calculating the probability of A and B.

2827
04:20:53,552 --> 04:21:00,964
We might also reasonably want to calculate the probability of A or B. What is the probability that one thing happens or another thing happens?

2828
04:21:01,152 --> 04:21:10,020
So for example, I might want to calculate what is the probability that if I roll two dice, a red die and a blue die, what is the likelihood that A is a six or B is a six,

2829
04:21:10,222 --> 04:21:11,108
like one or the other.

2830
04:21:11,534 --> 04:21:20,512
And what you might imagine you could do, and the wrong way to approach it, would be just to say, all right, well, A comes up as a six with the red die comes up as a six with probability one

2831
04:21:21,518 --> 04:21:23,364
The same for the blue die, it's also one over six.

2832
04:21:23,662 --> 04:21:26,596
Add them together and you get two over six, otherwise known as one third.

2833
04:21:26,990 --> 04:21:35,396
Uh but this suffers from a problem of overcounting, that we've double counted the case where both a and b, both the red die and the blue die,

2834
04:21:35,632 --> 04:21:39,072
both come up as a six rule. And I've counted that instance twice.

2835
04:21:39,470 --> 04:21:47,472
So to resolve this, the actual expression for calculating the probability of A or B uses what we call the inclusion-exclusion formula.

2836
04:21:47,726 --> 04:21:56,032
So I take the probability of A, add it to the probability of B, that's all same as before, but then I need to exclude the cases that I've double counted.

2837
04:21:56,112 --> 04:21:57,380
So I subtract from that

2838
04:21:58,062 --> 04:22:00,672
the probability of A and B.

2839
04:22:01,198 --> 04:22:03,072
And that gets me the result for A or B.

2840
04:22:03,152 --> 04:22:06,752
I consider all the cases where A is true and all the cases where B is true.

2841
04:22:06,958 --> 04:22:15,792
And if you imagine this is like a Venn diagram of cases where A is true, cases where B is true, I just need to subtract out the middle to get rid of the cases that I have overcounted by double

2842
04:22:15,872 --> 04:22:19,620
counting them inside of both of these individual expressions.

2843
04:22:20,992 --> 04:22:25,028
one other rule that's going to be quite helpful is a rule called marginalization.

2844
04:22:25,422 --> 04:22:33,348
So marginalization is answering the question of how do I figure out the probability of A using some other variable that I might have access to, like B.

2845
04:22:33,582 --> 04:22:42,192
Even if I don't know additional information about it, I know that B, some event, can have two possible states, either B happens or B doesn't happen,

2846
04:22:42,446 --> 04:22:44,036
assuming it's a Boolean, true or false.

2847
04:22:44,686 --> 04:22:50,592
And well, what that means is that for me to be able to calculate the probability of A, there are only two cases.

2848
04:22:50,752 --> 04:22:55,472
Either A happens and B happens, or A happens and B doesn't happen.

2849
04:22:55,552 --> 04:23:00,804
And those are two disjoint, meaning they can't both happen together, either B happens or B doesn't happen.

2850
04:23:01,134 --> 04:23:02,980
They're disjoint or separate cases.

2851
04:23:03,246 --> 04:23:07,712
And so I can figure out the probability of A just by adding up those two cases.

2852
04:23:07,886 --> 04:23:16,068
The probability that A is true is the probability that A and B is true, plus the probability that A is true and B isn't true.

2853
04:23:16,462 --> 04:23:23,364
So by marginalizing, I've looked at the two possible cases that might take place. Either B happens or B doesn't happen.

2854
04:23:23,726 --> 04:23:26,992
And in either of those cases, I look at what's the probability that A happens.

2855
04:23:27,214 --> 04:23:31,524
And if I add those together, well then I get the probability that A happens as a whole.

2856
04:23:32,366 --> 04:23:33,552
So take a look at that rule.

2857
04:23:33,632 --> 04:23:41,860
It doesn't matter what B is or how it's related to A, so long as I know these joint distributions, I can figure out the overall probability of A.

2858
04:23:42,062 --> 04:23:51,072
And this can be a useful way if I have a joint distribution, like the joint distribution of A and B, to just figure out some unconditional probability, like the probability of A,

2859
04:23:51,278 --> 04:23:53,572
and we'll see examples of this soon as well.

2860
04:23:54,126 --> 04:24:00,512
Now sometimes these might not just be random, might not just be variables that are events that are like they happened or they didn't happen, like B is here.

2861
04:24:00,782 --> 04:24:05,124
They might be some broader probability distribution where there are multiple possible values.

2862
04:24:05,390 --> 04:24:16,004
And so here, in order to use this marginalization rule, I need to sum up not just over B and not B, but for all of the possible values that the other random variable could take on.

2863
04:24:16,302 --> 04:24:24,964
And so here we'll see a version of this rule for random variables, and it's going to include that summation notation to indicate that I'm summing up, adding up a whole bunch of individual values.

2864
04:24:25,742 --> 04:24:26,592
So here's the rule.

2865
04:24:26,832 --> 04:24:30,628
Looks a lot more complicated, but it's actually equivalent exactly the same rule.

2866
04:24:30,958 --> 04:24:40,548
What I'm saying here is that if I have two random variables, one called x, And one called y, well, the probability that x is equal to some value x sub

2867
04:24:40,800 --> 04:24:44,772
i, this is just some value that this variable takes on. How do I figure it out?

2868
04:24:45,102 --> 04:24:52,420
Well, I'm going to sum up over j, where j is going to range over all of the possible values that y can take on.

2869
04:24:52,974 --> 04:24:57,924
Well, let's look at the probability that x equals xi and y equals yj.

2870
04:24:58,510 --> 04:25:04,080
So the exact same rule, the only difference here is now I'm summing up over all of the possible values that y can take on,

2871
04:25:04,430 --> 04:25:12,580
saying let's add up all of those possible cases and look at this joint distribution, this joint probability that x takes on the value I care about

2872
04:25:12,814 --> 04:25:15,332
given all of the possible values for y.

2873
04:25:15,598 --> 04:25:17,860
And if I add all those up, then I can get

2874
04:25:18,574 --> 04:25:25,920
this unconditional probability of what x is equal to, whether or not x is equal to some value x sub i.

2875
04:25:26,542 --> 04:25:28,960
So let's take a look at this rule, because it does look a little bit complicated.

2876
04:25:29,040 --> 04:25:30,852
Let's try and put a concrete example to it.

2877
04:25:31,310 --> 04:25:43,748
Here again is that same joint distribution from before. I have cloud, not cloudy, rainy, not rainy, and maybe I want to access some variable. I want to know what is the probability that it is cloudy.

2878
04:25:44,494 --> 04:25:52,676
Well, marginalization says that if I have this joint distribution and I want to know, what is the probability that it is cloudy, well, I need to consider

2879
04:25:53,102 --> 04:26:00,164
the other variable, the variable that's not here, the idea that it's rainy, and I consider the two cases, either it's raining or it's not raining,

2880
04:26:00,366 --> 04:26:03,076
and I just sum up the values for each of those possibilities.

2881
04:26:03,918 --> 04:26:08,292
In other words, the probability that it is cloudy is equal to the sum

2882
04:26:09,006 --> 04:26:11,364
of the probability that it's cloudy and it's rainy,

2883
04:26:12,270 --> 04:26:15,268
and the probability that it's cloudy and it is not

2884
04:26:16,110 --> 04:26:16,484
raining.

2885
04:26:17,678 --> 04:26:20,000
And so these now are values that I have access to.

2886
04:26:20,160 --> 04:26:24,132
These are values that are just inside of this joint probability table.

2887
04:26:24,462 --> 04:26:27,200
What is the probability that it is both cloudy and rainy?

2888
04:26:27,598 --> 04:26:37,360
Well, it's just the intersection of these two here, which is 0.08, and the probability that it's cloudy and not raining is all right, here's cloudy, here's not raining, it's 0.32,

2889
04:26:37,646 --> 04:26:41,840
so it's 0.08 plus 0.32, which just gives us equal to 0.4.

2890
04:26:42,254 --> 04:26:46,148
That is the unconditional probability that it is in fact cloudy.

2891
04:26:46,510 --> 04:26:53,680
And so marginalization gives us a way to go from these joint distributions to just some individual probability that I might care about.

2892
04:26:53,934 --> 04:27:00,420
And you'll see a little bit later why it is that we care about that and why that's actually useful to us as we begin doing some of these calculations.

2893
04:27:02,094 --> 04:27:09,508
Last rule we'll take a look at before transitioning to something a little bit different is this rule of conditioning, very similar to the marginalization rule.

2894
04:27:09,774 --> 04:27:17,680
But it says that again, if I have two events a and b, but instead of having access to their joint probabilities, I have access to their conditional probabilities,

2895
04:27:17,840 --> 04:27:19,012
how they relate to each other.

2896
04:27:19,534 --> 04:27:30,000
Well, again, if I want to know the probability that a happens, and I know that there's some other variable b, either b happens or b doesn't happen, and so I can say that the probability of a

2897
04:27:30,254 --> 04:27:38,564
is the probability of a given b times the probability of B, meaning B happened, and given that I know B happened, what's the likelihood that A happened?

2898
04:27:39,054 --> 04:27:41,732
And then I consider the other case, that B didn't happen.

2899
04:27:42,126 --> 04:27:49,252
So here's the probability that B didn't happen, and here's the probability that A happens given that I know that B didn't happen.

2900
04:27:49,486 --> 04:27:58,640
And this is really the equivalent rule just using conditional probability instead of joint probability, where I'm saying let's look at both of these two cases and condition

2901
04:27:58,862 --> 04:27:59,280
on B.

2902
04:27:59,440 --> 04:28:05,440
Look at the case where B happens, and look at the case where B doesn't happen, and look at what probabilities I get as a result.

2903
04:28:06,126 --> 04:28:14,224
And just as in the case of marginalization, where there was an equivalent rule for random variables that could take on multiple possible values in a domain of possible values,

2904
04:28:14,478 --> 04:28:16,864
here too, conditioning has the same equivalent rule.

2905
04:28:17,102 --> 04:28:23,344
Again, there's a summation to mean I'm summing over all of the possible values that some random variable y could take on.

2906
04:28:23,630 --> 04:28:32,064
But if I want to know what is the probability that x takes on this value, then I'm going to sum up over all the values j that y could take on and say,

2907
04:28:32,224 --> 04:28:41,744
all right, what's the chance that y takes on that value yj, and multiply it by the conditional probability that x takes on this value given that y took on that value

2908
04:28:41,998 --> 04:28:42,468
yj?

2909
04:28:42,862 --> 04:28:47,524
So equivalent rule just using conditional probabilities instead of joint probabilities.

2910
04:28:47,726 --> 04:28:52,708
And using the equation we know about joint probabilities, we can translate between these two.

2911
04:28:53,710 --> 04:28:57,584
So, all right, we've seen a whole lot of mathematics, and we've just sort of laid the foundation for mathematics,

2912
04:28:57,678 --> 04:29:01,744
and no need to worry if you haven't seen probability in too much detail up until this point.

2913
04:29:01,998 --> 04:29:10,064
These are sort of the foundations of the ideas that are going to come up as we begin to explore how we can now take these ideas from probability and begin

2914
04:29:10,064 --> 04:29:18,624
to apply them to represent something inside of our computer, something inside of the AI agent we're trying to design that is able to represent information and probabilities

2915
04:29:18,894 --> 04:29:21,476
and the likelihoods between various different events.

2916
04:29:22,126 --> 04:29:25,444
So there are a number of different probabilistic models that we can generate.

2917
04:29:25,824 --> 04:29:29,764
But the first of the models we're going to talk about are what are known as Bayesian networks.

2918
04:29:30,222 --> 04:29:39,744
And a Bayesian network is just going to be some network of random variables, connected random variables, that are going to represent the dependence between these random variables.

2919
04:29:39,904 --> 04:29:48,224
That odds are most random variables in this world are not independent from each other, that there's some relationship between things that are happening that we care about.

2920
04:29:48,464 --> 04:29:54,224
If it is rainy today, that might increase the likelihood that my flight or my train gets delayed, for example.

2921
04:29:54,397 --> 04:30:01,060
There is some dependence between these random variables, and a Bayesian network is going to be able to capture those dependencies.

2922
04:30:01,998 --> 04:30:05,444
So what is a Bayesian network? What is its actual structure and how does it work?

2923
04:30:06,030 --> 04:30:08,292
Well, a Bayesian network is going to be a directed graph.

2924
04:30:08,750 --> 04:30:10,564
And again, we've seen directed graphs before.

2925
04:30:10,784 --> 04:30:17,668
They are individual nodes with arrows or edges that connect one node to another node, pointing in a particular direction.

2926
04:30:18,478 --> 04:30:25,700
And so this directed graph is going to have nodes as well, where each node in this directed graph is going to represent a random variable,

2927
04:30:25,904 --> 04:30:29,988
something like the weather, or something like whether my train was on time or delayed.

2928
04:30:30,830 --> 04:30:42,084
And we're going to have an arrow from a node x to a node y to mean that x is a parent of y. So that'll be our notation. If there's an arrow from x to y, x is going to be considered a parent of y.

2929
04:30:42,542 --> 04:30:51,812
And the reason that's important is because each of these nodes is going to have a probability distribution that we're going to store along with it, which is the distribution of x

2930
04:30:52,334 --> 04:30:55,364
given some evidence, given the parents of x.

2931
04:30:56,206 --> 04:31:03,364
So the way to more intuitively think about this is that the parents seem to be thought of as sort of causes for some effect that we're going to observe.

2932
04:31:04,174 --> 04:31:11,584
And so let's take a look at an actual example of a Bayesian network and think about the types of logic that might be involved in reasoning about that network.

2933
04:31:11,744 --> 04:31:18,116
Let's imagine for a moment that I have an appointment out of town and I need to take a train in order to get to that appointment.

2934
04:31:18,638 --> 04:31:25,732
So what are the things I might care about? Well, I care about getting to my appointment on time. Either I make it to my appointment and I'm able to attend it, or I miss the appointment.

2935
04:31:26,224 --> 04:31:28,864
and you might imagine that that's influenced by the train.

2936
04:31:29,070 --> 04:31:33,060
That the train is either on time or it's delayed, for example.

2937
04:31:33,646 --> 04:31:35,876
But that train itself is also influenced.

2938
04:31:36,064 --> 04:31:39,264
Whether the train is on time or not depends maybe on the rain.

2939
04:31:39,664 --> 04:31:42,020
is there no rain? Is it light rain? Is there heavy rain?

2940
04:31:42,304 --> 04:31:49,024
and it might also be influenced by other variables too. It might be influenced as well by whether or not there's maintenance on the train track, for example.

2941
04:31:49,184 --> 04:31:54,116
If there is maintenance on the train track, that probably increases the likelihood that my train is delayed.

2942
04:31:55,406 --> 04:32:00,484
and so we can represent all of these ideas using a Bayesian network that looks a little something like this.

2943
04:32:01,038 --> 04:32:06,276
Here I have four nodes representing four random variables that I would like to keep track of.

2944
04:32:06,544 --> 04:32:14,544
I have one random variable called rain that can take on three possible values in its domain, either none or light or heavy, for no rain,

2945
04:32:14,704 --> 04:32:15,972
light rain, or heavy rain.

2946
04:32:16,398 --> 04:32:22,464
I have a variable called maintenance for whether or not there's maintenance on the train track, which it has two possible values, just either yes or no.

2947
04:32:22,624 --> 04:32:25,540
Either there is maintenance or there's no maintenance happening on the track.

2948
04:32:26,158 --> 04:32:29,744
Then I have a random variable for the train, indicating whether or not the train was on time or not.

2949
04:32:30,224 --> 04:32:33,264
That random variable has two possible values in its domain.

2950
04:32:33,486 --> 04:32:36,580
The train is either on time or the train is delayed.

2951
04:32:37,326 --> 04:32:41,024
And then finally, I have a random variable for whether I make it to my appointment.

2952
04:32:41,184 --> 04:32:48,292
For my appointment down here, I have a random variable called appointment that itself has two possible values, attend and miss.

2953
04:32:49,102 --> 04:32:50,464
And so here are the possible values.

2954
04:32:50,544 --> 04:32:57,540
Here are my four nodes, each of which represents a random variable, each of which has a domain of possible values that it can take on.

2955
04:32:58,094 --> 04:33:05,796
And the arrows, the edges pointing from one node to another, encode some notion of dependence inside of this graph.

2956
04:33:05,984 --> 04:33:11,812
That whether I make it to my appointment or not is dependent upon whether the train is on time or delayed.

2957
04:33:12,142 --> 04:33:18,148
And whether the train is on time or delayed is dependent on two things given by the two arrows pointing at this node.

2958
04:33:18,510 --> 04:33:26,564
It is dependent on whether or not there was maintenance on the train track, and it is also dependent upon whether or not it was raining or whether it is raining.

2959
04:33:27,310 --> 04:33:34,756
And just to make things a little complicated, let's say as well that whether or not there's maintenance on the track, this too might be influenced by the rain.

2960
04:33:34,894 --> 04:33:40,132
That if there's heavier rain, well maybe it's less likely that it's going to be maintenance on the train track that day,

2961
04:33:40,334 --> 04:33:44,704
because they're more likely to want to do maintenance on the track on days when it's not raining, for example.

2962
04:33:44,974 --> 04:33:55,076
And so these nodes might have different relationships between them, but the idea is that we can come up with a probability distribution for any of these nodes based only upon its parents.

2963
04:33:55,950 --> 04:34:00,304
And so let's look node by node at what this probability distribution might actually look like.

2964
04:34:00,384 --> 04:34:06,916
And we'll go ahead and begin with this root node, this rain node here, which is at the top, and has no arrows pointing into it.

2965
04:34:07,246 --> 04:34:13,188
Which means its probability distribution is not going to be a conditional distribution. It's not based on anything.

2966
04:34:13,454 --> 04:34:19,556
I just have some probability distribution over the possible values for the rain random variable.

2967
04:34:20,238 --> 04:34:25,504
And that distribution might look a little something like this: none, light, and heavy each have a possible value.

2968
04:34:25,838 --> 04:34:33,284
Here I'm saying the likelihood of no rain is 0.7, of light rain is 0.2, of heavy rain is 0.1, for example.

2969
04:34:33,902 --> 04:34:38,468
So here is a probability distribution for this root node in this Bayesian network.

2970
04:34:39,310 --> 04:34:42,148
And let's now consider the next node in the network, maintenance.

2971
04:34:42,606 --> 04:34:44,464
Track maintenance is yes or no.

2972
04:34:44,624 --> 04:34:51,652
And the general idea of what this distribution is going to encode, at least in this story, is the idea that the heavier the rain is,

2973
04:34:52,142 --> 04:34:55,184
the less likely it is that there's going to be maintenance on the track.

2974
04:34:55,264 --> 04:35:02,180
Because the people that are doing maintenance on the track probably want to wait until a day when it's not as rainy in order to do the track maintenance, for example.

2975
04:35:02,478 --> 04:35:04,864
And so what might that probability distribution look like?

2976
04:35:05,102 --> 04:35:08,452
Well, this now is going to be a conditional probability distribution.

2977
04:35:08,686 --> 04:35:16,388
That here are the three possible values for the rain random variable, which I'm here just going to abbreviate to R, either no rain, light rain, or heavy rain.

2978
04:35:17,038 --> 04:35:25,348
And for each of those possible values, either there is yes track maintenance or no track maintenance, and those have probabilities associated with them.

2979
04:35:25,742 --> 04:35:34,544
That I see here that if it is um if it is not raining, then there is a probability of 0.4 that there's track maintenance and a probability of 0.6

2980
04:35:34,862 --> 04:35:35,556
that there isn't.

2981
04:35:35,982 --> 04:35:43,716
But if there's heavy rain, then here the chance that there is track maintenance is 0.1, and the chance that there is not track maintenance is 0.9.

2982
04:35:44,174 --> 04:35:54,020
Each of these rows is going to sum up to 1, because each of these represent different values of whether or not it's raining, the three possible values that that random variable can take on,

2983
04:35:54,304 --> 04:36:00,836
and each is associated with its own probability distribution that is ultimately all going to add up to the number one.

2984
04:36:02,126 --> 04:36:08,740
So that there is our distribution for this random variable called maintenance, about whether or not there is maintenance on the train track.

2985
04:36:09,678 --> 04:36:11,236
And now let's consider the next variable.

2986
04:36:11,694 --> 04:36:17,744
Here we have a node inside of our Bayesian network called train that has two possible values, on time and delayed.

2987
04:36:18,064 --> 04:36:22,724
And this node is going to be dependent Upon the two nodes that are pointing towards it.

2988
04:36:22,926 --> 04:36:30,276
That this, whether or not the train is on time or delayed, depends on whether or not there's track maintenance, and it depends on whether or not there is rain.

2989
04:36:30,446 --> 04:36:34,308
That heavier rain probably means more likely that my train is delayed.

2990
04:36:35,152 --> 04:36:41,552
And if there is track maintenance, that also probably means it's more likely that my train is delayed as well.

2991
04:36:41,870 --> 04:36:50,724
And so you could construct a larger probability distribution, a conditional probability distribution, that instead of conditioning on just one variable, as was the case here,

2992
04:36:51,152 --> 04:36:58,468
is now conditioning on two variables, conditioning both on rain represented by r and on maintenance represented by yes.

2993
04:36:58,862 --> 04:37:06,084
Again, each of these rows has two values that sum up to the number one, one for whether the train is on time, one for whether the train is delayed.

2994
04:37:06,862 --> 04:37:15,492
And here I can say something like, all right, if I know there was light rain and track maintenance, well, okay, that would be R is light and M is yes,

2995
04:37:16,110 --> 04:37:23,072
well then there's a probability of 0.6 that my train is on time, and a probability of 0.4 that the train is delayed.

2996
04:37:23,152 --> 04:37:31,620
And you can imagine gathering this data just by looking at real-world data, looking at data about, all right, if I knew that it was light rain and there was track maintenance,

2997
04:37:31,792 --> 04:37:35,392
how often was a train delayed or not delayed? And you could begin to construct this thing.

2998
04:37:35,566 --> 04:37:44,592
But the interesting thing is intelligently being able to try to figure out how might you go about ordering these things, what things might influence other nodes inside of this Bayesian

2999
04:37:44,752 --> 04:37:45,712
network.

3000
04:37:46,318 --> 04:37:49,988
And the last thing I care about is whether or not I make it to my appointment.

3001
04:37:50,542 --> 04:37:52,592
So did I attend or miss the appointment?

3002
04:37:52,814 --> 04:37:59,712
And ultimately, whether I attend or miss the appointment, it is influenced by track maintenance, because it's indirectly this idea that, right,

3003
04:37:59,872 --> 04:38:06,372
if there is track maintenance, well then my train might more likely be delayed, and if my train is more likely to be delayed, then I'm more likely to miss my appointment.

3004
04:38:06,926 --> 04:38:12,352
But what we encode in this Bayesian network are just what we might consider to be more direct relationships.

3005
04:38:12,432 --> 04:38:15,044
So the train has a direct influence on the appointment.

3006
04:38:15,278 --> 04:38:23,952
And given that I know whether the train is on time or delayed, knowing whether there's track maintenance isn't going to give me any additional information that I didn't already have.

3007
04:38:24,112 --> 04:38:29,892
That if I know train, these other nodes that are up above isn't really going to influence the result.

3008
04:38:30,734 --> 04:38:36,772
And so here we might represent it using another conditional probability distribution that looks a little something like this.

3009
04:38:36,977 --> 04:38:41,956
that train can take on two possible values, either my train is on time or my train is delayed.

3010
04:38:42,350 --> 04:38:49,552
And for each of those two possible values, I have a distribution for what are the odds that I'm able to attend the meeting and what are the odds that I miss the meeting.

3011
04:38:49,712 --> 04:38:58,084
And obviously, if my train is on time, I'm much more likely to be able to attend the meeting than if my train is delayed, in which case I'm more likely to miss that meeting.

3012
04:38:58,958 --> 04:39:06,756
So all of these nodes put all together here represent this Bayesian network, this network of random variables, whose values I ultimately care about,

3013
04:39:07,086 --> 04:39:09,712
and that have some sort of relationship between them.

3014
04:39:09,966 --> 04:39:17,124
some sort of dependence where these arrows from one node to another indicate some dependence, that I can calculate the probability of some node.

3015
04:39:17,646 --> 04:39:20,804
given the parents that happen to exist. there.

3016
04:39:21,454 --> 04:39:27,152
So now that we've been able to describe the structure of this Bayesian network and the relationships between each of these nodes,

3017
04:39:27,312 --> 04:39:34,272
by associating each of the nodes in the network with a probability distribution, whether that's an unconditional probability distribution.

3018
04:39:34,446 --> 04:39:38,912
in the case of this root node, here, like rain, and a conditional probability distribution.

3019
04:39:39,118 --> 04:39:44,164
in the case of all of the other nodes whose probabilities are dependent upon the values of their parents,

3020
04:39:44,526 --> 04:39:49,632
we can begin to do some computation and calculation using the information inside of that table.

3021
04:39:50,030 --> 04:39:55,072
So let's imagine, for example, that I just wanted to compute something simple like the probability of light rain.

3022
04:39:55,534 --> 04:39:57,444
How would I get the probability of light rain?

3023
04:39:57,760 --> 04:40:06,148
Well, light rain, rain here is a root node, and so if I wanted to calculate that probability, I could just look at the probability distribution for rain

3024
04:40:06,320 --> 04:40:09,200
and extract from it the probability of light rain.

3025
04:40:09,280 --> 04:40:11,812
It's just a single value that I already have access to.

3026
04:40:12,846 --> 04:40:20,800
But we could also imagine wanting to compute more complex joint probabilities, like the probability that there is light rain and also

3027
04:40:22,240 --> 04:40:27,460
This is a joint probability of two values, light rain and no track maintenance.

3028
04:40:27,918 --> 04:40:32,900
And the way I might do that is first by starting by saying, all right, well, let me get the probability of light rain.

3029
04:40:33,390 --> 04:40:36,324
But now I also want the probability of no track maintenance.

3030
04:40:36,814 --> 04:40:40,868
But of course, this node is dependent upon the value of rain.

3031
04:40:41,294 --> 04:40:46,960
So what I really want is the probability of no track maintenance given that I know that there was light rain.

3032
04:40:47,120 --> 04:40:55,620
And so the expression for calculating this idea that the probability of light rain and no track maintenance is really just the probability of light rain

3033
04:40:56,080 --> 04:41:01,680
and the probability that there is no track maintenance given that I know that there already is light rain.

3034
04:41:01,870 --> 04:41:11,556
So I take the unconditional probability of light rain, multiply it by the conditional probability of no track maintenance given that I know there's light rain.

3035
04:41:12,238 --> 04:41:19,200
And you can continue to do this again and again for every variable that you want to add into this joint probability that I might want to calculate.

3036
04:41:19,360 --> 04:41:27,280
If I wanted to know the probability of light rain and no track maintenance and a delayed train, well, that's going to be the probability of light

3037
04:41:27,918 --> 04:41:30,560
rain multiplied by the probability of no track maintenance.

3038
04:41:30,574 --> 04:41:39,396
given light rain multiplied by the probability of a delayed train given light rain and no track maintenance, because whether the train is on time or delayed

3039
04:41:39,760 --> 04:41:42,560
is dependent upon both of these other two variables.

3040
04:41:42,766 --> 04:41:48,228
And so I have two pieces of evidence that go into the calculation of that conditional probability.

3041
04:41:48,462 --> 04:41:58,400
And each of these three values is just a value that I can look up by looking at one of these individual probability distributions that is encoded into my Bayesian

3042
04:41:58,560 --> 04:41:59,200
network.

3043
04:41:59,726 --> 04:42:09,316
And if I wanted a joint probability over all four of the variables, something like the probability of light rain and no track maintenance and a delayed train and I miss my appointment,

3044
04:42:09,742 --> 04:42:14,240
well that's going to be multiplying four different values, one from each of these individual nodes.

3045
04:42:14,478 --> 04:42:22,340
It's going to be the probability of light rain, then of no track maintenance given light rain, then of a delayed train given light rain and no track maintenance.

3046
04:42:22,670 --> 04:42:31,840
And then finally, for this node here, for whether I make it to my appointment or not, it's not dependent upon these two variables given that I know whether or not the train is on time.

3047
04:42:31,920 --> 04:42:39,760
I only need to care about the conditional probability that I miss my train, or that I miss my appointment, given that the train happens to be delayed.

3048
04:42:39,840 --> 04:42:47,908
And so that's represented here by four probabilities, each of which is located inside of one of these probability distributions for each of the nodes,

3049
04:42:48,320 --> 04:42:49,988
all multiplied together.

3050
04:42:50,734 --> 04:42:58,948
And so I can take a variable like that and figure out what the joint probability is by multiplying a whole bunch of these individual probabilities from the Bayesian network.

3051
04:42:59,630 --> 04:43:07,908
But of course, just as with last time, where what I really wanted to do was to be able to get new pieces of information, here too, this is what we're going to want to do with our Bayesian network.

3052
04:43:08,240 --> 04:43:11,204
In the context of knowledge, we talked about the problem of inference.

3053
04:43:11,440 --> 04:43:19,440
Given things that I know to be true, can I draw conclusions, make deductions about other facts about the world that I also know to be true?

3054
04:43:19,854 --> 04:43:23,652
And what we're going to do now is apply the same sort of idea to probability.

3055
04:43:23,840 --> 04:43:31,780
Using information about which I have some knowledge, whether some evidence or some probabilities, can I figure out not other variables for certain,

3056
04:43:32,014 --> 04:43:36,640
but can I figure out the probabilities of other variables taking on particular values?

3057
04:43:36,800 --> 04:43:44,640
And so here we introduce the problem of inference in a probabilistic setting, in a case where variables might not necessarily be true for sure,

3058
04:43:44,910 --> 04:43:49,380
but they might be random variables that take on different values with some probability.

3059
04:43:50,158 --> 04:43:53,700
So how do we formally define what exactly this inference problem actually is?

3060
04:43:54,126 --> 04:43:56,548
Well, the inference problem has a couple of parts to it.

3061
04:43:56,974 --> 04:44:01,280
We have some query, some variable x that we want to compute the distribution for.

3062
04:44:01,440 --> 04:44:06,276
Maybe I want the probability that I miss my train, or I want the probability that

3063
04:44:07,200 --> 04:44:10,400
there is track maintenance, something that I want information about.

3064
04:44:11,150 --> 04:44:13,088
And then I have some evidence variables.

3065
04:44:13,168 --> 04:44:19,652
Maybe it's just one piece of evidence, maybe it's multiple pieces of evidence, but I've observed certain variables for some sort of event.

3066
04:44:20,238 --> 04:44:23,332
So for example, I might have observed that it is raining.

3067
04:44:23,488 --> 04:44:24,528
This is evidence that I have.

3068
04:44:24,688 --> 04:44:33,540
I know that there is light rain, or I know that there is heavy rain, and that is evidence I have, and using that evidence, I want to know what is the probability that my train is delayed,

3069
04:44:33,728 --> 04:44:34,244
for example.

3070
04:44:34,894 --> 04:44:37,968
And that is a query that I might want to ask based on this evidence.

3071
04:44:38,126 --> 04:44:43,844
So I have a query, some variable, evidence, which are some other variables that I have observed inside of my Bayesian network.

3072
04:44:44,206 --> 04:44:46,848
And of course, that does leave some hidden variables.

3073
04:44:47,088 --> 04:44:47,428
Why?

3074
04:44:47,728 --> 04:44:51,780
These are variables that are not evidence variables and not query variables.

3075
04:44:52,206 --> 04:45:01,956
So you might imagine in the case where I know whether or not it's raining, and I want to know whether my train is going to be delayed or not, the hidden variable, the thing I don't have access to,

3076
04:45:02,158 --> 04:45:04,196
is something like is there maintenance on the track?

3077
04:45:04,494 --> 04:45:06,756
Or am I going to make or not make my appointment, for example?

3078
04:45:06,928 --> 04:45:09,008
These are variables that I don't have access to.

3079
04:45:09,088 --> 04:45:14,340
They're hidden, because they're not things I observed, and they're also not the query, the thing that I'm asking.

3080
04:45:14,702 --> 04:45:22,528
And so ultimately what we want to calculate is I want to know the probability distribution of x given e, the event that I observe.

3081
04:45:22,608 --> 04:45:31,968
So given that I observe some event, I observe that it is raining, I would like to know what is the distribution over the possible values of the train random variable. Is it on time?

3082
04:45:32,302 --> 04:45:32,848
Is it delayed?

3083
04:45:33,070 --> 04:45:34,916
What's the likelihood it's going to be there?

3084
04:45:35,342 --> 04:45:41,888
And it turns out we can do this calculation just using a lot of the probability rules that we've already seen in action.

3085
04:45:42,030 --> 04:45:46,608
And ultimately, we're going to take a look at the math at a little bit of a high level, at an abstract level,

3086
04:45:46,798 --> 04:45:52,128
but ultimately we can allow computers and programming libraries that already exist to begin to do some of this math for us.

3087
04:45:52,238 --> 04:45:56,868
But it's good to get a general sense for what's actually happening when this inference process takes place.

3088
04:45:57,582 --> 04:46:06,788
Let's imagine, for example, that I want to compute the probability distribution of the appointment random variable given some evidence, given that I know that there was light rain

3089
04:46:07,168 --> 04:46:08,452
and no track maintenance.

3090
04:46:08,942 --> 04:46:12,164
So there's my evidence, these two variables that I observe the values of.

3091
04:46:12,448 --> 04:46:18,148
I observe the value of rain, I know there's light rain, and I know that there is no track maintenance going on today.

3092
04:46:18,446 --> 04:46:22,368
And what I care about knowing, my query, is this random variable, appointment.

3093
04:46:22,528 --> 04:46:31,300
I want to know the distribution of this random variable appointment, like what is the chance that I'm able to attend my appointment, what is the chance that I miss my appointment given this evidence.

3094
04:46:31,950 --> 04:46:36,676
And the hidden variable, the information that I don't have access to, is this variable train.

3095
04:46:36,848 --> 04:46:43,652
This is information that is not part of the evidence that I see, not something that I observe, but it is also not the query that I'm asking for.

3096
04:46:44,526 --> 04:46:52,516
And so what might this inference procedure look like? Well, if you recall back from when we were defining conditional probability and doing math with conditional probabilities,

3097
04:46:52,878 --> 04:46:58,212
we know that a conditional probability is proportional to the joint probability.

3098
04:46:58,638 --> 04:47:07,248
And we remember this by recalling that the probability of a given b is just some constant factor alpha multiplied by the probability of a and b.

3099
04:47:07,502 --> 04:47:16,288
That constant factor alpha turns out to be like dividing over the probability of b, but the important thing is that it's just some constant multiplied by the joint distribution,

3100
04:47:16,494 --> 04:47:19,012
the probability that all of these individual things happen.

3101
04:47:19,630 --> 04:47:28,928
So in this case, I can take the probability of the appointment random variable given light rain and no track maintenance, and say that is just going to be proportional.

3102
04:47:29,198 --> 04:47:37,092
some constant alpha, multiplied by the joint probability, the probability of a particular value for the appointment, random variable and light rain,

3103
04:47:37,568 --> 04:47:39,488
and no track maintenance.

3104
04:47:39,982 --> 04:47:46,884
Well, all right, how do I calculate this probability of appointment and light rain and no track maintenance when what I really care about is knowing like

3105
04:47:47,088 --> 04:47:55,328
I need all four of these values to be able to calculate a joint distribution across everything, because in a particular appointment depends upon the value of train.

3106
04:47:56,014 --> 04:47:59,652
Well, in order to do that, here I can begin to use that marginalization trick.

3107
04:48:00,000 --> 04:48:05,156
That there are only two ways I can get any configuration of an appointment, light rain, and no track maintenance.

3108
04:48:05,520 --> 04:48:13,760
Either this particular setting of variables happens and the train is on time, or this particular setting of variables happens and the train is delayed.

3109
04:48:13,920 --> 04:48:16,880
Those are two possible cases that I would want to consider.

3110
04:48:17,134 --> 04:48:26,468
And if I add those two cases up, well then I get the result just by adding up all of the possibilities for the hidden variable, or variables if there are multiple.

3111
