let me reject all of the samples that don't match my evidence, and only consider the samples that do match what it is that I have in my evidence

3292
05:05:19,022 --> 05:05:20,720
that I want to make some sort of calculation about.

3293
05:05:21,614 --> 05:05:24,676
And it turns out, using the libraries that we've had for Bayesian networks,

3294
05:05:25,486 --> 05:05:35,172
we can begin to implement this same sort of idea, like implement rejection sampling, which is what this method is called, to be able to figure out some probability, not via direct inference,

3295
05:05:35,504 --> 05:05:36,996
but instead by sampling.

3296
05:05:37,614 --> 05:05:41,412
So what I have here is a program called sample.py imports the exact same model.

3297
05:05:41,774 --> 05:05:44,704
And what I define first is a program to generate a sample.

3298
05:05:44,974 --> 05:05:48,516
And the way I generate a sample is just by looping over all of the states.

3299
05:05:48,864 --> 05:05:52,004
the states need to be in some sort of order to make sure I'm looping in the correct order.

3300
05:05:52,366 --> 05:05:57,572
But effectively, if it is a conditional distribution, I'm going to sample based on the parents.

3301
05:05:57,998 --> 05:06:04,100
And otherwise I'm just going to directly sample the variable, like rain, which has no parents, it's just an unconditional distribution,

3302
05:06:04,544 --> 05:06:07,556
and keep track of all those parent samples and return the final sample.

3303
05:06:08,064 --> 05:06:15,012
The exact syntax of this again, not particularly important, it just happens to be part of the implementation details of this particular library.

3304
05:06:15,438 --> 05:06:25,744
The interesting logic is down below, now that I have the ability to generate a sample, if I want to know the distribution of the appointment random variable given that the train is delayed,

3305
05:06:26,094 --> 05:06:28,036
well then I can begin to do calculations like this.

3306
05:06:28,304 --> 05:06:33,060
Let me take 10,000 samples and assemble all my results in this list called data.

3307
05:06:33,422 --> 05:06:36,324
I'll go ahead and loop n times, in this case 10,000 times.

3308
05:06:36,718 --> 05:06:37,956
I'll generate a sample,

3309
05:06:38,638 --> 05:06:42,784
and I want to know the distribution of appointment given that the train is delayed.

3310
05:06:43,054 --> 05:06:50,980
So according to rejection sampling, I'm only going to consider samples where the train is delayed. If the train's not delayed, I'm not going to consider those values at all.

3311
05:06:51,438 --> 05:06:55,396
So I'm going to say, all right, if I take the sample, look at the value of the train random variable.

3312
05:06:55,790 --> 05:07:05,104
If the train is delayed, well let me go ahead and add to my data that I'm collecting the value of the appointment random variable that took it that it took on in this particular sample.

3313
05:07:05,422 --> 05:07:12,144
So I'm only considering the samples where the train is delayed, and for each of those samples, considering what the value of appointment is.

3314
05:07:12,462 --> 05:07:17,860
And then at the end I'm using a Python class called counter, which quickly counts up all the values inside of a data set.

3315
05:07:18,126 --> 05:07:25,892
So I can take this list of data and figure out how many times was my appointment made and how many times was my appointment missed.

3316
05:07:27,022 --> 05:07:32,228
And so this here with just a couple lines of code is an implementation of rejection sampling.

3317
05:07:32,686 --> 05:07:35,748
And I can run it by going ahead and running pythonsample.py.

3318
05:07:37,774 --> 05:07:41,188
And when I do that, here's the result I get. This is the result of the counter.

3319
05:07:42,327 --> 05:07:48,356
1,251 times I was able to attend the meeting, and 856 times I was able to miss the meeting.

3320
05:07:48,544 --> 05:07:53,636
And you can imagine by doing more and more samples, I'll be able to get a better and better, more accurate result.

3321
05:07:54,062 --> 05:07:55,652
And this is a randomized process.

3322
05:07:55,822 --> 05:07:58,224
It's going to be an approximation of the probability.

3323
05:07:58,510 --> 05:08:06,464
If I run it a different time, you'll notice the numbers are similar, 1272 and 905, but they're not identical because there's some randomization,

3324
05:08:06,624 --> 05:08:09,104
some likelihood that things might be higher or lower.

3325
05:08:09,264 --> 05:08:17,984
And so this is why we generally want to try and use more samples, so that we can have a greater amount of confidence in our result, be more sure about the result that we're getting,

3326
05:08:18,190 --> 05:08:25,252
of whether or not it accurately reflects or represents the actual underlying probabilities that are inherent inside of this distribution.

3327
05:08:26,638 --> 05:08:29,424
And so this then was an instance of rejection sampling.

3328
05:08:29,584 --> 05:08:34,624
And it turns out there are a number of other sampling methods that you could use to begin to try to sample.

3329
05:08:34,784 --> 05:08:43,844
One problem that rejection sampling has is that if the evidence you're looking for is a fairly unlikely event, well, you're going to be rejecting a lot of samples.

3330
05:08:44,206 --> 05:08:52,036
Like if I'm looking for the probability of X given some evidence E, if E is very unlikely to occur, like occurs maybe one every 1,000 times,

3331
05:08:52,302 --> 05:08:59,664
then I'm only going to be considering one out of every thousand samples that I do, which is a pretty inefficient method for trying to do this sort of calculation.

3332
05:08:59,744 --> 05:09:06,852
I'm throwing away a lot of samples, and it takes computational effort to be able to generate those samples, so I'd like to not have to do something like that.

3333
05:09:07,310 --> 05:09:09,636
So there are other sampling methods that can try and address this.

3334
05:09:09,904 --> 05:09:12,516
One such sampling method is called likelihood weighting.

3335
05:09:13,262 --> 05:09:21,796
In likelihood weighting, we follow a slightly different procedure, and the goal is to avoid needing to throw out samples that didn't match the evidence.

3336
05:09:22,190 --> 05:09:26,864
And so what we'll do is we'll start by fixing the values for the evidence variables.

3337
05:09:27,024 --> 05:09:32,676
Rather than sample everything, we're going to fix the values of the evidence variables and not sample those.

3338
05:09:33,486 --> 05:09:42,852
Then we're going to sample all the other non-evidence variables in the same way, just using the Bayesian network, looking at the probability distributions, sampling all the non-evidence variables.

3339
05:09:43,630 --> 05:09:47,696
But then what we need to do is weight each sample by its likelihood.

3340
05:09:48,016 --> 05:09:55,696
If our evidence is really unlikely, we want to make sure that we've taken into account how likely was the evidence to actually show up in the sample.

3341
05:09:55,886 --> 05:10:02,336
If I have a sample where the evidence was much more likely to show up than another sample, then I want to weight the more likely one higher.

3342
05:10:02,702 --> 05:10:08,896
So we're going to weight each sample by its likelihood, where likelihood is just defined as the probability of all of the evidence.

3343
05:10:09,102 --> 05:10:13,732
Given all the evidence we have, what is the probability that it would happen in that particular sample?

3344
05:10:14,318 --> 05:10:20,324
So before, all of our samples were weighted equally, they all had a weight of one when we were calculating like the overall average.

3345
05:10:20,576 --> 05:10:27,908
In this case, we're going to weight each sample, multiply each sample by its likelihood in order to get the more accurate distribution.

3346
05:10:28,686 --> 05:10:29,696
So what would this look like?

3347
05:10:29,966 --> 05:10:34,948
Well, if I ask the same question, what is the probability of light rain given that the train is on time?

3348
05:10:35,214 --> 05:10:44,176
When I do the sampling procedure and start by trying to sample, I'm going to start by fixing the evidence variable. I'm already going to have in my sample the train is on time.

3349
05:10:44,336 --> 05:10:46,336
That way I don't have to throw out anything.

3350
05:10:46,446 --> 05:10:52,484
I'm only sampling the things where I know the value of the variables that are my evidence are what I expect them to be.

3351
05:10:53,198 --> 05:10:57,828
So I'll go ahead and sample from rain, and maybe this time I sample light rain instead of no rain.

3352
05:10:58,176 --> 05:11:01,412
then I'll sample from track maintenance and say maybe yes, there's track maintenance.

3353
05:11:01,776 --> 05:11:04,656
Then for train, well, I've already fixed it in place.

3354
05:11:04,896 --> 05:11:08,644
Train was an evidence variable, so I'm not going to bother sampling again.

3355
05:11:08,942 --> 05:11:10,176
I'll just go ahead and move on.

3356
05:11:10,446 --> 05:11:13,860
I'll move on to appointment and go ahead and sample from appointment as well.

3357
05:11:14,702 --> 05:11:16,260
So now I've generated a sample.

3358
05:11:16,622 --> 05:11:21,284
I've generated a sample by fixing this evidence variable and sampling the other three.

3359
05:11:21,966 --> 05:11:23,748
And the last step is now weighting the sample.

3360
05:11:24,096 --> 05:11:25,284
how much weight should it have?

3361
05:11:25,518 --> 05:11:33,616
And the weight is based on how probable is it that the train was actually on time, this evidence actually happened, given the values of these other variables,

3362
05:11:33,776 --> 05:11:36,388
light rain and the fact that yes, there was track maintenance.

3363
05:11:37,262 --> 05:11:46,532
Well, to do that, I can just go back to the train variable and say, all right, if there was light rain and track maintenance, the likelihood of my evidence, the likelihood that my train was on time,

3364
05:11:46,766 --> 05:11:47,776
is 0.6.

3365
05:11:48,174 --> 05:11:52,576
And so this train, uh this particular sample would have a weight of 0.6.

3366
05:11:52,816 --> 05:11:55,136
And I could repeat the sampling procedure again and again.

3367
05:11:55,342 --> 05:12:02,020
Each time every sample would be given a weight according to the probability of the evidence that I see associated with it.

3368
05:12:02,766 --> 05:12:11,876
And there are other sampling methods that exist as well, but all of them are designed to try and get it the same idea, to approximate the inference procedure of figuring out the value of a variable.

3369
05:12:13,102 --> 05:12:18,244
So we've now dealt with probability as it pertains to particular variables that have these discrete values.

3370
05:12:18,478 --> 05:12:22,564
But what we haven't really considered is how values might change over time.

3371
05:12:22,926 --> 05:12:28,644
That we've considered something like a variable for rain, where rain can take on values of none or light rain or heavy rain.

3372
05:12:28,910 --> 05:12:37,376
But in practice, usually when we consider values for variables like rain, we like to consider it for over time, how do the values of these variables change?

3373
05:12:37,582 --> 05:12:41,296
What do we do with when we're dealing with uncertainty over a period of time?

3374
05:12:41,550 --> 05:12:43,856
Which can come up in the context of weather, for example.

3375
05:12:44,110 --> 05:12:50,372
If I have sunny days and I have rainy days, and I'd like to know not just like what is the probability that it's raining now,

3376
05:12:51,022 --> 05:12:55,236
but what is the probability that it rains tomorrow, or the day after that, or the day after that.

3377
05:12:55,886 --> 05:12:58,660
And so to do this, we're going to introduce a slightly different kind of model.

3378
05:12:58,990 --> 05:13:05,296
But here we're going to have a random variable, not just one for the weather, but for every possible time step.

3379
05:13:05,614 --> 05:13:09,732
And you can define time step however you like. A simple way is just to use days as your time step.

3380
05:13:10,254 --> 05:13:16,016
And so we can define a variable called X sub t, which is going to be the weather at time t.

3381
05:13:16,302 --> 05:13:23,780
So x sub zero might be the weather on day zero, x sub one might be the weather on day one, so on and so forth, x sub two is the weather on day two.

3382
05:13:24,846 --> 05:13:30,500
But as you can imagine, if we start to do this over longer and longer periods of time, there's an incredible amount of data that might go into this.

3383
05:13:30,656 --> 05:13:36,164
If you're keeping track of data about the weather for a year, now suddenly you might be trying to predict the weather tomorrow,

3384
05:13:36,398 --> 05:13:42,976
given 365 days of previous pieces of evidence, and that's a lot of evidence to have to deal with and manipulate and calculate.

3385
05:13:43,216 --> 05:13:49,412
Probably nobody knows what the exact conditional probability distribution is for all of those combinations of variables.

3386
05:13:49,838 --> 05:13:55,456
And so when we're trying to do this inference inside of a computer, when we're trying to reasonably do this sort of analysis.

3387
05:13:56,206 --> 05:14:03,520
It's helpful to make some simplifying assumptions, some assumptions about the problem that we can just assume are true to make our lives a little bit easier.

3388
05:14:03,600 --> 05:14:09,280
Even if they're not totally accurate assumptions, if they're close to accurate or approximate, they're usually pretty good.

3389
05:14:09,454 --> 05:14:19,332
And the assumption we're going to make is called the Markov assumption, which is the assumption that the current state depends only on a finite fixed number of previous states.

3390
05:14:19,886 --> 05:14:29,156
So the current day's weather depends not on all the previous days' weather for all of history, but the current day's weather I can predict just based on yesterday's weather,

3391
05:14:29,454 --> 05:14:32,548
or just based on the last two days' weather, or the last three days weather.

3392
05:14:32,686 --> 05:14:38,788
But oftentimes we're going to deal with just the one previous state helps to predict this current state.

3393
05:14:39,694 --> 05:14:45,892
And by putting a whole bunch of these random variables together using this Markov assumption, we can create what's called a Markov chain,

3394
05:14:46,094 --> 05:14:53,120
where a Markov chain is just some sequence of random variables where each of the variables distribution follows that Markov assumption.

3395
05:14:53,390 --> 05:14:57,040
And so we'll do an example of this where the Markov assumption is I can predict the weather.

3396
05:14:57,200 --> 05:14:58,400
Is it sunny or rainy?

3397
05:14:58,720 --> 05:15:02,532
and we'll just consider those two possibilities for now, even though there are other types of weather.

3398
05:15:02,830 --> 05:15:06,116
But I can predict each day's weather just on the prior day's weather.

3399
05:15:06,400 --> 05:15:11,012
Using today's weather, I can come up with a probability distribution for tomorrow's weather.

3400
05:15:11,822 --> 05:15:13,200
And here's what this weather might look like.

3401
05:15:13,486 --> 05:15:18,884
It's formatted in terms of a matrix, as you might describe it, as sort of rows and columns of values.

3402
05:15:19,598 --> 05:15:24,800
Where on the left hand side, I have today's weather, represented by the variable x sub t.

3403
05:15:25,422 --> 05:15:34,052
And over here in the columns, I have tomorrow's weather, represented by the variable x sub t plus one, t plus one day's weather instead.

3404
05:15:34,382 --> 05:15:41,636
And what this matrix is saying is if today is sunny, well then, it's more likely than not that tomorrow is also sunny.

3405
05:15:42,080 --> 05:15:45,252
Oftentimes the weather stays consistent for multiple days in a row.

3406
05:15:45,486 --> 05:15:53,520
And for example, let's say that if today is sunny, our model says that tomorrow, with probability 0 eight, it will also be sunny, and with probability 0 two,

3407
05:15:53,680 --> 05:15:54,180
it will be raining.

3408
05:15:55,182 --> 05:15:58,212
And likewise, if today is raining, then

3409
05:15:58,894 --> 05:16:01,028
it's more likely than not that tomorrow is also raining.

3410
05:16:01,166 --> 05:16:03,076
With probability 0.7, it'll be raining.

3411
05:16:03,280 --> 05:16:05,828
With probability 0.3, it will be sunny.

3412
05:16:06,382 --> 05:16:13,668
So this matrix, this description of how it is we transition from one state to the next state, is what we're going to call the transition model.

3413
05:16:14,126 --> 05:16:23,600
And using the transition model, you can begin to construct this Markov chain by just predicting, given today's weather, what's the likelihood of tomorrow's weather happening?

3414
05:16:23,680 --> 05:16:33,440
And you can imagine doing a similar sampling procedure where you take this information, you sample what tomorrow's weather is going to be, using that you sample the next day's weather.

3415
05:16:33,600 --> 05:16:39,680
and the result of that is, you can form this Markov chain of like x zero, time at time. day zero is sunny.

3416
05:16:39,950 --> 05:16:43,588
the next day is sunny. maybe the next day it changes to raining, then raining, then raining.

3417
05:16:43,840 --> 05:16:53,120
And the pattern that this Markov chain follows, given the distribution that we had access to, this transition model here, is that when it's sunny, it tends to stay sunny for a little while.

3418
05:16:53,280 --> 05:16:58,852
The next couple of days tend to be sunny too, and when it's raining, it tends to be raining as well.

3419
05:16:59,310 --> 05:17:07,360
And so you get a Markov chain that looks like this, and you can do analysis on this. You can say, given that today is raining, what is the probability that tomorrow is raining?

3420
05:17:07,440 --> 05:17:16,240
Or you can begin to ask probability questions like what is the probability of this sequence of five values, sun, sun, rain, rain, rain, and answer those sorts of questions

3421
05:17:16,400 --> 05:17:16,720
too.

3422
05:17:17,102 --> 05:17:25,120
And it turns out there are again many Python libraries for interacting with models like this of probabilities that have distributions and random variables

3423
05:17:25,120 --> 05:17:28,580
that are based on previous variables according to this Markov assumption.

3424
05:17:29,294 --> 05:17:34,116
And pomegranate 2 has ways of dealing with these sorts of variables. So I'll go ahead and go into

3425
05:17:38,400 --> 05:17:41,540
the chain directory where I have some information about Markov chains.

3426
05:17:42,158 --> 05:17:44,452
And here I've defined a file called model.py.

3427
05:17:45,230 --> 05:17:53,828
Where I've defined in a very similar syntax, and again, the exact syntax doesn't matter so much as the idea that I'm encoding this information into a Python program

3428
05:17:54,094 --> 05:17:56,324
so that the program has access to these distributions.

3429
05:17:56,910 --> 05:18:04,400
I've here defined some starting distribution. So every Markov model begins at some point in time, and I need to give it some starting distribution.

3430
05:18:04,686 --> 05:18:08,880
And so we'll just say, you know, at the start, you can pick 50-50 between sunny and rainy.

3431
05:18:09,102 --> 05:18:12,324
We'll say it's sunny 50% of the time, rainy 50% of the time.

3432
05:18:12,942 --> 05:18:18,852
And then down below, I've here defined the transition model, how it is that I transition from one day to the next.

3433
05:18:19,246 --> 05:18:21,840
And here I've encoded that exact same matrix from before.

3434
05:18:22,158 --> 05:18:25,920
That if it was sunny today, then with probability 0.8, it will be sunny tomorrow.

3435
05:18:26,222 --> 05:18:28,720
And it'll be rainy tomorrow with probability 0.2.

3436
05:18:29,134 --> 05:18:33,348
And I likewise have another distribution for if it was raining today instead.

3437
05:18:34,350 --> 05:18:36,480
And so that alone defines the Markov model.

3438
05:18:36,640 --> 05:18:38,788
You can begin to answer questions using that model.

3439
05:18:39,054 --> 05:18:42,160
But one thing I'll just do is sample from the Markov chain.

3440
05:18:42,382 --> 05:18:51,396
And it turns out there is a method built into this Markov chain library that allows me to sample 50 states from the chain, basically just simulating like 50 instances of weather.

3441
05:18:52,590 --> 05:18:56,647
And so let me go ahead and run this python model.py.

3442
05:18:57,774 --> 05:19:06,000
And when I run it, what I get is that it's going to sample from this Markov chain 50 states, 50 days worth of weather that it's just going to randomly sample.

3443
05:19:06,126 --> 05:19:10,180
And you can imagine sampling many times to be able to get more data, to be able to do more analysis.

3444
05:19:10,480 --> 05:19:16,640
But here, for example, it's sunny two days in a row, rainy a whole bunch of days in a row before it changes back to sun.

3445
05:19:16,942 --> 05:19:21,200
And so you get this model that follows the distribution that we originally described.

3446
05:19:21,360 --> 05:19:28,388
That follows the distribution of sunny days tend to lead to more sunny days, rainy days tend to lead to more rainy days.

3447
05:19:29,358 --> 05:19:35,840
And that then is a Markov model. And Markov models rely on us knowing the values of these individual states.

3448
05:19:36,000 --> 05:19:43,492
I know that today is sunny or that today is rainy, and using that information, I can draw some sort of inference about what tomorrow is going to be like.

3449
05:19:44,334 --> 05:19:46,480
But in practice, this often isn't the case.

3450
05:19:46,640 --> 05:19:50,960
It often isn't the case that I know for certain what the exact state of the world is.

3451
05:19:51,214 --> 05:19:59,588
Oftentimes the state of the world is exactly unknown, but I'm able to somehow sense some information about that state, that a robot or an AI

3452
05:19:59,760 --> 05:20:03,280
doesn't have exact knowledge about the world around it, but it has some sort of sensor.

3453
05:20:03,440 --> 05:20:09,547
Whether that sensor is a camera or sensors that detect distance or just a microphone that is sensing audio, for example.

3454
05:20:09,547 --> 05:20:16,800
for example, it is sensing data and using that data, that data is somehow related to the state of the world, even if it doesn't actually know,

3455
05:20:16,960 --> 05:20:21,412
our AI doesn't know what the underlying true state of the world actually is.

3456
05:20:22,286 --> 05:20:31,280
And for that we need to get into the world of sensor models, the way of describing how it is that we translate what the hidden state, the underlying true state of the world

3457
05:20:31,520 --> 05:20:39,332
is, with what the observation, what it is that the AI knows or the AI has access to, actually is. And so for example,

3458
05:20:40,046 --> 05:20:42,320
a hidden state might be a robot's position.

3459
05:20:42,480 --> 05:20:49,440
If a robot is exploring new uncharted territory, the robot likely doesn't know exactly where it is, but it does have an observation.

3460
05:20:49,520 --> 05:20:58,564
It has robot sensor data where it can sense, how far away are possible obstacles around it, and using that information, using the observed information that it has,

3461
05:20:58,800 --> 05:21:01,840
it can infer something about the hidden state.

3462
05:21:01,920 --> 05:21:05,732
Because what the true hidden state is influences those observations.

3463
05:21:05,920 --> 05:21:16,640
Whatever the robot's true position is affects or has some effect upon what the sensor data the robot is able to collect is, even if the robot doesn't actually know for certain what its true

3464
05:21:16,960 --> 05:21:17,636
position is.

3465
05:21:18,638 --> 05:21:26,400
Likewise, if you think about a voice recognition or a speech recognition program that listens to you and is able to respond to you, something like Alexa

3466
05:21:27,120 --> 05:21:35,280
or what Apple and Google are doing with their voice recognition as well, that you might imagine that the hidden state, the underlying state, is what words are actually spoken.

3467
05:21:35,470 --> 05:21:45,380
The true nature of the world contains you saying a particular sequence of words, but your phone or your smart home device doesn't know for sure exactly what words you said.

3468
05:21:45,600 --> 05:21:50,276
The only observation that the AI has access to is some audio waveforms.

3469
05:21:50,624 --> 05:21:54,404
And those audio waveforms are, of course, dependent upon this hidden state.

3470
05:21:54,734 --> 05:21:59,396
And you can infer based on those audio waveforms what the words spoken likely were,

3471
05:22:00,142 --> 05:22:04,704
but you might not know with 100% certainty what that hidden state actually is.

3472
05:22:04,784 --> 05:22:12,964
And it might be a task to try and predict, given this observation, given these audio waveforms, can you figure out what the actual words spoken are?

3473
05:22:13,678 --> 05:22:20,224
Likewise, you might imagine on a website, true user engagement might be information you don't directly have access to, but you can observe data,

3474
05:22:20,384 --> 05:22:26,624
like website or app analytics, about how often was this button clicked or how often are people interacting with a page in a particular way,

3475
05:22:26,798 --> 05:22:30,340
and you can use that to infer things about your users as well.

3476
05:22:30,734 --> 05:22:40,132
So this type of problem comes up all the time when we're dealing with AI and trying to infer things about the world. That often AI doesn't really know the hidden true state of the world.

3477
05:22:40,366 --> 05:22:46,944
All the AI has access to is some observation that is related to the hidden true state, but it's not direct.

3478
05:22:47,104 --> 05:22:51,780
There might be some noise there. The audio waveform might have some additional noise that might be difficult to parse.

3479
05:22:51,984 --> 05:22:54,464
The sensor data might not be exactly correct.

3480
05:22:54,544 --> 05:23:01,124
There's some noise that might not allow you to conclude with certainty what the hidden state is, but can allow you to infer what it might be.

3481
05:23:01,806 --> 05:23:07,396
And so the simple example we'll take a look at here is imagining the hidden state as the weather, whether it's sunny or rainy or not.

3482
05:23:07,664 --> 05:23:14,144
And imagine you are programming an AI inside of a building that maybe has access to just a camera to inside the building,

3483
05:23:14,318 --> 05:23:23,140
and all you have access to is an observation as to whether or not employees are bringing an umbrella into the building or not. You can detect whether it's an umbrella or

3484
05:23:23,950 --> 05:23:30,144
not, and so you might have an observation as to whether or not an umbrella is brought into the building or not, and using that information,

3485
05:23:30,224 --> 05:23:35,504
you want to predict whether it's sunny or rainy, even if you don't know what the underlying weather is.

3486
05:23:35,584 --> 05:23:40,836
So the underlying weather might be sunny or rainy, and if it's raining, obviously people are more likely to bring an umbrella.

3487
05:23:41,070 --> 05:23:46,340
And so whether or not people bring an umbrella, your observation tells you something about the hidden state.

3488
05:23:46,510 --> 05:23:53,744
And of course, this is a bit of a contrived example, but the idea here is to think about this more broadly, in terms of more generally, any time you observe something,

3489
05:23:53,934 --> 05:23:57,124
it having to do with some underlying hidden state.

3490
05:23:57,838 --> 05:24:05,264
And so to try and model this type of idea where we have these hidden states and observations, rather than just use a Markov model, which has state, state, state, state,

3491
05:24:05,344 --> 05:24:12,004
each of which is connected by that transition matrix that we described before, we're going to use what we call a hidden Markov model.

3492
05:24:12,270 --> 05:24:22,788
Very similar to a Markov model, but this is going to allow us to model a system that has hidden states that we don't directly observe, along with some observed event that we do actually see.

3493
05:24:23,342 --> 05:24:31,620
And so, in addition to that transition model that we still need of saying, given the underlying state of the world, if it's sunny or rainy, what's the probability of tomorrow's weather?

3494
05:24:32,144 --> 05:24:40,100
We also need another model that, given some state, is going to give us an observation of like green, yes, someone brings an umbrella into the office,

3495
05:24:40,304 --> 05:24:43,300
or red, no, nobody brings umbrellas into the office.

3496
05:24:43,534 --> 05:24:44,964
And so the observation might be

3497
05:24:45,614 --> 05:24:53,604
that if it's sunny, then odds are nobody's gonna bring an umbrella to the office. But maybe some people are just being cautious and they do bring an umbrella to the office anyways.

3498
05:24:54,062 --> 05:25:03,264
And if it's raining, then with much higher probability, then people are going to bring umbrellas into the office, but maybe if the rain was unexpected, people didn't bring an umbrella, and so

3499
05:25:03,502 --> 05:25:05,252
it might have some other probability as well.

3500
05:25:05,550 --> 05:25:14,944
So using the observations, you can begin to predict with reasonable likelihood what the underlying state is, even if you don't actually get to observe the underlying state,

3501
05:25:15,118 --> 05:25:18,304
if you don't get to see what the hidden state is actually equal to.

3502
05:25:18,638 --> 05:25:26,304
This here we'll often call the sensor model. it's also often called the emission probabilities because the state, the underlying state, emits.

3503
05:25:26,862 --> 05:25:28,880
Some sort of emission that you then observe.

3504
05:25:29,134 --> 05:25:31,748
And so that can be another way of describing that same idea.

3505
05:25:32,814 --> 05:25:42,820
And the sensor Markov assumption that we're going to use is this assumption that the evidence variable, the thing we observe, the emission that gets produced, depends only on the corresponding state.

3506
05:25:43,118 --> 05:25:50,720
Meaning it can predict whether or not people will bring umbrellas or not, entirely dependent just on whether it is sunny or rainy today.

3507
05:25:50,894 --> 05:25:58,240
Of course, again, this assumption might not hold in practice, that in practice it might depend whether or not people bring umbrellas, might depend not just on today's weather,

3508
05:25:58,320 --> 05:26:00,292
but also on yesterday's weather and the day before.

3509
05:26:00,560 --> 05:26:09,124
But for simplification purposes, it can be helpful to apply this sort of assumption just to allow us to be able to reason about these probabilities a little more easily.

3510
05:26:09,614 --> 05:26:13,440
And if we're able to approximate it, we can still often get a very good

3511
05:26:13,440 --> 05:26:18,052
answer. And so what these hidden Markov models end up looking like is a little something like this.

3512
05:26:18,510 --> 05:26:30,400
Where now, rather than just have one chain of states like sun, sun, rain, rain, rain, we instead have this upper level, which is the underlying state of the world. Is it sunny or is it rainy?

3513
05:26:30,560 --> 05:26:33,988
And those are connected by that transition matrix we described before.

3514
05:26:34,350 --> 05:26:39,440
But each of these states produces an emission, produces an observation that I see.

3515
05:26:39,662 --> 05:26:42,820
That on this day it was sunny and people didn't bring umbrellas.

3516
05:26:43,150 --> 05:26:45,920
And on this day it was sunny, but people did bring umbrellas.

3517
05:26:46,030 --> 05:26:49,220
And on this day it was raining and people did bring umbrellas and so on and so forth.

3518
05:26:49,550 --> 05:27:00,320
And so each of these underlying states, represented by x sub t for x sub 1, 0, 1, 2, so on and so forth, produces some sort of observation or emission, which is what the E stands for,

3519
05:27:00,622 --> 05:27:04,516
E sub 0, E sub 1, E sub 2, so on and so forth.

3520
05:27:05,262 --> 05:27:08,480
And so this too is a way of trying to represent this idea.

3521
05:27:08,560 --> 05:27:15,200
And what you want to think about is that these underlying states are the true nature of the world, the robot's position as it moves over time,

3522
05:27:15,374 --> 05:27:25,520
and that produces some sort of sensor data that might be observed, or what people are actually saying, and using the emission data of what audio waveforms do you detect in order to process that data

3523
05:27:25,742 --> 05:27:26,820
and try and figure it out.

3524
05:27:27,118 --> 05:27:31,440
And there are a number of possible tasks that you might want to do given this kind of information.

3525
05:27:31,630 --> 05:27:38,724
And one of the simplest is trying to infer something about the future or the past or about these sort of hidden states that might exist.

3526
05:27:39,182 --> 05:27:46,480
And so the tasks that you'll often see, and we're not going to go into the mathematics of these tasks, but they're all based on the same idea of conditional probabilities

3527
05:27:46,640 --> 05:27:51,012
and using the probability distributions we have to draw these sorts of conclusions.

3528
05:27:51,758 --> 05:27:59,588
One task is called filtering, which is given observations from the start until now, calculate the distribution for the current state.

3529
05:27:59,918 --> 05:28:07,396
Meaning, given information about from the beginning of time until now, on which days do people bring an umbrella or not bring an umbrella,

3530
05:28:07,600 --> 05:28:12,676
can I calculate the probability of the current state, that today is it sunny or is it raining?

3531
05:28:13,120 --> 05:28:16,644
another task that might be possible is prediction, which is looking towards the future.

3532
05:28:17,006 --> 05:28:26,532
Given observations about people bringing umbrellas from the beginning of when we started counting time until now, can I figure out the distribution that tomorrow is it sunny or is it raining?

3533
05:28:27,374 --> 05:28:35,524
And you can also go backwards as well by a smoothing, where I can say, given observations from start until now, calculate the distributions for some past state.

3534
05:28:35,854 --> 05:28:40,228
Like I know that today people brought umbrellas, and tomorrow people brought umbrellas.

3535
05:28:40,494 --> 05:28:45,956
And so given two days worth of data of people bringing umbrellas, what's the probability that yesterday it was raining?

3536
05:28:46,318 --> 05:28:52,868
And that I know that people brought umbrellas today, that might inform that decision as well. It might influence those probabilities.

3537
05:28:53,326 --> 05:28:54,116
And there's also

3538
05:28:54,798 --> 05:29:02,960
a most likely explanation task, in addition to other tasks that might exist as well, which is combining some of these, given observations from the start up until now,

3539
05:29:03,214 --> 05:29:05,360
figuring out the most likely sequence of states.

3540
05:29:05,486 --> 05:29:07,120
And this is what we're going to take a look at now.

3541
05:29:07,280 --> 05:29:15,200
This idea that if I have all these observations, umbrella, no umbrella, umbrella, no umbrella, can I calculate the most likely states of sun, rain,

3542
05:29:15,280 --> 05:29:20,836
sun, rain, and whatnot that actually represented the true weather that would produce these observations?

3543
05:29:21,200 --> 05:29:29,280
And this is quite common when you're trying to do something like voice recognition, for example, that you have these emissions of these audio waveforms, and you would like to calculate,

3544
05:29:29,454 --> 05:29:39,600
based on all of the observations that you have, what is the most likely sequence of actual words or syllables or sounds that the user actually made when they were speaking to this particular device,

3545
05:29:39,760 --> 05:29:42,724
or other tasks that might come up in that context as well.

3546
05:29:43,438 --> 05:29:49,956
And so we can try this out by going ahead and going into the HMM directory, HMM for hidden Markov model.

3547
05:29:51,342 --> 05:29:52,036
And here,

3548
05:29:53,102 --> 05:29:54,564
what I've done is I've defined a model

3549
05:29:55,374 --> 05:30:04,900
where this model first defines my possible state sun and rain along with their emission probabilities, the observation model or the emission model.

3550
05:30:05,326 --> 05:30:14,432
Where here, given that I know that it's sunny, the probability that I see people bring an umbrella is 0.2, the probability of no umbrella is 0.8.

3551
05:30:15,054 --> 05:30:21,872
And likewise, if it's raining, then people are more likely to bring an umbrella. Umbrella has probability 0.9, no umbrella has probability 0.1.

3552
05:30:22,254 --> 05:30:29,872
So the actual underlying hidden states, those states are sun and rain, but the things that I observe, the observations that I can see,

3553
05:30:30,094 --> 05:30:34,500
are either umbrella or no umbrella, as the things that I observe as a result.

3554
05:30:35,726 --> 05:30:45,072
So this then, I also need to add to it a transition matrix, same as before, saying that if today is sunny, then tomorrow is more likely to be sunny, and if today is rainy,

3555
05:30:45,294 --> 05:30:47,108
then tomorrow is more likely to be raining.

3556
05:30:47,406 --> 05:30:56,740
As with before, I give it some starting probabilities saying, at first, 50-50 chance for whether it's sunny or rainy, and then I can create the model based on that information.

3557
05:30:57,038 --> 05:31:04,004
Again, the exact syntax of this is not so important, so much as it is the data that I am now encoding into a program, such that now

3558
05:31:04,878 --> 05:31:06,116
I can begin to do some inference.

3559
05:31:06,926 --> 05:31:10,276
So I can give my program, for example, a list of observations.

3560
05:31:10,912 --> 05:31:15,140
umbrella, umbrella, no umbrella, umbrella, umbrella, so on and so forth, no umbrella, no umbrella.

3561
05:31:15,470 --> 05:31:20,592
And I would like to calculate, I would like to figure out the most likely explanation for these observations.

3562
05:31:20,752 --> 05:31:21,552
What is likely?

3563
05:31:21,712 --> 05:31:28,112
Is is was it rain, rain, is this rain, or is it more likely that this was actually sunny and then it switched back to it being rainy?

3564
05:31:28,366 --> 05:31:36,644
And you know, that's an interesting question. We might not be sure, because it might just be that it just so happened on this rainy day, people decided not to bring an umbrella.

3565
05:31:36,878 --> 05:31:43,300
Or it could be that it switched from rainy to sunny back to rainy, which doesn't seem too likely, but it certainly could happen.

3566
05:31:44,014 --> 05:31:49,668
And using the data we give to the hidden Markov model, our model can begin to predict these answers, can begin to figure it out.

3567
05:31:50,286 --> 05:31:56,752
So we're going to go ahead and just predict these observations, and then for each of those predictions, go ahead and print out what the prediction is.

3568
05:31:56,832 --> 05:32:01,988
And this library just so happens to have a function called predict that does this prediction process for me.

3569
05:32:02,958 --> 05:32:05,252
So I run python sequence.py,

3570
05:32:06,222 --> 05:32:07,780
and the result I get is this.

3571
05:32:07,952 --> 05:32:14,192
This is the prediction based on the observations of what all of those states are likely to be, and it's likely to be rain, then rain.

3572
05:32:14,352 --> 05:32:19,392
In this case it thinks that what most likely happened is that it was sunny for a day and then went back to being rainy.

3573
05:32:19,566 --> 05:32:27,552
But in different situations, if it was rainy for longer, maybe, or if the probabilities were slightly different, you might imagine that it's more likely that it was rainy all the way through,

3574
05:32:27,790 --> 05:32:31,972
and it just so happened on one rainy day, people decided not to bring umbrellas.

3575
05:32:32,718 --> 05:32:37,988
And so here too, Python libraries can begin to allow for this sort of inference procedure.

3576
05:32:38,222 --> 05:32:46,352
And by taking what we know, and by putting it in terms of these tasks that already exist, these general tasks that work with hidden Markov models, then any

3577
05:32:46,512 --> 05:32:55,108
time we can take an idea and formulate it as a hidden Markov model, formulate it as something that has hidden states and observed emissions that result from those states.

3578
05:32:55,312 --> 05:33:00,548
then we can take advantage of these algorithms that are known to exist for trying to do this sort of inference.

3579
05:33:01,294 --> 05:33:05,124
So now we've seen a couple of ways that AI can begin to deal with uncertainty.

3580
05:33:05,326 --> 05:33:14,352
We've taken a look at probability and how we can use probability to describe numerically things that are likely or more likely or less likely to happen than other events or other variables.

3581
05:33:14,606 --> 05:33:22,112
And using that information, we can begin to construct these standard types of models, things like Bayesian networks and Markov chains and hidden Markov

3582
05:33:22,352 --> 05:33:30,660
models that all allow us to be able to describe how particular events relate to other events or how the values of particular variables relate to other variables,

3583
05:33:30,832 --> 05:33:33,952
not for certain, but with some sort of probability distribution.

3584
05:33:34,158 --> 05:33:41,604
And by formulating things in terms of these models that already exist, we can take advantage of Python libraries that implement these sort of models already

3585
05:33:41,742 --> 05:33:46,244
and allow us just to be able to use them to produce some sort of resulting effect.

3586
05:33:46,510 --> 05:33:56,132
So all of this then allows our AI to begin to deal with these sort of uncertain problems, so that our AI doesn't need to know things for certain, but can infer based on information it doesn't know.

3587
05:33:56,654 --> 05:34:05,472
Next time, we'll take a look at additional types of problems that we can solve by taking advantage of AI-related algorithms even beyond the world of the types of problems we've already explored.

3588
05:34:05,742 --> 05:34:06,564
We'll see you next time.

3589
05:34:26,926 --> 05:34:30,816
Okay, welcome back everyone to an introduction to artificial intelligence with Python.

3590
05:34:31,054 --> 05:34:34,244
And now so far we've taken a look at a couple of different types of problems.

3591
05:34:34,606 --> 05:34:40,336
We've seen classical search problems where we're trying to get from an initial state to a goal by figuring out some optimal path.

3592
05:34:40,558 --> 05:34:45,136
We've taken a look at adversarial search, where we have a game playing agent that is trying to make the best move.

3593
05:34:45,390 --> 05:34:51,536
We've seen knowledge-based problems where we're trying to use logic and inference to be able to figure out and draw some additional conclusions.

3594
05:34:51,790 --> 05:34:56,096
And we've seen some probabilistic models as well, where we might not have certain information about the world,

3595
05:34:56,302 --> 05:35:00,900
but we want to use the knowledge about probabilities that we do have to be able to draw some conclusions.

3596
05:35:01,518 --> 05:35:06,404
Today we're going to turn our attention to another category of problems, generally known as optimization problems,

3597
05:35:06,862 --> 05:35:11,940
where optimization is really all about choosing the best option from a set of possible options.

3598
05:35:12,366 --> 05:35:19,456
And we've already seen optimization in some contexts, like game playing, where we're trying to create an AI that chooses the best move out of a set of possible moves.

3599
05:35:19,758 --> 05:35:29,124
But what we'll take a look at today is a category of types of problems and algorithms to solve them that can be used in order to deal with a broader range of potential optimization problems.

3600
05:35:29,806 --> 05:35:33,700
And the first of the algorithms that we'll take a look at is known as a local search.

3601
05:35:34,190 --> 05:35:42,016
And local search differs from search algorithms we've seen before in the sense that the search algorithms we've looked at so far, which are things like breadth first search or A star

3602
05:35:42,256 --> 05:35:49,732
search, for example, generally maintain a whole bunch of different paths that we're simultaneously exploring, and we're looking at a bunch of different paths at once,

3603
05:35:49,966 --> 05:35:51,492
trying to find our way to the solution.

3604
05:35:51,854 --> 05:35:58,788
On the other hand, in local search, this is going to be a search algorithm that's really just going to maintain a single node, looking at a single state,

3605
05:35:59,182 --> 05:36:06,916
and we'll generally run this algorithm by maintaining that single node and then moving ourselves to one of the neighboring nodes throughout this search process.

3606
05:36:07,376 --> 05:36:15,296
And this is generally useful in contexts not like these problems, which we've seen before, like a maze solving situation where we're trying to find our way from the initial

3607
05:36:15,310 --> 05:36:17,252
state to the goal by following some path.

3608
05:36:17,646 --> 05:36:27,376
But local search is most applicable when we really don't care about the path at all, and all we care about is what the solution is. And in the case of solving a maze, the solution was always obvious.

3609
05:36:27,456 --> 05:36:32,896
You could point to the solution, you know exactly what the goal is, and the real question is, what is the path to get there?

3610
05:36:33,166 --> 05:36:41,284
But local search is going to come up in cases where figuring out exactly what the solution is, exactly what the goal looks like, is actually the heart of the challenge.

3611
05:36:41,870 --> 05:36:47,296
And to give an example of one of these kinds of problems, we'll consider a scenario where we have two types of buildings, for example.

3612
05:36:47,536 --> 05:36:56,196
We have houses and hospitals, and our goal might be in a world that's formatted as this grid, where we have a whole bunch of houses, a house here, a house here, two houses over there.

3613
05:36:56,496 --> 05:37:00,976
Maybe we want to try and find a way to place two hospitals on this map.

3614
05:37:01,262 --> 05:37:03,620
So maybe a hospital here and a hospital there.

3615
05:37:04,078 --> 05:37:09,776
And the problem now is we want to place two hospitals on the map, but we want to do so with some sort of objective.

3616
05:37:09,936 --> 05:37:16,164
And our objective in this case is to try and minimize the distance of any of the houses from a hospital.

3617
05:37:16,686 --> 05:37:20,256
So you might imagine, all right, what's the distance from each of the houses to their nearest hospital?

3618
05:37:20,416 --> 05:37:26,564
There are a number of ways we could calculate that distance, but one way is using a heuristic we've looked at before, which is the Manhattan distance,

3619
05:37:26,896 --> 05:37:33,892
this idea of how many rows and columns would you have to move inside of this grid layout in order to get to a hospital, for example.

3620
05:37:34,414 --> 05:37:39,376
And it turns out if you take each of these four houses and figure out, all right, how close are they to their nearest hospital,

3621
05:37:39,566 --> 05:37:47,844
you get something like this, where this house is three away from a hospital, this house is six away, these two houses are each four away, and if you add all those numbers up together,

3622
05:37:48,016 --> 05:37:51,204
you get a total cost of 17, for example.

3623
05:37:51,790 --> 05:37:59,364
So for this particular configuration of hospitals, a hospital here and a hospital there, that state, we might say has a cost of 17.

3624
05:37:59,886 --> 05:38:08,260
And the goal of this problem now that we would like to apply a search algorithm to figure out is can you solve this problem to find a way to minimize that cost?

3625
05:38:08,576 --> 05:38:16,004
Minimize the total amount if you sum up all of the distances from all the houses to the nearest hospital, how can we minimize that final value?

3626
05:38:16,974 --> 05:38:23,652
And if we think about this problem a little bit more abstractly, abstracting away from this specific problem and thinking more generally about problems like it,

3627
05:38:23,856 --> 05:38:29,316
you can often formulate these problems by thinking about them as a state space landscape, as we'll soon call it.

3628
05:38:29,710 --> 05:38:36,896
Here in this diagram of a state space landscape, each of these vertical bars represents a particular state that our world could be in.

3629
05:38:37,056 --> 05:38:42,336
So for example, each of these vertical bars represents a particular configuration of two hospitals.

3630
05:38:43,118 --> 05:38:49,924
And the height of this vertical bar is generally going to represent some function of that state, some value of that state.

3631
05:38:50,158 --> 05:38:56,896
So maybe in this case, the height of the vertical bar represents what is the cost of this particular configuration of hospitals

3632
05:38:57,056 --> 05:39:02,468
in terms of what is the sum total of all of the distances from all of the houses to their nearest hospital.

3633
05:39:03,246 --> 05:39:08,004
And generally speaking, when we have a state space landscape, we want to do one of two things.

3634
05:39:08,526 --> 05:39:16,004
We might be trying to maximize the value of this function, trying to find a global maximum, so to speak, of this state space landscape.

3635
05:39:16,398 --> 05:39:21,636
A single state whose value is higher than all of the other states that we could possibly choose from.

3636
05:39:21,998 --> 05:39:29,124
And generally in this case, when we're trying to find a global maximum, we'll call the function that we're trying to optimize some objective function.

3637
05:39:29,376 --> 05:39:33,700
Some function that measures for any given state how good is that state.

3638
05:39:34,094 --> 05:39:39,140
Such that we can take any state, pass it into the objective function, and get a value for how good that state is.

3639
05:39:39,598 --> 05:39:45,828
And ultimately, what our goal is is to find one of these states that has the highest possible value for that objective function.

3640
05:39:46,798 --> 05:39:53,924
An equivalent but reversed problem is the problem of finding a global minimum, some state that has a value after you pass it into this function

3641
05:39:54,094 --> 05:39:57,540
that is lower than all of the other possible values that we might choose from.

3642
05:39:57,934 --> 05:40:03,364
And generally speaking, when we're trying to find a global minimum, we call the function that we're calculating a cost function.

3643
05:40:03,726 --> 05:40:11,856
Generally, each state has some sort of cost, whether that cost is a monetary cost or a time cost, or in the case of the houses and hospitals we've been looking at just now,

3644
05:40:12,096 --> 05:40:16,324
a distance cost in terms of how far away each of the houses is from a hospital.

3645
05:40:16,942 --> 05:40:22,724
And we're trying to minimize the cost, find the state that has the lowest possible value of that cost.

3646
05:40:23,566 --> 05:40:31,936
So these are the general types of ideas that we might be trying to go for within a state space landscape, trying to find a global maximum or trying to find a global minimum.

3647
05:40:32,174 --> 05:40:33,536
And how exactly do we do that?

3648
05:40:33,902 --> 05:40:43,296
We'll recall that in local search, we generally operate this algorithm by maintaining just a single state, just some current state represented inside of some node, maybe inside of a data structure,

3649
05:40:43,534 --> 05:40:45,700
where we're keeping track of where we are currently.

3650
05:40:46,222 --> 05:40:51,332
And then ultimately what we're going to do is from that state, move to one of its neighbor states.

3651
05:40:51,662 --> 05:40:56,868
So in this case, represented in this one-dimensional space by just the state immediately to the left or to the right of it.

3652
05:40:57,134 --> 05:41:01,924
But for any different problem, you might define what it means for there to be a neighbor of a particular state.

3653
05:41:02,190 --> 05:41:10,148
In the case of a hospitals, for example, that we were just looking at, a neighbor might be moving one hospital, one space to the left or to the right or up or down.

3654
05:41:10,336 --> 05:41:20,896
Some state that is close to our current state, but slightly different, and as a result, might have a slightly different value in terms of its objective function or in terms of its cost function.

3655
05:41:21,582 --> 05:41:26,980
So this is going to be our general strategy in local search, to be able to take a state, maintaining some current node,

3656
05:41:27,136 --> 05:41:33,476
and move where we're looking at in the state space landscape in order to try to find a global maximum or a global minimum somehow.

3657
05:41:33,742 --> 05:41:40,484
And perhaps the simplest of algorithms that we could use to implement this idea of local search is an algorithm known as hill climbing.

3658
05:41:41,070 --> 05:41:46,496
And the basic idea of hill climbing is let's say I'm trying to maximize the value of my state.

3659
05:41:46,670 --> 05:41:48,816
I'm trying to figure out where the global maximum is.

3660
05:41:49,102 --> 05:41:55,492
I'm going to start at a state, and generally what hill climbing is going to do is it's going to consider the neighbors of that state.

3661
05:41:55,886 --> 05:42:01,536
That from this state, you know, all right, I could go left or I could go right, and this neighbor happens to be higher, and this neighbor happens to be lower.

3662
05:42:01,838 --> 05:42:06,788
And in hill climbing, if I'm trying to maximize the value, I'll generally pick the highest one I can.

3663
05:42:07,086 --> 05:42:13,348
Between the state to the left and right of me, this one is higher. So I'll go ahead and move myself to consider that state instead.

3664
05:42:13,806 --> 05:42:21,604
And then I'll repeat this process, continually looking at all of my neighbors and picking the highest neighbor, doing the same thing, looking at my neighbors picking the highest of my neighbors,

3665
05:42:21,856 --> 05:42:28,896
until I get to a point like right here, where I consider both of my neighbors, and both of my neighbors have a lower value than I do.

3666
05:42:29,056 --> 05:42:32,740
This current state has a value that is higher than any of its neighbors.

3667
05:42:33,006 --> 05:42:37,572
And at that point the algorithm terminates and I can say, all right, here I have now found the solution.

3668
05:42:38,254 --> 05:42:43,776
And the same thing works in exactly the opposite way for trying to find a global minimum, but the algorithm is fundamentally the same.

3669
05:42:44,078 --> 05:42:52,900
If I'm trying to find a global minimum and say my current state starts here, I'll continually look at my neighbors, pick the lowest value that I possibly can, until I eventually, hopefully,

3670
05:42:53,216 --> 05:42:54,576
find that global minimum.

3671
05:42:54,736 --> 05:43:02,736
A point at which when I look at both of my neighbors, they each have a higher value, and I'm trying to minimize the total score or cost or value that I

3672
05:43:02,736 --> 05:43:05,508
get as a result of calculating some sort of cost function.

3673
05:43:06,830 --> 05:43:09,952
So we can formulate this graphical idea in terms of pseudocode.

3674
05:43:10,126 --> 05:43:12,036
And the pseudocode for hillclimbing might look like this.

3675
05:43:12,462 --> 05:43:17,316
We define some function called hillclimb that takes as input the problem that we're trying to solve.

3676
05:43:17,742 --> 05:43:20,912
And generally we're going to start in some sort of initial state.

3677
05:43:21,230 --> 05:43:27,492
So I'll start with a variable called current that is keeping track of my initial state, like an initial configuration of hospitals.

3678
05:43:27,918 --> 05:43:31,588
And maybe some problems lend themselves to an initial state, some place where you begin.

3679
05:43:31,792 --> 05:43:39,792
In other cases, maybe not, in which case we might just randomly generate some initial state just by choosing two locations for hospitals at random, for example,

3680
05:43:39,982 --> 05:43:42,436
and figuring out from there how we might be able to improve.

3681
05:43:42,798 --> 05:43:45,572
But that initial state, we're going to store inside of current.

3682
05:43:46,190 --> 05:43:51,492
And now here comes our loop, some repetitive process we're going to do again and again until the algorithm terminates.

3683
05:43:52,014 --> 05:43:57,792
And what we're going to do is first say, let's figure out all of the neighbors of the current state.

3684
05:43:57,966 --> 05:44:02,276
From my state, what are all of the neighboring states for some definition of what it means to be a neighbor?

3685
05:44:02,766 --> 05:44:08,832
And I'll go ahead and choose the highest valued of all of those neighbors and save it inside of this variable called neighbor.

3686
05:44:09,262 --> 05:44:10,916
So we'll keep track of the highest valued neighbor.

3687
05:44:11,152 --> 05:44:18,672
This is in the case where I'm trying to maximize the value. In the case where I'm trying to minimize the value, you might imagine here you'll pick the neighbor with the lowest possible value.

3688
05:44:18,894 --> 05:44:21,252
But these ideas are really fundamentally interchangeable.

3689
05:44:21,582 --> 05:44:29,092
And it's possible in some cases, there might be multiple neighbors that each have an equally high value or an equally low value in the minimizing case.

3690
05:44:29,454 --> 05:44:31,632
And in that case, we can just choose randomly from among them.

3691
05:44:31,918 --> 05:44:34,660
Just choose one of them and save it inside of this variable neighbor.

3692
05:44:35,406 --> 05:44:40,580
And then the key question to ask is, is this neighbor better than my current state?

3693
05:44:41,518 --> 05:44:46,660
And if the neighbor, the best neighbor that I was able to find, is not better than my current state,

3694
05:44:47,374 --> 05:44:48,548
well then the algorithm is over.

3695
05:44:48,782 --> 05:44:50,432
And I'll just go ahead and return the current state.

3696
05:44:50,512 --> 05:44:55,844
If none of my neighbors are better, then I may as well stay where I am, is the general logic of the hill climbing algorithm.

3697
05:44:56,526 --> 05:45:00,112
But otherwise, if the neighbor is better, then I may as well move to that neighbor.

3698
05:45:00,334 --> 05:45:08,112
So you might imagine setting current equal to neighbor, where the general idea is if I'm at a current state and I see a neighbor that is better than me, then I'll go ahead and move there.

3699
05:45:08,206 --> 05:45:15,472
And then I'll repeat the process, continually moving to a better neighbor, until I reach a point at which none of my neighbors are better than I am,

3700
05:45:15,726 --> 05:45:18,660
and at that point, we'd say the algorithm can just terminate there.

3701
05:45:19,630 --> 05:45:22,980
So let's take a look at a real example of this with these houses and hospitals.

3702
05:45:23,278 --> 05:45:28,068
So we've seen now that if we put the hospitals in these two locations, that has a total cost of 17.

3703
05:45:28,302 --> 05:45:36,740
And now we need to define, if we're going to implement this hill climbing algorithm, what it means to take this particular configuration of hospitals, this particular state,

3704
05:45:37,134 --> 05:45:39,172
and get a neighbor of that state.

3705
05:45:39,726 --> 05:45:48,112
And a simple definition of neighbor might be just let's pick one of the hospitals and move it by one square, the left or right or up or down, for example.

3706
05:45:48,398 --> 05:45:52,192
And that would mean we have six possible neighbors from this particular configuration.

3707
05:45:52,352 --> 05:45:59,716
We could take this hospital and move it to any of these three possible squares, or we take this hospital and move it to any of those three possible squares,

3708
05:45:59,952 --> 05:46:01,860
and each of those would generate a neighbor.

3709
05:46:02,574 --> 05:46:08,804
And what I might do is say, all right, here is the locations and the distances between each of the houses and their nearest hospital.

3710
05:46:09,230 --> 05:46:14,500
Let me consider all of the neighbors and see if any of them can do better than a cost of 17.

