3717
05:46:51,150 --> 05:46:56,260
The real idea, the goal should be to be able to take this hospital and move it one square to the left.

3718
05:46:56,718 --> 05:47:03,312
By moving it one square to the left, we move it closer to both of these houses on the right without changing anything about the houses on the left.

3719
05:47:03,392 --> 05:47:06,404
For them, this hospital is still the closer one, so they aren't affected.

3720
05:47:06,734 --> 05:47:12,708
So we're able to improve the situation by picking a neighbor that results in a decrease in our total cost.

3721
05:47:12,942 --> 05:47:13,764
And so we might do that.

3722
05:47:13,952 --> 05:47:18,660
Move ourselves from this current state to a neighbor by just taking that hospital and moving it.

3723
05:47:19,726 --> 05:47:27,552
And at this point, there's not a whole lot that can be done with this hospital, but there's still other optimizations we can make, other neighbors we can move to that are going to have a better value.

3724
05:47:27,854 --> 05:47:34,512
If we consider this hospital, for example, we might imagine that right now it's a bit far up, that both of these houses are a little bit lower.

3725
05:47:34,734 --> 05:47:38,832
So we might be able to do better by taking this hospital and moving it one square down.

3726
05:47:39,072 --> 05:47:44,992
Moving it down so that now instead of a cost of 15, we're down to a cost of 13 for this particular configuration.

3727
05:47:45,262 --> 05:47:49,156
And we can do even better by taking the hospital and moving it one square to the left.

3728
05:47:49,454 --> 05:47:52,228
Now instead of a cost of 13, we have a cost of 11.

3729
05:47:52,352 --> 05:47:59,152
Because this house is one away from the hospital, this one is four away, this one is three away, and this one is also three away.

3730
05:47:59,470 --> 05:48:07,332
So we've been able to do much better than that initial cost that we had using the initial configuration, just by taking every state and asking ourselves the question:

3731
05:48:07,712 --> 05:48:10,948
can we do better by just making small incremental changes?

3732
05:48:11,152 --> 05:48:14,148
Moving to a neighbor, moving to a neighbor, and moving to a neighbor after that.

3733
05:48:15,310 --> 05:48:20,100
And now at this point, we can potentially see that at this point the algorithm is going to terminate.

3734
05:48:20,352 --> 05:48:26,992
There's actually no neighbor we can move to that is going to improve the situation, get us a cost that is less than 11.

3735
05:48:27,086 --> 05:48:30,980
Because if we take this hospital and move it up or to the right, well, that's going to make it further away.

3736
05:48:31,246 --> 05:48:36,932
If we take it and move it down, that doesn't really change the situation. It gets further away from this house but closer to that house.

3737
05:48:37,262 --> 05:48:39,952
And likewise, the same story was true for this hospital.

3738
05:48:40,112 --> 05:48:49,988
Any neighbor we move it to, up, left, down, or right, is either going to make it further away from the houses and increase the cost, or it's going to have no effect on the cost whatsoever.

3739
05:48:51,022 --> 05:48:54,180
And so the question we might now ask is, is this the best we could do?

3740
05:48:54,352 --> 05:48:57,284
Is this the best placement of the hospitals we could possibly have?

3741
05:48:57,806 --> 05:49:02,352
And it turns out the answer is no, because there's a better way that we could place these hospitals.

3742
05:49:02,670 --> 05:49:09,472
And in particular, there are a number of ways you could do this, but one of the ways is by taking this hospital here and moving it to this square, for example.

3743
05:49:09,632 --> 05:49:15,236
Moving it diagonally by one square, which was not part of our definition of neighbor. We could only move left, right, up, or down.

3744
05:49:15,726 --> 05:49:18,660
But this is in fact better, it has a total cost of nine.

3745
05:49:18,752 --> 05:49:23,428
It is now closer to both of these houses, and as a result, the total cost is less.

3746
05:49:24,238 --> 05:49:32,900
But we weren't able to find it, because in order to get there, we had to go through a state that actually wasn't any better than the current state that we had been on previously.

3747
05:49:33,550 --> 05:49:42,596
And so this appears to be a limitation or a concern you might have as you go about trying to implement a hill climbing algorithm, is that it might not always give you the optimal solution.

3748
05:49:43,150 --> 05:49:52,912
If we're trying to maximize the value of any particular state, we're trying to find the global maximum, a concern might be that we could get stuck at one of the local maxima

3749
05:49:53,072 --> 05:49:59,268
highlighted here in blue, where a local maxima is any state whose value is higher than any of its neighbors.

3750
05:49:59,392 --> 05:50:05,712
If we ever find ourselves at one of these two states when we're trying to maximize the value of the state, we're not going to make any changes.

3751
05:50:05,806 --> 05:50:13,232
We're not going to move left or right, we're not going to move left here, because those states are worse, but yet we haven't found the global optimum.

3752
05:50:13,312 --> 05:50:15,140
We haven't done as best as we could do.

3753
05:50:15,726 --> 05:50:22,308
And likewise, in the case of the hospitals, what we're ultimately trying to do is find a global minimum, find a value that is lower than all of the others,

3754
05:50:22,702 --> 05:50:32,804
but we have the potential to get stuck at one of the local minima, any of these states whose value is lower than all of its neighbors, but still not as low as the local minima.

3755
05:50:33,614 --> 05:50:42,192
And so the takeaway here is that it's not always going to be the case that when we run this naive hill climbing algorithm, that we're always going to find the optimal solution.

3756
05:50:42,352 --> 05:50:43,620
There are things that could go wrong.

3757
05:50:43,918 --> 05:50:52,164
If we started here, for example, and tried to maximize our value as much as possible, we might move to the highest possible neighbor, move to the highest possible neighbor,

3758
05:50:52,352 --> 05:50:59,716
move to the highest possible neighbor, and stop and never realize that there's actually a better state way over there that we could have gone to instead.

3759
05:51:00,334 --> 05:51:06,436
And other problems you might imagine, just by taking a look at this state space landscape, are these various different types of plateaus.

3760
05:51:06,766 --> 05:51:12,612
Something like this flat local maximum here, where all six of these states each have the exact same value.

3761
05:51:12,832 --> 05:51:19,552
And so, in the case of the algorithm we showed before, none of the neighbors are better, so we might just get stuck at this flat local maximum.

3762
05:51:19,712 --> 05:51:26,832
And even if you allowed yourself to move to one of the neighbors, it wouldn't be clear which neighbor you would ultimately move to, and you could get stuck here as well.

3763
05:51:27,246 --> 05:51:28,352
And there's another one over here.

3764
05:51:28,590 --> 05:51:34,992
This one is called a shoulder. It's not really a local maximum because there's still places where we can go higher, not a local minimum because we can go lower.

3765
05:51:35,246 --> 05:51:42,592
So we can still make progress, but it's still this flat area where if you have a local search algorithm, there's potential to get lost here,

3766
05:51:42,798 --> 05:51:47,780
unable to make some upward or downward progress depending on whether we're trying to maximize or minimize,

3767
05:51:48,014 --> 05:51:54,180
and therefore another potential for us to be able to find a solution that might not actually be the optimal solution.

3768
05:51:54,926 --> 05:52:00,164
And so, because of this potential, the potential that hill climbing has to not always find us the optimal result,

3769
05:52:00,464 --> 05:52:08,452
it turns out there are a number of different varieties and variations on the hill climbing algorithm that help to solve the problem better depending on the context.

3770
05:52:08,544 --> 05:52:12,864
And depending on the specific type of problem, some of these variants might be more applicable than others.

3771
05:52:13,198 --> 05:52:18,820
What we've taken a look at so far is a version of hill climbing generally called steepest ascent hill climbing,

3772
05:52:19,246 --> 05:52:27,104
where the idea of steepest ascent hill climbing is we are going to choose the highest valued neighbor in the case where we're trying to maximize or the lowest valued neighbor

3773
05:52:27,278 --> 05:52:28,624
in cases where we're trying to minimize.

3774
05:52:28,784 --> 05:52:35,584
But generally speaking, if I have five neighbors and they're all better than my current state, I will pick the best one of those five.

3775
05:52:35,950 --> 05:52:41,828
Now, sometimes that might work pretty well. It's sort of a greedy approach of trying to take the best operation at any particular time step.

3776
05:52:42,254 --> 05:52:43,264
But it might not always work.

3777
05:52:43,344 --> 05:52:51,812
There might be cases where actually I want to choose an option that is slightly better than me, but maybe not the best one, because that later on might lead to a better outcome ultimately.

3778
05:52:52,078 --> 05:52:55,780
So there are other variants that we might consider of this basic hill climbing algorithm.

3779
05:52:56,430 --> 05:52:58,244
One is known as stochastic hill climbing.

3780
05:52:58,510 --> 05:53:02,064
And in this case, we choose randomly from all of our higher value neighbors.

3781
05:53:02,222 --> 05:53:08,932
So if I'm at my current state and there are five neighbors that are all better than I am, rather than choosing the best one, as steep as descent would do,

3782
05:53:09,230 --> 05:53:15,844
stochastic will just choose randomly from one of them, thinking that if it's better, then it's better, and maybe there's a potential to make forward progress,

3783
05:53:16,078 --> 05:53:19,584
even if it is not locally the best option I could possibly

3784
05:53:19,584 --> 05:53:26,864
choose. First choice hill climbing ends up just choosing the very first highest valued neighbor that it follows, behaving on a similar idea.

3785
05:53:27,054 --> 05:53:32,704
Rather than consider all of the neighbors, as soon as we find a neighbor that is better than our current state, we'll go ahead and move there.

3786
05:53:33,006 --> 05:53:39,556
So maybe some efficiency improvements there, and maybe has the potential to find a solution that the other strategies weren't able to find.

3787
05:53:39,886 --> 05:53:43,620
And with all of these variants, we still suffer from the same potential risk.

3788
05:53:43,824 --> 05:53:47,620
This risk that we might end up at a local minimum or a local maximum.

3789
05:53:48,078 --> 05:53:51,556
And we can reduce that risk by repeating the process multiple times.

3790
05:53:52,078 --> 05:53:59,396
So one variant of hill climbing is random restart hill climbing, where the general idea is we'll conduct hill climbing multiple times.

3791
05:53:59,822 --> 05:54:05,744
If we apply steepest ascent hill climbing, for example, we'll start at some random state, try and figure out how to solve the problem,

3792
05:54:05,824 --> 05:54:11,584
and figure out what is the local maximum or local minimum we get to, and then we'll just randomly restart and try again.

3793
05:54:11,744 --> 05:54:19,744
Choose a new starting configuration, try and figure out what the local maximum or minimum is, and do this some number of times, and then after we've done it some number of times,

3794
05:54:19,918 --> 05:54:22,788
we can pick the best one out of all of the ones that we've taken a look at.

3795
05:54:23,310 --> 05:54:25,956
So there's another option we have access to as well.

3796
05:54:26,542 --> 05:54:32,932
And then, although I said that generally local search will usually just keep track of a single node and then move to one of its neighbors,

3797
05:54:33,166 --> 05:54:41,184
there are variants of hill climbing that are known as local beam searches, where rather than keep track of just one current best state, we're keeping track of K.

3798
05:54:41,646 --> 05:54:50,704
Highest valued neighbors, such that rather than starting at one random initial configuration, I might start with three or four or five, randomly generate all the neighbors, and then pick

3799
05:54:50,944 --> 05:54:56,164
the three or four or five best of all of the neighbors that I find, and continually repeat this process.

3800
05:54:56,494 --> 05:55:06,148
With the idea being that now I have more options that I'm considering, more ways that I could potentially navigate myself to the optimal solution that might exist for a particular problem.

