I'll randomly generate 15 houses that I just go ahead and add in random locations.

3837
05:58:20,654 --> 05:58:26,596
And now I'm going to run this hill climbing algorithm in order to try and figure out where we should place those hospitals.

3838
05:58:27,502 --> 05:58:30,020
So I'll go ahead and run this program by running Python hospitals.

3839
05:58:31,374 --> 05:58:39,328
And we see that we started, our initial state had a cost of 72, but we were able to continually find neighbors that were able to decrease that cost, decrease to 69,

3840
05:58:39,488 --> 05:58:45,636
66, 63, so on and so forth, all the way down to 53 as the best neighbor we were able to ultimately find.

3841
05:58:46,094 --> 05:58:49,508
And we can take a look at what that looked like by just opening up these files.

3842
05:58:50,158 --> 05:58:53,088
So here, for example, was the initial configuration.

3843
05:58:53,248 --> 05:59:04,452
We randomly selected a location for each of these 15 different houses, and then randomly selected locations for one, two, three hospitals that were just located somewhere inside of this state space.

3844
05:59:04,814 --> 05:59:10,692
And if you add up all the distances from each of the houses to their nearest hospital, you get a total cost of about 72.

3845
05:59:11,310 --> 05:59:15,808
And so now the question is, what neighbors can we move to that improve the situation?

3846
05:59:16,078 --> 05:59:23,204
And it looks like the first one the algorithm found was by taking this house that was over there on the right and just moving it to the left.

3847
05:59:23,854 --> 05:59:30,836
And that probably makes sense because if you look at the houses in that general area, really these five houses look like they're probably the ones that are going to

3848
05:59:30,928 --> 05:59:32,964
be closest to this hospital over here.

3849
05:59:33,248 --> 05:59:37,968
Moving it to the left decreases the total distance, at least to most of these houses.

3850
05:59:38,094 --> 05:59:40,128
So it does increase that distance for one of them.

3851
05:59:40,430 --> 05:59:46,628
And so we're able to make these improvements to the situation by continually finding ways that we can move these hospitals around

3852
05:59:47,214 --> 05:59:51,808
until we eventually settle at this particular state that has a cost of 53.

3853
05:59:52,174 --> 05:59:59,408
where we figured out a position for each of the hospitals, and now none of the neighbors that we could move to are actually going to improve the situation.

3854
05:59:59,694 --> 06:00:07,328
We can take this hospital and this hospital and that hospital and look at each of the neighbors, and none of those are going to be better than this particular configuration.

3855
06:00:07,630 --> 06:00:09,888
And again, that's not to say that this is the best we could do.

3856
06:00:10,048 --> 06:00:18,276
There might be some other configuration of hospitals that is a global minimum, and this might just be a local minimum, that is the best of all of its neighbors,

3857
06:00:18,448 --> 06:00:21,796
but maybe not the best in the entire possible state space.

3858
06:00:22,126 --> 06:00:29,488
And you could search through the entire state space by considering all of the possible configurations for hospitals, but ultimately that's going to be very time-intensive,

3859
06:00:29,568 --> 06:00:33,284
especially as our state space gets bigger and there might be more and more possible states.

3860
06:00:33,550 --> 06:00:35,888
It's going to take quite a long time to look through all of them.

3861
06:00:36,142 --> 06:00:42,368
And so being able to use these sort of local search algorithms can often be quite good for trying to find the best solution we can do.

3862
06:00:42,542 --> 06:00:49,860
And especially if we don't care about doing the best possible and we just care about doing pretty good and finding a pretty good placement of those hospitals,

3863
06:00:50,158 --> 06:00:52,100
then these methods can be particularly powerful.

3864
06:00:53,230 --> 06:00:59,408
But of course, we can try and mitigate some of this concern by instead of using hill climbing to use random restart.

3865
06:00:59,488 --> 06:01:06,948
This idea of rather than just hill climb one time, we can hill climb multiple times and say, try hill climbing a whole bunch of times on the exact same map

3866
06:01:07,214 --> 06:01:09,956
and figure out what is the best one that we've been able to find.

3867
06:01:10,286 --> 06:01:16,644
And so I've here implemented a function for random restart that restarts some maximum number of times.

3868
06:01:17,454 --> 06:01:24,100
And what we're going to do is repeat that number of times this process of just go ahead and run the hill climbing algorithm,

3869
06:01:24,430 --> 06:01:30,980
figure out what the cost is of getting from all the houses to the hospitals, and then figure out, is this better than we've done so far?

3870
06:01:31,566 --> 06:01:36,740
So I can try this exact same idea where instead of running hill climbing, I'll go ahead and run random restart.

3871
06:01:37,326 --> 06:01:40,260
And I'll randomly restart maybe 20 times, for example.

3872
06:01:41,198 --> 06:01:45,284
And we'll go ahead and now I'll remove all the images and then rerun the program.

3873
06:01:46,222 --> 06:01:48,768
And now we started by finding a original state.

3874
06:01:49,088 --> 06:01:52,416
when we initially ran hill climbing, the best cost we were able to find was 56.

3875
06:01:52,942 --> 06:01:57,248
Each of these iterations is a different iteration of the hill climbing algorithm.

3876
06:01:57,328 --> 06:02:03,908
We're running hill climbing not one time, but 20 times here, each time going until we find a local minimum, in this case.

3877
06:02:04,334 --> 06:02:10,768
And we look and see each time, did we do better than we did the best time we've done so far? So we went from 56 to 46.

3878
06:02:11,150 --> 06:02:12,580
This one was greater, so we ignored it.

3879
06:02:12,768 --> 06:02:16,196
This one was 41, which was less, so we went ahead and kept that one.

3880
06:02:16,398 --> 06:02:24,868
And for all of the remaining 16 times that we tried to implement hill climbing, and we tried to run the hill climbing algorithm, we couldn't do any better than that 41.

3881
06:02:25,088 --> 06:02:32,288
Again, maybe there is a way to do better that we just didn't find, but it looks like that way ended up being a pretty good solution to the problem.

3882
06:02:32,608 --> 06:02:36,196
That was attempt number three, starting from counting at zero.

3883
06:02:36,878 --> 06:02:37,860
So we can take a look at that.

3884
06:02:38,286 --> 06:02:48,484
Open up number three, and this was the state that happened to have a cost of 41. That after running the hill climbing algorithm on some particular random initial configuration of hospitals,

3885
06:02:48,768 --> 06:02:54,528
this is what we found was the local minimum in terms of trying to minimize the cost, and it looks like we did pretty well.

3886
06:02:54,608 --> 06:03:03,408
That this hospital is pretty close to this region, this one is pretty close to these houses here, this hospital looks about as good as we can do for trying to capture those houses over on that side.

3887
06:03:03,694 --> 06:03:08,292
And so these sorts of algorithms can be quite useful for trying to solve these problems.

3888
06:03:09,198 --> 06:03:18,660
But the real problem with many of these different types of hill climbing, steepest ascent, stochastic, first choice, and so forth, is that they never make a move that makes our situation worse.

3889
06:03:19,088 --> 06:03:25,888
They're always going to take ourselves in our current state, look at the neighbors, and consider can we do better than our current state and move to one of those neighbors.

3890
06:03:26,048 --> 06:03:35,328
Which of those neighbors we choose might vary among these various different types of algorithms, but we never go from a current position to a position that is worse than our current position.

3891
06:03:35,534 --> 06:03:40,768
And ultimately, that's what we're going to need to do if we want to be able to find a global maximum or a global minimum.

3892
06:03:40,942 --> 06:03:49,968
Because sometimes if we get stuck, we want to find some way of dislodging ourselves from our local maximum or local minimum in order to find the global maximum or the global minimum,

3893
06:03:50,048 --> 06:03:52,196
or increase the probability that we do find it.

3894
06:03:53,006 --> 06:03:58,660
And so the most popular technique for trying to approach the problem from that angle is a technique known as simulated annealing.

3895
06:03:58,848 --> 06:04:08,048
Simulated because it's modeling after a real physical process of annealing, where you can think about this in terms of physics, a physical situation where you have some system of particles.

3896
06:04:08,302 --> 06:04:12,688
and you might imagine that when you heat up a particular physical system, there's a lot of energy there.

3897
06:04:12,848 --> 06:04:19,716
things are moving around quite randomly, but over time as the system cools down, it eventually settles into some final position.

3898
06:04:20,238 --> 06:04:23,204
And that's going to be the general idea of simulated annealing.

3899
06:04:23,328 --> 06:04:31,808
We're going to simulate that process of some high temperature system where things are moving around randomly quite frequently, but over time decreasing that temperature

3900
06:04:32,014 --> 06:04:34,660
until we eventually settle at our ultimate solution.

3901
06:04:35,022 --> 06:04:40,004
And the idea is going to be: if we have some state space landscape that looks like this, and we begin

3902
06:04:40,686 --> 06:04:46,564
at its initial state here, if we're looking for a global maximum, and we're trying to maximize the value of the state,

3903
06:04:46,894 --> 06:04:55,524
our traditional hill climbing algorithms would just take the state and look at the two neighbor ones and always pick the one that is going to increase the value of the state.

3904
06:04:55,950 --> 06:05:00,740
But if we want some chance of being able to find the global maximum, we can't always make good moves.

3905
06:05:01,518 --> 06:05:05,956
We have to sometimes make bad moves and allow ourselves to make a move in a direction

3906
06:05:06,638 --> 06:05:12,516
that actually seems for now to make our situation worse, such that later we can find our way up to

3907
06:05:13,198 --> 06:05:15,968
that global maximum in terms of trying to solve that problem.

3908
06:05:16,238 --> 06:05:23,780
Of course, once we get up to this global maximum, once we've done a whole lot of the searching, then we probably don't want to be moving to states that are worse than our current state.

3909
06:05:24,046 --> 06:05:29,888
And so this is where this metaphor for annealing starts to come in, where we want to start making more random moves,

3910
06:05:30,062 --> 06:05:35,172
and over time start to make fewer of those random moves based on a particular temperature schedule.

3911
06:05:36,174 --> 06:05:37,892
So the basic outline looks something like this.

3912
06:05:38,208 --> 06:05:42,148
Early on in simulated annealing, we have a higher temperature state.

3913
06:05:42,478 --> 06:05:48,928
And what we mean by a higher temperature state is that we are more likely to accept neighbors that are worse than our current state.

3914
06:05:49,134 --> 06:05:56,708
That we might look at our neighbors, and if one of our neighbors is worse than the current state, especially if it's not all that much worse, if it's pretty close but just slightly worse,

3915
06:05:57,168 --> 06:06:01,252
then we might be more likely to accept that and go ahead and move to that neighbor anyways.

3916
06:06:02,062 --> 06:06:11,968
But later on, as we run simulated annealing, we're going to decrease that temperature, and at a lower temperature, we're going to be less likely to accept neighbors that are worse than our current

3917
06:06:11,968 --> 06:06:16,740
state. Now to formalize this and put a little bit of pseudocode to it, here is what that algorithm might look like.

3918
06:06:17,008 --> 06:06:22,608
We have a function called simulated annealing that takes as input the problem we're trying to solve, and also potentially

3919
06:06:22,830 --> 06:06:29,088
some maximum number of times that we might want to run the simulated annealing process, how many different neighbors we're going to try and look for.

3920
06:06:29,262 --> 06:06:31,940
And that value is going to vary based on the problem you're trying to solve.

3921
06:06:33,166 --> 06:06:36,868
We'll again start with some current state that will be equal to the initial state of the problem.

3922
06:06:37,328 --> 06:06:42,436
But now we need to repeat this process over and over for max number of times.

3923
06:06:42,734 --> 06:06:47,620
Repeat some process some number of times, where we're first going to calculate a temperature.

3924
06:06:48,046 --> 06:06:57,168
And this temperature function takes the current time t starting at one, going all the way up to max, and then gives us some temperature that we can use in our computation.

3925
06:06:57,328 --> 06:07:02,688
Where the idea is that this temperature is going to be higher early on and it's going to be lower later on.

3926
06:07:02,862 --> 06:07:05,408
So there are a number of ways this temperature function could often work.

3927
06:07:05,614 --> 06:07:10,608
One of the simplest ways is just to say it is like the proportion of time that we still have remaining.

3928
06:07:10,768 --> 06:07:13,888
Out of max units of time, how much time do we have remaining?

3929
06:07:14,048 --> 06:07:21,668
You start off with a lot of that time remaining, and as time goes on, the temperature is going to decrease because you have less and less of that remaining time still available to you.

3930
06:07:22,382 --> 06:07:28,048
So we calculate a temperature for the current time, and then we pick a random neighbor of the current state.

3931
06:07:28,208 --> 06:07:33,248
No longer are we going to be picking the best neighbor that we possibly can, or just one of the better neighbors that we can.

3932
06:07:33,422 --> 06:07:34,688
We're going to pick a random neighbor.

3933
06:07:34,798 --> 06:07:37,248
It might be better, it might be worse, but we're going to calculate that.

3934
06:07:37,328 --> 06:07:44,612
We're going to calculate delta E, E for energy in this case, which is just how much better is the neighbor than the current state.

3935
06:07:45,230 --> 06:07:49,060
So if delta E is positive, that means the neighbor is better than our current state.

3936
06:07:49,358 --> 06:07:53,540
If delta E is negative, that means the neighbor is worse than our current state.

3937
06:07:54,094 --> 06:07:55,888
And so we can then have a condition that looks like this.

3938
06:07:56,078 --> 06:08:01,540
If delta E is greater than zero, that means the neighbor state is better than our current state.

3939
06:08:01,870 --> 06:08:06,448
And if ever that situation arises, we'll just go ahead and update current to be that neighbor.

3940
06:08:06,608 --> 06:08:12,676
Same as before, move where we are currently to be the neighbor, because the neighbor is better than our current state. We'll go ahead and accept that.

3941
06:08:13,230 --> 06:08:20,688
But now the difference is that whereas before we never ever wanted to take a move that made our situation worse, now we sometimes want to

3942
06:08:21,168 --> 06:08:23,648
make a move that is actually going to make our situation worse.

3943
06:08:23,918 --> 06:08:31,968
Because sometimes we're going to need to dislodge ourselves from a local minimum or local maximum to increase the probability that we're able to find the global minimum or the global

3944
06:08:31,968 --> 06:08:33,220
maximum a little bit later.

3945
06:08:34,062 --> 06:08:39,140
And so how do we do that? How do we decide to sometimes accept some state that might actually be worse?

3946
06:08:39,470 --> 06:08:45,700
Well, we're going to accept a worse state with some probability, and that probability needs to be based on a couple of factors.

3947
06:08:45,966 --> 06:08:52,740
It needs to be based in part on the temperature, where if the temperature is higher, we're more likely to move to a worse neighbor.

3948
06:08:52,910 --> 06:08:56,164
And if the temperature is lower, we're less likely to move to a worse neighbor.

3949
06:08:56,686 --> 06:08:59,972
But it also to some degree should be based on delta e.

3950
06:09:00,526 --> 06:09:08,900
If the neighbor is much worse than the current state, we probably want to be less likely to choose that than if the neighbor is just a little bit worse than the current state.

3951
06:09:09,678 --> 06:09:19,088
So again, there are a couple of ways you could calculate this, but it turns out one of the most popular is just to calculate e to the power of delta e over t, where e is just a constant.

3952
06:09:19,374 --> 06:09:22,724
Delta e over t are based on delta e and t here.

3953
06:09:22,928 --> 06:09:30,948
We calculate that value, and that'll be some value between 0 and 1, and that is the probability with which we should just say, all right, let's go ahead and move to that neighbor.

3954
06:09:31,182 --> 06:09:37,860
And it turns out that if you do the math for this value, when delta e is such that the neighbor is not that much worse than the current state,

3955
06:09:38,048 --> 06:09:40,804
that's going to be more likely that we're going to go ahead and move to that state.

3956
06:09:41,006 --> 06:09:46,180
And likewise, when the temperature is lower, we're going to be less likely to move to that neighboring state as well.

3957
06:09:47,054 --> 06:09:49,904
So now this is the big picture for simulated annealing.

3958
06:09:49,984 --> 06:09:56,676
This process of taking the problem and going ahead and generating random neighbors will always move to a neighbor if it's better than our current state.

3959
06:09:56,974 --> 06:10:04,260
But even if the neighbor is worse than our current state, we'll sometimes move there, depending on how much worse it is, and also based on the temperature.

3960
06:10:04,654 --> 06:10:12,624
And as a result, the hope, the goal of this whole process is that as we begin to try and find our way to the local the global maximum or the global minimum,

3961
06:10:12,784 --> 06:10:21,572
we can dislodge ourselves if we ever get stuck at a local maximum or local minimum in order to eventually make our way to exploring the part of the state space that is going to be the best.

3962
06:10:22,030 --> 06:10:31,044
And then as the temperature decreases, eventually we settle there without moving around too much from what we've found to be the globally best thing that we can do thus far.

3963
06:10:31,438 --> 06:10:37,188
So at the very end, we just return whatever the current state happens to be, and that is the conclusion of this algorithm.

3964
06:10:37,584 --> 06:10:39,620
We've been able to figure out what the solution is.

3965
06:10:40,462 --> 06:10:43,824
And these types of algorithms have a lot of different applications.

3966
06:10:43,984 --> 06:10:50,304
Anytime you can take a problem and formulate it as something where you can explore a particular configuration and then ask,

3967
06:10:50,542 --> 06:10:59,428
are any of the neighbors better than this current configuration and have some way of measuring that, then there's an applicable case for these hill climbing, simulated annealing types of algorithms.

3968
06:10:59,822 --> 06:11:06,276
So sometimes it can be for facility location type problems, like for when you're trying to plan a city and figure out where the hospitals should be,

3969
06:11:06,478 --> 06:11:08,452
but there are definitely other applications as well.

3970
06:11:08,686 --> 06:11:12,944
And one of the most famous problems in computer science is the traveling salesman problem.

3971
06:11:13,230 --> 06:11:15,876
Traveling salesman problem generally is formulated like this.

3972
06:11:16,206 --> 06:11:18,852
I have a whole bunch of cities here indicated by these dots.

3973
06:11:19,342 --> 06:11:25,316
And what I'd like to do is find some route that takes me through all of the cities and ends up back where I started.

3974
06:11:25,614 --> 06:11:31,428
So some route that starts here, goes through all these cities and ends up back where I originally started.

3975
06:11:32,014 --> 06:11:39,744
And what I might like to do is minimize the total distance that I have to travel or the total cost of taking this entire path.

3976
06:11:40,014 --> 06:11:48,304
And you can imagine this is a problem that's very applicable in situations like when delivery companies are trying to deliver things to a whole bunch of different houses.

3977
06:11:48,464 --> 06:11:57,504
They want to figure out how do I get from the warehouse to all these various different houses and get back again, all using as minimal time and distance and energy as possible.

3978
06:11:57,838 --> 06:12:00,064
So you might want to try to solve these sorts of

3979
06:12:00,064 --> 06:12:09,184
problems. But it turns out that solving this particular kind of problem is very computationally difficult. It is a very computationally expensive task to be able to figure it out.

3980
06:12:09,344 --> 06:12:17,092
This falls under the category of what are known as NP-complete problems, problems that there is no known efficient way to try and solve these sorts of problems.

3981
06:12:17,422 --> 06:12:27,824
And so what we ultimately have to do is come up with some approximation, some ways of trying to find a good solution, even if we're not going to find the globally best solution that we possibly can,

3982
06:12:27,984 --> 06:12:30,660
at least not in a feasible or tractable amount of time.

3983
06:12:31,246 --> 06:12:39,424
And so what we could do is take the traveling salesman problem and try to formulate it using local search and ask a question, like, all right, I can pick some state,

3984
06:12:39,584 --> 06:12:45,700
some configuration, some route between all of these nodes, and I can measure the cost of that state, figure out what the distance is,

3985
06:12:46,030 --> 06:12:49,124
and I might now want to try to minimize that cost as much as possible.

3986
06:12:49,902 --> 06:12:53,860
And then the only question now is, what does it mean to have a neighbor of this state?

3987
06:12:54,094 --> 06:13:01,380
What does it mean to take this particular route and have some neighboring route that is close to it but slightly different such that it might have a different total distance?

3988
06:13:01,774 --> 06:13:06,660
And there are a number of different definitions for what a neighbor of a traveling salesman configuration might look like.

3989
06:13:06,990 --> 06:13:15,012
But one way is just to say, a neighbor is what happens if we pick two of these edges between nodes and switch them

3990
06:13:15,278 --> 06:13:15,748
effectively.

3991
06:13:16,384 --> 06:13:22,372
So for example, I might pick these two edges here, these two that just happen to cross, this node goes here, this node goes there,

3992
06:13:23,150 --> 06:13:24,484
and go ahead and switch them.

3993
06:13:24,846 --> 06:13:33,504
And what that process will generally look like is removing both of these edges from the graph, taking this node and connecting it to the node it wasn't connected to.

3994
06:13:33,646 --> 06:13:35,588
So connecting it up here instead.

3995
06:13:36,014 --> 06:13:42,660
We'll need to take these arrows that were originally going this way and reverse them, so move them going the other way, and then just fill in that last remaining blank,

3996
06:13:42,894 --> 06:13:44,900
add an arrow that goes in that direction instead.

3997
06:13:45,294 --> 06:13:54,784
So by taking two edges and just switching them, I have been able to consider one possible neighbor of this particular configuration, and it looks like this neighbor is actually better.

3998
06:13:54,864 --> 06:14:01,744
It looks like this probably travels a shorter distance in order to get through all the cities through this route than the current state did.

3999
06:14:02,062 --> 06:14:11,504
And so you could imagine implementing this idea inside of a hill climbing or simulated annealing algorithm, where we repeat this process to try and take a state of this traveling salesman problem,

4000
06:14:11,664 --> 06:14:16,612
look at all the neighbors, and then move to the neighbors if they're better, or maybe even move to the neighbors if they're worse,

4001
06:14:16,878 --> 06:14:20,864
until we eventually settle upon some best solution that we've been able to

4002
06:14:20,864 --> 06:14:30,544
find. And it turns out that these types of approximation algorithms, even if they don't always find the very best solution, can often do pretty well at trying to find solutions that are helpful

4003
06:14:30,704 --> 06:14:30,948
too.

4004
06:14:32,174 --> 06:14:40,928
So that then was a look at local search, a particular category of algorithms that can be used for solving a particular type of problem, where we don't really care about the path to the solution.

4005
06:14:41,134 --> 06:14:44,548
I didn't care about the steps I took to decide where the hospitals should go.

4006
06:14:44,768 --> 06:14:46,528
I just cared about the solution itself.

4007
06:14:46,688 --> 06:14:52,548
I just care about where the hospitals should be or what the route through the traveling salesman journey really ought to be.

4008
06:14:53,454 --> 06:14:58,692
Another type of algorithm that might come up are known as these categories of linear programming types of problems.

4009
06:14:59,054 --> 06:15:08,688
And linear programming often comes up in the context where we're trying to optimize for some mathematical function, but oftentimes linear programming will come up when we might have real

4010
06:15:09,168 --> 06:15:15,748
numbered values, that it's not just like discrete fixed values that we might have, but any decimal values that we might want to be able to calculate.

4011
06:15:16,174 --> 06:15:26,528
And so linear programming is a family of types of problems where we might have a situation that looks like this, where the goal of linear programming is to minimize a cost function.

4012
06:15:26,608 --> 06:15:31,556
And you can invert the numbers and say try and maximize it, but often we'll frame it as trying to minimize a cost function

4013
06:15:32,366 --> 06:15:36,768
that has some number of variables, x1, x2, x3, all the way up to xn.

4014
06:15:36,916 --> 06:15:40,740
just some number of variables that are involved, things that I want to know the values to.

4015
06:15:41,230 --> 06:15:44,644
And this cost function might have coefficients in front of those variables.

4016
06:15:44,942 --> 06:15:51,728
And this is what we would call like a linear equation, where we just have all of these variables that might be multiplied by a coefficient and then added together.

4017
06:15:51,982 --> 06:15:55,728
We're not going to square anything or cube anything, because that'll give us different types of equations.

4018
06:15:56,014 --> 06:16:01,764
With linear programming, we're just dealing with linear equations in addition to linear constraints.

4019
06:16:02,350 --> 06:16:13,008
Where a constraint is going to look something like if we sum up this particular equation that is just some linear combination of all of these variables, it is less than or equal to some bound B.

4020
06:16:13,454 --> 06:16:20,420
And we might have a whole number of these various different constraints that we might place onto our linear programming exercise.

4021
06:16:21,230 --> 06:16:28,608
And likewise, just as we can have constraints that are saying this linear equation is less than or equal to some bound b, it might also be equal to something.

4022
06:16:28,750 --> 06:16:33,648
That if you want some sum of some combination of variables to be equal to a value, you can specify that.

4023
06:16:33,934 --> 06:16:42,928
And we can also maybe specify that each variable has lower and upper bounds, that it needs to be a positive number, for example, or it needs to be a number that is less than 50, for example.

4024
06:16:43,150 --> 06:16:47,460
And there are a number of other choices that we can make there for defining what the bounds of a variable are.

4025
06:16:47,918 --> 06:16:56,260
But it turns out that if you can take a problem and formulate it in these terms, formulate the problem as your goal is to minimize a cost function.

4026
06:16:56,526 --> 06:17:00,100
and you're minimizing that cost function subject to particular constraints.

4027
06:17:00,462 --> 06:17:07,588
subject to equations that are of the form, like this: of some sequence of variables, is less than a bound or is equal to some particular value.

4028
06:17:07,968 --> 06:17:12,932
then there are a number of algorithms that already exist for solving these sorts of problems.

4029
06:17:13,870 --> 06:17:15,812
So let's go ahead and take a look at an example.

4030
06:17:16,368 --> 06:17:19,648
here's an example of a problem that might come up in the world of linear programming.

4031
06:17:19,888 --> 06:17:27,328
Often this is going to come up when we're trying to optimize for something, and we want to be able to do some calculations and we have constraints on what we're trying to optimize.

4032
06:17:27,822 --> 06:17:29,124
And so it might be something like this.

4033
06:17:29,728 --> 06:17:33,568
in the context of a factory, we have two machines, x1 and x2.

4034
06:17:34,048 --> 06:17:43,908
X1 costs $50 an hour to run, x2 costs $80 an hour to run, and our goal, what we're trying to do, our objective, is to minimize the total cost.

4035
06:17:44,974 --> 06:17:46,020
So that's what we'd like to do.

4036
06:17:46,606 --> 06:17:48,900
But we need to do so subject to certain constraints.

4037
06:17:49,582 --> 06:17:59,492
So there might be a labor constraint that X1 requires five units of labor per hour, x2 requires two units of labor per hour, and we have a total of 20 units of labor that we have to spend.

4038
06:17:59,982 --> 06:18:01,008
So this is a constraint.

4039
06:18:01,168 --> 06:18:09,828
We have no more than 20 units of labor that we can spend, and we have to spend it across x1 and x2, each of which requires a different amount of labor.

4040
06:18:10,830 --> 06:18:19,364
And we might also have a constraint like this that tells us X1 is going to produce 10 units of output per hour, X2 is going to produce 12 units of output per hour,

4041
06:18:19,758 --> 06:18:22,512
and the company needs 90 units of output.

4042
06:18:22,702 --> 06:18:24,592
So we have some goal, something we need to achieve.

4043
06:18:24,752 --> 06:18:33,540
We need to achieve 90 units of output, but there are some constraints that X1 can only produce 10 units of output per hour, x2 produces 12 units of output per hour.

4044
06:18:33,998 --> 06:18:39,300
These types of problems come up quite frequently, and you can start to notice patterns in these types of problems.

4045
06:18:39,472 --> 06:18:46,512
Problems where I am trying to optimize for some goal, minimizing cost, maximizing output, maximizing profits, or something like that.

4046
06:18:46,734 --> 06:18:49,444
And there are constraints that are placed on that process.

4047
06:18:49,998 --> 06:18:54,148
And so now we just need to formulate this problem in terms of linear equations.

4048
06:18:55,054 --> 06:18:56,260
So let's start with this first point.

4049
06:18:56,558 --> 06:19:01,412
Two machines, x1 and x2, x costs $50 an hour, x2 costs $80 an hour.

4050
06:19:01,806 --> 06:19:05,152
Here we can come up with an objective function that might look like this.

4051
06:19:05,312 --> 06:19:06,884
This is our cost function, rather.

4052
06:19:07,310 --> 06:19:14,032
50 times x1 plus 80 times x2, where x1 is going to be a variable, representing how many hours we run.

4053
06:19:14,032 --> 06:19:19,844
machine x1 for, x2 is going to be a variable, representing how many hours are we running machine x2 for.

4054
06:19:20,238 --> 06:19:27,364
and what we're trying to minimize is this cost function, which is just how much it costs to run. each of these machines per hour, summed up.

4055
06:19:27,632 --> 06:19:36,516
This is an example of a linear equation, just some combination of these variables plus coefficients that are placed in front of them, and I would like to minimize that total value.

4056
06:19:37,038 --> 06:19:39,844
But I need to do so subject to these constraints.

4057
06:19:40,302 --> 06:19:46,468
x1 requires 50 units of labor per hour, x2 requires two, and we have a total of 20 units of labor to spend.

4058
06:19:46,766 --> 06:19:49,316
And so that gives us a constraint of this form.

4059
06:19:50,254 --> 06:19:54,500
5 times x1 plus 2 times x2 is less than or equal to 20.

4060
06:19:54,672 --> 06:20:04,516
20 is the total number of units of labor we have to spend, and that's spent across x1 and x2, each of which requires a different number of units of labor per hour, for example.

4061
06:20:05,070 --> 06:20:06,992
And finally, we have this constraint here.

4062
06:20:07,310 --> 06:20:13,028
X1 produces 10 units of output per hour, x2 produces 12, and we need 90 units of output.

4063
06:20:13,486 --> 06:20:14,852
And so this might look something like this:

4064
06:20:15,854 --> 06:20:24,476
that 10x1 plus 12x2, this is the amount of output per hour, it needs to be at least 90. If we can do better, great, but it needs to be at least 90.

4065
06:20:25,198 --> 06:20:33,156
And if you recall from my formulation before, I said that generally speaking in linear programming, we deal with equals constraints or less than or equal to constraints.

4066
06:20:33,486 --> 06:20:35,312
So we have a greater than or equal to sign here.

4067
06:20:35,502 --> 06:20:43,976
That's not a problem. Whenever we have a greater than or equal to sign, we can just multiply the equation by negative one, and that'll flip it around to a less than or equals negative 90,

4068
06:20:44,192 --> 06:20:46,876
for example, instead of a greater than or equal to 90.

4069
06:20:47,312 --> 06:20:50,980
and that's going to be an equivalent expression that we can use to represent this problem.

4070
06:20:51,822 --> 06:21:01,952
So now that we have this cost function and these constraints that it's subject to, it turns out there are a number of algorithms that can be used in order to solve these types of problems.

4071
06:21:02,032 --> 06:21:06,596
And these problems go a little bit more into geometry and linear algebra than we're really going to get into.

4072
06:21:06,832 --> 06:21:14,512
But the most popular of these types of algorithms are simplex, which was one of the first algorithms discovered for trying to solve linear programs.

4073
06:21:14,670 --> 06:21:19,952
And later on, a class of interior point algorithms can be used to solve this type of problem as well.

4074
06:21:20,206 --> 06:21:29,668
The key is not to understand exactly how these algorithms work, but to realize that these algorithms exist for efficiently finding solutions anytime we have a problem of this particular form.

4075
06:21:30,350 --> 06:21:30,912
And so

4076
06:21:31,694 --> 06:21:34,020
we can take a look, for example, at

4077
06:21:35,072 --> 06:21:37,860
the production directory here.

4078
06:21:39,758 --> 06:21:48,676
Where here I have a file called production.py, where here I'm using scipy, which is the library for a lot of science-related functions within Python.

4079
06:21:48,974 --> 06:21:57,700
and I can go ahead and just run this optimization function in order to run a linear program. linprog here is going to try and solve this linear program for me,

4080
06:21:58,032 --> 06:22:03,712
where I provide to this expression, to this function. call, all of the data about my linear program.

4081
06:22:03,822 --> 06:22:06,724
So it needs to be in a particular format, which might be a little confusing at first.

4082
06:22:07,214 --> 06:22:16,100
But this first argument to scipy.optimize.lin programming is the cost function, which is in this case just an array or a list that has 50 and 80,

4083
06:22:16,302 --> 06:22:20,912
because my original cost function was 50 times x1 plus 80 times x2.

4084
06:22:21,294 --> 06:22:27,236
So I just tell Python 50 and 80, those are the coefficients that I am now trying to optimize for.

4085
06:22:27,886 --> 06:22:30,468
And then I provide all of the constraints.

4086
06:22:30,926 --> 06:22:38,716
So the constraints, and I wrote them up above in comments, is the constraint one is five x one plus two x two is less than or equal to 20.

4087
06:22:39,246 --> 06:22:46,036
and constraint two is negative 10x1 plus negative twelve x two is less than or equal to negative 90.

4088
06:22:47,086 --> 06:22:51,076
And so SciPy expects these constraints to be in a particular format.

4089
06:22:51,408 --> 06:22:56,288
It first expects me to provide all of the coefficients for the upper bound equations.

4090
06:22:56,448 --> 06:23:05,764
Ub is just sort for upper bound, where the coefficients of the first equation are five and two, because we have five x1 and two x2, and the coefficients for the second equation

4091
06:23:06,062 --> 06:23:11,876
are negative ten and negative twelve, because I have negative ten x1 plus negative twelve x2.

4092
06:23:12,526 --> 06:23:17,408
And then here we provide it as a separate argument, just to keep things separate, what the actual bound is.

4093
06:23:17,568 --> 06:23:19,780
What is the upper bound for each of these constraints?

4094
06:23:20,110 --> 06:23:22,368
Well, for the first constraint, the upper bound is 20.

4095
06:23:22,448 --> 06:23:27,332
That was constraint number one, and then for constraint number two, the upper bound is 90.

4096
06:23:28,142 --> 06:23:33,476
So a bit of a cryptic way of representing it. It's not quite as simple as just writing the mathematical equations.

4097
06:23:33,648 --> 06:23:41,888
What really is being expected here are all of the coefficients and all of the numbers that are in these equations by first providing the coefficients for the cost function,

4098
06:23:42,094 --> 06:23:49,956
then providing all the coefficients for the inequality constraints, and then providing all of the upper bounds for those inequality constraints.

4099
06:23:50,510 --> 06:23:56,900
And once all of that information is there, then we can run any of these interior point algorithms or the simplex algorithm.

4100
06:23:57,088 --> 06:24:02,368
Even if you don't understand how it works, you can just run the function and figure out what the result should be.

4101
06:24:02,606 --> 06:24:07,588
And here I said if the result is a success, we were able to solve this problem, go ahead and print out

4102
06:24:08,366 --> 06:24:10,276
what the value of x1 and x2 should be.

4103
06:24:10,574 --> 06:24:12,836
Otherwise, go ahead and print out no solution.

4104
06:24:13,294 --> 06:24:16,164
And so if I run this program by running python

4105
06:24:17,070 --> 06:24:18,848
production.py,

4106
06:24:19,726 --> 06:24:24,368
it takes a second to calculate, but then we see here is what the optimal solution should be.

4107
06:24:24,608 --> 06:24:29,540
x1 should run for 1.5 hours, x2 should run for 6.25 hours.

4108
06:24:30,030 --> 06:24:37,892
And we were able to do this by just formulating the problem as a linear equation that we were trying to optimize, some cost that we were trying to minimize,

4109
06:24:38,158 --> 06:24:40,068
and then some constraints that were placed on that.

4110
06:24:40,398 --> 06:24:50,848
And many, many problems fall into this category of problems that you can solve if you can just figure out how to use equations and use these constraints to represent that general idea.

4111
06:24:51,150 --> 06:25:00,100
And that's a theme that's going to come up a couple of times today, where we want to be able to take some problem and reduce it down to some problem we know how to solve in order to begin to

4112
06:25:00,718 --> 06:25:07,204
find a solution and to use existing methods that we can use in order to find a solution more effectively or more efficiently.

4113
06:25:08,110 --> 06:25:12,768
And it turns out that these types of problems, where we have constraints, show up in other ways too.

4114
06:25:13,038 --> 06:25:18,532
And there's an entire class of problems that's more generally just known as constraint satisfaction problems.

4115
06:25:18,830 --> 06:25:25,636
And we're going to now take a look at how you might formulate a constraint satisfaction problem and how you might go about solving a constraint satisfaction problem.

4116
06:25:25,998 --> 06:25:32,288
But the basic idea of a constraint satisfaction problem is we have some number of variables that need to take on some values,

4117
06:25:32,368 --> 06:25:40,164
and we need to figure out what values each of those variables should take on, but those variables are subject to particular constraints that are going to limit

4118
06:25:40,288 --> 06:25:42,628
what values those variables can actually take on.

4119
06:25:43,342 --> 06:25:46,116
So let's take a look at a real world example, for example.

4120
06:25:46,448 --> 06:25:48,048
let's look at exam scheduling.

4121
06:25:48,238 --> 06:25:51,008
That I have four students here, students one, two, three, and four.

4122
06:25:51,408 --> 06:25:53,648
each of them is taking some number of different classes.

4123
06:25:53,998 --> 06:25:56,004
Classes here are going to be represented by letters.

4124
06:25:56,462 --> 06:26:03,652
so student one is enrolled in courses A, B, and C, student two is enrolled in courses B, D, and E, so on and so forth.

4125
06:26:04,302 --> 06:26:12,528
And now, say university, for example, is trying to schedule exams for all of these courses, but there are only three exam slots on Monday,

4126
06:26:12,608 --> 06:26:16,612
Tuesday, and Wednesday, and we have to schedule an exam for each of these courses.

4127
06:26:17,294 --> 06:26:25,008
But the constraint now, the constraint we have to deal with with the scheduling, is that we don't want anyone to have to take two exams on the same day.

4128
06:26:25,134 --> 06:26:28,292
We would like to try and minimize that or eliminate it if at all possible.

4129
06:26:29,454 --> 06:26:37,408
So, how do we begin to represent this idea? How do we structure this in a way that a computer with an AI algorithm can begin to try and solve the problem?

4130
06:26:37,774 --> 06:26:45,316
Well, let's in particular just look at these classes that we might take and represent each of the courses as some node inside of a graph.

4131
06:26:45,870 --> 06:26:53,156
And what we'll do is we'll create an edge between two nodes in this graph if there is a constraint between those two nodes.

4132
06:26:54,286 --> 06:26:55,088
So what does this mean?

4133
06:26:55,406 --> 06:26:59,488
Well, we can start with student one, who's enrolled in courses A, B, and C.

4134
06:26:59,822 --> 06:27:08,804
What that means is that A and B can't have an exam at the same time, A and C can't have an exam at the same time, and B and C also can't have an exam at the same time.

4135
06:27:09,102 --> 06:27:12,048
And I can represent that in this graph by just drawing edges.

4136
06:27:12,208 --> 06:27:16,548
One edge between A and B, one between B and C, and then one between C and A.

4137
06:27:16,782 --> 06:27:25,636
And that encodes now the idea that between those nodes there is a constraint, and in particular, the constraint happens to be that these two can't be equal to each other,

4138
06:27:25,742 --> 06:27:30,052
though there are other types of constraints that are possible, depending on the type of problem that you're trying to solve.

4139
06:27:31,182 --> 06:27:33,712
And then we can do the same thing for each of the other students.

4140
06:27:33,838 --> 06:27:40,912
So for student two, who's enrolled in courses B, D, and E, well that means B, D, and E, those all need to have edges that connect each other as well.

4141
06:27:41,294 --> 06:27:48,324
Student three is enrolled in courses C, E, and F. So we'll go ahead and take C, E, and F and connect those by drawing edges between them too.

4142
06:27:48,590 --> 06:27:57,312
And then finally, student four is enrolled in courses E, F, and G, and we can represent that by drawing edges between E, F, and G, although E and F already had an edge between them.

4143
06:27:57,472 --> 06:28:05,060
We don't need another one, because this constraint is just encoding the idea that course E and course F cannot have an exam on the same day.

4144
06:28:05,678 --> 06:28:08,900
So this then is what we might call the constraint graph.

4145
06:28:09,326 --> 06:28:16,804
It is some graphical representation of all of my variables, so to speak, and the constraints between those possible variables.

4146
06:28:16,992 --> 06:28:23,312
Where in this particular case, each of the constraints represents an inequality constraint, that an edge between b and d

4147
06:28:23,534 --> 06:28:29,540
means whatever value the variable b takes on cannot be the value that the variable d takes on as well.

4148
06:28:30,286 --> 06:28:33,312
So what then actually is a constraint satisfaction problem?

4149
06:28:33,710 --> 06:28:39,140
Well, a constraint satisfaction problem is just some set of variables, x1 all the way through xn,

4150
06:28:39,886 --> 06:28:44,832
some set of domains for each of those variables. So every variable needs to take on some values.

4151
06:28:45,102 --> 06:28:48,964
Maybe every variable has the same domain, but maybe each variable has a slightly different domain.

4152
06:28:49,582 --> 06:28:55,428
And then there's a set of constraints, and we'll just call a set c that is some constraints that are placed upon these variables.

4153
06:28:55,632 --> 06:28:57,712
Like x1 is not equal to x2.

4154
06:28:57,998 --> 06:29:06,180
But they could be of other forms too, like maybe x1 equals x2 plus one if these variables are taking on numerical values in their domain, for example.

4155
06:29:06,432 --> 06:29:09,632
The types of constraints are going to vary based on the types of

4156
06:29:09,632 --> 06:29:18,628
problems. And constraint satisfaction shows up all over the place as well in any situation where we have variables that are subject to particular constraints.

4157
06:29:19,214 --> 06:29:26,112
So one popular game is Sudoku, for example, this nine by nine grid where you need to fill in numbers into each of these cells, but you want to

4158
06:29:27,152 --> 06:29:33,604
make sure there's never a duplicate number in any row or in any column or in any grid of three by three cells, for example.

4159
06:29:34,126 --> 06:29:37,380
So what might this look like as a constraint satisfaction problem?

4160
06:29:37,838 --> 06:29:41,632
Well, my variables are all of the empty squares in the puzzle.

4161
06:29:41,934 --> 06:29:49,956
So represented here is just like an x, comma, y coordinate, for example, as all of the squares where I need to plug in a value, where I don't know what value it should take on.

4162
06:29:50,606 --> 06:29:51,492
The domain

4163
06:29:52,142 --> 06:29:59,684
is just going to be all of the numbers from one through nine, any value that I could fill in to one of these cells. So that is going to be the domain for each of these variables.

4164
06:30:00,174 --> 06:30:08,032
And then the constraints are going to be of the form like this cell can't be equal to this cell, can't be equal to this cell, can't be all of these need to be different, for example,

4165
06:30:08,270 --> 06:30:12,388
and same for all of the rows and the columns and the three by three squares as well.

4166
06:30:12,686 --> 06:30:17,028
So those constraints are going to enforce what values are actually allowed.

4167
06:30:17,902 --> 06:30:25,252
And we can formulate the same idea in the case of this exam scheduling problem, where the variables we have are the different courses, a up through G.

4168
06:30:25,806 --> 06:30:30,084
The domain for each of these variables is going to be Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday.

4169
06:30:30,192 --> 06:30:37,060
Those are the possible values each of the variables can take on, that in this case just represent when is the exam for that class.

4170
06:30:38,062 --> 06:30:48,004
And then the constraints are of this form: a is not equal to B, A is not equal to C, meaning A and B can't have an exam on the same day, A and C can't have an exam on the same day,

4171
06:30:48,192 --> 06:30:54,512
or more formally, these two variables cannot take on the same value within their domain.

4172
06:30:56,078 --> 06:31:02,660
So that then is this formulation of a constraint satisfaction problem that we can begin to use to try and solve this problem.

4173
06:31:03,086 --> 06:31:05,152
And constraints can come in a number of different forms.

4174
06:31:05,392 --> 06:31:09,952
There are hard constraints, which are constraints that must be satisfied for a correct solution.

4175
06:31:10,254 --> 06:31:17,232
So something like in the Sudoku puzzle, you cannot have this cell and this cell that are in the same row take on the same value.

4176
06:31:17,312 --> 06:31:18,500
That is a hard constraint.

4177
06:31:18,926 --> 06:31:27,472
But problems can also have soft constraints, where these are constraints that express some notion of preference, that maybe A and B can't have an exam on the same day,

4178
06:31:27,792 --> 06:31:32,032
but maybe someone has a preference that A's exam is earlier than B's exam.

4179
06:31:32,142 --> 06:31:37,312
It doesn't need to be the case with some expression that some solution is better than another solution.

4180
06:31:37,422 --> 06:31:42,788
And in that case, you might formulate the problem as trying to optimize for maximizing people's preferences.

4181
06:31:42,992 --> 06:31:46,116
You want people's preferences to be satisfied as much as possible.

4182
06:31:46,830 --> 06:31:54,032
In this case, though, we'll mostly just deal with hard constraints, constraints that must be met in order to have a correct solution to the problem.

4183
06:31:54,318 --> 06:32:02,244
So we want to figure out some assignment of these variables to their particular values that is ultimately going to give us a solution to the problem

4184
06:32:02,352 --> 06:32:08,324
by allowing us to assign some day to each of the classes such that we don't have any conflicts between classes.

4185
06:32:09,198 --> 06:32:15,556
So it turns out that we can classify the constraints in a constraint satisfaction problem into a number of different categories.

4186
06:32:16,160 --> 06:32:25,892
The first of those categories are perhaps the simplest of the types of constraints, which are known as unary constraints, where a unary constraint is a constraint that just involves a single variable.

4187
06:32:26,222 --> 06:32:33,280
For example, a unary constraint might be something like A does not equal Monday, meaning course A cannot have its exam on Monday.

4188
06:32:33,422 --> 06:32:41,360
If for some reason the instructor for the course isn't available on Monday, you might have a constraint in your problem that looks like this, something that just has a single variable A in it,

4189
06:32:41,614 --> 06:32:50,800
and maybe says A is not equal to Monday or A is equal to something, or in the case of numbers greater than or less than something, a constraint that just has one variable we consider to be a unary

4190
06:32:51,022 --> 06:32:51,364
constraint.

4191
06:32:51,886 --> 06:32:58,000
And this is in contrast to something like a binary constraint, which is a constraint that involves two variables, for example.

4192
06:32:58,222 --> 06:33:01,156
So this would be a constraint like the ones we were looking at before.

4193
06:33:01,454 --> 06:33:09,520
Something like a does not equal b is an example of a binary constraint because it is a constraint that has two variables involved in it,

4194
06:33:09,760 --> 06:33:17,284
a and b, and we represented that using some arc or some edge that connects variable a to variable b.

4195
06:33:17,902 --> 06:33:26,692
And using this knowledge of, okay, what is a unary constraint, what is a binary constraint, there are different types of things we can say about a particular constraint satisfaction problem.

4196
06:33:26,880 --> 06:33:31,012
And one thing we can say is we can try and make the problem node consistent.

4197
06:33:31,598 --> 06:33:33,120
So what does node consistency mean?

4198
06:33:33,360 --> 06:33:40,932
Node consistency means we have all of the values in a variable's domain satisfying that variable's unary constraints.

4199
06:33:41,486 --> 06:33:50,240
So for each of the variables inside of our constraint satisfaction problem, if all of the values satisfy the unary constraints for that particular variable,

4200
06:33:50,414 --> 06:33:58,308
we can say that the entire problem is node consistent, or we can even say that a particular variable is node consistent if we just want to make one node

4201
06:33:58,560 --> 06:33:59,716
consistent within itself.

4202
06:34:00,558 --> 06:34:02,000
So what does that actually look like?

4203
06:34:02,286 --> 06:34:07,684
Let's look at now a simplified example where instead of having a whole bunch of different classes, we just have two classes,

4204
06:34:08,000 --> 06:34:12,080
a and b, each of which has an exam on either Monday or Tuesday or Wednesday.

4205
06:34:12,270 --> 06:34:16,484
So this is the domain for the variable a, and this is the domain for the variable b.

4206
06:34:17,102 --> 06:34:19,300
And now let's imagine we have these constraints.

4207
06:34:19,918 --> 06:34:24,420
a not equal to Monday, B not equal to Tuesday, B not equal to Monday, A not equal to B.

4208
06:34:24,942 --> 06:34:27,908
So those are the constraints that we have on this particular problem.

4209
06:34:28,558 --> 06:34:32,228
And what we can now try to do is enforce node consistency.

4210
06:34:32,526 --> 06:34:40,612
And node consistency just means we make sure that all of the values for any variable's domain satisfy its unary constraints.

4211
06:34:41,230 --> 06:34:45,124
And so we can start by trying to make node A node consistent.

4212
06:34:45,550 --> 06:34:50,788
Like, is it consistent? Does every value inside of A's domain satisfy its unary constraints?

4213
06:34:51,118 --> 06:35:00,260
Well, initially we'll see that Monday does not satisfy A's unary constraints, because we have a constraint, a unary constraint here, that A is not equal to Monday,

4214
06:35:00,686 --> 06:35:02,692
but Monday is still in A's domain.

4215
06:35:03,246 --> 06:35:10,788
And so this is something that is not node consistent, because we have Monday in the domain, but this is not a valid value for this particular node.

4216
06:35:11,150 --> 06:35:13,120
And so how do we make this node consistent?

4217
06:35:13,390 --> 06:35:18,564
Well, to make the node node consistent, what we'll do is we'll just go ahead and remove Monday from A's domain.

4218
06:35:18,830 --> 06:35:24,548
Now A can only be on Tuesday or Wednesday because we had this constraint that said A is not equal to Monday.

4219
06:35:25,390 --> 06:35:28,480
And at this point now, A is node consistent.

4220
06:35:28,590 --> 06:35:36,640
For each of the values that A can take on, Tuesday and Wednesday, there is no constraint that is a unary constraint that conflicts with that idea.

4221
06:35:36,720 --> 06:35:39,012
There is no constraint that says that A can't be Tuesday.

4222
06:35:39,150 --> 06:35:42,308
There is no unary constraint that says that A cannot be on Wednesday.

4223
06:35:42,990 --> 06:35:44,560
And so now we can turn our attention to B.

4224
06:35:44,880 --> 06:35:52,880
B also has a domain, Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday, and we can begin to see whether those variables satisfy the unary constraints as well.

4225
06:35:53,166 --> 06:35:54,480
Well, here's a unary constraint.

4226
06:35:54,720 --> 06:36:04,708
B is not equal to Tuesday, and that does not appear to be satisfied by this domain of Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday, because Tuesday, this possible value that the variable b could take on,

4227
06:36:05,120 --> 06:36:09,060
is not consistent with this unary constraint, that b is not equal to Tuesday.

4228
06:36:09,582 --> 06:36:13,440
So to solve that problem, we'll go ahead and remove Tuesday from B's domain.

4229
06:36:13,582 --> 06:36:21,280
Now B's domain only contains Monday and Wednesday, but as it turns out, there's yet another unary constraint that we placed on the variable b, which is here.

4230
06:36:21,646 --> 06:36:23,140
B is not equal to Monday.

4231
06:36:23,840 --> 06:36:30,000
That means that this value, Monday, inside of B's domain, is not consistent with B's unary constraints,

4232
06:36:30,190 --> 06:36:35,172
because we have a constraint that says the B cannot be Monday, and so we can remove Monday from B's domain.

4233
06:36:35,360 --> 06:36:38,480
And now we've made it through all of the unary constraints.

4234
06:36:38,560 --> 06:36:46,948
We've not yet considered this constraint, which is a binary constraint, but we've considered all of the unary constraints, all of the constraints that involve just a single variable,

4235
06:36:47,310 --> 06:36:51,840
and we've made sure that every node is consistent with those unary constraints.

4236
06:36:51,950 --> 06:36:55,268
So we can say that now we have enforced node consistency.

4237
06:36:55,600 --> 06:37:04,388
That for each of these possible nodes, we can pick any of these values in the domain, and there won't be a unary constraint that is violated as a result of it.

4238
06:37:05,582 --> 06:37:07,664
So, node consistency is fairly easy to enforce.

4239
06:37:07,824 --> 06:37:12,100
We just take each node, make sure the values in the domain satisfy the unary constraints.

4240
06:37:12,366 --> 06:37:17,156
Where things get a little bit more interesting is when we consider different types of consistency.

4241
06:37:17,504 --> 06:37:20,228
Something like arc consistency, for example.

4242
06:37:20,718 --> 06:37:27,876
And arc consistency refers to when all of the values in a variable's domain satisfy the variable's binary constraints.

4243
06:37:28,302 --> 06:37:34,788
So when we're looking at trying to make a arc consistent, we're no longer just considering the unary constraints that involve a.

4244
06:37:35,054 --> 06:37:39,664
We're trying to consider all of the binary constraints that involve a as well.

4245
06:37:39,790 --> 06:37:46,852
So any edge that connects a to another variable inside of that constraint graph that we were taking a look at before.

4246
06:37:47,598 --> 06:37:55,524
Put a little bit more formally, arc consistency, and arc really is just another word for an edge that connects two of these nodes inside of our constraint graph.

4247
06:37:55,918 --> 06:37:58,564
We can define our consistency a little more precisely like this.

4248
06:37:59,086 --> 06:38:07,664
In order to make some variable x arc consistent with respect to some other variable y, we need to remove any element

4249
06:38:08,078 --> 06:38:16,420
from x's domain to make sure that every choice for x, every choice in x's domain has a possible choice for y.

4250
06:38:17,518 --> 06:38:25,252
So put another way, if I have a variable x and I want to make x n arc consistent, then I'm going to look at all of the possible values that x can take on

4251
06:38:25,550 --> 06:38:31,184
and make sure that for all of those possible values, there is still some choice that I can make for y.

4252
06:38:31,438 --> 06:38:38,276
if there's some arc between x and y, to make sure that y has a possible option that I can choose as well.

4253
06:38:39,182 --> 06:38:40,544
So let's look at an example of that.

4254
06:38:40,782 --> 06:38:49,380
Going back to this example from before, we enforced node consistency already by saying that A can only be on Tuesday or Wednesday, because we knew that A could not be on Monday.

4255
06:38:49,646 --> 06:38:57,540
And we also said that B's only domain only consists of Wednesday, because we know that B does not equal Tuesday, and also B does not equal Monday.

4256
06:38:58,382 --> 06:39:04,676
So now let's begin to consider arc consistency. Let's try and make A arc consistent with B.

4257
06:39:05,006 --> 06:39:15,908
And what that means is to make A arc consistent with respect to B means that for any choice we make in A's domain, there is some choice we can make in B's domain that is going to be consistent.

4258
06:39:16,558 --> 06:39:17,188
And we can try that.

4259
06:39:17,486 --> 06:39:20,464
For A, we can choose Tuesday as a possible value for A.

4260
06:39:20,718 --> 06:39:26,144
If I choose Tuesday for A, is there a value for B that satisfies the binary constraint?

4261
06:39:26,382 --> 06:39:32,868
Well yes, B Wednesday would satisfy this constraint that A does not equal B because Tuesday does not equal Wednesday.

4262
06:39:33,504 --> 06:39:36,580
However, if we chose Wednesday for A,

4263
06:39:37,230 --> 06:39:42,624
well then there is no choice in B's domain that satisfies this binary constraint.

4264
06:39:42,784 --> 06:39:49,220
There is no way I can choose something for B that satisfies A does not equal B because I know B must be Wednesday.

4265
06:39:49,902 --> 06:39:54,596
And so if ever I run into a situation like this where I see that here is a possible value for A,

4266
06:39:55,310 --> 06:40:01,892
such that there is no choice of value for B that satisfies the binary constraint, well then this is not art consistent.

4267
06:40:02,350 --> 06:40:07,588
And to make it art consistent, I would need to take Wednesday and remove it from A's domain.

4268
06:40:07,824 --> 06:40:14,624
Because Wednesday was not going to be a possible choice I can make for A, because it wasn't consistent with this binary constraint for B.

4269
06:40:14,784 --> 06:40:21,796
There was no way I could choose Wednesday for A and still have an available solution by choosing something for B as well.

4270
06:40:22,542 --> 06:40:25,744
So here now I've been able to enforce arc consistency.

4271
06:40:26,030 --> 06:40:35,716
And in doing so, I've actually solved this entire problem. That given these constraints where A and B can have exams on either Monday or Tuesday or Wednesday, the only solution, as it would appear,

4272
06:40:35,918 --> 06:40:40,304
is that A's exam must be on Tuesday and B's exam must be on Wednesday.

4273
06:40:40,494 --> 06:40:42,468
And that is the only option available to me.

4274
06:40:43,822 --> 06:40:49,540
So if we want to apply our consistency to a larger graph, not just looking at one particular pair of our consistency,

4275
06:40:49,824 --> 06:40:56,964
there are ways we can do that too, and we can begin to formalize what the pseudocode would look like for trying to write an algorithm that enforces our consistency.

4276
06:40:57,486 --> 06:41:00,544
And we'll start by defining a function called revise.

4277
06:41:01,070 --> 06:41:08,548
Revise is going to take as input a CSP, otherwise known as a constraint satisfaction problem, and also two variables x and y.

4278
06:41:08,942 --> 06:41:17,184
And what revise is going to do is it is going to make x arc consistent with respect to y, meaning remove anything from x's

4279
06:41:17,344 --> 06:41:20,772
domain that doesn't allow for a possible option for y.

4280
06:41:21,710 --> 06:41:22,464
How does this work?

4281
06:41:22,830 --> 06:41:29,024
Well, we'll go ahead and first keep track of whether or not we've made a revision. Revise is ultimately going to return true or false.

4282
06:41:29,230 --> 06:41:33,344
It'll return true in the event that we did make a revision to X's domain.

4283
06:41:33,550 --> 06:41:38,916
It'll return false if we didn't make any change to X's domain, and we'll see in a moment why that's going to be helpful.

4284
06:41:39,758 --> 06:41:42,884
But we start by saying revised equals false, we haven't made any changes.

4285
06:41:43,886 --> 06:41:48,800
Then we'll say, all right, let's go ahead and loop over all of the possible values in X's domain.

4286
06:41:49,070 --> 06:41:57,520
So loop over x's domain for each little x in x's domain, I want to make sure that for each of those choices, I have some available choice in y

4287
06:41:57,840 --> 06:42:04,516
that satisfies the binary constraints that are defined inside of my CSP, inside of my constraint satisfaction problem.

4288
06:42:05,120 --> 06:42:05,440
So

4289
06:42:06,286 --> 06:42:13,700
if ever it's the case that there is no value y in y's domain that satisfies the constraint for x and y,

4290
06:42:14,766 --> 06:42:19,204
well, if that's the case, that means that this value x shouldn't be in x's domain.

4291
06:42:19,886 --> 06:42:25,956
So I'll go ahead and delete x from x's domain, and I'll set revised equal to true because I did change x's domain.

4292
06:42:26,080 --> 06:42:38,180
I changed x's domain by removing little x, and I removed little x because it wasn't art consistent. There was no way I could choose a value for y that would satisfy this xy constraint.

4293
06:42:39,342 --> 06:42:44,676
So in this case we'll go ahead and set revised equal true, and we'll do this again and again for every value in x's domain.

4294
06:42:44,880 --> 06:42:53,028
Sometimes it might be fine, in other cases it might not allow for a possible choice for y, in which case we need to remove this value from x's domain.

4295
06:42:53,198 --> 06:42:57,988
And at the end, we just return revised to indicate whether or not we actually made a change.

4296
06:42:59,022 --> 06:43:08,960
So this function then, this revised function, is effectively an implementation of what you saw me do graphically a moment ago, and it makes one variable, x, arc consistent with another variable,

4297
06:43:09,166 --> 06:43:09,988
in this case y.

4298
06:43:10,862 --> 06:43:20,068
But generally speaking, when we want to enforce our consistency, we'll often want to enforce arc consistency not just for a single arc, but for the entire constraint satisfaction problem.

4299
06:43:20,430 --> 06:43:24,720
And it turns out there's an algorithm to do that as well, and that algorithm is known as AC3.

4300
06:43:25,166 --> 06:43:31,300
AC3 takes a constraint satisfaction problem and it enforces arc consistency across the entire problem.

4301
06:43:32,110 --> 06:43:32,800
How does it do that?

4302
06:43:33,102 --> 06:43:39,396
Well, it's going to basically maintain a queue, or basically just a line of all of the arcs that it needs to make consistent.

4303
06:43:39,726 --> 06:43:46,800
And over time, we might remove things from that queue as we begin dealing with arc consistency, and we might need to add things to that queue as well

4304
06:43:46,958 --> 06:43:49,700
if there are more things we need to make arc consistent.

4305
06:43:50,606 --> 06:43:56,388
So we'll go ahead and start with a queue that contains all of the arcs in the constraint satisfaction problem,

4306
06:43:56,560 --> 06:44:01,220
all of the edges that connect two nodes that have some sort of binary constraint between them.

4307
06:44:02,158 --> 06:44:06,160
And now, as long as the queue is non-empty, there is work to be done.

4308
06:44:06,320 --> 06:44:09,840
The queue is all of the things that we need to make arc consistent.

4309
06:44:10,062 --> 06:44:13,060
So as long as the queue is non-empty, there's still things we have to do.

4310
06:44:13,582 --> 06:44:14,372
What do we have to do?

4311
06:44:15,118 --> 06:44:19,440
Well, we'll start by dequeuing from the queue. Remove something from the queue.

4312
06:44:19,598 --> 06:44:23,108
And strictly speaking, it doesn't need to be a queue, but a queue is a traditional way of doing this.

4313
06:44:23,438 --> 06:44:32,196
We'll dequeue from the queue, and that'll give us an arc, x and y, these two variables where I would like to make x arc consistent with y.

4314
06:44:32,942 --> 06:44:35,600
So how do we make x arc consistent with y?

4315
06:44:35,822 --> 06:44:39,360
Well, we can go ahead and just use that revise function that we talked about a moment ago.

4316
06:44:39,630 --> 06:44:48,160
We call the revise function, passing as input the constraint satisfaction problem, and also these variables x and y, because I want to make x arc

4317
06:44:48,320 --> 06:44:55,200
consistent with y. In other words, remove any values from x's domain that don't leave an available option for y.

4318
06:44:55,854 --> 06:44:57,604
And recall, what does revised return?

4319
06:44:57,902 --> 06:45:06,320
Well, it returns true if we actually made a change, if we removed something from X's domain because there wasn't an available option for y, for example.

4320
06:45:06,638 --> 06:45:10,340
And it returns false if we didn't make any change to x's domain at all.

4321
06:45:10,734 --> 06:45:17,040
And it turns out if revised returns false, if we didn't make any changes, well then there's not a whole lot more work to be done here for this arc.

4322
06:45:17,134 --> 06:45:19,524
We can just move ahead to the next arc that's in the queue.

4323
06:45:20,622 --> 06:45:30,912
But if we did make a change, if we did reduce x's domain by removing values from x's domain, well, then what we might realize is that this creates potential problems later on,

4324
06:45:31,150 --> 06:45:35,364
that it might mean that some arc that was arc consistent with x.

4325
06:45:35,758 --> 06:45:41,252
that node might no longer be arc consistent with x, because while there used to be an option that we could choose for x,

4326
06:45:41,632 --> 06:45:48,836
now, there might not be, because now we might have removed something from x that was necessary for some other arc to be arc consistent.

4327
06:45:49,230 --> 06:45:56,836
And so if ever we did revise X's domain, we're going to need to add some things to the queue, some additional arcs that we might want to check.

4328
06:45:57,294 --> 06:45:58,084
How do we do that?

4329
06:45:58,606 --> 06:46:02,592
Well, first thing we want to check is to make sure that x's domain is not zero.

4330
06:46:02,926 --> 06:46:10,592
If x's domain is zero, that means there are no available options for x at all, and that means that there's no way you can solve the constraint satisfaction problem.

4331
06:46:10,798 --> 06:46:19,044
If we've removed everything from X's domain, we'll go ahead and just return false here to indicate there's no way to solve the problem because there's nothing left in X's domain.

4332
06:46:19,630 --> 06:46:20,388
But otherwise,

4333
06:46:21,422 --> 06:46:32,272
if there are things left in X's domain, but fewer things than before, well then what we'll do is we'll loop over each variable z that is in all of x's neighbors, except for y;

4334
06:46:32,432 --> 06:46:43,204
y we already handled, but we'll consider all of x's other's neighbors and ask ourselves: all right, well that arc from each of those z to x, that arc might no longer be arc consistent.

4335
06:46:43,392 --> 06:46:51,556
Because while for each z, there might have been a possible option and we could choose for x to correspond with each of z's possible values, now there might not be

4336
06:46:51,822 --> 06:46:54,148
because we removed some elements from x's domain.

4337
06:46:54,670 --> 06:47:02,052
And so what we'll do here is we'll go ahead and enqueue, adding something to the q, this arc zx, for all of those neighbors z.

4338
06:47:02,830 --> 06:47:08,356
So we need to add back some arcs to the q in order to continue to enforce arc consistency.

4339
06:47:09,198 --> 06:47:12,548
At the very end, if we make it through all this process, then we can return true.

4340
06:47:13,678 --> 06:47:20,032
But this now is AC3, this algorithm for enforcing arc consistency on a constraint satisfaction problem.

4341
06:47:20,192 --> 06:47:28,228
And the big idea is really just keep track of all of the arcs that we might need to make arc consistent, make it arc consistent by calling the revise function.

4342
06:47:28,494 --> 06:47:36,356
and if we did revise it, then there are some new arcs that might need to be added to the queue in order to make sure that everything is still arc consistent.

4343
06:47:36,592 --> 06:47:41,060
even after we've removed some of the elements from a particular variable's domain.

4344
06:47:42,030 --> 06:47:51,268
So what then would happen if we tried to enforce arc consistency on a graph like this, on a graph where each of these variables has a domain of Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday?

4345
06:47:51,662 --> 06:47:57,092
Well, it turns out that by enforcing arc consistency on this graph, well, it can solve some types of problems.

4346
06:47:57,486 --> 06:47:59,392
Nothing actually changes here.

4347
06:47:59,726 --> 06:48:08,672
For any particular arc, just considering two variables, there's always a way for me to just, for any of the choices I make for one of them, make a choice for the other one,

4348
06:48:08,846 --> 06:48:12,352
because there are three options and I just need the two to be different from each other.

4349
06:48:12,654 --> 06:48:22,112
So this is actually quite easy to just take an arc and just declare that it is arc consistent, because if I pick, Monday for D, then I just pick something that isn't Monday for B.

4350
06:48:22,350 --> 06:48:31,716
In arc consistency, we only consider consistency between a binary constraint between two nodes, and we're not really considering all of the rest of the nodes yet.

4351
06:48:32,494 --> 06:48:40,868
So just using AC3, the enforcement of arc consistency, that can sometimes have the effect of reducing domains to make it easier to find solutions,

4352
06:48:41,230 --> 06:48:43,780
but it will not always actually solve the problem.

4353
06:48:44,174 --> 06:48:47,780
We might still need to somehow search to try and find a solution.

4354
06:48:48,302 --> 06:48:51,712
And we can use classical traditional search algorithms to try to do so.

4355
06:48:51,982 --> 06:48:55,152
You'll recall that a search problem generally consists of these parts.

4356
06:48:55,312 --> 06:49:05,252
We have some initial state, some actions, a transition model that takes me from one state to another state, a goal test to tell me have I satisfied my objective correctly,

4357
06:49:05,582 --> 06:49:11,460
and then some path cost function, because in the case of maze solving, I was trying to get to my goal as quickly as possible.

4358
06:49:12,192 --> 06:49:18,436
So you could formulate a CSP or a constraint satisfaction problem as one of these types of search problems.

4359
06:49:18,832 --> 06:49:27,312
The initial state will just be an empty assignment, where an assignment is just a way for me to assign any particular variable to any particular value.

4360
06:49:27,662 --> 06:49:31,236
So if an empty assignment is no variables are assigned to any values yet,

4361
06:49:31,950 --> 06:49:39,872
then the action I can take is adding some new variable equals value pair to that assignment, saying for this assignment,

4362
06:49:40,032 --> 06:49:42,212
let me add a new value for this variable.

4363
06:49:42,894 --> 06:49:50,436
And the transition model just defines what happens when you take that action. You get a new assignment that has that variable equal to that value inside of it.

4364
06:49:51,086 --> 06:49:57,092
The goal test is just checking to make sure all the variables have been assigned and making sure all the constraints have been satisfied.

4365
06:49:57,632 --> 06:50:02,592
and the path cost function is sort of irrelevant. I don't really care about what the path really is.

4366
06:50:02,766 --> 06:50:07,332
I just care about finding some assignment that actually satisfies all of the constraints.

4367
06:50:07,662 --> 06:50:09,552
So really all the paths have the same cost.

4368
06:50:09,632 --> 06:50:12,036
I don't really care about the path to the goal.

4369
06:50:12,272 --> 06:50:16,100
I just care about the solution itself, much as we've talked about now before.

4370
06:50:17,230 --> 06:50:25,776
The problem here though is that if we just implement this naive search algorithm just by implementing like breadth-first search or depth-first search, this is going to be very, very inefficient.

4371
06:50:25,902 --> 06:50:31,460
And there are ways we can take advantage of efficiencies in the structure of a constraint satisfaction problem itself.

4372
06:50:31,918 --> 06:50:39,776
And one of the key ideas is that we can really just order these variables, and it doesn't matter what order we assign variables in.

4373
06:50:39,936 --> 06:50:47,204
The assignment a equals two and then b equals eight is identical to the assignment of b equals eight and then a equals two.

4374
06:50:47,616 --> 06:50:52,548
Switching the order doesn't really change anything about the fundamental nature of that assignment.

4375
06:50:53,294 --> 06:51:01,732
And so there are some ways that we can try and revise this idea of a search algorithm to apply it specifically for a problem like a constraint satisfaction problem.

4376
06:51:02,190 --> 06:51:08,996
And it turns out the search algorithm we'll generally use when talking about constraint satisfaction problems is something known as backtracking search.

4377
06:51:09,358 --> 06:51:14,532
And the big idea of backtracking search is we'll go ahead and make assignments from variables to values.

4378
06:51:14,830 --> 06:51:22,980
And if ever we get stuck, we arrive at a place where there is no way we can make any forward progress while still preserving the constraints that we need to enforce.

4379
06:51:23,598 --> 06:51:26,628
We'll go ahead and backtrack and try something else instead.

4380
06:51:27,630 --> 06:51:35,332
So the very basic sketch of what backtracking search looks like is it looks like this a function called backtrack that takes as input an assignment

4381
06:51:35,726 --> 06:51:37,540
and a constraint satisfaction problem.

4382
06:51:37,838 --> 06:51:40,256
So initially, we don't have any assigned variables.

4383
06:51:40,398 --> 06:51:45,776
So when we begin backtracking search, this assignment is just going to be the empty assignment with no variables inside of it.

4384
06:51:46,062 --> 06:51:48,452
But we'll see later this is going to be a recursive function.

4385
06:51:49,422 --> 06:51:52,132
So backtrack takes as input the assignment and the problem.

4386
06:51:53,262 --> 06:51:58,884
If the assignment is complete, meaning all of the variables have been assigned, we just return that assignment.

4387
06:51:59,246 --> 06:52:02,564
That of course won't be true initially, because we start with an empty assignment.

4388
06:52:02,766 --> 06:52:04,816
But over time we might add things to that assignment.

4389
06:52:04,942 --> 06:52:07,776
So if ever the assignment actually is complete, then we're done.

4390
06:52:07,982 --> 06:52:10,212
Then just go ahead and return that assignment.

4391
06:52:10,862 --> 06:52:12,484
But otherwise, there's some work to be done.

4392
06:52:13,294 --> 06:52:18,496
So what we'll need to do is select an unassigned variable for this particular problem.

4393
06:52:18,766 --> 06:52:25,572
So we need to take the problem, look at the variables that have already been assigned, and pick a variable that has not yet been assigned.

4394
06:52:26,414 --> 06:52:27,716
And I'll go ahead and take that variable.

4395
06:52:28,238 --> 06:52:32,164
And then I need to consider all of the values in that variable's domain.

4396
06:52:32,654 --> 06:52:41,508
So we'll go ahead and call this domain values function, we'll talk a little more about that later, that takes a variable and just gives me back an ordered list of all of the values in its domain.

4397
06:52:41,934 --> 06:52:52,196
So I've taken a random unselected variable, I'm going to loop over all of the possible values, and the idea is let me just try all of these values as possible values for the variable.

4398
06:52:52,976 --> 06:52:58,724
So if the value is consistent with the assignment so far, it doesn't violate any of the constraints.

4399
06:52:59,342 --> 06:53:04,036
Well then let's go ahead and add variable equals value to the assignment because it's so far consistent.

4400
06:53:04,622 --> 06:53:10,084
And now let's recursively call backtrack to try and make the rest of the assignments also consistent.

4401
06:53:10,894 --> 06:53:16,580
So I'll go ahead and call backtrack on this new assignment that I've added this newest the variable equals value to.

4402
06:53:17,358 --> 06:53:19,972
And now I recursively call backtrack and see what the result is.

4403
06:53:20,654 --> 06:53:22,468
And if the result isn't a failure,

4404
06:53:23,150 --> 06:53:24,580
well then let me just return that result.

4405
06:53:26,958 --> 06:53:29,776
And otherwise, what else could happen?

4406
06:53:29,998 --> 06:53:39,428
Well, if it turns out the result was a failure, well then that means this value was probably a bad choice for this particular variable, because when I assigned this variable equal to that value,

4407
06:53:39,616 --> 06:53:44,932
eventually down the road, I ran into a situation where I violated constraints, there was nothing more I could do.

4408
06:53:45,198 --> 06:53:51,780
So now I'll remove variable equals value from the assignment, effectively backtracking to say, all right, that value didn't work.

4409
06:53:52,096 --> 06:53:54,020
Let's try another value instead.

4410
06:53:55,150 --> 06:54:04,292
And then at the very end, if we were never able to return a complete assignment, we'll just go ahead and return failure, because that means that none of the values worked for this particular variable.

4411
06:54:05,550 --> 06:54:13,860
This now is the idea for backtracking search to take each of the variables, try values for them, and recursively try backtracking search, see if we can make progress.

4412
06:54:14,190 --> 06:54:20,516
And if ever we run into a dead end, we run into a situation where there is no possible value we can choose that satisfies the constraints,

4413
06:54:20,910 --> 06:54:27,972
we return failure, and that propagates up, and eventually we make a different choice by going back and trying something else instead.

4414
06:54:28,910 --> 06:54:30,948
So let's put this algorithm into practice.

4415
06:54:31,150 --> 06:54:38,896
Let's actually try and use backtracking search to solve this problem now, where I need to figure out how to assign each of these courses to an exam slot on Monday

4416
06:54:39,086 --> 06:54:47,268
or Tuesday or Wednesday in such a way that it satisfies these constraints, that each of these edges mean those two classes cannot have an exam on the same day.

4417
06:54:47,886 --> 06:54:50,336
So I can start by just starting at a node.

4418
06:54:50,576 --> 06:54:53,220
Doesn't really matter which I start with, but in this case I'll just start with A.

4419
06:54:54,062 --> 06:54:57,616
And I'll ask a question like, all right, let me loop over the values in the domain.

4420
06:54:57,934 --> 06:55:01,936
And maybe in this case I'll just start with Monday and say, all right, let's go ahead and assign A to Monday.

4421
06:55:02,094 --> 06:55:04,036
We'll just go in order Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday.

4422
06:55:04,750 --> 06:55:06,896
And now let's consider node B. Alright,

4423
06:55:06,976 --> 06:55:13,856
so I've made an assignment to A, so I recursively call backtrack with this new part of the assignment. Now I'm looking to pick another unassigned variable like B.

4424
06:55:14,254 --> 06:55:18,276
And I'll say, all right, maybe I'll start with Monday, because that's the very first value in B's domain.

4425
06:55:18,894 --> 06:55:21,636
And I ask, all right, does Monday violate any constraints?

4426
06:55:22,158 --> 06:55:31,492
And it turns out, yes, it does. It violates this constraint here between A and B, because A and B are now both on Monday, and that doesn't work, because B can't be on the same day as A.

4427
06:55:32,334 --> 06:55:34,896
So that doesn't work, so we might instead try Tuesday.

4428
06:55:35,056 --> 06:55:36,964
Try the next value in B's domain.

4429
06:55:37,136 --> 06:55:39,492
And is that consistent with the assignment so far?

4430
06:55:39,918 --> 06:55:44,452
Well, yeah, B, Tuesday, A, Monday, that is consistent so far because they're not on the same day.

4431
06:55:44,814 --> 06:55:45,296
So that's good.

4432
06:55:45,376 --> 06:55:48,016
Now we can recursively call backtrack, try again.

4433
06:55:48,270 --> 06:55:52,976
Pick another unassigned variable, something like D, and say, all right, let's go through its possible values.

4434
06:55:53,136 --> 06:55:55,140
Is Monday consistent with this assignment?

4435
06:55:55,598 --> 06:55:56,336
Well, yes it is.

4436
06:55:56,622 --> 06:56:02,276
B and D are on different days Monday versus Tuesday, and A and B are also on different days Monday versus Tuesday.

4437
06:56:02,542 --> 06:56:03,456
So that's fine so far

4438
06:56:03,616 --> 06:56:06,976
too. We'll go ahead and try again. Maybe we'll go to this variable here, E.

4439
06:56:07,278 --> 06:56:08,740
Say, can we make that consistent?

4440
06:56:09,038 --> 06:56:12,256
Let's go through the possible values. We've recursively called backtrack.

4441
06:56:12,558 --> 06:56:18,756
We might start with Monday and say, all right, that's not consistent because D and E now have exams on the same day.

4442
06:56:19,118 --> 06:56:21,456
So we might try Tuesday instead, going to the next one.

4443
06:56:21,710 --> 06:56:23,136
Ask, is that consistent?

4444
06:56:23,438 --> 06:56:26,788
Well, no, it's not, because B and E those have exams on the same day.

4445
06:56:27,214 --> 06:56:30,976
And so we try, all right, is Wednesday consistent? And it turns out, all right, yes it is.

4446
06:56:31,150 --> 06:56:34,416
Wednesday is consistent because D and E now have exams on different days.

4447
06:56:34,736 --> 06:56:36,932
B and E now have exams on different days.

4448
06:56:37,166 --> 06:56:38,496
All seems to be well so far.

4449
06:56:38,734 --> 06:56:40,740
I recursively call backtrack,

4450
06:56:41,518 --> 06:56:49,188
select another unassigned variable, we'll say maybe choose C this time, and say, all right, let's try the values that C could take on. Let's start with Monday.

4451
06:56:49,582 --> 06:57:00,164
And it turns out that's not consistent, because now A and C both have exams on the same day. So I try Tuesday and say that's not consistent either because B and C now have exams on the same day.

4452
06:57:00,718 --> 06:57:09,056
And then I say, all right, let's go ahead and try Wednesday, but that's not consistent either, because C and E each have exams on the same day

4453
06:57:09,216 --> 06:57:09,476
too.

4454
06:57:09,902 --> 06:57:15,376
So now we've gone through all of the possible values for C, Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday, and none of them are consistent.

4455
06:57:15,536 --> 06:57:18,016
There is no way we can have a consistent assignment.

4456
06:57:18,350 --> 06:57:20,484
Backtrack in this case will return a failure.

4457
06:57:21,422 --> 06:57:24,260
And so then we'd say, all right, we have to backtrack back to here.

4458
06:57:24,910 --> 06:57:32,708
Well now for E, we've tried all of Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday, and none of those work, because Wednesday, which seemed to work, turned out to be a failure.

4459
06:57:33,422 --> 06:57:35,776
So that means there's no possible way we can assign E.

4460
06:57:36,206 --> 06:57:41,809
So that's a failure too. We have to go back up to D, which means that Monday assignment to D, that must be wrong.

4461
06:57:42,016 --> 06:57:42,896
We must try something else.

4462
06:57:43,374 --> 06:57:43,936
So we can try, all right,

4463
06:57:45,136 --> 06:57:47,428
what if instead of Monday, we try Tuesday?

4464
06:57:47,918 --> 06:57:54,372
Tuesday, it turns out is not consistent, because B and D now have an exam on the same day, but Wednesday, as it turns out, works.

4465
06:57:55,502 --> 06:57:57,456
And now we can begin to make some forward progress again.

4466
06:57:57,614 --> 06:58:00,356
We go back to E and say, all right, which of these values works?

4467
06:58:00,622 --> 06:58:03,396
Monday turns out to work by not violating any constraints.

4468
06:58:03,758 --> 06:58:05,060
Then we go up to C now.

4469
06:58:05,422 --> 06:58:07,588
Monday doesn't work because it violates a constraint.

4470
06:58:08,016 --> 06:58:09,136
violates two actually.

4471
06:58:09,536 --> 06:58:11,936
Tuesday doesn't work because it violates a constraint as well.

4472
06:58:12,142 --> 06:58:13,296
But Wednesday does work.

4473
06:58:13,582 --> 06:58:16,336
Then we can go to the next variable F and say, all right, does Monday work?

4474
06:58:16,496 --> 06:58:19,456
Well, no, it violates a constraint, but Tuesday does work.

4475
06:58:19,726 --> 06:58:23,972
And then finally we can look at the last variable g, recursively calling backtrack one more time.

4476
06:58:24,238 --> 06:58:26,852
Monday is inconsistent, that violates a constraint.

4477
06:58:27,310 --> 06:58:32,196
Tuesday also violates a constraint, but Wednesday, that doesn't violate a constraint.

4478
06:58:33,070 --> 06:58:36,576
And so now at this point, we recursively call backtrack one last time.

4479
06:58:36,814 --> 06:58:42,416
We now have a satisfactory assignment of all of the variables, and at this point, we can say that we are now done.

4480
06:58:42,606 --> 06:58:45,668
We have now been able to successfully assign a variable

4481
06:58:46,350 --> 06:58:51,296
or a value to each one of these variables in such a way that we're not violating any constraints.

4482
06:58:51,456 --> 06:58:59,376
We're going to go ahead and have classes A and E have their exams on Monday, classes B and F can have their exams on Tuesday, and classes C,

4483
06:58:59,616 --> 06:59:05,220
D, and G can have their exams on Wednesday, and there's no violated constraints that might come up there.

4484
06:59:06,222 --> 06:59:08,548
So that then was a graphical look at how this might work.

4485
06:59:08,814 --> 06:59:13,316
Let's now take a look at some code we could use to actually try and solve this problem as well.

4486
06:59:14,478 --> 06:59:18,436
So here I'll go ahead and go into the scheduling directory.

4487
06:59:20,110 --> 06:59:20,816
We're here now.

4488
06:59:21,134 --> 06:59:23,189
We'll start by looking at schedule0.py,

4489
06:59:24,149 --> 06:59:28,256
where here, I define a list of variables, a, b, c, d, e, f, g.

4490
06:59:28,576 --> 06:59:30,116
Those are all of the different classes.

4491
06:59:31,278 --> 06:59:33,988
