4523
07:02:06,318 --> 07:02:10,596
This is just one example of a Python constraint library, where now,

4524
07:02:11,310 --> 07:02:18,564
rather than having to do all the work from scratch inside of schedule one.py, I'm just taking advantage of a library that implements a lot of these ideas already.

4525
07:02:19,118 --> 07:02:23,716
So here, I create a new problem, add variables to it with particular domains.

4526
07:02:24,110 --> 07:02:31,936
I add a whole bunch of these individual constraints where I call add constraint and pass in a function describing what the constraint is.

4527
07:02:32,238 --> 07:02:42,596
And the constraint basically says the function that takes two variables, x and y, and makes sure that x is not equal to y, enforcing the idea that these two classes cannot have exams on the same day.

4528
07:02:43,406 --> 07:02:52,096
And then for any constraint satisfaction problem, I can call get solutions to get all the solutions to that problem, and then for each of those solutions,

4529
07:02:52,336 --> 07:02:54,948
print out what that solution happens to be.

4530
07:02:55,470 --> 07:03:03,216
And if I run Python schedule1.py, I now see there are actually a number of different solutions that can be used to solve the problem.

4531
07:03:03,536 --> 07:03:11,812
there are in fact six different solutions, assignments of variables to values that will give me a satisfactory answer to this constraint satisfaction problem.

4532
07:03:13,038 --> 07:03:20,868
So this then was an implementation of a very basic backtracking search method, where really we just went through each of the variables, picked one that wasn't assigned,

4533
07:03:21,136 --> 07:03:24,096
tried the possible values the variable could take on, and then

4534
07:03:24,896 --> 07:03:28,736
if it worked, if it didn't violate any constraints, then we kept trying other variables.

4535
07:03:28,910 --> 07:03:31,108
And if ever we hit a dead end, we had to backtrack.

4536
07:03:31,502 --> 07:03:39,140
But ultimately, we might be able to be a little bit more intelligent about how we do this in order to improve the efficiency of how we solve these sorts of problems.

4537
07:03:39,502 --> 07:03:48,576
And one thing we might imagine trying to do is going back to this idea of inference, using the knowledge we know to be able to draw conclusions in order to make the rest of the problem

4538
07:03:48,656 --> 07:03:50,404
solving process a little bit easier.

4539
07:03:51,118 --> 07:03:55,136
And let's now go back to where we got stuck in this problem the first time.

4540
07:03:55,310 --> 07:04:02,976
When we were solving this constraint satisfaction problem, we dealt with B, and then we went on to D, and we went ahead and just assigned D to Monday,

4541
07:04:03,086 --> 07:04:05,028
because that seemed to work with the assignment so far.

4542
07:04:05,198 --> 07:04:06,724
It didn't violate any constraints.

4543
07:04:07,534 --> 07:04:11,204
But it turned out that later on that choice turned out to be a bad one.

4544
07:04:11,438 --> 07:04:16,068
That that choice wasn't consistent with the rest of the values that we could take on here.

4545
07:04:16,878 --> 07:04:21,456
And the question is, is there anything we could do to avoid getting into a situation like this?

4546
07:04:21,710 --> 07:04:27,876
Avoid trying to go down a path that's ultimately not going to lead anywhere by taking advantage of knowledge that we have initially.

4547
07:04:28,302 --> 07:04:30,416
And it turns out we do have that kind of knowledge.

4548
07:04:30,656 --> 07:04:39,172
We can look at just the structure of this graph so far, and we can say that right now, C's domain, for example, contains values Monday,

4549
07:04:39,376 --> 07:04:40,836
Tuesday, and Wednesday.

4550
07:04:41,294 --> 07:04:45,856
And based on those values, we can say that this graph is not arc consistent.

4551
07:04:46,126 --> 07:04:55,296
Recall that arc consistency is all about making sure that for every possible value for a particular node, that there is some other value that we are able to choose.

4552
07:04:55,534 --> 07:05:01,536
And as we can see here, Monday and Tuesday are not going to be possible values that we can choose for C.

4553
07:05:01,696 --> 07:05:09,508
They're not going to be consistent with a node like B, for example, because B is equal to Tuesday, which means that C cannot be Tuesday.

4554
07:05:09,774 --> 07:05:12,900
And because A is equal to Monday, C also cannot be Monday.

4555
07:05:13,376 --> 07:05:21,220
So using that information by making C arc consistent with A and B, we could remove Monday and Tuesday from C's domain

4556
07:05:21,550 --> 07:05:24,740
and just leave C with Wednesday, for example.

4557
07:05:25,390 --> 07:05:31,136
And if we continued to try and enforce arc consistency, we'd see there are some other conclusions we can draw as well.

4558
07:05:31,374 --> 07:05:35,012
We see that B's only option is Tuesday and C's only option is Wednesday.

4559
07:05:35,342 --> 07:05:45,092
And so if we want to make E arc consistent, well E can't be Tuesday, because that wouldn't be arc consistent with B, and E can't be Wednesday, because that wouldn't be arc consistent with C.

4560
07:05:45,454 --> 07:05:48,676
So we can go ahead and say E and just set that equal to Monday, for example.

4561
07:05:49,198 --> 07:05:57,536
And then we can begin to do this process again and again, that in order to make D arc consistent with B and E, then D would have to be Wednesday, that's the only possible option.

4562
07:05:57,870 --> 07:06:00,996
And likewise we can make the same judgments for F and G as well.

4563
07:06:01,422 --> 07:06:09,376
And it turns out that without having to do any additional search, just by enforcing arc consistency, we were able to actually figure out what

4564
07:06:09,376 --> 07:06:13,604
the assignment of all the variables should be without needing to backtrack at all.

4565
07:06:14,318 --> 07:06:22,656
And the way we did that is by interleaving the search process and the inference step, by this step of trying to enforce arc consistency.

4566
07:06:22,894 --> 07:06:34,532
And the algorithm to do this is often called just the maintaining arc consistency algorithm, which just enforces arc consistency every time we make a new assignment of a value to an existing variable.

4567
07:06:34,894 --> 07:06:41,696
So sometimes we can enforce our consistency using that AC3 algorithm at the very beginning of the problem, before we even begin searching,

4568
07:06:41,870 --> 07:06:45,316
in order to limit the domain of the variables in order to make it easier to search.

4569
07:06:45,646 --> 07:06:54,052
But we can also take advantage of the interleaving of enforcing arc consistency with search, such that every time in the search process we make a new assignment,

4570
07:06:54,382 --> 07:07:01,892
we go ahead and enforce arc consistency as well to make sure that we're just eliminating possible values from domains whenever possible.

4571
07:07:02,606 --> 07:07:03,492
And how do we do this?

4572
07:07:03,822 --> 07:07:12,132
Well, this is really equivalent to just every time we make a new assignment to a variable x, we'll go ahead and call our AC3 algorithm,

4573
07:07:12,366 --> 07:07:16,164
this algorithm that enforces arc consistency on a constraint satisfaction problem.

4574
07:07:16,622 --> 07:07:26,372
And we go ahead and call that starting out with a Q not of all of the arcs, which we did originally, but just of all of the arcs that we want to make arc consistent with x,

4575
07:07:26,576 --> 07:07:28,580
this thing that we have just made an assignment to.

4576
07:07:28,942 --> 07:07:35,616
So all arcs y x, where y is a neighbor of x, something that shares a constraint with x, for example.

4577
07:07:36,494 --> 07:07:43,620
And by maintaining our consistency in the backtracking search process, we can ultimately make our search process a little bit more efficient.

4578
07:07:43,918 --> 07:07:47,424
And so this is the revised version of this backtrack function.

4579
07:07:47,726 --> 07:07:50,180
Same as before, the changes here are highlighted in yellow.

4580
07:07:50,464 --> 07:07:57,744
Every time we add a new variable equals value to our assignment, we'll go ahead and run this inference procedure, which might do a number of different things,

4581
07:07:57,824 --> 07:08:04,708
but one thing it could do is call the maintaining arc consistency algorithm to make sure we're able to enforce art consistency on the problem.

4582
07:08:05,166 --> 07:08:09,184
And we might be able to draw new inferences as a result of that process.

4583
07:08:09,424 --> 07:08:14,064
Get new guarantees of this variable needs to be equal to that value, for example.

4584
07:08:14,286 --> 07:08:16,356
That might happen one time, it might happen many times.

4585
07:08:16,654 --> 07:08:23,204
And so long as those inferences are not a failure, as long as they don't lead to a situation where there is no possible way to make forward progress,

4586
07:08:23,758 --> 07:08:30,916
well then we can go ahead and add those inferences, those new knowledge, that new pieces of knowledge I know about what variables should be assigned to what values.

4587
07:08:31,184 --> 07:08:39,824
I can add those to the assignment in order to more quickly make forward progress by taking advantage of information that I can just deduce, information I know

4588
07:08:40,078 --> 07:08:43,396
based on the rest of the structure of the constraint satisfaction problem.

4589
07:08:44,206 --> 07:08:52,224
And the only other change I'll need to make now is if it turns out this value doesn't work, well then down here I'll go ahead and need to remove not only variable equals value,

4590
07:08:52,384 --> 07:08:56,932
but also any of those inferences that I made, remove that from the assignment as well.

4591
07:08:57,358 --> 07:09:03,172
And so here, then, we're often able to solve the problem by backtracking less than we might originally have needed to,

4592
07:09:03,344 --> 07:09:11,744
just by taking advantage of the fact that every time we make a new assignment of one variable to one value, that might reduce the domains of other variables as well.

4593
07:09:11,982 --> 07:09:18,564
and we can use that information to begin to more quickly draw conclusions in order to try and solve the problem more efficiently as well.

4594
07:09:19,374 --> 07:09:25,664
And it turns out there are other heuristics we can use to try and improve the efficiency of our search process as well.

4595
07:09:25,902 --> 07:09:31,748
And it really boils down to a couple of these functions that I've talked about, but we haven't really talked about how they're working.

4596
07:09:32,238 --> 07:09:41,764
And one of them is this function here, select unassigned variable, where we're selecting some variable in the constraint satisfaction problem that has not yet been assigned.

4597
07:09:42,286 --> 07:09:48,864
So far, I've sort of just been selecting variables random, just like picking one variable and one unassigned variable, in order to decide,

4598
07:09:49,134 --> 07:09:52,864
all right, this is the variable that we're going to assign next, and then going from there.

4599
07:09:53,230 --> 07:10:02,304
But it turns out that by being a little bit intelligent, by following certain heuristics, we might be able to make the search process much more efficient just by choosing very carefully

4600
07:10:02,544 --> 07:10:04,452
which variable we should explore next.

4601
07:10:05,390 --> 07:10:14,308
So some of those heuristics include the minimum remaining values, or MRV heuristic, which generally says that if I have a choice between which variable I should select,

4602
07:10:14,830 --> 07:10:21,092
I should select the variable with the smallest domain, the variable that has the fewest number of remaining values left.

4603
07:10:21,454 --> 07:10:30,692
With the idea being, if there are only two remaining values left, well I may as well prune one of them very quickly in order to get to the other, because one of those two has got to be the solution

4604
07:10:30,864 --> 07:10:32,644
if a solution does exist.

4605
07:10:33,518 --> 07:10:40,544
Sometimes minimum remaining values might not give a conclusive result if all the nodes have the same number of remaining values, for example.

4606
07:10:40,878 --> 07:10:45,316
And in that case, another heuristic that can be helpful to look at is the degree heuristic.

4607
07:10:45,664 --> 07:10:52,544
The degree of a node is the number of nodes that are attached to that node, the number of nodes that are constrained by that particular node.

4608
07:10:52,814 --> 07:10:58,852
And if you imagine which variable should I choose, should I choose a variable that has a high degree that is connected to a lot of different things,

4609
07:10:59,182 --> 07:11:02,500
or a variable with a low degree that is not connected to a lot of different things?

4610
07:11:02,894 --> 07:11:11,140
Well, it can often make sense to choose the variable that has the highest degree, that is connected to the most other nodes, as the thing you would search first.

4611
07:11:11,726 --> 07:11:12,544
Why is that the case?

4612
07:11:12,878 --> 07:11:19,744
Well, it's because by choosing a variable with a high degree, that is immediately going to constrain the rest of the variables more,

4613
07:11:19,982 --> 07:11:26,404
and it's more likely to be able to eliminate large sections of the state space that you don't need to search through at all.

4614
07:11:26,926 --> 07:11:28,388
So, what could this actually look like?

4615
07:11:29,230 --> 07:11:30,884
Let's go back to this search problem here.

4616
07:11:31,342 --> 07:11:37,664
In this particular case, I've made an assignment here, I've made an assignment here, and the question is, what should I look at

4617
07:11:37,664 --> 07:11:45,796
next? And according to the minimum remaining values heuristic, what I should choose is the variable that has the fewest remaining possible values.

4618
07:11:46,126 --> 07:11:52,864
And in this case, that's this node here, node C, that only has one variable left in this domain, which in this case is Wednesday, which is a

4619
07:11:53,664 --> 07:11:58,064
very reasonable choice of a next assignment to make, because I know it's the only option, for example.

4620
07:11:58,224 --> 07:12:06,468
I know that the only possible option for C is Wednesday, so I may as well make that assignment and then potentially explore the rest of the space after that.

4621
07:12:07,310 --> 07:12:12,708
But meanwhile, at the very start of the problem, when I didn't have any knowledge of what nodes should have what values yet,

4622
07:12:12,944 --> 07:12:20,624
I still had to pick what node should be the first one that I try and assign a value to. And I arbitrarily just chose the one at the top, node A, originally.

4623
07:12:20,974 --> 07:12:22,500
But we can be more intelligent about that.

4624
07:12:23,438 --> 07:12:24,964
We can look at this particular graph.

4625
07:12:25,422 --> 07:12:30,916
All of them have domains of the same size, domain of size three. So minimum remaining values doesn't really help us there.

4626
07:12:31,246 --> 07:12:34,624
But we might notice that node E has the highest degree.

4627
07:12:34,784 --> 07:12:36,624
It is connected to the most things.

4628
07:12:36,910 --> 07:12:43,492
And so perhaps it makes sense to begin our search rather than starting at node A at the very top, start with the node with the highest degree.

4629
07:12:43,694 --> 07:12:50,784
Start by searching from node E, because from there, that's going to much more easily allow us to enforce the constraints that are nearby,

4630
07:12:50,944 --> 07:12:54,820
eliminating large portions of the search space that I might not need to search through.

4631
07:12:55,342 --> 07:13:04,356
And in fact, by starting with E, we can immediately then assign other variables, and following that, we can actually assign the rest of the variables without needing to do any backtracking at all,

4632
07:13:04,544 --> 07:13:06,628
even if I'm not using this inference procedure.

4633
07:13:06,864 --> 07:13:13,956
Just by starting with a node that has a high degree that is going to very quickly restrict the possible values that other nodes can take on.

4634
07:13:14,862 --> 07:13:19,744
So that then is how we can go about selecting an unassigned variable in a particular order.

4635
07:13:19,904 --> 07:13:25,744
Rather than randomly picking a variable, if we're a little bit intelligent about how we choose it, we can make our search process much,

4636
07:13:25,824 --> 07:13:31,812
much more efficient by making sure we don't have to search through portions of the search space that ultimately aren't going to matter.

4637
07:13:32,782 --> 07:13:37,664
The other variable we haven't really talked about, the other function here, is this domain values function.

4638
07:13:37,870 --> 07:13:44,708
This domain values function that takes a variable and gives me back a sequence of all of the values inside of that variable's domain.

4639
07:13:45,870 --> 07:13:51,588
The naive way to approach it is what we did before, which is just go in order. Go Monday, then Tuesday, then Wednesday.

4640
07:13:51,918 --> 07:13:55,664
But the problem is that going in that order might not be the most efficient order to search in.

4641
07:13:55,918 --> 07:14:03,812
That sometimes it might be more efficient to choose values that are likely to be solutions first and then go to other values.

4642
07:14:04,302 --> 07:14:09,764
Now, how do you assess whether a value is likelier to lead to a solution or less likely to lead to a solution?

4643
07:14:10,190 --> 07:14:13,220
Well, one thing you can take a look at is how many

4644
07:14:14,030 --> 07:14:21,156
constraints get added, how many things get removed from domains as you make this new assignment of a variable to this particular value.

4645
07:14:21,454 --> 07:14:28,612
And the heuristic we can use here is the least constraining value heuristic, which is the idea that we should return variables in order

4646
07:14:28,942 --> 07:14:39,620
based on the number of choices that are ruled out for neighboring values. And I want to start with the least constraining value, the value that rules out the fewest possible options.

4647
07:14:40,014 --> 07:14:49,284
And the idea there is that if all I care about doing is finding a solution, if I start with a value that rules out a lot of other choices,

4648
07:14:49,582 --> 07:14:56,064
I'm ruling out a lot of possibilities that maybe is going to make it less likely that this particular choice leads to a solution.

4649
07:14:56,398 --> 07:15:01,904
Whereas on the other hand, if I have a variable and I start by choosing a value that doesn't rule out very much,

4650
07:15:02,126 --> 07:15:06,244
well then I still have a lot of space where there might be a solution that I could ultimately find.

4651
07:15:06,574 --> 07:15:14,980
And this might seem a little bit counterintuitive and a little bit at odds with what we were talking about before, where I said, when you're picking a variable, you should pick the variable

4652
07:15:15,344 --> 07:15:18,212
that is going to have the fewest possible values remaining.

4653
07:15:18,384 --> 07:15:21,924
But here I want to pick the value for the variable that is the least constraining.

4654
07:15:22,158 --> 07:15:30,144
But the general idea is that when I am picking a variable, I would like to prune large portions of the search space by just choosing a variable that is going to allow me

4655
07:15:30,144 --> 07:15:31,972
to quickly eliminate possible options.

4656
07:15:32,526 --> 07:15:40,064
Whereas here, within a particular variable, as I'm considering values that that variable could take on, I would like to just find a solution.

4657
07:15:40,334 --> 07:15:47,984
And so what I want to do is ultimately choose a value that still leaves open the possibility of me finding a solution to be as likely as possible.

4658
07:15:48,144 --> 07:15:55,588
By not ruling out many options, I leave open the possibility that I can still find a solution without needing to go back later and backtrack.

4659
07:15:56,110 --> 07:16:00,464
So an example of that might be in this particular situation here, if I'm trying to choose a

4660
07:16:01,584 --> 07:16:05,824
value for node C here, that C is equal to either Tuesday or Wednesday.

4661
07:16:06,062 --> 07:16:12,644
We know it can't be Monday, because it conflicts with this domain here, where we already know that A is Monday, so C must be Tuesday or Wednesday.

4662
07:16:13,326 --> 07:16:17,476
And the question is, should I try Tuesday first, or should I try Wednesday first?

4663
07:16:18,062 --> 07:16:20,996
And if I try Tuesday, what gets ruled out?

4664
07:16:21,264 --> 07:16:27,424
Well, one option gets ruled out here, a second option gets ruled out here, and a third option gets ruled out here.

4665
07:16:27,726 --> 07:16:30,468
So choosing Tuesday would rule out three possible options.

4666
07:16:30,894 --> 07:16:32,260
And what about choosing Wednesday?

4667
07:16:32,590 --> 07:16:38,340
Well, choosing Wednesday would rule out one option here, and it would rule out one option there, and so I have two choices.

4668
07:16:38,702 --> 07:16:46,404
I can choose Tuesday that rules out three options, or Wednesday that rules out two options, and according to the least constraining value heuristic,

4669
07:16:46,624 --> 07:16:56,049
what I should probably do is go ahead and choose Wednesday, the one that rules out the fewest number of possible options, leaving open as many chances as possible for me to eventually find the

4670
07:16:56,224 --> 07:16:58,020
solution inside of this state space.

4671
07:16:58,286 --> 07:17:07,904
And ultimately, if you continue this process, we will find a solution, an assignment of variables to values that allows us to give each of these exams, uh

4672
07:17:08,174 --> 07:17:15,044
each of these classes an exam date that doesn't conflict with anyone that happens to be enrolled in two classes at the same time.

4673
07:17:16,174 --> 07:17:21,284
So the big takeaway now with all of this is that there are a number of different ways we can formulate a problem.

4674
07:17:21,742 --> 07:17:29,072
The ways we've looked at today are we can formulate a problem as a local search problem, a problem where we're looking at a current node and moving to a neighbor

4675
07:17:29,230 --> 07:17:32,804
based on whether that neighbor is better or worse than the current node that we are looking at.

4676
07:17:33,166 --> 07:17:41,028
We looked at formulating problems as linear programs, where just by putting things in terms of equations and constraints, we're able to solve problems a little bit more efficiently.

4677
07:17:41,806 --> 07:17:51,012
And we saw formulating a problem as a constraint satisfaction problem, creating this graph of all of the constraints that connect two variables that have some constraint between them,

4678
07:17:51,246 --> 07:17:55,428
and using that information to be able to figure out what the solution should be.

4679
07:17:56,302 --> 07:18:02,992
And so the takeaway of all of this now is that if we have some problem in artificial intelligence that we would like to use AI to be able to solve,

4680
07:18:03,278 --> 07:18:10,352
whether that's trying to figure out where hospitals should be, or trying to solve the traveling salesman problem, trying to optimize productions and costs and whatnot,

4681
07:18:10,510 --> 07:18:18,272
or trying to figure out how to satisfy certain constraints, whether that's in a Sudoku puzzle, or whether that's in trying to figure out how to schedule exams for a university,

4682
07:18:18,432 --> 07:18:20,964
Or any number of a wide variety of types of problems.

4683
07:18:21,232 --> 07:18:26,832
if we can formulate that problem as one of these sorts of problems, then we can use these known algorithms.

4684
07:18:26,912 --> 07:18:33,028
these algorithms for enforcing art, consistency and backtracking search, these hill climbing and simulated annealing algorithms,

4685
07:18:33,232 --> 07:18:37,792
these simplex algorithms and interior point algorithms that can be used to solve linear programs.

4686
07:18:37,998 --> 07:18:45,988
That we can use those techniques to begin to solve a whole wide variety of problems, all in this world of optimization inside of artificial intelligence.

4687
07:18:46,606 --> 07:18:49,472
This was an introduction to artificial intelligence with Python for today.

4688
07:18:49,710 --> 07:18:50,628
We will see you next time.

4689
07:19:29,710 --> 07:19:37,792
Today, we're going to turn to the world of learning, in particular the idea of machine learning, which generally refers to the idea where we are not going to give the computer explicit

4690
07:19:37,838 --> 07:19:39,620
instructions for how to perform a task.

4691
07:19:40,078 --> 07:19:48,512
but rather, we are going to give the computer access to information in the form of data or patterns that it can learn from, and let the computer try and figure out what those patterns are,

4692
07:19:48,752 --> 07:19:52,324
try and understand that data to be able to perform a task on its own.

4693
07:19:52,718 --> 07:19:55,872
Now, machine learning comes in a number of different forms, and it's a very wide field.

4694
07:19:56,110 --> 07:20:03,072
So today we'll explore some of the foundational algorithms and ideas that are behind a lot of the different areas within machine learning.

4695
07:20:03,246 --> 07:20:08,452
And one of the most popular is the idea of supervised machine learning, or just supervised learning.

4696
07:20:08,750 --> 07:20:11,012
And supervised learning is a particular type of task.

4697
07:20:11,406 --> 07:20:18,596
It refers to the task where we give the computer access to a data set, where that data set consists of input-output pairs.

4698
07:20:19,086 --> 07:20:26,692
And what we would like the computer to do is we would like our AI to be able to figure out some function that maps inputs to outputs.

4699
07:20:27,374 --> 07:20:33,380
So we have a whole bunch of data that generally consists of some kind of input, some evidence, some information that the computer will have access to.

4700
07:20:33,774 --> 07:20:39,652
And we would like the computer, based on that input information, to predict what some output is going to be.

4701
07:20:39,982 --> 07:20:48,932
And we'll give it some data so that the computer can train its model on to begin to understand how it is that this information works and how it is that the inputs and outputs relate to each other.

4702
07:20:49,454 --> 07:20:55,876
But ultimately, we hope that our computer will be able to figure out some function that, given those inputs, is able to get those outputs.

4703
07:20:56,622 --> 07:20:59,232
There are a couple of different tasks within supervised learning.

4704
07:20:59,438 --> 07:21:02,564
The one we'll focus on and start with is known as classification.

4705
07:21:02,798 --> 07:21:11,392
And classification is the problem where if I give you a whole bunch of inputs, you need to figure out some way to map those inputs into discrete categories,

4706
07:21:11,566 --> 07:21:17,152
where you can decide what those categories are, and it's the job of the computer to predict what those categories are going to be.

4707
07:21:17,358 --> 07:21:26,212
So that might be, for example, I give you information about a banknote, like a US dollar, and I'm asking you to predict for me does it belong to the category of authentic banknotes,

4708
07:21:26,446 --> 07:21:29,060
or does it belong to the category of counterfeit banknotes?

4709
07:21:29,472 --> 07:21:35,236
You need to categorize the input, and we want to train the computer to figure out some function to be able to do that calculation.

4710
07:21:36,110 --> 07:21:44,672
Another example might be the case of weather, something we've talked about a little bit so far in this class, where we would like to predict on a given day, is it going to rain on that day,

4711
07:21:44,910 --> 07:21:46,244
is it going to be cloudy on that day?

4712
07:21:46,574 --> 07:21:54,532
And before we've seen how we could do this if we really give the computer all the exact probabilities for, if these are the conditions, what's the probability of rain?

4713
07:21:54,766 --> 07:22:00,196
Oftentimes we don't have access to that information though, but what we do have access to is a whole bunch of data.

4714
07:22:00,622 --> 07:22:08,272
So if we wanted to be able to predict something like, is it going to rain or is it not going to rain, we would give the computer historical information about days when it was

4715
07:22:08,272 --> 07:22:13,668
raining and days when it was not raining, and ask the computer to look for patterns in that data.

4716
07:22:14,126 --> 07:22:15,428
So what might that data look like?

4717
07:22:15,758 --> 07:22:18,192
Well, we could structure that data in a table like this.

4718
07:22:18,432 --> 07:22:27,872
might be what our table looks like, where for any particular day, going back, we have information about that day's humidity, that day's air pressure, and then importantly, we have a label,

4719
07:22:28,110 --> 07:22:32,752
something where the human has said that on this particular day it was raining or it was not raining.

4720
07:22:33,006 --> 07:22:42,272
So you could fill in this table with a whole bunch of data, and what makes this what we would call a supervised learning exercise is that a human has gone in and labeled each of these data points.

4721
07:22:42,432 --> 07:22:48,612
Said that on this day, when these were the values for the humidity and pressure, that day was a rainy day, and this day was a not rainy day.

4722
07:22:49,230 --> 07:22:58,272
And what we would like the computer to be able to do then is to be able to figure out, given these inputs, given like the humidity and the pressure, can the computer predict what label

4723
07:22:58,432 --> 07:22:59,712
should be associated with that day?

4724
07:22:59,872 --> 07:23:05,700
Does that day look more like it's going to be a day that rains, or does it look more like a day when it's not going to rain?

4725
07:23:06,606 --> 07:23:14,752
Put a little bit more mathematically, you can think of this as a function that takes two inputs, the inputs being the data points that our computer will have access to,

4726
07:23:14,832 --> 07:23:16,272
things like humidity and pressure.

4727
07:23:16,558 --> 07:23:20,004
So we could write a function f that takes as input, both humidity and pressure.

4728
07:23:20,526 --> 07:23:29,072
And then the output is going to be what category we would ascribe to these particular input points, what label we would associate with that input.

4729
07:23:29,262 --> 07:23:37,392
So we've seen a couple of example data points here, where given this value for humidity and this value for pressure, we predict is it going to rain or is it not going to rain?

4730
07:23:37,552 --> 07:23:40,432
And that's information that we just gathered from the world.

4731
07:23:40,592 --> 07:23:47,556
We measured on various different days what the humidity and pressure were, we observed whether or not we saw rain or no rain on that particular day.

4732
07:23:48,046 --> 07:23:51,428
And this function f is what we would like to approximate.

4733
07:23:51,822 --> 07:23:55,552
Now the computer and we humans don't really know exactly how this function f works.

4734
07:23:55,632 --> 07:23:57,156
It's probably quite a complex function.

4735
07:23:57,806 --> 07:24:00,548
So what we're going to do instead is attempt to estimate it.

4736
07:24:00,942 --> 07:24:04,272
We would like to come up with a hypothesis function, h,

4737
07:24:05,102 --> 07:24:08,112
which is going to try to approximate what f does.

4738
07:24:08,272 --> 07:24:15,312
We want to come up with some function h that will also take the same inputs and will also produce an output, rain or no rain.

4739
07:24:15,694 --> 07:24:19,492
And ideally, we'd like these two functions to agree as much as possible.

4740
07:24:19,982 --> 07:24:26,752
So the goal then of the supervised learning classification tasks is going to be to figure out what does that function h look like?

4741
07:24:26,912 --> 07:24:34,468
How can we begin to estimate, given all of this information, in all of this data, what category or what label should be assigned to a particular data point?

4742
07:24:35,278 --> 07:24:36,992
So where could you begin doing this?

4743
07:24:37,358 --> 07:24:43,152
Well, a reasonable thing to do, especially in this situation, I have two numerical values, is I could try to plot this on

4744
07:24:43,952 --> 07:24:46,948
a graph that has two axes, an x-axis and a y-axis.

4745
07:24:47,438 --> 07:24:53,952
And in this case, we're just going to be using two numerical values as input, but these same types of ideas scale as you add more and more inputs as well.

4746
07:24:54,112 --> 07:25:00,592
We'll be plotting things in two dimensions, but as we'll soon see, you could add more inputs and just imagine things in multiple dimensions.

4747
07:25:00,672 --> 07:25:10,032
And while we humans have trouble conceptualizing anything really beyond three dimensions, at least visually, a computer has no problem with trying to imagine things in many, many more dimensions.

4748
07:25:10,192 --> 07:25:14,352
That for a computer, each dimension is just some separate number that it is keeping track of.

4749
07:25:14,446 --> 07:25:20,484
So it wouldn't be unreasonable for a computer to think in ten dimensions or 100 dimensions to be able to try to solve a problem.

4750
07:25:20,814 --> 07:25:28,708
But for now we've got two inputs, so we'll graph things along two axes, an x-axis, which will here represent humidity, and a y axis, which here represents pressure.

4751
07:25:29,358 --> 07:25:36,912
And what we might do is say, let's take all of the days that were raining and just try to plot them on this graph and see where they fall on this graph.

4752
07:25:37,006 --> 07:25:45,892
And here might be all of the rainy days, where each rainy day is one of these blue dots here that corresponds to a particular value for humidity and a particular value for pressure.

4753
07:25:46,766 --> 07:25:48,992
And then I might do the same thing with the days that were not rainy.

4754
07:25:49,262 --> 07:25:56,420
Say take all the not rainy days, figure out what their values were for each of these two inputs, and go ahead and plot them on this graph as well.

4755
07:25:56,878 --> 07:25:58,032
And I've here plotted them in red.

4756
07:25:58,112 --> 07:26:02,180
So blue here stands for a rainy day, red here stands for a not rainy day.

4757
07:26:02,734 --> 07:26:06,372
And this then is the input that my computer has access to all of this input.

4758
07:26:07,022 --> 07:26:15,268
And what I would like the computer to be able to do is to train a model such that if I'm ever presented with a new input, that doesn't have a label associated with it,

4759
07:26:15,552 --> 07:26:17,508
something like this white dot here.

4760
07:26:18,030 --> 07:26:26,592
I would like to predict, given those values for each of the two inputs, is: should we classify it as a blue dot, a rainy day, or should we classify it as a red dot?

4761
07:26:26,862 --> 07:26:27,588
a not rainy day?

4762
07:26:28,494 --> 07:26:33,872
And if you're just looking at this picture graphically, trying to say, all right, this white dot, does it look like it belongs to the blue category,

4763
07:26:33,952 --> 07:26:36,068
or does it look like it belongs to the red category?

4764
07:26:36,432 --> 07:26:40,192
I think most people would agree that it probably belongs to the blue category.

4765
07:26:40,272 --> 07:26:44,516
And why is that? Well, it looks like it's close to other blue dots.

4766
07:26:45,198 --> 07:26:48,912
And that's not a very formal notion, but it's a notion that we'll formalize in just a moment.

4767
07:26:49,006 --> 07:26:56,432
That because it seems to be close to this blue dot here, like nothing else is closer to it, then we might say that it should be categorized as blue.

4768
07:26:56,592 --> 07:27:01,472
It should fall into that category of I think that day is going to be a rainy day, based on that input.

4769
07:27:01,712 --> 07:27:04,032
Might not be totally accurate, but it's a pretty good

4770
07:27:04,032 --> 07:27:11,472
guess. And this type of algorithm is actually a very popular and common machine learning algorithm known as nearest neighbor classification.

4771
07:27:11,694 --> 07:27:18,192
It's an algorithm for solving these classification type problems. And in nearest neighbor classification, it's going to perform this algorithm.

4772
07:27:18,272 --> 07:27:24,352
What it'll do is given an input, it will choose the class of the nearest data point to that input.

4773
07:27:24,558 --> 07:27:29,316
By class, we just here mean category, like rain or no rain, counterfeit or not counterfeit.

4774
07:27:29,742 --> 07:27:34,116
And we choose the category or the class based on the nearest data point.

4775
07:27:34,638 --> 07:27:39,460
So given all that data, we just looked at is the nearest data point a blue point or is it a red point?

4776
07:27:39,918 --> 07:27:44,432
And depending on the answer to that question, we were able to make some sort of judgment.

4777
07:27:44,592 --> 07:27:48,452
We were able to say something like, we think it's going to be blue or we think it's going to be

4778
07:27:48,512 --> 07:27:52,432
red. So likewise we could apply this to other data points that we encounter as well.

4779
07:27:52,750 --> 07:27:59,396
If suddenly this data point comes about, well, its nearest data is red, so we would go ahead and classify this as a red point, not raining.

4780
07:28:00,206 --> 07:28:05,092
Things get a little bit trickier though, when you look at a point like this white point over here.

4781
07:28:05,710 --> 07:28:06,992
And you ask the same sort of question.

4782
07:28:07,232 --> 07:28:13,952
Should it belong to the category of blue points, the rainy days, or should it belong to the category of red points, the not rainy

4783
07:28:13,952 --> 07:28:20,832
days? Now, nearest neighbor classification would say the way you solve this problem is look at which point is nearest to that point.

4784
07:28:20,992 --> 07:28:28,932
You look at this nearest point and say it's red, it's a not rainy day, and therefore, according to nearest neighbor classification, I would say that this unlabeled point,

4785
07:28:29,230 --> 07:28:32,900
well, that should also be red. It should also be classified as a not rainy day.

4786
07:28:33,742 --> 07:28:41,220
But your intuition, might think that that's a reasonable judgment to make, that it's the closest thing is a not rainy day, so may as well guess that it's a not rainy day.

4787
07:28:41,486 --> 07:28:49,220
But it's probably also reasonable to look at the bigger picture of things, and to say, yes, it is true that the nearest point to it was a red point,

4788
07:28:49,582 --> 07:28:52,420
but it's surrounded by a whole bunch of other blue points.

4789
07:28:53,070 --> 07:28:58,820
So looking at the bigger picture, there's potentially an argument to be made that this point should actually be blue.

4790
07:28:59,310 --> 07:29:01,392
And with only this data, we actually don't know for sure.

4791
07:29:01,632 --> 07:29:10,148
We are given some input, something we're trying to predict, and we don't necessarily know what the output is going to be. So in this case, which one is correct is difficult to say.

4792
07:29:10,432 --> 07:29:17,444
But oftentimes considering more than just a single neighbor, considering multiple neighbors, can sometimes give us a better result.

4793
07:29:18,158 --> 07:29:26,032
And so there's a variant on the nearest neighbor classification algorithm that is known as the K nearest neighbor classification algorithm, where k

4794
07:29:26,254 --> 07:29:30,724
is some parameter, some number that we choose for how many neighbors are we going to look at.

4795
07:29:30,992 --> 07:29:37,156
So one nearest neighbor classification is what we saw before. Just pick the one nearest neighbor and use that category.

4796
07:29:37,710 --> 07:29:46,980
But with k nearest neighbor classification, where k might be three or five or seven, to say look at the three or five or seven closest neighbors, closest data points to that point,

4797
07:29:47,438 --> 07:29:48,484
works a little bit differently.

4798
07:29:48,814 --> 07:29:55,312
This algorithm will give it an input, choose the most common class out of the k nearest data points to that input.

4799
07:29:55,534 --> 07:30:03,472
So if we look at the five nearest points, and you know, three of them say it's raining and two of them say it's not raining, we'll go with the three instead of the two.

4800
07:30:03,872 --> 07:30:13,732
Because each one effectively gets one vote towards what they believe the category ought to be, and ultimately you choose the category that has the most votes as a consequence of that.

4801
07:30:14,606 --> 07:30:18,752
So, K-nearest neighbor classification, fairly straightforward one to understand intuitively.

4802
07:30:18,912 --> 07:30:21,572
You just look at the neighbors and figure out what the answer might be.

4803
07:30:21,806 --> 07:30:27,716
And it turns out this can work very, very well for solving a whole variety of different types of classification problems.

4804
07:30:28,366 --> 07:30:30,832
But not every model is going to work under every situation.

4805
07:30:30,912 --> 07:30:38,272
And so one of the things we'll take a look at today, especially in the context of supervised machine learning, is that there are a number of different approaches to machine learning,

4806
07:30:38,432 --> 07:30:45,392
a number of different algorithms that we can apply, all solving the same type of problem, all solving some kind of classification problem

4807
07:30:45,550 --> 07:30:48,676
where we want to take inputs and organize it into different categories.

4808
07:30:49,038 --> 07:30:53,312
And no one algorithm is necessarily always going to be better than some other algorithm.

4809
07:30:53,550 --> 07:30:54,564
They each have their trade-offs.

4810
07:30:54,672 --> 07:31:01,472
And maybe depending on the data, one type of algorithm is going to be better suited to trying to model that information than some other algorithm.

4811
07:31:01,646 --> 07:31:04,352
And so this is what a lot of machine learning research ends up being about.

4812
07:31:04,432 --> 07:31:10,916
That when you're trying to apply machine learning techniques, you're often looking not just at one particular algorithm, but trying multiple different algorithms,

4813
07:31:11,312 --> 07:31:17,700
trying to see what is going to give you the best results for trying to predict some function that maps inputs to outputs.

4814
07:31:18,542 --> 07:31:22,032
So what then are the drawbacks of K nearest neighbor classification?

4815
07:31:22,286 --> 07:31:23,268
Well, there are a couple.

4816
07:31:23,534 --> 07:31:31,632
One might be that in a naive approach, at least, it could be fairly slow to have to go through and measure the distance between a point and every single one of these points that exist

4817
07:31:31,792 --> 07:31:31,952
here.

4818
07:31:32,206 --> 07:31:37,872
Now there are ways of trying to get around that. There are data structures that can help to make it more quickly to be able to find these neighbors.

4819
07:31:38,062 --> 07:31:46,404
There are also techniques you can use to try and prune some of this data, remove some of the data points so that you're only left with the relevant data points just to make it a little bit easier.

4820
07:31:47,342 --> 07:31:52,996
But ultimately, what we might like to do is come up with another way of trying to do this classification.

4821
07:31:53,294 --> 07:31:57,348
And one way of trying to do the classification was looking at what are the neighboring points.

4822
07:31:57,712 --> 07:32:08,420
But another way might be to try to look at all of the data and see if we can come up with some decision boundary, some boundary that will separate the rainy days from the not rainy days.

4823
07:32:08,718 --> 07:32:12,004
And in the case of two dimensions, we can do that by drawing a line, for example.

4824
07:32:12,462 --> 07:32:22,500
So what we might want to try to do is just find some line, find some separator that divides the rainy days, the blue points over here, from the not rainy days, the red points over there.

4825
07:32:22,926 --> 07:32:31,012
We're now trying a different approach in contrast with the nearest neighbor approach, which just looked at local data around the input data point that we cared about.

4826
07:32:31,278 --> 07:32:39,300
Now what we're doing is trying to use a technique known as linear regression to find some sort of line that will separate the two halves from each other.

4827
07:32:39,918 --> 07:32:46,148
Now sometimes it'll actually be possible to come up with some line that perfectly separates all the rainy days from the not rainy days.

4828
07:32:46,512 --> 07:32:50,752
Realistically, though, this is probably cleaner than many data sets will actually be.

4829
07:32:50,926 --> 07:32:52,420
Oftentimes data is messier.

4830
07:32:52,512 --> 07:32:56,432
There are outliers, there's random noise that happens inside of a particular system.

4831
07:32:56,718 --> 07:33:00,196
And what we'd like to do is still be able to figure out what a line might look like.

4832
07:33:00,494 --> 07:33:04,672
So in practice, the data will not always be linearly separable.

4833
07:33:04,910 --> 07:33:11,524
Where linearly separable refers to some data set where I could draw a line just to separate the two halves of it perfectly.

4834
07:33:11,822 --> 07:33:19,312
Instead, you might have a situation like this, where there are some rainy points that are on this side of the line and some not rainy points that are on that side of the line,

4835
07:33:19,472 --> 07:33:22,852
and there may not be a line that perfectly separates

4836
07:33:23,662 --> 07:33:29,124
what half of the inputs from the other half, that perfectly separates all the rainy days from the not rainy days.

4837
07:33:29,422 --> 07:33:32,672
But we can still say that this line does a pretty good job.

4838
07:33:32,942 --> 07:33:39,684
And we'll try to formalize a little bit later what we mean when we say something like this line does a pretty good job of trying to make that prediction.

4839
07:33:40,014 --> 07:33:48,804
But for now, let's just say we're looking for a line that does as good of a job as we can at trying to separate one category of things from another category of things.

4840
07:33:49,614 --> 07:33:52,900
So let's now try to formalize this a little bit more mathematically.

4841
07:33:53,104 --> 07:34:01,220
We want to come up with some sort of function, some way we can define this line, and our inputs are things like humidity and pressure in this case.

4842
07:34:01,774 --> 07:34:08,224
So our inputs we might call x1 is going to represent humidity, and x2 is going to represent pressure.

4843
07:34:08,384 --> 07:34:17,024
These are our inputs that we are going to provide to our machine learning algorithm, and given those inputs, we would like for our model to be able to predict some sort of output.

4844
07:34:17,104 --> 07:34:21,092
And we are going to predict that using our hypothesis function, which we called H.

4845
07:34:21,486 --> 07:34:27,492
Our hypothesis function is going to take as input x1 and x2, humidity and pressure in this case.

4846
07:34:27,664 --> 07:34:35,024
And you can imagine if we didn't just have two inputs, we had three or four or five inputs or more, we could have this hypothesis function take all of those as input.

4847
07:34:35,104 --> 07:34:37,700
And we'll see examples of that a little bit later as well.

4848
07:34:38,382 --> 07:34:41,604
And now the question is, what does this hypothesis function do?

4849
07:34:42,254 --> 07:34:51,264
Well, it really just needs to measure like, is this data point on one side of the boundary, or is it on the other side of the boundary?

4850
07:34:51,534 --> 07:34:53,156
And how do we formalize that boundary?

4851
07:34:53,518 --> 07:35:00,784
Well, the boundary is generally going to be a linear combination of these input variables, at least in this particular case.

4852
07:35:01,184 --> 07:35:08,304
what we're trying to do when we say linear combination is take each of these inputs and multiply them by some number that we're going to have to figure out.

4853
07:35:08,544 --> 07:35:14,464
We'll generally call that number a weight for how important should these variables be in trying to determine the answer.

4854
07:35:14,766 --> 07:35:21,124
So we'll weight each of these variables with some weight, and we might add a constant to it just to try and make the function a little bit different.

4855
07:35:21,486 --> 07:35:29,156
And the result we just need to compare. Like, is it greater than zero or is it less than zero to say, does it belong on one side of the line or the other side of the line?

4856
07:35:29,998 --> 07:35:32,900
And so what that mathematical expression might look like is this.

4857
07:35:33,966 --> 07:35:38,944
We would take each of my variables, x1 and x2, multiply them by some weight.

4858
07:35:39,118 --> 07:35:43,104
I don't yet know what that weight is, but it's going to be some number, weight one and weight two.

4859
07:35:43,598 --> 07:35:50,916
And maybe we just want to add some other weight zero to it, because the function might require us to shift the entire value up or down by a certain amount.

4860
07:35:51,470 --> 07:35:52,464
And then we just compare.

4861
07:35:52,718 --> 07:35:55,584
If we do all this math, is it greater than or equal to zero?

4862
07:35:55,758 --> 07:36:01,380
If so, we might categorize that data point as a rainy day, and otherwise, we might say no rain.

4863
07:36:02,286 --> 07:36:08,464
So the key here then is that this expression is how we are going to calculate whether it's a rainy day or not.

4864
07:36:08,544 --> 07:36:16,836
We're going to do a bunch of math where we take each of the variables, multiply them by a weight, maybe add an extra weight to it, see if the result is greater than or equal to zero,

4865
07:36:16,974 --> 07:36:22,052
and using that result of that expression, we're able to determine whether it's raining or not raining.

4866
07:36:22,544 --> 07:36:28,836
This expression here is in this case going to refer to just some line. If you were to plot that graphically, it would just be some line.

4867
07:36:29,070 --> 07:36:32,944
And what the line actually looks like depends upon these weights.

4868
07:36:33,198 --> 07:36:41,092
X1 and X2 are the inputs, but these weights are really what determine the shape of that line, the slope of that line, and what that line actually looks like.

4869
07:36:41,934 --> 07:36:44,836
So we then would like to figure out what these weights should be.

4870
07:36:45,264 --> 07:36:52,260
We can choose whatever weights we want, but we want to choose weights in such a way that if you pass in a rainy day's humidity and pressure,

4871
07:36:52,526 --> 07:36:55,076
then you end up with a result that is greater than or equal to zero.

4872
07:36:55,310 --> 07:37:02,724
And we would like it such that if we passed into our hypothesis function a not rainy day's inputs, then the output that we get should be not rainy.

4873
07:37:03,790 --> 07:37:10,224
So before we get there, let's try and formalize this a little bit more mathematically just to get a sense for how it is that you'll often see this

4874
07:37:10,304 --> 07:37:14,020
if you ever go further into supervised machine learning and explore this idea.

4875
07:37:14,350 --> 07:37:20,036
One thing is that generally for these categories, we'll sometimes just use the names of the categories, like rain and not rain.

4876
07:37:20,304 --> 07:37:25,744
Often mathematically, if we're trying to do comparisons between these things, it's easier just to deal in the world of numbers.

4877
07:37:25,966 --> 07:37:30,544
So we could just say one and zero, one for raining, zero for not raining.

4878
07:37:30,734 --> 07:37:36,784
So we do all this math, and if the result is greater than or equal to zero, we'll go ahead and say our hypothesis function outputs one,

4879
07:37:37,038 --> 07:37:40,516
meaning raining, and otherwise it outputs zero, meaning not raining.

4880
07:37:41,102 --> 07:37:47,664
And oftentimes this type of expression will instead express using vector mathematics.

4881
07:37:47,854 --> 07:37:52,900
And all a vector is, if you're not familiar with the term, is it refers to a sequence of numerical values.

4882
07:37:53,294 --> 07:37:58,404
You could represent that in Python using a list of numerical values or a tuple with numerical values.

4883
07:37:59,214 --> 07:38:02,564
And here we have a couple of sequences of numerical values.

4884
07:38:02,990 --> 07:38:10,704
One of our vectors, one of our sequences of numerical values, are all of these individual weights, w0, w1, and w2.

4885
07:38:11,278 --> 07:38:19,460
So we could construct what we'll call a weight vector, and we'll see why this is useful in a moment, called w, generally represented using a bold face w,

4886
07:38:19,790 --> 07:38:23,812
that is just a sequence of these three weights, weight 0, weight 1, and weight 2.

4887
07:38:24,558 --> 07:38:33,264
And to be able to calculate based on those weights whether we think a day is raining or not raining, we're going to multiply each of those weights by

4888
07:38:33,838 --> 07:38:35,012
one of our input variables.

4889
07:38:35,406 --> 07:38:39,140
That w2, this weight, is going to be multiplied by input variable x2.

4890
07:38:39,534 --> 07:38:42,436
W1 is going to be multiplied by input variable x1.

4891
07:38:42,670 --> 07:38:51,824
And w0, well, it's not being multiplied by anything, but to make sure the vectors are the same length, and we'll see why that's useful in just a second, we'll just go ahead and say w0

4892
07:38:52,144 --> 07:38:53,604
is being multiplied by one.

4893
07:38:54,094 --> 07:38:57,476
Because you can multiply by something by one, and you end up getting the exact same number.

4894
07:38:57,934 --> 07:39:06,944
So in addition to the weight vector w, we'll also have an input vector that we'll call x that has three values, one, again, because we're just multiplying w0

4895
07:39:07,104 --> 07:39:10,116
by one eventually, and then x1 and x2.

4896
07:39:10,990 --> 07:39:16,196
So here then we've represented two distinct vectors, a vector of weights that we need to somehow learn.

4897
07:39:16,494 --> 07:39:21,104
The goal of our machine learning algorithm is to learn what this weight vector is supposed to be.

4898
07:39:21,264 --> 07:39:27,824
We could choose any arbitrary set of numbers and it would produce a function that tries to predict rain or not rain, but it probably wouldn't be very good.

4899
07:39:28,046 --> 07:39:34,084
What we want to do is come up with a good choice of these weights so that we're able to do the accurate predictions.

4900
07:39:34,862 --> 07:39:43,172
And then this input vector represents a particular input to the function, a data point for which we would like to estimate is that day a rainy day,

4901
07:39:43,344 --> 07:39:45,028
or is that day a not rainy day?

4902
07:39:45,454 --> 07:39:50,628
And so that's going to vary just depending on what input is provided to our function, what it is that we are trying to estimate.

4903
07:39:51,182 --> 07:39:59,972
And then to do the calculation, we want to calculate this expression here, and it turns out that expression is what we would call the dot product of these two vectors.

4904
07:40:00,304 --> 07:40:06,304
The dot product of two vectors just means taking each of the terms in the vectors and multiplying them together.

4905
07:40:06,544 --> 07:40:13,668
W0 multiply it by one, w1 multiply it by x1, w2 multiply it by x2, and that's why these vectors need to be the same length.

4906
07:40:14,286 --> 07:40:17,024
And then we just add all of the results together.

4907
07:40:17,326 --> 07:40:25,264
So the dot product of dot product of w and x, our weight vector and our input vector, that's just going to be w0 times one,

4908
07:40:25,424 --> 07:40:34,756
or just w0, plus w1 times x1, multiplying these two terms together, plus w2 times x2 multiplying those terms together.

4909
07:40:35,598 --> 07:40:40,932
So we have our weight vector, which we need to figure out. We need our machine learning algorithm to figure out what the weights should be.

4910
07:40:41,184 --> 07:40:47,044
We have the input vector representing the data point that we're trying to predict a category for, predict a label for.

4911
07:40:47,502 --> 07:40:54,304
And we're able to do that calculation by taking this dot product, which you'll often see represented in vector form, but if you haven't seen vectors before,

4912
07:40:54,384 --> 07:41:00,580
you can think of it as identical to just this mathematical expression, just doing the multiplication, adding the results together,

4913
07:41:01,038 --> 07:41:04,132
and then seeing whether the result is greater than or equal to zero or not.

4914
07:41:04,384 --> 07:41:13,572
This expression here is identical to the expression that we're calculating to see whether or not that answer is greater than or equal to zero in this case.

4915
07:41:14,126 --> 07:41:22,624
And so for that reason, you'll often see the hypothesis function written as something like this: a simpler representation where the hypothesis takes as input

4916
07:41:22,798 --> 07:41:32,900
some input vector x, some humidity and pressure for some day, and we want to predict an output, like rain or no rain, or one or zero if we choose to represent things numerically.

4917
07:41:33,358 --> 07:41:34,180
And the way we do

4918
07:41:34,926 --> 07:41:38,212
that is by taking the dot product of the weights and our input.

4919
07:41:38,606 --> 07:41:41,664
If it's greater than or equal to zero, we'll go ahead and say the output is one.

4920
07:41:42,064 --> 07:41:44,224
Otherwise, the output is going to be

4921
07:41:44,910 --> 07:41:48,964
zero. And this hypothesis we say is parameterized by the weights.

4922
07:41:49,198 --> 07:41:53,092
Depending on what weights we choose, we'll end up getting a different hypothesis.

4923
07:41:53,358 --> 07:41:57,264
If we choose the weights randomly, we're probably not going to get a very good hypothesis function.

4924
07:41:57,344 --> 07:42:03,908
We'll get a one or a zero, but it's probably not accurately going to reflect whether we think a day is going to be rainy or not rainy.

4925
07:42:04,270 --> 07:42:13,028
But if we choose the weights right, we can often do a pretty good job of trying to estimate whether we think the output of the function should be a one or a zero.

4926
07:42:13,806 --> 07:42:19,524
And so the question then is how to figure out what these weights should be, how to be able to tune those parameters.

4927
07:42:19,886 --> 07:42:21,412
And there are a number of ways you can do that.

4928
07:42:21,792 --> 07:42:26,916
One of the most common is known as the perceptron learning rule. And we'll see more of this later.

4929
07:42:27,246 --> 07:42:32,900
But the idea of the perceptron learning rule, and we're not going to get too deep into the mathematics, we'll mostly just introduce it more conceptually,

4930
07:42:33,312 --> 07:42:42,352
is to say that given some data point that we would like to learn from, some data point that has an input X and an output y, where y is like one

4931
07:42:42,512 --> 07:42:46,032
for rain or zero for not rain, then we're going to update the weights.

4932
07:42:46,126 --> 07:42:53,392
And we'll look at the formula in just a moment, but the big picture idea is that we can start with random weights, but then learn from the data.

4933
07:42:53,552 --> 07:43:01,632
Like take the data points one at a time, and for each one of the data points, figure out, all right, what parameters do we need to change inside of the

4934
07:43:01,632 --> 07:43:04,612
weights in order to better match that input point.

4935
07:43:05,134 --> 07:43:12,992
And so, that is, the value of having access to a lot of data in the supervised machine learning algorithm: is that you take each of the data points and maybe look at them multiple times

4936
07:43:13,166 --> 07:43:21,952
and constantly try and figure out whether you need to shift your weights in order to better create some weight vector that is able to correctly or more accurately

4937
07:43:22,158 --> 07:43:27,844
try to estimate what the output should be, whether we think it's going to be raining, or whether we think it's not going to be raining.

4938
07:43:28,654 --> 07:43:30,192
So what does that weight update look like?

4939
07:43:30,382 --> 07:43:39,072
Without going into too much of the mathematics, we're going to update each of the weights to be the result of the original weight plus some additional expression.

4940
07:43:39,342 --> 07:43:41,472
And to understand this expression, why?

4941
07:43:41,710 --> 07:43:44,100
Well, y is what the actual output is.

4942
07:43:44,752 --> 07:43:50,436
And hypothesis of x the input, that's going to be what we thought the input was.

4943
07:43:50,990 --> 07:43:56,100
And so I can replace this by saying what the actual value was minus what our estimate was.

4944
07:43:56,686 --> 07:44:05,284
And based on the difference between the actual value and what our estimate was, we might want to change our hypothesis, change the way that we do that estimation.

4945
07:44:06,190 --> 07:44:12,676
If the actual value and the estimate were the same thing, meaning we were correctly able to predict what category this data point belonged to,

4946
07:44:13,070 --> 07:44:20,324
well then actual value minus estimate, that's just going to be zero, which means this whole term on the right-hand side goes to be zero, and the weight doesn't change.

4947
07:44:20,686 --> 07:44:26,084
Weight i, where i is like weight one or weight two or weight zero, weight i just stays at weight i.

4948
07:44:26,414 --> 07:44:31,460
And none of the weights change if we were able to correctly predict what category the input belonged to.

4949
07:44:32,206 --> 07:44:39,632
But if our hypothesis didn't correctly predict what category the input belonged to, well then maybe then we need to make some changes,

4950
07:44:39,872 --> 07:44:44,452
adjust the weights so that we're better able to predict this kind of data point in the future.

4951
07:44:44,974 --> 07:44:46,212
And what is the way we might do that?

4952
07:44:46,926 --> 07:44:50,436
Well, if the actual value was bigger than the estimate,

4953
07:44:51,086 --> 07:44:57,152
then, and for now we'll go ahead and assume that these x's are positive values, then if the actual value was bigger than the estimate,

4954
07:44:57,326 --> 07:45:05,636
well that means we need to increase the weight in order to make it such that the output is bigger, and therefore we're more likely to get to the right actual value.

4955
07:45:05,998 --> 07:45:11,072
And so if the actual value is bigger than the estimate, then actual value minus estimate, that'll be a positive number.

4956
07:45:11,278 --> 07:45:16,196
And so you imagine we're just adding some positive number to the weight just to increase it ever so slightly.

4957
07:45:16,590 --> 07:45:18,180
And likewise the inverse case is true.

4958
07:45:18,478 --> 07:45:28,100
That if the actual value was less than the estimate, the actual value was zero, but we estimated one, meaning it actually was not raining, but we predicted it was going to be raining.

4959
07:45:28,494 --> 07:45:36,352
Well then we want to decrease the value of the weight, because then in that case we want to try and lower the total value of computing that dot product in

4960
07:45:36,352 --> 07:45:40,100
order to make it less likely that we would predict that it would actually be raining.

4961
07:45:40,832 --> 07:45:46,692
So no need to get too deep into the mathematics of that, but the general idea is that every time we encounter some data point,

4962
07:45:46,832 --> 07:45:53,552
we can adjust these weights accordingly to try and make the weights better line up with the actual data that we have access to.

4963
07:45:53,742 --> 07:46:01,764
And you can repeat this process with data point after data point until eventually, hopefully, your algorithm converges to some set of weights that do a pretty good job

4964
07:46:01,934 --> 07:46:05,232
of trying to figure out whether a day is going to be rainy or not rainy.

4965
07:46:05,934 --> 07:46:12,100
And just as a final point about this particular equation, this value alpha here is generally what we'll call the learning rate.

4966
07:46:12,366 --> 07:46:18,416
It's just some parameter, some number we choose for how quickly we're actually going to be updating these weight values.

4967
07:46:18,576 --> 07:46:26,816
That if alpha is bigger, then we're going to update these weight values by a lot, and if alpha is smaller, then we'll update the weight values by less. And you can choose a value of alpha.

4968
07:46:26,896 --> 07:46:31,588
Depending on the problem, different values might suit the situation better or worse than others.

4969
07:46:32,718 --> 07:46:38,736
So after all of that, after we've done this training process of take all this data and using this learning rule,

4970
07:46:38,896 --> 07:46:44,656
look at all the pieces of data and use each piece of data as an indication to us of do the weights stay the same?

4971
07:46:44,846 --> 07:46:45,856
Do we increase the weights?

4972
07:46:46,016 --> 07:46:48,548
Do we decrease the weights? And if so, by how much?

4973
07:46:48,846 --> 07:46:52,356
What you end up with is effectively a threshold function.

4974
07:46:53,006 --> 07:46:55,492
And we can look at what the threshold function looks like like this.

4975
07:46:56,078 --> 07:47:02,244
On the x-axis here, we have the output of that function, taking the weights, taking the dot product of it with the input.

4976
07:47:03,022 --> 07:47:11,364
And on the y-axis, we have what the output is going to be. Zero, which in this case represented not raining, and one, which in this case represented raining.

4977
07:47:11,822 --> 07:47:19,908
And the way that our hypothesis function works is it calculates this value, and if it's greater than zero or greater than some threshold value,

4978
07:47:20,302 --> 07:47:24,932
then we declare that it's a rainy day, and otherwise we declare that it's a not rainy day.

4979
07:47:25,216 --> 07:47:28,336
And this then graphically is what that function looks like.

4980
07:47:28,558 --> 07:47:33,604
That initially, when the value of this dot product is small, it's not raining, it's not raining, it's not raining.

4981
07:47:33,806 --> 07:47:39,396
But as soon as it crosses that threshold, we suddenly say, okay, now it's raining, now it's raining, now it's raining.

4982
07:47:39,616 --> 07:47:47,652
And the way to interpret this kind of representation is that anything on this side of the line, that would be the category of data points where we say, yes, it's raining.

4983
07:47:47,886 --> 07:47:52,256
Anything that falls on this side of the line are the data points where we would say, it's not raining.

4984
07:47:52,398 --> 07:47:59,300
And again, we want to choose some value for the weights that results in a function that does a pretty good job of trying to do this estimation.

4985
07:48:00,174 --> 07:48:06,660
But one tricky thing with this type of hard threshold is that it only leaves two possible outcomes.

4986
07:48:07,296 --> 07:48:15,716
We plug in some data as input, and the output we get is raining or not raining, and there's no room for anywhere in between.

4987
07:48:16,014 --> 07:48:23,908
And maybe that's what you want. Maybe all you want is given some data point, you would like to be able to classify it into one or two or more of these various different categories.

4988
07:48:24,878 --> 07:48:30,576
But it might also be the case that you care about knowing how strong that prediction is, for example.

4989
07:48:30,958 --> 07:48:37,216
So if we go back to this instance here, where we have rainy days on this side of the line, not rainy days on that side of the

4990
07:48:37,216 --> 07:48:41,776
line, you might imagine that let's look now at these two white data points.

4991
07:48:41,936 --> 07:48:50,852
This data point here that we would like to predict a label or a category for, and this data point over here that we would also like to predict a label or a category for.

4992
07:48:51,406 --> 07:48:55,972
It seems likely that you could pretty confidently say that this data point, that should be a rainy day.

4993
07:48:56,398 --> 07:49:06,096
Seems close to the other rainy days if we're going by the nearest neighbor strategy, it's on this side of the line if we're going by the strategy of just saying, which side of the line does it fall on

4994
07:49:06,256 --> 07:49:08,004
by figuring out what those weights should be.

4995
07:49:08,558 --> 07:49:16,896
And if we're using the line strategy of just which side of the line does it fall on, which side of this decision boundary, well, we'd also say that this point here

4996
07:49:17,102 --> 07:49:22,436
is also a rainy day, because it falls on the side of the line that corresponds to rainy days.

4997
07:49:23,502 --> 07:49:32,896
But it's likely that even in this case, we would know that we don't feel nearly as confident about this data point on the left as compared to this data point on the right.

4998
07:49:33,166 --> 07:49:36,708
That for this one on the right, we can feel very confident that yes, it's a rainy day.

4999
07:49:36,976 --> 07:49:40,900
This one, you know, it's pretty close to the line if we're judging just by distance.

5000
07:49:41,326 --> 07:49:43,492
And so you might be less sure.

5001
07:49:44,206 --> 07:49:49,764
But our threshold function doesn't allow for a notion of less sure or more sure about something.

5002
07:49:49,966 --> 07:49:51,684
It's what we would call a hard threshold.

5003
07:49:51,936 --> 07:49:57,216
It's once you've crossed this line, then immediately we say, yes, this is going to be a rainy day.

5004
07:49:57,390 --> 07:50:00,100
Anywhere before it, we're going to say it's not a rainy day.

5005
07:50:00,462 --> 07:50:02,896
And that may not be helpful in a number of cases.

5006
07:50:03,150 --> 07:50:05,696
One, this is not a particularly easy function to deal with.

5007
07:50:06,416 --> 07:50:13,988
as you get deeper into the world of machine learning and are trying to do things like taking derivatives of these curves, this type of function makes things challenging.

5008
07:50:14,382 --> 07:50:17,828
But the other challenge is that we don't really have any notion of gradation between things.

5009
07:50:17,936 --> 07:50:23,616
We don't have a notion of yes, this is a very strong belief that it's going to be raining, as opposed to,

5010
07:50:24,096 --> 07:50:29,056
it's probably more likely than not that it's going to be raining, but maybe not totally sure about that either.

5011
07:50:29,902 --> 07:50:36,932
So, what we can do by taking advantage of a technique known as logistic regression is instead of using this hard threshold type of function,

5012
07:50:37,184 --> 07:50:41,348
we can use instead a logistic function, something we might call a soft threshold.

5013
07:50:41,744 --> 07:50:47,684
And that's going to transform this into looking something a little more like this, something that more nicely curves.

5014
07:50:48,078 --> 07:50:54,596
And as a result, the possible output values are no longer just zero and one, zero for not raining, one for raining.

5015
07:50:54,944 --> 07:50:59,024
But you can actually get any real numbered value between zero and one.

5016
07:50:59,278 --> 07:51:02,784
That if you're way over on this side, then you get a value of zero.

5017
07:51:02,990 --> 07:51:05,184
Okay, it's not going to be raining, and we're pretty sure about that.

5018
07:51:05,518 --> 07:51:09,700
And if you're over on this side, you get a value of one, like yes, we're very sure that it's going to be raining.

5019
07:51:10,254 --> 07:51:17,184
But in between, you could get some real numbered value, where a value like 0.7 might mean, we think it's going to rain.

5020
07:51:17,294 --> 07:51:24,784
It's more probable that it's going to rain than not based on the data, but we're not as confident as some of the other data points might be.

5021
07:51:25,038 --> 07:51:33,344
So one of the advantages of the soft threshold is that it allows us to have an output that could be some real number, that potentially reflects some sort of probability,

5022
07:51:33,504 --> 07:51:38,724
the likelihood that we think that this particular data point belongs to that particular category.

5023
07:51:39,406 --> 07:51:42,544
And there are some other nice mathematical properties of that as well.

5024
07:51:43,822 --> 07:51:48,356
So that then is two different approaches to trying to solve this type of classification problem.

5025
07:51:48,624 --> 07:51:57,668
One is this nearest neighbor type of approach, where you just take a data point and look at the data points that are nearby to try and estimate what category we think it belongs to.

5026
07:51:58,350 --> 07:52:07,012
And the other approach is the approach of saying, all right, let's just try and use linear regression, and figure out what these weights should be, adjust the weights in order to figure out

5027
07:52:07,424 --> 07:52:11,652
what line or what decision boundary is going to best separate these two categories.

5028
07:52:12,622 --> 07:52:20,864
It turns out that another popular approach, a very popular approach if you just have a data set and you want to start trying to do some learning on it, is what we call the support vector machine.

5029
07:52:21,198 --> 07:52:27,108
And we're not going to go too much into the mathematics of the support vector machine, but we'll at least explore it graphically to see what it is that it looks like.

5030
07:52:27,566 --> 07:52:34,864
And the idea or the motivation behind the support vector machine is the idea that there are actually a lot of different lines that we could draw,

5031
07:52:35,024 --> 07:52:38,660
a lot of different decision boundaries that we could draw to separate two groups.

5032
07:52:39,246 --> 07:52:46,852
So if, for example, I had the red data points over here and the blue data points over here, one possible line I could draw is a line like this.

5033
07:52:47,534 --> 07:52:51,504
That this line here would separate the red points from the blue points, and it does so perfectly.

5034
07:52:51,664 --> 07:52:55,904
All the red points are on one side of the line, all the blue points are on the other side of the

5035
07:52:55,904 --> 07:53:03,664
line. But this should probably make you a little bit nervous if you come up with a model and the model comes up with a line that looks like this.

5036
07:53:03,744 --> 07:53:12,164
And the reason why is that you worry about how well it's going to generalize to other data points that are not necessarily in the data set that we have access to.

5037
07:53:12,718 --> 07:53:22,788
For example, if there was a point that fell, like right here, for example, on the right side of the line, well then based on that, we might want to guess that it is in fact a red point,

5038
07:53:23,150 --> 07:53:28,484
but it falls on the side of the line where instead we would estimate that it's a blue point instead.

5039
07:53:29,134 --> 07:53:36,068
And so based on that, this line is probably not a great choice just because it is so close to these various data points.

5040
07:53:36,590 --> 07:53:41,424
We might instead prefer like a diagonal line that just goes diagonally through the data set like we've seen before.

5041
07:53:41,678 --> 07:53:44,624
But there too, there's a lot of diagonal lines that we could draw as well.

5042
07:53:44,864 --> 07:53:51,664
For example, I could draw this diagonal line here, which also successfully separates all the red points from all of the blue points.

5043
07:53:51,824 --> 07:53:57,700
From the perspective of something like just trying to figure out some setting of weights that allows us to predict the correct output,

5044
07:53:58,064 --> 07:54:02,784
this line will predict the correct output for this particular set of data every single time.

5045
07:54:02,958 --> 07:54:06,052
Because the red points are on one side, the blue points are on the other.

5046
07:54:06,510 --> 07:54:08,452
But yet again, you should probably be a little nervous.

5047
07:54:08,864 --> 07:54:14,564
Because this line is so close to these red points, even though we're able to correctly predict on the input data.

5048
07:54:15,248 --> 07:54:22,436
If there was a point that fell, somewhere in this general area, our algorithm, this model, would say that, yeah, we think it's a blue point,

5049
07:54:22,798 --> 07:54:29,412
when in actuality it might belong to the red category instead, just because it looks like it's close to the other red points.

5050
07:54:29,808 --> 07:54:36,484
What we really want to be able to say, given this data, how can you generalize this as best as possible, is to come up with a line like this.

5051
07:54:37,006 --> 07:54:39,008
That seems like the intuitive line to draw.

5052
07:54:39,246 --> 07:54:47,168
And the reason why it's intuitive is because it seems to be as far apart as possible from the red data and the blue data.

5053
07:54:47,310 --> 07:54:55,488
So that if we generalize a little bit and assume that maybe we have some points that are different from the input, but still slightly further away, we can still say that something on this side,

5054
07:54:55,648 --> 07:55:00,836
probably red, something on that side, probably blue, and we can make those judgments that way.

5055
07:55:01,550 --> 07:55:08,356
And that is what support vector machines are designed to do. They're designed to try and find what we call the maximum margin separator.

5056
07:55:08,590 --> 07:55:14,596
Where the maximum margin separator is just some boundary that maximizes the distance between the groups of points.

5057
07:55:14,768 --> 07:55:22,368
Rather than come up with some boundary that's very close to one set or the other, where in the case before we wouldn't have cared, as long as we're categorizing the input well,

5058
07:55:22,448 --> 07:55:23,684
that seems all we need to do.

5059
07:55:24,078 --> 07:55:31,588
The support, the support vector machine will try and find this maximum margin separator, some way of trying to maximize that particular distance.

5060
07:55:31,982 --> 07:55:41,924
And it does so by finding what we call the support vectors, which are the vectors that are closest to the line and trying to maximize the distance between the line and those particular points.

5061
07:55:42,798 --> 07:55:44,368
And it works that way in two dimensions.

5062
07:55:44,558 --> 07:55:52,580
It also works in higher dimensions, where we're not looking for some line that separates the two data points, but instead looking for what we generally call a hyperplane,

5063
07:55:52,928 --> 07:55:58,768
some decision boundary effectively that separates one set of data from the other set of data.

5064
07:55:59,022 --> 07:56:04,420
And this ability of support vector machines to work in higher dimensions actually has a number of other applications as well.

5065
07:56:04,608 --> 07:56:10,288
But one is that it helpfully deals with cases where data may not be linearly separable.

5066
07:56:10,574 --> 07:56:18,288
So we talked about linear separability before, this idea that you can take data and just draw a line or some linear combination of the inputs that

5067
07:56:18,288 --> 07:56:21,188
allows us to perfectly separate the two sets from each other.

5068
07:56:21,550 --> 07:56:31,968
There are some data sets that are not linearly separable, and some were even too, you would not be able to find a good line at all that would try to do that kind of separation.

5069
07:56:32,208 --> 07:56:33,572
Something like this, for example.

5070
07:56:34,254 --> 07:56:38,116
Or if you imagine here are the red points and the blue points surround it.

5071
07:56:38,670 --> 07:56:46,208
If you try to find a line that divides the red points from the blue points, it's actually going to be difficult, if not impossible, to do.

5072
07:56:46,288 --> 07:56:53,168
That any line you choose, well, if you draw a line here, then you've ignored all of these blue points that should actually be blue and not red.

5073
07:56:53,328 --> 07:56:59,172
Anywhere else you draw a line, there's going to be a lot of error, a lot of mistakes, a lot of what we'll soon call loss

5074
07:56:59,854 --> 07:57:01,060
to that line that you draw.

5075
07:57:01,248 --> 07:57:04,356
A lot of points that you're going to categorize incorrectly.

5076
07:57:04,942 --> 07:57:08,676
What we really want is to be able to find a better decision boundary that may not

5077
07:57:09,358 --> 07:57:12,292
be just a straight line through this two-dimensional space.

5078
07:57:12,654 --> 07:57:19,648
And what support vector machines can do is they can begin to operate in higher dimensions and be able to find some other decision boundary,

5079
07:57:19,808 --> 07:57:26,448
like the circle in this case, that actually is able to separate one of these sets of data from the other set of data a lot better.

5080
07:57:26,702 --> 07:57:36,356
So oftentimes in data sets where the data is not linearly separable, support vector machines by working in higher dimensions can actually figure out a way to solve that kind of problem effectively.

5081
07:57:37,134 --> 07:57:41,008
So that then, three different approaches to trying to solve these sorts of problems.

5082
07:57:41,088 --> 07:57:49,728
We've seen support vector machines and we've seen trying to use linear regression and the perceptron learning rule to be able to figure out how to categorize inputs and outputs.

5083
07:57:49,838 --> 07:57:51,300
We've seen the nearest neighbor approach.

5084
07:57:51,568 --> 07:57:53,968
No one necessarily better than any other again.

5085
07:57:54,128 --> 07:58:01,124
It's going to depend on the data set, the information you have access to, it's going to depend on what the function looks like that you're ultimately trying to predict.

5086
07:58:01,262 --> 07:58:08,688
And this is where a lot of research and experimentation can be involved in trying to figure out how it is to best perform that kind of estimation.

5087
07:58:09,582 --> 07:58:14,372
But classification is only one of the tasks that you might encounter in supervised machine learning.

5088
07:58:14,702 --> 07:58:19,360
Because in classification, what we're trying to predict is some discrete category.

5089
07:58:19,520 --> 07:58:24,100
We're trying to predict red or blue, rain or not rain, authentic or counterfeit.

5090
07:58:24,910 --> 07:58:27,908
But sometimes what we want to predict is a real numbered value.

5091
07:58:28,302 --> 07:58:32,900
And for that we have a related problem, not classification, but instead known as regression.

5092
07:58:33,390 --> 07:58:42,560
And regression is the supervised learning problem where we try and learn a function mapping inputs to outputs, same as before, but instead of the outputs being discrete categories,

5093
07:58:42,734 --> 07:58:51,428
things like rain or not rain, in a regression problem, the output values are generally continuous values, some real number that we would like to predict.

5094
07:58:52,014 --> 07:58:53,200
This happens all the time as well.

5095
07:58:53,454 --> 07:58:59,520
You might imagine that a company might take this approach if it's trying to figure out, for instance, what the effect of its advertising is.

5096
07:58:59,680 --> 07:59:05,252
Like how do advertising dollars spent translate into sales for the company's product, for example.

5097
07:59:05,806 --> 07:59:11,520
And so they might like to try to predict some function that takes as input the amount of money spent on advertising.

5098
07:59:11,680 --> 07:59:18,276
And here we're just going to use one input, but again, you could scale this up to many more inputs as well if you have a lot of different kinds of data you have access to.

5099
07:59:18,670 --> 07:59:23,680
And the goal is to learn a function that given this amount of spending on advertising, we're going to get this amount in sales.

5100
07:59:23,760 --> 07:59:32,036
And you might judge based on having access to a whole bunch of data, like for every past month, here is how much we spent on advertising, and here is what sales were.

5101
07:59:32,270 --> 07:59:40,640
And we would like to predict some sort of hypothesis function that again, given the amount spent on advertising, can predict, in this case,

5102
07:59:40,800 --> 07:59:50,980
some real number, some number estimate of how much sales we expect that company to do in this month or in this quarter or whatever unit of time we're choosing to measure things in.

5103
07:59:51,886 --> 07:59:59,652
And so again, the approach to solving this type of problem, we could try using a linear regression type approach where we take this data and we just plot it.

5104
07:59:59,918 --> 08:00:07,840
On the x-axis we have advertising dollars spent, on the y-axis we have sales, and we might just want to try and draw a line that does a pretty good

5105
08:00:07,840 --> 08:00:12,720
job of trying to estimate this relationship between advertising and sales.

5106
08:00:12,880 --> 08:00:18,960
And in this case, unlike before, we're not trying to separate the data points into discrete categories, but instead, in this case,

5107
08:00:19,040 --> 08:00:24,880
we're just trying to find a line that approximates this relationship between advertising and sales.

5108
08:00:25,040 --> 08:00:33,200
So that if we want to figure out what the estimated sales are for a particular advertising budget, you just look it up in this line, figure out for this amount of advertising,

5109
08:00:33,360 --> 08:00:37,120
we would have this amount of sales, and just try and make the estimate that way.

5110
08:00:37,422 --> 08:00:46,148
And so you can try and come up with a line, again, figuring out how to modify the weights using various different techniques to try and make it so that this line fits as well as possible.

5111
08:00:47,694 --> 08:00:54,788
So, with all of these approaches then to trying to solve machine learning style problems, the question becomes how do we evaluate these approaches?

5112
08:00:54,960 --> 08:00:59,200
How do we evaluate the various different hypotheses that we could come up with?

5113
08:00:59,280 --> 08:01:09,124
Because each of these algorithms will give us some sort of hypothesis, some function that maps inputs to outputs, and we want to know how well does that function work.

5114
08:01:09,582 --> 08:01:16,132
And you can think of evaluating these hypotheses and trying to get a better hypothesis as kind of like an optimization problem.

5115
08:01:16,366 --> 08:01:18,724
In an optimization problem, as you recall from before,

5116
08:01:19,374 --> 08:01:23,844
we were either trying to maximize some objective function by trying to find

5117
08:01:24,526 --> 08:01:30,596
a global maximum, or we were trying to minimize some cost function by trying to find some global minimum.

5118
08:01:30,990 --> 08:01:37,988
And in the case of evaluating these hypotheses, one thing we might say is that this cost function, the thing we're trying to minimize,

5119
08:01:38,160 --> 08:01:41,700
we might be trying to minimize what we would call a loss function.

5120
08:01:42,062 --> 08:01:48,960
And what a loss function is, is it is a function that is going to estimate for us how poorly our function performs.

5121
08:01:49,120 --> 08:01:58,628
More formally, it's like a loss of utility by whenever we predict something that is wrong, that is a loss of utility that's going to add to the output of our loss function.

5122
08:01:59,438 --> 08:02:06,880
And you could come up with any loss function that you want, just some mathematical way of estimating, given each of these data points, given what the actual output is,

5123
08:02:06,960 --> 08:02:12,388
and given what our projected output is, our estimate, you could calculate some sort of numerical loss for it.

5124
08:02:12,782 --> 08:02:17,316
But there are a couple of popular loss functions that are worth discussing, just so that you've seen them before.

5125
08:02:18,062 --> 08:02:26,116
When it comes to discrete categories, things like rain or not rain, counterfeit or not counterfeit, one approach is the zero, one loss function.

5126
08:02:26,478 --> 08:02:28,740
And the way that works is for each of the data points,

5127
08:02:29,454 --> 08:02:37,440
our loss function takes as input what the actual output is, like whether it was actually raining or not raining, and takes our prediction into account.

5128
08:02:37,614 --> 08:02:41,508
Did we predict, given this data point, that it was raining or not raining?

5129
08:02:41,902 --> 08:02:47,396
And if the actual value equals the prediction, well then the 0 1 loss function will just say the loss is zero.

5130
08:02:47,520 --> 08:02:50,820
There was no loss of utility because we were able to predict correctly.

5131
08:02:51,758 --> 08:02:57,840
And otherwise, if the actual value was not the same thing as what we predicted, well then in that case our loss is one.

5132
08:02:58,160 --> 08:03:05,028
We lost something, lost some utility, because what we predicted was the output of the function was not what it actually was.

5133
08:03:05,646 --> 08:03:16,292
And the goal then in a situation like this would be to come up with some hypothesis that minimizes the total empirical loss, the total amount that we've lost, if you add up for all these data points,

5134
08:03:16,480 --> 08:03:20,100
what the actual output is and what your hypothesis would have predicted.

5135
08:03:20,974 --> 08:03:29,600
So in this case, for example, if we go back to classifying days as raining or not raining, and we came up with this decision boundary, how would we evaluate this decision boundary?

5136
08:03:29,680 --> 08:03:33,120
How much better is it than drawing the line here or drawing the line there?

5137
08:03:33,390 --> 08:03:41,840
Well, we could take each of the input data points, and each input data point has a label, whether it was raining or whether it was not raining, and we could compare it to the prediction,

5138
08:03:42,000 --> 08:03:46,884
whether we predicted it would be raining or not raining, and assign it a numerical value as a result.

5139
08:03:47,790 --> 08:03:54,880
So for example, these points over here, they were all rainy days, and we predicted they would be rainy, because they fall on the bottom side of the line.

5140
08:03:55,150 --> 08:03:56,228
So they have a loss of zero.

5141
08:03:56,480 --> 08:03:58,160
Nothing lost from those situations.

5142
08:03:58,382 --> 08:04:04,548
And likewise, same is true for some of these points over here, where it was not raining and we predicted it would not be raining either.

5143
08:04:05,134 --> 08:04:15,204
Where we do have loss are points like this point here and that point there, where we predicted that it would not be raining, but in actuality it's a blue point, it was raining.

5144
08:04:15,694 --> 08:04:21,636
Or likewise here, we predicted that it would be raining, but in actuality it's a red point, it was not raining.

5145
08:04:21,902 --> 08:04:26,276
And so as a result, we miscategorized these data points that we were trying to train on.

5146
08:04:27,054 --> 08:04:29,120
And as a result, there's some loss here.

5147
08:04:29,280 --> 08:04:34,436
One loss here, there, here, and there, for a total loss of four, for example, in this case.

5148
08:04:34,798 --> 08:04:42,916
And that might be how we would estimate or how we would say that this line is better than a line that goes somewhere else, or a line that's further down,

5149
08:04:43,120 --> 08:04:45,520
because this line might minimize the loss.

5150
08:04:45,614 --> 08:04:52,644
That there's no way to do better than just these four points of loss if you're just drawing a straight line through our

5151
08:04:52,800 --> 08:04:55,588
space. So the 0-1 loss function checks.

5152
08:04:56,238 --> 08:04:57,680
Did we get it right? Did we get it wrong?

5153
08:04:57,902 --> 08:05:00,196
If we got it right, the loss is zero, nothing lost.

5154
08:05:00,560 --> 08:05:04,080
If we got it wrong, then our loss function for that data point says one.

5155
08:05:04,400 --> 08:05:14,948
And we add up all of those losses across all of our data points to get some sort of empirical loss, how much we have lost across all of these original data points that our algorithm had access to.

5156
08:05:16,334 --> 08:05:26,404
There are other forms of loss as well that work especially well when we deal with more real-valued cases, cases like the mapping between advertising budget and amount that we do in sales, for example.

5157
08:05:26,640 --> 08:05:33,284
Because in that case, you care not just that you get the number exactly right, but you care how close you were to the actual value.

5158
08:05:33,600 --> 08:05:42,080
If the actual value is you did like $2,800 in sales, and you predicted that you would do $2,900 in sales, maybe that's pretty good.

5159
08:05:42,160 --> 08:05:45,988
That's much better than if you had predicted you'd do $1,000 in sales, for example.

5160
08:05:46,414 --> 08:05:50,560
And so we would like our loss function to be able to take that into account as well.

5161
08:05:51,440 --> 08:06:00,932
take into account not just whether the actual value and the expected value are exactly the same, but also take into account how far apart they were.

5162
08:06:01,742 --> 08:06:04,960
And so for that, one approach is what we call L1 loss.

5163
08:06:05,294 --> 08:06:09,060
L1 loss doesn't just look at whether actual and predicted are equal to each other,

5164
08:06:09,966 --> 08:06:14,692
but we take the absolute value of the actual value minus the predicted value.

5165
08:06:14,960 --> 08:06:25,764
In other words, we just ask how far apart were the actual and predicted values, and we sum that up across all of the data points to be able to get what our answer ultimately is.

5166
08:06:26,830 --> 08:06:29,312
So, what might this actually look like for our data set?

5167
08:06:29,582 --> 08:06:40,004
Well, if we go back to this representation where we had advertising along the x-axis, sales along the y-axis, our line was our prediction, our estimate for any given amount of advertising,

5168
08:06:40,206 --> 08:06:47,812
what we predicted sales was going to be, and our L1 loss is just how far apart vertically along the sales axis.

5169
08:06:48,206 --> 08:06:50,628
our prediction was from each of the data points.

5170
08:06:50,990 --> 08:06:58,884
So we could figure out exactly how far apart our prediction was from each of the data points, and figure out as a result of that what our loss is overall

5171
08:06:59,152 --> 08:07:05,668
for this particular hypothesis, just by adding up all of these various different individual losses for each of these data points.

5172
08:07:05,998 --> 08:07:09,540
And our goal then is to try and minimize that loss, to try and come up

5173
08:07:10,286 --> 08:07:18,244
with some line that minimizes what the utility loss is by judging how far away our estimate amount of sales is from the actual amount of sales.

5174
08:07:18,894 --> 08:07:23,268
And turns out there are other loss functions as well. One that's quite popular is the L2 loss.

5175
08:07:23,662 --> 08:07:33,232
The L2 loss, instead of just using the absolute value, like how far away the actual value is from the predicted value, it uses the square of actual minus predicted.

5176
08:07:33,312 --> 08:07:42,912
So how far apart are the actual and predicted value, and it squares that value, effectively penalizing much more harshly, anything that is a worse prediction.

5177
08:07:43,150 --> 08:07:53,072
So you imagine if you have two data points that you predict as being, one value away from their actual value, as opposed to one data point that you predict as being two

5178
08:07:53,232 --> 08:07:54,948
away from its actual value.

5179
08:07:55,374 --> 08:08:05,156
The L2 loss function will more harshly penalize that one that is two away, because it's going to square however much the difference is between the actual value and the predicted value.

5180
08:08:05,422 --> 08:08:10,308
And depending on the situation, you might want to choose a loss function depending on what you care about minimizing.

5181
08:08:10,512 --> 08:08:15,712
If you really care about minimizing the error on more outlier cases, then you might want to consider something like this.

5182
08:08:15,854 --> 08:08:21,472
But if you've got a lot of outliers and you don't necessarily care about modeling them, then maybe an L1 loss function is preferable.

5183
08:08:21,646 --> 08:08:25,636
But there are trade-offs here that you need to decide based on a particular set of data.

5184
08:08:26,478 --> 08:08:33,152
But what you do run the risk of with any of these loss functions, with anything that we're trying to do, is a problem known as overfitting.

5185
08:08:33,422 --> 08:08:42,192
And overfitting is a big problem that you can encounter in machine learning, which happens any time a model fits too closely with a data set, and as a result

5186
08:08:42,352 --> 08:08:43,684
fails to generalize.

5187
08:08:44,398 --> 08:08:47,364
We would like our model to be able to accurately predict

5188
08:08:48,110 --> 08:08:51,908
data and inputs and output pairs for the data that we have access to.

5189
08:08:52,334 --> 08:08:59,268
But the reason we wanted to do so is because we want our model to generalize well to data that we haven't seen before.

5190
08:08:59,566 --> 08:09:07,152
I would like to take data from the past year of whether it was raining and not raining and use that data to generalize it towards the future, to say in the future,

5191
08:09:07,406 --> 08:09:13,764
is it going to be raining or not raining? Or if I have a whole bunch of data on what counterfeit and not counterfeit US dollar bills look like

5192
08:09:14,510 --> 08:09:20,164
in the past when people have encountered them, I'd like to train a computer to be able to, in the future, generalize

5193
08:09:20,974 --> 08:09:22,884
to other dollar bills that I might see

5194
08:09:23,758 --> 08:09:24,228
as well.

5195
08:09:24,814 --> 08:09:34,752
And the problem with overfitting is that if you try and tie yourself too closely to the data set that you're training your model on, you can end up not generalizing very well.

5196
08:09:35,214 --> 08:09:43,620
So what does this look like? Well, we might imagine the rainy day and not rainy day example again from here, where the blue points indicate rainy days and the red points indicate not rainy days.

5197
08:09:43,854 --> 08:09:51,872
And we decided that we felt pretty comfortable with drawing a line like this as the decision boundary between rainy days and not rainy days.

5198
08:09:52,032 --> 08:09:59,268
So we can pretty comfortably say that points on this side more likely to be rainy days, points on that side more likely to be not rainy days.

5199
08:09:59,790 --> 08:10:06,212
But the loss, the empirical loss, isn't zero in this particular case, because we didn't categorize everything perfectly.

5200
08:10:06,958 --> 08:10:13,220
There was this one outlier, this one day that it wasn't raining, but yet our model still predicts that it is raining.

5201
08:10:13,518 --> 08:10:17,828
But that doesn't necessarily mean our model is bad, it just means the model isn't 100% accurate.

5202
08:10:18,702 --> 08:10:26,464
If you really wanted to try and find a hypothesis that resulted in minimizing the loss, you could come up with a different decision boundary.

5203
08:10:26,544 --> 08:10:29,028
It wouldn't be a line, but it would look something like this.

5204
08:10:29,806 --> 08:10:39,492
Like this decision boundary does separate all of the red points from all of the blue points, because the red points fall on this side of this decision boundary,

5205
08:10:39,664 --> 08:10:42,180
the blue points fall on the other side of the decision boundary.

5206
08:10:42,638 --> 08:10:47,268
But this we would probably argue is not as good of a prediction.

5207
08:10:47,504 --> 08:10:55,012
Even though it seems to be more accurate based on all of the available training data that we have for training this machine learning model,

5208
08:10:55,470 --> 08:10:58,064
we might say that it's probably not going to generalize well.

5209
08:10:58,144 --> 08:11:06,084
That if there were other data points like here and there, we might still want to consider those to be rainy days, because we think this was probably just an outlier.

5210
08:11:06,606 --> 08:11:13,028
So if the only thing you care about is minimizing the loss on the data you have available to you, you run the risk of overfitting.

5211
08:11:13,230 --> 08:11:15,108
And this can happen in the classification case.

5212
08:11:15,438 --> 08:11:18,052
It can also happen in the regression case.

5213
08:11:18,382 --> 08:11:26,692
That here we predicted what we thought was a pretty good line relating advertising to sales, trying to predict what sales were going to be for a given amount of advertising.

5214
08:11:26,926 --> 08:11:35,268
But I could come up with a line that does a better job of predicting the training data, and it would be something that looks like this, just connecting all of the various different data points,

5215
08:11:35,584 --> 08:11:37,504
and now there is no loss at all.

5216
08:11:37,664 --> 08:11:44,708
Now I've perfectly predicted, given any advertising, what sales are, and for all the data available to me, it's going to be accurate.

5217
08:11:45,294 --> 08:11:47,824
But it's probably not going to generalize very well.

5218
08:11:47,984 --> 08:11:52,260
I have overfit my model on the training data that is available to me.

5219
08:11:52,878 --> 08:11:59,716
And so in general, we want to avoid overfitting. We'd like strategies to make sure that we haven't overfit our model to a particular data set.

5220
08:12:00,078 --> 08:12:02,052
And there are a number of ways that you could try to do this.

5221
08:12:02,638 --> 08:12:05,476
One way is by examining what it is that we're optimizing for.

5222
08:12:05,710 --> 08:12:11,620
In an optimization problem, all we do is we say, you know, there is some cost, and I want to minimize that cost.

5223
08:12:12,462 --> 08:12:21,092
And so far, we've defined that cost function, the cost of a hypothesis, just as being equal to the empirical loss of that hypothesis.

5224
08:12:21,184 --> 08:12:28,996
Like how far away are the actual data points, the outputs, away from what I predicted them to be based on that particular hypothesis.

5225
08:12:29,358 --> 08:12:36,676
And if all you're trying to do is minimize cost, meaning minimizing the loss in this case, then the result is going to be that you might overfit.

5226
08:12:37,024 --> 08:12:47,300
That to minimize cost, you're going to try and find a way to perfectly match all the input data, and that might happen as a result of overfitting on that particular input data.

5227
08:12:48,494 --> 08:12:52,228
So in order to address this, you could add something to the cost function.

5228
08:12:52,558 --> 08:12:59,332
What counts as cost Well, not just loss, but also some measure of the complexity of the hypothesis.

5229
08:12:59,534 --> 08:13:05,984
The complexity of the hypothesis is something that you would need to define for, how complicated does our line look?

5230
08:13:06,144 --> 08:13:15,044
This is sort of an Occam's razor style approach where we want to give preference to a simpler decision boundary, like a straight line, for example, some simpler curve,

5231
08:13:15,264 --> 08:13:21,264
as opposed to something far more complex that might represent the training data better, but might not generalize as well.

5232
08:13:21,424 --> 08:13:30,148
We'll generally say that a simpler solution is probably the better solution and probably the one that is more likely to generalize well to other inputs.

5233
08:13:31,182 --> 08:13:38,372
So we measure what the loss is, but we also measure the complexity, and now that all gets taken into account when we consider the overall cost.

5234
08:13:38,670 --> 08:13:47,744
That yes, something might have less loss if it better predicts the training data, but if it's much more complex, it still might not be the best option that we have.

5235
08:13:48,014 --> 08:13:51,744
And we need to come up with some balance between loss and complexity.

5236
08:13:51,918 --> 08:14:00,772
And for that reason, you'll often see this represented as multiplying the complexity by some parameter that we have to choose, parameter lambda in this case, where we're saying,

5237
08:14:01,344 --> 08:14:06,544
if lambda is a greater value, then we really want to penalize more complex hypotheses.

5238
08:14:06,798 --> 08:14:11,104
Whereas if lambda is smaller, we're going to penalize more complex hypotheses a little bit.

5239
08:14:11,342 --> 08:14:18,562
And it's up to the machine learning programmer to decide where they want to set that value of lambda for how much do I want to

5240
08:14:18,624 --> 08:14:23,024
penalize a more complex hypothesis that might fit the data a little better.

5241
08:14:23,342 --> 08:14:25,664
And again, there's no one right answer to a lot of these things.

5242
08:14:25,824 --> 08:14:32,784
That depending on the data set, depending on the data you have available to you and the problem you're trying to solve, your choice of these parameters may vary,

5243
08:14:32,910 --> 08:14:37,892
and you may need to experiment a little bit to figure out what the right choice of that is ultimately going to be.

5244
08:14:38,638 --> 08:14:45,604
This process then of considering not only loss, but also some measure of the complexity is known as regularization.

5245
08:14:45,904 --> 08:14:55,744
Regularization is the process of penalizing a hypothesis that is more complex in order to favor a simpler hypothesis that is more likely to generalize well,

5246
08:14:55,918 --> 08:15:04,144
more likely to be able to apply to other situations that are dealing with other input points unlike the ones that we've necessarily seen before.

5247
08:15:04,494 --> 08:15:12,932
So oftentimes you'll see us add some regularizing term to what we're trying to minimize in order to avoid this problem of overfitting.

5248
08:15:14,094 --> 08:15:23,396
Now, another way of making sure we don't overfit is to run some experiments and to see whether or not we are able to generalize our model that we've created

5249
08:15:23,776 --> 08:15:25,776
to other data sets as well.

5250
08:15:26,126 --> 08:15:34,212
And it's for that reason that oftentimes when you're doing a machine learning experiment, when you've got some data and you want to try and come up with some function that predicts, given some input,

5251
08:15:34,414 --> 08:15:43,856
what the output is going to be, you don't necessarily want to do your training on all of the data you have available to you, that you could employ a method known as holdout

5252
08:15:44,016 --> 08:15:44,964
cross-validation.

5253
08:15:45,326 --> 08:15:48,256
Where in holdout cross-validation, we split up our data.

5254
08:15:48,416 --> 08:15:52,964
We split up our data into a training set and a testing set.

5255
08:15:53,518 --> 08:15:57,156
The training set is the set of data that we're going to use to train our machine learning model.

5256
08:15:57,742 --> 08:16:05,604
And the testing set is the set of data that we're going to use in order to test to see how well our machine learning model actually performed.

5257
08:16:06,510 --> 08:16:08,388
So the learning happens on the training set.

5258
08:16:08,656 --> 08:16:12,132
We figure out what the parameters should be, we figure out what the right model is.

5259
08:16:12,526 --> 08:16:21,776
And then we see, all right, now that we've trained the model, see how well it does at predicting things inside of the testing set, some set of data that we haven't seen before.

5260
08:16:22,126 --> 08:16:30,736
And the hope then is that we're going to be able to predict the testing set pretty well if we're able to generalize based on the training data that's available to us.

5261
08:16:30,926 --> 08:16:36,132
If we've overfit the training data though, and we're not able to generalize, well then when we look at the testing set,

5262
08:16:36,302 --> 08:16:41,060
it's likely going to be the case that we're not going to predict things in the testing set nearly as effectively.

5263
08:16:41,806 --> 08:16:49,380
So this is one method of cross-validation, validating to make sure that the work we have done is actually going to generalize to other data sets as well.

5264
08:16:49,646 --> 08:16:52,256
And there are other statistical techniques we can use as well.

5265
08:16:52,526 --> 08:17:01,456
One of the downsides of this just holdout cross-validation is if you say I just split it 50-50, I train using 50% of the data and test using the other 50%,

5266
08:17:01,776 --> 08:17:11,652
or you could choose other percentages as well, is that there is a fair amount of data that I am now not using to train, that I might be able to get a better model as a result, for example.

5267
08:17:12,526 --> 08:17:16,004
So one approach is known as k-fold cross-validation.

5268
08:17:16,398 --> 08:17:24,816
In k-fold cross-validation, rather than just divide things into two sets and run one experiment, we divide things into k different sets.

5269
08:17:24,910 --> 08:17:29,444
So maybe I divide things up into ten different sets and then run 10 different experiments.

5270
08:17:30,222 --> 08:17:39,616
So if I split up my data into 10 different sets of data, then what I'll do is each time for each of my 10 experiments, I will hold out one of those sets of data.

5271
08:17:39,918 --> 08:17:42,496
Where I'll say, let me train my model on these nine

5272
08:17:42,496 --> 08:17:46,176
sets, and then test to see how well it predicts on set number

5273
08:17:46,176 --> 08:17:52,036
10. And then pick another set of nine sets to train on, and then test it on the other one that I held out.

5274
08:17:52,256 --> 08:18:00,656
Where each time I train the model on everything minus the one set that I'm holding out, and then test to see how well our model performs on the test

5275
08:18:00,816 --> 08:18:01,604
that I did hold out.

5276
08:18:01,998 --> 08:18:07,140
And what you end up getting is 10 different results, 10 different answers for how accurately our model worked,

5277
08:18:07,296 --> 08:18:13,936
and oftentimes you could just take the average of those 10 to get an approximation for how well we think our model performs overall.

5278
08:18:14,096 --> 08:18:23,296
But the key idea is separating the training data from the testing data, because you want to test your model on data that is different from what you trained the model on.

5279
08:18:23,376 --> 08:18:29,252
Because the training, you want to avoid overfitting, you want to be able to generalize, and the way you test whether you're able to generalize

5280
08:18:29,456 --> 08:18:35,012
is by looking at some data that you haven't seen before and seeing how well we're actually able to perform.

5281
08:18:36,174 --> 08:18:42,500
And so if we want to actually implement any of these techniques inside of a programming language like Python, number of ways we could do that.

5282
08:18:42,736 --> 08:18:50,020
We could write this from scratch on our own, but there are libraries out there that allow us to take advantage of existing implementations of these algorithms.

5283
08:18:50,336 --> 08:18:54,416
That we can use the same types of algorithms in a lot of different situations.

5284
08:18:54,702 --> 08:19:03,696
