5399
08:29:58,062 --> 08:30:03,792
And our agent is situated in their environment, where the environment is where they're going to make their actions,

5400
08:30:04,014 --> 08:30:08,100
and it's what's going to give them rewards or punishments for various actions that they're in.

5401
08:30:08,878 --> 08:30:14,436
So for example, the environment is going to start off by putting our agent inside of a state.

5402
08:30:14,862 --> 08:30:19,632
Our agent has some state that in a game might be the state of the game that the agent is playing.

5403
08:30:19,822 --> 08:30:25,552
In a world that the agent is exploring might be some position inside of a grid representing the world that they're exploring.

5404
08:30:25,712 --> 08:30:31,712
But the agent is in some sort of state, and in that state, the agent needs to choose to take an action.

5405
08:30:32,046 --> 08:30:35,812
The agent likely has multiple actions they can choose from, but they pick an action.

5406
08:30:36,270 --> 08:30:38,788
So they may they take an action in a particular state.

5407
08:30:39,182 --> 08:30:44,708
And as a result of that, the agent will generally get two things in response as we model them.

5408
08:30:44,992 --> 08:30:47,552
The agent gets a new state that they find themselves in.

5409
08:30:47,712 --> 08:30:51,364
After being in this state, taking one action, they end up in some other state.

5410
08:30:52,078 --> 08:30:55,232
And they're also given some sort of numerical reward.

5411
08:30:55,392 --> 08:30:58,244
Positive meaning reward meaning it was a good thing.

5412
08:30:58,592 --> 08:31:02,340
Negative generally meaning they did something bad, they received some sort of punishment.

5413
08:31:03,182 --> 08:31:05,828
And that is all the information the agent has.

5414
08:31:06,094 --> 08:31:07,428
It's told what state it's

5415
08:31:08,142 --> 08:31:16,512
in, it makes some sort of action, and based on that it ends up in another state, and it ends up getting some particular reward, and it needs to learn based on that information

5416
08:31:16,752 --> 08:31:19,312
what actions to begin to take in the future.

5417
08:31:19,630 --> 08:31:22,672
And so you could imagine generalizing this to a lot of different situations.

5418
08:31:22,894 --> 08:31:28,740
This is oftentimes how you train, if you've ever seen those robots that are now able to walk around sort of the way humans do,

5419
08:31:29,006 --> 08:31:33,956
it would be quite difficult to program the robot in exactly the right way to get it to walk the way humans do.

5420
08:31:34,222 --> 08:31:41,860
You could instead train it through reinforcement learning, give it some sort of numerical reward every time it does something good, like take steps forward,

5421
08:31:42,094 --> 08:31:46,948
and punish it every time it does something bad, like fall over, and then let the AI just learn.

5422
08:31:47,072 --> 08:31:55,268
Based on that sequence of rewards, based on trying to take various different actions, you can begin to have the agent learn what to do in the future and what not to do.

5423
08:31:56,046 --> 08:32:05,220
So in order to begin to formalize this, the first thing we need to do is formalize this notion of what we mean about states and actions and rewards, like what does this world look like?

5424
08:32:05,678 --> 08:32:10,564
And oftentimes we'll formulate this world as what's known as a Markov decision process.

5425
08:32:10,926 --> 08:32:14,032
Similar in spirit to Markov chains, which you might recall from before.

5426
08:32:14,350 --> 08:32:21,284
But a Markov decision process is a model that we can use for decision making, for an agent trying to make decisions in its environment.

5427
08:32:21,454 --> 08:32:29,552
and it's a model that allows us to represent the various different states that an agent can be in, the various different actions that they can take, and also

5428
08:32:29,742 --> 08:32:34,020
what the reward is for taking one action as opposed to another action.

5429
08:32:35,054 --> 08:32:37,092
So what then does it actually look like?

5430
08:32:37,422 --> 08:32:45,284
Well, if you recall a Markov chain from before, a Markov chain looked a little something like this, where we had a whole bunch of these individual states,

5431
08:32:45,710 --> 08:32:50,592
and each state immediately transitioned to another state based on some probability distribution.

5432
08:32:50,798 --> 08:32:59,012
We saw this in the context of the weather before, where if it was sunny, we said with some probability it'll be sunny the next day, with some other probability it'll be rainy, for example.

5433
08:32:59,726 --> 08:33:02,112
But we could also imagine generalizing this.

5434
08:33:02,272 --> 08:33:03,812
It's not just sun and rain anymore.

5435
08:33:04,032 --> 08:33:09,252
We just have these states, where one state leads to another state according to some probability distribution.

5436
08:33:09,742 --> 08:33:14,272
But in this original model, there was no agent that had any control over this process.

5437
08:33:14,432 --> 08:33:21,860
It was just entirely probability-based, where with some probability we move to this next state, but maybe it's going to be some other state with some other probability.

5438
08:33:22,350 --> 08:33:29,924
What we'll now have is the ability for the agent in this state to choose from a set of actions, where maybe instead of just one path forward,

5439
08:33:30,112 --> 08:33:33,952
they have three different choices of actions that each lead down different paths.

5440
08:33:34,032 --> 08:33:41,636
And even this is a bit of an oversimplification, because in each of these states you might imagine more branching points where there are more decisions that can be taken as well.

5441
08:33:42,062 --> 08:33:43,684
So we've extended the Markov chain

5442
08:33:44,430 --> 08:33:48,192
to say that from a state, you now have available action choices.

5443
08:33:48,352 --> 08:33:54,884
And each of those actions might be associated with its own probability distribution of going to various different states.

5444
08:33:55,918 --> 08:33:57,956
Then in addition, we'll add another extension

5445
08:33:58,766 --> 08:34:06,436
where any time you move from a state, taking an action, going into this other state, we can associate a reward with that outcome.

5446
08:34:07,022 --> 08:34:12,420
Saying either R is positive, meaning some positive reward, or R is negative, meaning there was some sort of punishment.

5447
08:34:13,262 --> 08:34:16,192
And this then is what we'll consider to be a Markov decision process.

5448
08:34:16,422 --> 08:34:21,156
that a Markov decision process has some initial set of states, of states in the world that we can be in.

5449
08:34:21,582 --> 08:34:29,412
We have some set of actions that, given a state, I can say what are the actions that are available to me in that state, an action that I can choose from.

5450
08:34:30,510 --> 08:34:32,068
Then we have some transition model.

5451
08:34:32,462 --> 08:34:39,396
The transition model before just said that given my current state, what is the probability that I end up in that next state or this other state?

5452
08:34:39,822 --> 08:34:51,136
The transition model now has effectively two things we're conditioning on. We're saying, given that I'm in this state and that I take this action, what's the probability that I end up in this next

5453
08:34:51,136 --> 08:35:00,388
state? Now maybe we live in a very deterministic world in this Markov decision process where given a state and given an action, we know for sure what next state we'll end up in.

5454
08:35:00,622 --> 08:35:06,916
But maybe there's some randomness in the world that when you take in a state and you take an action, you might not always end up in the exact same state.

5455
08:35:07,150 --> 08:35:09,376
There might be some probabilities involved there as well.

5456
08:35:09,742 --> 08:35:13,028
The Markov decision process can handle both of those possible cases.

5457
08:35:14,030 --> 08:35:24,816
And then finally, we have a reward function, generally called R, that in this case says, what is the reward for being in this state, taking this action, and then getting to S prime,

5458
08:35:26,798 --> 08:35:29,604
So I'm in this original state, I take this action, I get to this next state.

5459
08:35:29,936 --> 08:35:32,196
What is the reward for doing that process?

5460
08:35:32,558 --> 08:35:38,404
And you can add up these rewards every time you take an action to get the total amount of rewards that an agent might get

5461
08:35:39,246 --> 08:35:43,296
from interacting in a particular environment modeled using this Markov decision

5462
08:35:43,296 --> 08:35:46,308
process. So what might this actually look like in practice?

5463
08:35:46,606 --> 08:35:53,668
Well, let's just create a little simulated world here where I have this agent that is just trying to navigate its way, this agent is this yellow dot here,

5464
08:35:53,998 --> 08:35:59,856
like a robot in the world, trying to navigate its way through this grid, and ultimately it's trying to find its way to the goal.

5465
08:36:00,046 --> 08:36:03,364
And if it gets to the green goal, then it's going to get some sort of reward.

5466
08:36:04,174 --> 08:36:12,096
But then we might also have some, red squares that are places where you get some sort of punishment, some bad place where we don't want the agent to go.

5467
08:36:12,366 --> 08:36:17,924
And if it ends up in the red square, then our agent is going to get some sort of punishment as a result of that.

5468
08:36:18,222 --> 08:36:21,456
But the agent originally doesn't know all of these details.

5469
08:36:21,536 --> 08:36:27,876
It doesn't know that these states are associated with punishments, but maybe it does know that this state is associated with a reward, maybe it doesn't,

5470
08:36:28,110 --> 08:36:33,028
but it just needs to sort of interact with the environment to try and figure out what to do and what not to

5471
08:36:33,870 --> 08:36:42,532
do. So the first thing the agent might do is given no additional information, if it doesn't know what the punishments are, it doesn't know where the rewards are, it just might try and take an action.

5472
08:36:43,022 --> 08:36:46,980
And it takes an action and ends up realizing that it got some sort of punishment.

5473
08:36:47,502 --> 08:36:49,412
And so what does it learn from that experience?

5474
08:36:49,742 --> 08:36:55,696
Well, it might learn that when you're in this state in the future, don't take the action, move to the right.

5475
08:36:55,776 --> 08:36:57,056
That that is a bad action to take.

5476
08:36:57,166 --> 08:37:04,996
That in the future, if you ever find yourself back in the state, don't take this action of going to the right when you're in this particular state, because that leads to punishment.

5477
08:37:05,390 --> 08:37:06,564
That might be the intuition at least.

5478
08:37:06,656 --> 08:37:13,956
And so you could try doing other actions. You move up, all right, that didn't lead to any immediate rewards, maybe try something else, then maybe try something else.

5479
08:37:14,606 --> 08:37:18,656
And all right, now you found that you got another punishment, and so you learn something from that experience.

5480
08:37:18,736 --> 08:37:28,644
So the next time you do this whole process, you know that if you ever end up in this square, you shouldn't take the down action, because being in this state and taking that action ultimately leads to

5481
08:37:29,294 --> 08:37:32,420
some sort of punishment, a negative reward, in other words.

5482
08:37:32,974 --> 08:37:41,856
And this process repeats. You might imagine just letting our agent explore the world, learning over time. what states tend to correspond with poor actions, learning over time.

5483
08:37:41,856 --> 08:37:50,180
what states correspond with poor actions, until eventually, if it tries enough things randomly, it might find that eventually, when you get to this state,

5484
08:37:50,510 --> 08:37:55,364
if you take the up action in this state, it might find that you actually get a reward from that.

5485
08:37:56,046 --> 08:38:02,148
And what it can learn from that is that if you're in this state, you should take the up action, because that leads to a reward.

5486
08:38:02,510 --> 08:38:10,176
And over time, you can also learn that if you're in this state, you should take the left action because that leads to this state that also lets you eventually get to the reward.

5487
08:38:10,256 --> 08:38:18,016
So you begin to learn over time not only which actions are good in particular states, but also which actions are bad.

5488
08:38:18,350 --> 08:38:27,492
Such that once you know some sequence of good actions that leads you to some sort of reward, our agent can just follow those instructions, follow the experience that it has learned.

5489
08:38:27,696 --> 08:38:35,536
We didn't tell the agent what the goal was, we didn't tell the agent where the punishments were, but the agent can begin to learn from this experience

5490
08:38:35,630 --> 08:38:39,556
and learn to begin to perform these sorts of tasks better in the future.

5491
08:38:40,654 --> 08:38:42,496
And so let's now try to formalize this idea.

5492
08:38:42,656 --> 08:38:49,296
Formalize the idea that we would like to be able to learn in this state, taking this action, is that a good thing or a bad thing?

5493
08:38:49,614 --> 08:38:53,284
There are lots of different models for reinforcement learning. We're just going to look at one of them today.

5494
08:38:53,582 --> 08:38:56,836
And the one that we're going to look at is a method known as Q learning.

5495
08:38:57,230 --> 08:39:07,332
And what Q learning is all about is it's about learning a function, a function q, that takes inputs S and A, where S is a state and A is an action that you take in that state.

5496
08:39:07,726 --> 08:39:15,876
And what this Q function is going to do is it is going to estimate the value how much reward will I get from taking this action

5497
08:39:16,416 --> 08:39:17,572
in this state?

5498
08:39:18,830 --> 08:39:28,560
Originally, we don't know what this q function should be, but over time, based on experience, based on trying things out and seeing what the result is, I would like to try and learn what q

5499
08:39:28,720 --> 08:39:34,320
of sa is for any particular state and any particular action that I might take in that state.

5500
08:39:34,670 --> 08:39:35,520
So what is the approach?

5501
08:39:35,758 --> 08:39:42,692
Well, the approach originally is we'll start with qSA equal to zero for all states S and for all actions A.

5502
08:39:42,880 --> 08:39:50,532
That initially, before I've ever started anything, before I've had any experiences, I don't know the value of taking any action in any given state.

5503
08:39:50,798 --> 08:39:54,372
So I'm going to assume that the value is just zero all across the board.

5504
08:39:55,214 --> 08:40:02,788
But then as I interact with the world, as I experience rewards or punishments, or maybe I go to a cell where I don't get either a reward or a punishment,

5505
08:40:03,310 --> 08:40:04,708
I want to somehow update

5506
08:40:05,454 --> 08:40:14,884
my estimate of QSA. I want to continually update my estimate of QSA based on the experiences and rewards and punishments that I've received, such that in the future,

5507
08:40:15,200 --> 08:40:18,596
my knowledge of what actions are good in what states will be better.

5508
08:40:19,150 --> 08:40:25,040
So when we take an action and receive some sort of reward, I want to estimate the new value of QSA.

5509
08:40:25,614 --> 08:40:27,748
And I estimate that based on a couple of different things.

5510
08:40:28,270 --> 08:40:33,188
I estimate it based on the reward that I'm getting from taking this action and getting into the next state.

5511
08:40:33,774 --> 08:40:44,228
But assuming the situation isn't over, assuming there are still future actions that I might take as well, I also need to take into account the expected future rewards.

5512
08:40:44,590 --> 08:40:52,708
That if you imagine an agent interacting with the environment, and sometimes you'll take an action and get a reward, but then you can keep taking more actions and get more rewards,

5513
08:40:52,880 --> 08:40:54,080
that these both are relevant.

5514
08:40:54,240 --> 08:40:58,212
Both the current reward I'm getting from this current step and also my future reward.

5515
08:40:58,446 --> 08:41:07,280
And it might be the case that I'll want to take a step that doesn't immediately lead to a reward, because later on down the line I know it will lead to more rewards as well.

5516
08:41:07,502 --> 08:41:15,140
So there's a balancing act between current rewards that the agent experiences and future rewards that the agent experiences as well.

5517
08:41:16,654 --> 08:41:19,040
And then we need to update QSA.

5518
08:41:19,534 --> 08:41:24,068
So we estimate the value of QSA based on the current reward and the expected future rewards.

5519
08:41:24,270 --> 08:41:29,124
And then we need to update this Q function to take into account this new estimate.

5520
08:41:29,422 --> 08:41:34,596
Now we already, as we go through this process, we'll already have an estimate for what we think the value is.

5521
08:41:35,054 --> 08:41:42,116
Now we have a new estimate, and then somehow we need to combine these two estimates together, and we'll look at more formal ways that we can actually begin to do that.

5522
08:41:42,926 --> 08:41:47,364
So to actually show you what this formula looks like, here is the approach we'll take with Q-learning.

5523
08:41:47,726 --> 08:41:51,716
We're going to again start with Q of S and A being equal to zero for all states.

5524
08:41:52,398 --> 08:41:56,068
And then every time we take an action A in state S

5525
08:41:56,960 --> 08:42:03,460
and observe a reward R, we're going to update our value, our estimate, for Q of SA.

5526
08:42:04,110 --> 08:42:11,524
And the idea is that we're going to figure out what the new value estimate is minus what our existing value estimate is.

5527
08:42:12,160 --> 08:42:17,188
So we have some preconceived notion for what the value is for taking this action in this state.

5528
08:42:17,440 --> 08:42:20,320
Maybe our expectation is we currently think the value is

5529
08:42:20,320 --> 08:42:24,240
10. But then we're going to estimate what we now think it's going to be.

5530
08:42:24,430 --> 08:42:26,628
Maybe the new value estimate is something like 20.

5531
08:42:27,214 --> 08:42:34,404
So there's a delta of like 10 that our new value estimate is 10 points higher than what our current value estimate happens to be.

5532
08:42:35,150 --> 08:42:36,580
And so we have a couple of options here.

5533
08:42:37,134 --> 08:42:45,188
We need to decide how much we want to adjust our current expectation of what the value is of taking this action in this particular state.

5534
08:42:45,680 --> 08:42:54,240
And what that difference is, how much we add or subtract from our existing notion of how much that we expect the value to be, is dependent on this parameter

5535
08:42:54,400 --> 08:42:56,388
alpha, also called a learning rate.

5536
08:42:56,654 --> 08:43:04,100
And alpha represents, in effect, how much we value new information compared to how much we value old information.

5537
08:43:04,654 --> 08:43:08,160
An alpha value of one means we really value new information.

5538
08:43:08,334 --> 08:43:11,972
But if we have a new estimate, then it doesn't matter what our old estimate is.

5539
08:43:12,160 --> 08:43:17,796
We're only going to consider our new estimate because we always just want to take into consideration, our new information.

5540
08:43:18,254 --> 08:43:27,040
So the way that works is that if you imagine alpha being one, well then we're taking the old value of QSA and then adding one

5541
08:43:27,200 --> 08:43:31,600
times the new value minus the old value, and that just leaves us with the new value.

5542
08:43:31,822 --> 08:43:36,900
So when alpha is one, all we take into consideration is what our new estimate happens to be.

5543
08:43:37,582 --> 08:43:42,320
But over time, as we go through a lot of experiences, we already have some existing information.

5544
08:43:42,480 --> 08:43:47,620
We might have tried taking this action nine times already, and now we just tried it a tenth time.

5545
08:43:47,982 --> 08:43:57,892
And we don't only want to consider this tenth experience, I also want to consider the fact that my prior nine experiences, those were meaningful too, and that's data I don't necessarily want to lose.

5546
08:43:58,190 --> 08:44:03,200
And so this alpha controls that decision, controls how important is the new information.

5547
08:44:03,440 --> 08:44:08,836
Zero would mean ignore all the new information, just keep this Q value the same.

5548
08:44:09,294 --> 08:44:16,836
One means replace the old information entirely with the new information, and somewhere in between, keep some sort of balance between these two values.

5549
08:44:17,838 --> 08:44:20,548
We can put this equation a little bit more formally as well.

5550
08:44:20,928 --> 08:44:27,332
The old value estimate is our old estimate for what the value is of taking this action in a particular state.

5551
08:44:27,598 --> 08:44:37,380
That's just q of s and a. So we have it once here, and we're going to add something to it. We're going to add alpha times the new value estimate minus the old value estimate.

5552
08:44:37,678 --> 08:44:41,188
But the old value estimate we just look up by calling this Q function.

5553
08:44:42,222 --> 08:44:44,128
And what then is the new value estimate?

5554
08:44:44,288 --> 08:44:50,948
Based on this experience we have just taken, what is our new estimate for the value of taking this action in this particular state?

5555
08:44:51,470 --> 08:44:53,476
Well, it's going to be composed of two parts.

5556
08:44:53,968 --> 08:44:59,396
It's going to be composed of what reward did I just get from taking this action in this state?

5557
08:44:59,918 --> 08:45:05,060
And then it's going to be what can I expect my future rewards to be from this point forward?

5558
08:45:05,614 --> 08:45:06,468
So it's going to be

5559
08:45:07,374 --> 08:45:13,892
R, some reward I'm getting right now, plus whatever I estimate I'm going to get in the future.

5560
08:45:14,446 --> 08:45:16,528
And how do I estimate what I'm going to get in the future?

5561
08:45:16,750 --> 08:45:19,888
Well, it's a bit of another call to this Q function.

5562
08:45:20,046 --> 08:45:28,356
It's going to be take the maximum across all possible actions I could take next and say, all right, of all of these possible actions I could take,

5563
08:45:28,768 --> 08:45:31,140
which one is going to have the highest reward?

5564
08:45:31,470 --> 08:45:33,408
So this then looks a little bit complicated.

5565
08:45:33,568 --> 08:45:37,156
This is going to be our notion for how we're going to perform this kind of update.

5566
08:45:37,408 --> 08:45:46,564
That I have some estimate, some old estimate for what the value is of taking this action in this state, and I'm going to update it based on new information.

5567
08:45:46,830 --> 08:45:57,444
That I experience some reward, I predict what my future reward is going to be, and using that I update what what I estimate the reward will be for taking this action in this particular state.

5568
08:45:57,806 --> 08:46:00,448
And there are other additions you might make to this algorithm as well.

5569
08:46:00,686 --> 08:46:05,568
Sometimes it might not be the case that future rewards you want to wait equally to current rewards.

5570
08:46:05,728 --> 08:46:10,048
Maybe you want an agent that values like reward now over reward later.

5571
08:46:10,350 --> 08:46:19,648
And so sometimes you can even add another term in here, some other parameter, where you discount future rewards and say future rewards are not as valuable as rewards immediately.

5572
08:46:19,790 --> 08:46:24,324
The getting reward in the current time step is better than waiting a year and getting rewards later.

5573
08:46:24,528 --> 08:46:28,484
But that's something up to the programmer to decide what that parameter ought to be.

5574
08:46:29,134 --> 08:46:36,740
But the big picture idea of this entire formula is to say that every time we experience some new reward, we take that into account.

5575
08:46:36,928 --> 08:46:40,228
We update our estimate of how good is this action.

5576
08:46:40,686 --> 08:46:43,908
And then in the future, we can make decisions based on that algorithm.

5577
08:46:44,128 --> 08:46:54,532
Once we have some good estimate for every state and for every action, what the value is of taking that action, then we can do something like implement a greedy decision-making policy.

5578
08:46:54,862 --> 08:47:05,248
That if I am in a state and I want to know what action should I take in that state, well then I consider for all of my possible actions, what is the value of QSA?

5579
08:47:05,648 --> 08:47:08,804
What is my estimated value of taking that action in that state?

5580
08:47:09,038 --> 08:47:14,404
And I will just pick the action that has the highest value after I evaluate that expression.

5581
08:47:15,246 --> 08:47:19,808
So I pick the action that has the highest value, and based on that, that tells me what action I should take.

5582
08:47:19,888 --> 08:47:27,716
At any given state that I'm in, I can just greedily say, across all my actions, this action gives me the highest expected value,

5583
08:47:27,950 --> 08:47:32,228
and so I'll go ahead and choose that action as the action that I take as well.

5584
08:47:33,262 --> 08:47:35,888
But there is a downside to this kind of approach.

5585
08:47:36,142 --> 08:47:42,628
And the downside comes up in a situation like this, where we know that, there is some solution that gets me to the reward,

5586
08:47:44,046 --> 08:47:49,700
and our agent has been able to figure that out, but it might not necessarily be the best way or the fastest way.

5587
08:47:49,968 --> 08:47:59,364
If the agent is allowed to explore a little bit more, it might find that it can get the reward faster by taking some other route instead, by going through this particular path

5588
08:47:59,630 --> 08:48:02,020
that is a faster way to get to

5589
08:48:02,702 --> 08:48:03,556
that ultimate goal.

5590
08:48:04,206 --> 08:48:07,364
And maybe we would like for the agent to be able to figure that out as well.

5591
08:48:07,598 --> 08:48:15,844
But if the agent always takes the actions that it knows to be best, well when it gets to this particular square, it doesn't know that this is a good action

5592
08:48:16,110 --> 08:48:17,508
because it's never really tried it.

5593
08:48:17,710 --> 08:48:21,568
But it knows that going down eventually leads its way to this reward.

5594
08:48:21,870 --> 08:48:28,804
So it might learn in the future that it should just always take this route and it's never going to explore and go along that route instead.

5595
08:48:29,582 --> 08:48:35,044
So in reinforcement learning, there is this tension between exploration and exploitation.

5596
08:48:35,342 --> 08:48:40,048
And exploitation generally refers to using knowledge that the AI already has.

5597
08:48:40,270 --> 08:48:44,964
The AI already knows that this is a move that leads to reward, so we'll go ahead and use that move.

5598
08:48:45,390 --> 08:48:54,628
And exploration is all about exploring other actions that we may not have explored as thoroughly before, because maybe one of these actions, even if I don't know anything about it,

5599
08:48:55,008 --> 08:48:59,236
might lead to better rewards faster or to more rewards in the future.

5600
08:49:00,046 --> 08:49:07,428
And so an agent that only ever exploits information and never explores might be able to get reward, but it might not maximize its rewards,

5601
08:49:07,712 --> 08:49:10,592
because it doesn't know what other possibilities are out there.

5602
08:49:10,798 --> 08:49:14,948
Possibilities that would only know about by taking advantage of exploration.

5603
08:49:15,758 --> 08:49:17,220
And so how can we try and address this?

5604
08:49:17,582 --> 08:49:25,700
Well, one possible solution is known as the epsilon greedy algorithm, where we set epsilon equal to how often we want to just make a random move.

5605
08:49:25,934 --> 08:49:31,748
Where occasionally we will just make a random move in order to say, let's try to explore and see what happens.

5606
08:49:33,102 --> 08:49:39,972
And then the logic of the algorithm will be with probability 1 minus epsilon, choose the estimated best move.

5607
08:49:40,718 --> 08:49:43,140
In a greedy case, we'd always choose the best move.

5608
08:49:43,566 --> 08:49:50,148
But in epsilon greedy, we're most of the time going to choose the best move, or sometimes going to choose the best move,

5609
08:49:50,446 --> 08:49:55,140
but sometimes with probability epsilon, we're going to choose a random move instead.

5610
08:49:55,982 --> 08:50:03,332
So every time we're faced with the ability to take an action, sometimes we're going to choose the best move, sometimes we're just going to choose a random move.

5611
08:50:03,886 --> 08:50:13,312
So this type of algorithm then can be quite powerful in a reinforcement learning context by not always just choosing the best possible move right now, but sometimes, especially early on,

5612
08:50:13,552 --> 08:50:19,268
allowing yourself to make random moves that allow you to explore various different possible states and actions more.

5613
08:50:19,438 --> 08:50:29,668
And maybe over time you might decrease your value of epsilon more and more often, choosing the best move after you're more confident that you've explored what all of the possibilities actually are.

5614
08:50:30,542 --> 08:50:35,684
So we can put this into practice, and one very common application of reinforcement learning is in game playing.

5615
08:50:35,886 --> 08:50:43,780
That if you want to teach an agent how to play a game, you just let the agent play the game a whole bunch, and then the reward signal happens at the end of the game.

5616
08:50:44,142 --> 08:50:49,380
When the game is over, if our AI won the game, it gets a reward of one, for example.

5617
08:50:49,614 --> 08:50:52,592
And if it lost the game, it gets a reward of negative one.

5618
08:50:52,974 --> 08:51:01,712
And from that it begins to learn what actions are good and what actions are bad. You don't have to tell the AI what's good and what's bad, but the AI figures it out based on that reward.

5619
08:51:01,872 --> 08:51:09,028
Winning the game is some signal, losing the game is some signal, and based on all of that, it begins to figure out what decisions it should actually make.

5620
08:51:09,870 --> 08:51:13,412
So one very simple game, which you may have played before, is a game called NIM.

5621
08:51:13,774 --> 08:51:17,792
And in the game of NIM, you've got a whole bunch of objects in a whole bunch of different piles.

5622
08:51:18,030 --> 08:51:20,672
Where here I've represented each pile as an individual row.

5623
08:51:21,038 --> 08:51:25,952
So you've got one object in the first pile, three in the second pile, five in the third pile, seven in the fourth pile.

5624
08:51:26,254 --> 08:51:31,632
And the game of NIM is a two-player game where players take turns removing objects from piles.

5625
08:51:31,854 --> 08:51:40,112
And the rule is that on any given turn, you are allowed to remove as many objects as you want from any one of these piles, any one of these rows.

5626
08:51:40,272 --> 08:51:46,500
You have to remove at least one object, but you can remove as many as you want from exactly one of the piles.

5627
08:51:46,766 --> 08:51:49,604
And whoever takes the last object loses.

5628
08:51:50,638 --> 08:51:54,352
So player one might remove four from this pile here.

5629
08:51:54,638 --> 08:52:00,272
Player two might remove four from this pile here. So now we've got four piles left, one, three, one, and three.

5630
08:52:00,494 --> 08:52:03,684
Player one might remove, the entirety of the second pile.

5631
08:52:03,952 --> 08:52:06,032
Player two, if they're being strategic,

5632
08:52:07,472 --> 08:52:09,508
might remove two from the third pile.

5633
08:52:09,806 --> 08:52:12,708
Now we've got three piles left, each with one object left.

5634
08:52:13,102 --> 08:52:21,828
Player one might remove one from one pile, player two removes one from the other pile, and now player one is left with choosing this one object from the last pile,

5635
08:52:22,062 --> 08:52:24,260
at which point player one loses the game.

5636
08:52:24,654 --> 08:52:26,912
So fairly simple game, piles of objects.

5637
08:52:27,118 --> 08:52:32,804
Any turn you choose how many objects to remove from a pile, whoever removes the last object loses.

5638
08:52:33,198 --> 08:52:39,312
And this is the type of game you could encode into an AI fairly easily, because the states are really just four numbers.

5639
08:52:39,472 --> 08:52:42,628
Every state is just how many objects in each of the four piles.

5640
08:52:43,022 --> 08:52:48,228
And the actions are things like how many am I going to remove from each one of these individual piles?

5641
08:52:48,974 --> 08:52:50,752
And the reward happens at the end.

5642
08:52:50,990 --> 08:52:55,460
That if you were the player that had to remove the last object, then you get some sort of punishment.

5643
08:52:55,822 --> 08:53:01,284
But if you were not and the other player had to remove the last object, well then you get some sort of reward.

5644
08:53:01,774 --> 08:53:07,620
So we could actually try and show a demonstration of this that I've implemented an AI to play the game of NIM.

5645
08:53:08,654 --> 08:53:08,992
All right.

5646
08:53:09,070 --> 08:53:16,912
so here what we're going to do is create an AI as a result of training the AI on some number of games that the AI is going to play against itself,

5647
08:53:17,070 --> 08:53:23,312
where the idea is the AI will play games against itself, learn from each of those experiences, and learn what to do in the future.

5648
08:53:23,598 --> 08:53:25,952
And then I, the human, will play against the AI.

5649
08:53:26,286 --> 08:53:33,732
So initially we'll say train zero times, meaning we're not going to let the AI play any practice games against itself in order to learn from its experiences.

5650
08:53:33,998 --> 08:53:35,748
We're just going to see how well it plays.

5651
08:53:36,782 --> 08:53:38,180
And it looks like there are four piles.

5652
08:53:38,352 --> 08:53:41,220
I can choose how many I remove from any one of the piles.

5653
08:53:41,518 --> 08:53:45,860
So maybe from pile three I will remove five objects, for example.

5654
08:53:46,672 --> 08:53:49,952
so now AI chose to take one item from pile zero.

5655
08:53:50,286 --> 08:53:52,548
So I'm left with these piles now, for example.

5656
08:53:52,942 --> 08:54:00,804
And so here I could choose maybe to say, I would like to remove from pile two, I'll remove all five of them, for example.

5657
08:54:01,646 --> 08:54:03,872
And so AI chose to take two away from pile one.

5658
08:54:04,206 --> 08:54:07,876
Now I'm left with one pile that has one object, one pile that has two objects.

5659
08:54:08,398 --> 08:54:17,156
So from pile three, I will remove two objects, and now I've left the AI with no choice but to take that last one, and so the game is over and I was able to win.

5660
08:54:17,678 --> 08:54:24,356
But I did so because the AI was really just playing randomly. It didn't have any prior experience that it was using in order to make these sorts of judgments.

5661
08:54:24,814 --> 08:54:28,832
Now let me let the AI train itself on like 10,000 games.

5662
08:54:29,038 --> 08:54:32,612
I'm going to let the AI play 10,000 games of NIM against itself.

5663
08:54:32,846 --> 08:54:39,140
Every time it wins or loses, it's going to learn from that experience and learn in the future what to do and what not to do.

5664
08:54:39,790 --> 08:54:40,452
So here then,

5665
08:54:41,102 --> 08:54:42,244
I'll go ahead and run this again.

5666
08:54:42,510 --> 08:54:50,272
And now you see the AI running through a whole bunch of training games, 10,000 training games against itself, and now it's going to let me make these sorts of decisions.

5667
08:54:50,382 --> 08:54:52,260
So now I'm going to play against the AI.

5668
08:54:52,526 --> 08:54:55,140
Maybe I'll remove one from pile three.

5669
08:54:55,712 --> 08:54:58,884
and the AI took everything from pile three, so I'm left with three piles.

5670
08:54:59,392 --> 08:55:03,172
I'll go ahead and from pile two, maybe remove three items.

5671
08:55:04,590 --> 08:55:09,732
and the AI removes one item from pile zero. I'm left with two piles, each of which has two items in it.

5672
08:55:10,352 --> 08:55:12,964
I'll remove one from pile one, I guess.

5673
08:55:14,350 --> 08:55:20,192
and the AI took two from pile two, leaving me with no choice but to take one away from pile one.

5674
08:55:20,462 --> 08:55:28,672
So it seems like after playing 10,000 games of NIM against itself, the AI has learned something about what states and what actions tend to be good,

5675
08:55:28,910 --> 08:55:36,352
and has begun to learn some sort of pattern for how to predict what actions are going to be good and what actions are going to be bad in any given

5676
08:55:36,352 --> 08:55:45,792
state. So reinforcement learning can be a very powerful technique for achieving these sorts of game-playing agents, agents that are able to play a game well just by learning from experience,

5677
08:55:45,952 --> 08:55:51,472
whether that's playing against other people, or by playing against itself and learning from those experiences as well.

5678
08:55:51,854 --> 08:55:56,912
Now, NIM is a bit of an easy game to use reinforcement learning for because there are so few states.

5679
08:55:57,072 --> 08:56:01,792
There are only states that are as many as how many different objects are in each of these various different piles.

5680
08:56:02,062 --> 08:56:11,076
You might imagine that it's going to be harder if you think of a game like chess, or games where there are many, many more states and many, many more actions that you can imagine taking,

5681
08:56:11,312 --> 08:56:17,312
where it's not going to be as easy to learn for every state and for every action what the value is going to be.

5682
08:56:17,550 --> 08:56:25,152
So oftentimes in that case, we can't necessarily learn exactly what the value is for every state and for every action, but we can approximate it.

5683
08:56:25,454 --> 08:56:33,792
So much as we saw with Minimax, that we could use a depth limiting approach to stop calculating at a certain point in time, we can do a similar type of approximation,

5684
08:56:34,062 --> 08:56:42,500
known as function approximation, in a reinforcement learning context, where instead of learning a value of Q for every state and every action,

5685
08:56:42,832 --> 08:56:52,032
we just have some function that estimates what the value is for taking this action in this particular state, that might be based on various different features of

5686
08:56:52,558 --> 08:56:53,072
the state

5687
08:56:53,966 --> 08:56:58,112
that the agent happens to be in, where you might have to choose what those features actually are.

5688
08:56:58,382 --> 08:57:08,192
But you can begin to learn some patterns that generalize beyond one specific state and one specific action, that you can begin to learn if certain features tend to be good things or bad things.

5689
08:57:08,494 --> 08:57:16,912
Reinforcement learning can allow you, using a very similar mechanism, to generalize beyond one particular state and say, if this other state looks kind of like this state,

5690
08:57:17,134 --> 08:57:22,532
then maybe the similar types of actions that worked in one state will also work in another state as well.

5691
08:57:23,182 --> 08:57:30,032
And so this type of approach can be quite helpful as you begin to deal with reinforcement learning that exists in larger and larger state spaces

5692
08:57:30,126 --> 08:57:34,672
where it's just not feasible to explore all of the possible states that could actually exist.

5693
08:57:36,078 --> 08:57:39,216
So, there then are two of the main categories of reinforcement learning.

5694
08:57:39,456 --> 08:57:47,204
Supervised learning, where you have labeled input and output pairs, and reinforcement learning, where an agent learns from rewards or punishments that it receives.

5695
08:57:47,456 --> 08:57:53,124
The third major category of machine learning that we'll just touch on briefly is known as unsupervised learning.

5696
08:57:53,326 --> 08:57:59,012
And unsupervised learning happens when we have data without any additional feedback, without labels.

5697
08:57:59,246 --> 08:58:02,576
That in the supervised learning case, all of our data had labels.

5698
08:58:02,736 --> 08:58:09,376
We labeled a data point with whether that was a rainy day or a not rainy day, and using those labels, we were able to infer what the pattern was.

5699
08:58:09,536 --> 08:58:17,616
Or we labeled data as a counterfeit banknote or not a counterfeit, and using those labels, we were able to draw inferences and patterns to figure out what

5700
08:58:17,870 --> 08:58:19,780
does a banknote look like versus not.

5701
08:58:20,718 --> 08:58:27,812
In unsupervised learning, we don't have any access to any of those labels, but we still would like to learn some of those patterns.

5702
08:58:28,336 --> 08:58:33,156
And one of the tasks that you might want to perform in unsupervised learning is something like clustering.

5703
08:58:33,614 --> 08:58:41,796
Where clustering is just a task of given some set of objects, organize it into distinct clusters, groups of objects that are similar to one another.

5704
08:58:42,126 --> 08:58:44,228
And there's lots of applications for clustering.

5705
08:58:44,416 --> 08:58:54,256
It comes up in genetic research where you might have a whole bunch of different genes and you want to cluster them into similar genes if you're trying to analyze across a population or across species.

5706
08:58:54,446 --> 08:58:59,216
It comes up in an image if you want to take all the pixels of an image, cluster them into different parts of the image.

5707
08:58:59,536 --> 08:59:08,416
Comes a lot up in market research, if you want to divide your consumers into different groups so you know which groups to target with certain types of product advertisements, for example,

5708
08:59:08,576 --> 08:59:12,836
and a number of other contexts as well in which clustering can be very applicable.

5709
08:59:13,216 --> 08:59:17,508
One technique for clustering is an algorithm known as k-means clustering.

5710
08:59:17,806 --> 08:59:24,292
And what k means clustering is going to do is it is going to divide all of our data points into k different clusters,

5711
08:59:24,718 --> 08:59:32,228
and it's going to do so by repeating this process of assigning points to clusters and then moving around those clusters at centers.

5712
08:59:32,590 --> 08:59:41,892
We're going to define a cluster by its center, the middle of the cluster, and then assign points to that cluster based on which center is closest to that point.

5713
08:59:42,350 --> 08:59:43,780
And I'll show you an example of that now.

5714
08:59:44,494 --> 08:59:55,204
Here, for example, I have a whole bunch of unlabeled data, just various data points that are in some sort of graphical space, and I would like to group them into various different clusters.

5715
08:59:55,566 --> 08:59:57,136
But I don't know how to do that originally.

5716
08:59:57,296 --> 09:00:00,576
And let's say I want to assign like three clusters to this group.

5717
09:00:00,782 --> 09:00:06,468
And you have to choose how many clusters you want in k-means clustering, that you could try multiple and see how well those values perform.

5718
09:00:06,798 --> 09:00:11,776
But I'll start just by randomly picking some places to put the centers of those clusters.

5719
09:00:12,176 --> 09:00:19,844
maybe I have a blue cluster, a red cluster, and a green cluster, and I'm going to start with the centers of those clusters just being in these three locations here.

5720
09:00:20,526 --> 09:00:31,652
And what k-means clustering tells us to do is once I have the centers of the clusters, assign every point to a cluster based on which cluster center it is closest to.

5721
09:00:32,462 --> 09:00:39,396
So we end up with something like this, where all of these points are closer to the blue cluster center than any other cluster center.

5722
09:00:40,176 --> 09:00:45,444
All of these points here are closer to the green cluster center than any other cluster center.

5723
09:00:45,742 --> 09:00:52,068
And then these two points plus these points over here, those are all closest to the red cluster center instead.

5724
09:00:53,102 --> 09:00:58,276
So here then is one possible assignment of all these points to three different clusters.

5725
09:00:58,862 --> 09:00:59,696
But it's not great.

5726
09:00:59,950 --> 09:01:05,616
That it seems like in this red cluster these points are kind of far apart, in this green cluster, these points are kind of far apart.

5727
09:01:05,776 --> 09:01:10,052
It might not be my ideal choice of how I would cluster these various different data points.

5728
09:01:10,606 --> 09:01:20,964
But k-means clustering is an iterative process that after I do this, there is a next step, which is that after I've assigned all of the points to the cluster center that it is nearest to,

5729
09:01:21,296 --> 09:01:23,908
we are going to recenter the clusters.

5730
09:01:24,270 --> 09:01:33,696
Meaning take the cluster centers, these diamond shapes here, and move them to the middle, or the average effectively, of all of the points that are in that cluster.

5731
09:01:33,998 --> 09:01:41,668
So we'll take this blue point, this blue center, and go ahead and move it to the middle or to the center of all of the points that were assigned to the blue cluster,

5732
09:01:41,936 --> 09:01:43,524
moving it slightly to the right in this case.

5733
09:01:43,918 --> 09:01:48,708
And we'll do the same thing for red. We'll move the cluster center to the middle of all of these

5734
09:01:49,422 --> 09:01:56,416
points, weighted by how many points there are. There are more points over here, so the red center ends up moving a little bit further that way.

5735
09:01:56,686 --> 09:02:05,136
And likewise for the green center, there are many more points on this side of the green center, so the green center ends up being pulled a little bit further in this direction.

5736
09:02:05,966 --> 09:02:09,920
so we re-center all of the clusters, and then we repeat the process.

5737
09:02:10,080 --> 09:02:15,588
We go ahead and now reassign all of the points to the cluster center that they are now closest to.

5738
09:02:16,014 --> 09:02:20,320
And now that we've moved around the cluster centers, these cluster assignments might change.

5739
09:02:20,622 --> 09:02:26,500
That this point originally was closer to the red cluster center, but now it's actually closer to the blue cluster center.

5740
09:02:26,894 --> 09:02:28,292
Same goes for this point as well.

5741
09:02:28,480 --> 09:02:35,748
And these three points that were originally closer to the green cluster center are now closer to the red cluster center instead.

5742
09:02:36,526 --> 09:02:43,716
So we can reassign what colors or which clusters each of these data points belongs to, and then repeat the process again.

5743
09:02:44,000 --> 09:02:50,308
Moving each of these cluster means on the middles of the clusters to the mean, the average of all of the other

5744
09:02:51,214 --> 09:02:53,956
points that happen to be there, and repeat the process again.

5745
09:02:54,318 --> 09:02:58,084
Go ahead and assign each of the points to the cluster that they are closest to.

5746
09:02:58,478 --> 09:03:07,680
So once we reach a point where we've assigned all the points to clusters to the cluster that they are nearest to, and nothing changed, we've reached a sort of equilibrium in this situation,

5747
09:03:07,840 --> 09:03:09,700
where no points are changing their allegiance.

5748
09:03:09,902 --> 09:03:17,040
And as a result, we can declare this algorithm is now over, and we now have some assignment of each of these points into three different clusters.

5749
09:03:17,120 --> 09:03:24,400
And it looks like we did a pretty good job of trying to identify which points are more similar to one another than they are to points in other groups.

5750
09:03:24,526 --> 09:03:29,796
So we have the green cluster down here, this blue cluster here, and then this red cluster over there as well.

5751
09:03:30,446 --> 09:03:35,716
And we did so without any access to some labels to tell us what these various different clusters were.

5752
09:03:36,000 --> 09:03:43,360
We just used an algorithm in an unsupervised sense, without any of those labels, to figure out which points belonged to which categories.

5753
09:03:43,598 --> 09:03:47,108
And again, lots of applications for this type of clustering technique.

5754
09:03:47,662 --> 09:03:53,924
And there are many more algorithms in each of these various different fields within machine learning, supervised and reinforcement and unsupervised.

5755
09:03:54,254 --> 09:03:58,960
But those are many of the big picture foundational ideas that underlie a lot of these techniques.

5756
09:03:59,212 --> 09:04:01,280
where These are the problems that we're trying to solve.

5757
09:04:01,486 --> 09:04:10,756
And we try and solve those problems using a number of different methods of trying to take data and learn patterns in that data, whether that's trying to find neighboring data points that are similar,

5758
09:04:10,894 --> 09:04:18,468
or trying to minimize some sort of loss function, or any number of other techniques that allow us to begin to try to solve these sorts of problems.

5759
09:04:19,342 --> 09:04:28,420
That then was a look at some of the principles that are at the foundation of modern machine learning, this ability to take data and learn from that data so that the computer can perform a task,

5760
09:04:28,560 --> 09:04:31,972
even if they haven't explicitly been given instructions in order to do so.

5761
09:04:32,430 --> 09:04:38,320
Next time we'll continue this conversation about machine learning, looking at other techniques we can use for solving these sorts of problems.

5762
09:04:38,574 --> 09:04:39,204
We'll see you then.

5763
09:05:11,520 --> 09:05:18,116
Learn how to perform a task, even if we the programmers didn't give the computer explicit instructions for how to perform that task.

5764
09:05:18,766 --> 09:05:24,068
Today we transition to one of the most popular techniques and tools within machine learning, that of neural networks.

5765
09:05:24,558 --> 09:05:32,452
And neural networks were inspired as early as the 1940s by researchers who were thinking about how it is that humans learn, studying neuroscience in the human brain,

5766
09:05:32,622 --> 09:05:38,852
and trying to see whether or not we could apply those same ideas to computers as well, and model computer learning off of human learning.

5767
09:05:39,470 --> 09:05:41,360
So, how is the brain structured?

5768
09:05:41,520 --> 09:05:49,348
Well, very simply put, the brain consists of a whole bunch of neurons, and those neurons are connected to one another and communicate with one another in some way.

5769
09:05:49,774 --> 09:05:56,932
In particular, if you think about the structure of a biological neural network, something like this, there are a couple of key properties that scientists observed.

5770
09:05:57,280 --> 09:06:05,284
One was that these neurons are connected to each other and receive electrical signals from one another, that one neuron can propagate electrical signals to another neuron.

5771
09:06:06,062 --> 09:06:12,452
And another point is that neurons process those input signals and then can be activated, that a neuron becomes activated at a certain point

5772
09:06:12,750 --> 09:06:16,196
and then can propagate further signals onto neurons in the future.

5773
09:06:16,718 --> 09:06:25,168
And so the question then became: could we take this biological idea of how it is that humans learn with brains and with neurons and apply that to a machine as well?

5774
09:06:25,390 --> 09:06:34,308
In effect, designing an artificial neural network, or an ANN, which will be a mathematical model for learning that is inspired by these biological neural networks.

5775
09:06:34,830 --> 09:06:40,688
And what artificial neural networks will allow us to do is they will first be able to model some sort of mathematical function.

5776
09:06:40,848 --> 09:06:49,508
Every time you look at a neural network, which we'll see more of later today, each one of them is really just some mathematical function that is mapping certain inputs to particular outputs

5777
09:06:49,870 --> 09:06:58,948
based on the structure of the network, that depending on where we place particular units inside of this neural network, that's going to determine how it is that the network is going to function.

5778
09:06:59,598 --> 09:07:07,008
And in particular, artificial neural networks are going to lend themselves to a way that we can learn what the network's parameters should be.

5779
09:07:07,246 --> 09:07:08,608
We'll see more on that in just a moment.

5780
09:07:08,814 --> 09:07:18,020
But in effect, we want a model such that it is easy for us to be able to write some code that allows for the network to be able to figure out how to model the right mathematical function

5781
09:07:18,190 --> 09:07:26,820
given a particular set of input data. So in order to create our artificial neural network, instead of using biological neurons, we're just going to use what we're going to call units.

5782
09:07:27,168 --> 09:07:33,956
Units inside of a neural network, which we can represent kind of like a node in a graph, which will here be represented just by a blue circle like this.

5783
09:07:34,446 --> 09:07:39,008
And these artificial units, these artificial neurons, can be connected to one another.

5784
09:07:39,214 --> 09:07:45,284
So here, for instance, we have two units that are connected by this edge inside of this graph, effectively.

5785
09:07:46,318 --> 09:07:51,568
And so what we're going to do now is think of this idea as some sort of mapping from inputs to outputs.

5786
09:07:51,728 --> 09:07:57,860
So we have one unit that is connected to another unit that we might think of this side as the input and that side of the output.

5787
09:07:58,414 --> 09:08:04,004
And what we're trying to do then is to figure out how to solve a problem, how to model some sort of mathematical function.

5788
09:08:04,238 --> 09:08:13,476
And this might take the form of something we saw last time, which was something like we have certain inputs like variables x1 and x2, and given those inputs, we want to perform some sort of task.

5789
09:08:13,808 --> 09:08:16,608
A task like predicting whether or not it's going to rain.

5790
09:08:16,782 --> 09:08:22,980
And ideally, we'd like some way, given these inputs x1 and x2, which stand for some sort of variables to do with the weather,

5791
09:08:23,168 --> 09:08:26,756
we would like to be able to predict, in this case, a Boolean classification.

5792
09:08:27,008 --> 09:08:29,476
Is it going to rain or is it not going to rain?

5793
09:08:30,126 --> 09:08:33,088
And we did this last time by way of a mathematical function.

5794
09:08:33,326 --> 09:08:40,964
We defined some function, h, for our hypothesis function, that took as input x1 and x2, the two inputs that we cared about processing,

5795
09:08:41,168 --> 09:08:45,540
in order to determine whether we thought it was going to rain or whether we thought it was not going to rain.

5796
09:08:46,158 --> 09:08:51,012
The question then becomes, what does this hypothesis function do in order to make that determination?

5797
09:08:51,374 --> 09:08:57,924
And we decided last time to use a linear combination of these input variables to determine what the output should be.

5798
09:08:58,190 --> 09:09:06,884
So our hypothesis function was equal to something like this weight zero plus weight one times x1 plus weight two times x2.

5799
09:09:07,598 --> 09:09:14,436
So what's going on here is that x1 and x2, those are input variables, the inputs to this hypothesis function.

5800
09:09:14,990 --> 09:09:20,128
And each of those input variables is being multiplied by some weight, which is just some number.

5801
09:09:20,398 --> 09:09:26,688
So x1 is being multiplied by weight one, x2 is being multiplied by weight two, and we have this additional weight,

5802
09:09:26,848 --> 09:09:33,568
weight zero, that doesn't get multiplied by an input variable at all, that just serves to either move the function up or move the function's value down.

5803
09:09:33,808 --> 09:09:41,380
You can think of this as either a weight that's just multiplied by some dummy value, like the number one, and it's multiplied by one, and so it's not multiplied by anything.

5804
09:09:41,774 --> 09:09:48,608
Or sometimes you'll see in the literature, people call this variable weight zero a bias, so that you can think of these variables as slightly different.

5805
09:09:48,688 --> 09:09:54,180
We have weights that are multiplied by the input, and we separately add some bias to the result as well.

5806
09:09:54,510 --> 09:09:58,948
You'll hear both of those terminologies used when people talk about neural networks and machine learning.

5807
09:09:59,982 --> 09:10:07,664
So, in effect, what we've done here is that in order to define a hypothesis function, we just need to decide and figure out what these weights should

5808
09:10:07,664 --> 09:10:11,876
be to determine what values to multiply by our inputs to get some sort of result.

5809
09:10:12,462 --> 09:10:17,664
Of course, at the end of this, what we need to do is make some sort of classification, like rainy or not rainy.

5810
09:10:18,062 --> 09:10:21,956
And to do that, we use some sort of function that defines some sort of threshold.

5811
09:10:22,318 --> 09:10:26,464
And so we saw, for instance, the step function, which is defined as one

5812
09:10:28,014 --> 09:10:32,384
if the result of multiplying the weights by the inputs is at least zero, otherwise it's zero.

5813
09:10:32,624 --> 09:10:35,424
And you can think of this line down the middle as kind of like a dotted line.

5814
09:10:35,598 --> 09:10:40,944
Effectively, it stays at zero all the way up to one point, and then the function steps or jumps up to one.

5815
09:10:41,294 --> 09:10:46,468
So it's zero before it reaches some threshold, and then it's one after it reaches a particular threshold.

5816
09:10:46,894 --> 09:10:50,916
And so this was one way we could define what we'll come to call an activation function.

5817
09:10:51,104 --> 09:10:57,540
A function that determines when it is that this output becomes active, changes to a zero one instead of being a zero.

5818
09:10:58,286 --> 09:11:06,544
But we also saw that if we didn't just want a purely binary classification, we didn't want purely one or zero, but we wanted to allow for some in-between

5819
09:11:06,734 --> 09:11:09,104
real-numbered values, we could use a different function.

5820
09:11:09,262 --> 09:11:15,492
And there are a number of choices, but the one that we looked at was the logistic sigmoid function that has sort of an S-shaped curve,

5821
09:11:15,758 --> 09:11:25,264
where we could represent this as a probability that maybe somewhere in between, the probability of rain is something like 0.5, maybe a little bit later the probability of rain is 0.8.

5822
09:11:25,614 --> 09:11:32,064
And so rather than just have a binary classification of 0 or 1, we could allow for numbers that are in between as well.

5823
09:11:32,270 --> 09:11:40,580
And it turns out there are many other different types of activation functions, where an activation function just takes the output of multiplying the weights together and adding that bias,

5824
09:11:40,942 --> 09:11:43,504
and then figuring out what the actual output should be.

5825
09:11:43,744 --> 09:11:47,424
Another popular one is the rectified linear unit, otherwise known as Relu.

5826
09:11:47,984 --> 09:11:52,772
And the way that works is that it just takes its input and takes the maximum of that input and zero.

5827
09:11:52,942 --> 09:11:58,784
So if it's positive, it remains unchanged, but if it's zero, if it's negative, it goes ahead and levels out at zero.

5828
09:11:58,958 --> 09:12:01,924
And there are other activation functions that we could choose as well.

5829
09:12:02,414 --> 09:12:10,224
But in short, each of these activation functions you can just think of as a function that gets applied to the result of all of this computation.

5830
09:12:10,384 --> 09:12:14,596
We take some function g and apply it to the result of all of that calculation.

5831
09:12:15,374 --> 09:12:23,524
And this then is what we saw last time: the way of defining some hypothesis function that takes in inputs, calculates some linear combination of those inputs,

5832
09:12:23,950 --> 09:12:28,164
and then passes it through some sort of activation function to get our output.

5833
09:12:28,718 --> 09:12:33,156
And this actually turns out to be the model for the simplest of neural networks.

5834
09:12:33,344 --> 09:12:38,788
That we're going to instead represent this mathematical idea graphically by using a structure like this.

5835
09:12:39,342 --> 09:12:45,572
Here then is a neural network that has two inputs, we can think of this as x1 and this is x2, and then one output,

5836
09:12:45,870 --> 09:12:51,972
which you can think of as classifying whether or not we think it's going to rain or not rain, for example, in this particular instance.

5837
09:12:52,622 --> 09:12:54,304
And so how exactly does this model work?

5838
09:12:54,574 --> 09:12:59,076
Well, each of these two inputs represents one of our input variables, x1 and x2.

5839
09:12:59,598 --> 09:13:06,532
And notice that these inputs are connected to this output via these edges, which are going to be defined by their weights.

5840
09:13:06,990 --> 09:13:11,172
So these edges each have a weight associated with them, weight one and weight two.

5841
09:13:11,950 --> 09:13:18,628
And then this output unit, what it's going to do is it is going to calculate an output based on those inputs and based on those weights.

5842
09:13:19,214 --> 09:13:27,716
This output unit is going to multiply all the inputs by their weights, add in this bias term, which you can think of as an extra W0 term that gets added into it,

5843
09:13:28,014 --> 09:13:30,340
and then we pass it through an activation function.

5844
09:13:31,054 --> 09:13:36,624
So this then is just a graphical way of representing the same idea we saw last time, just mathematically.

5845
09:13:36,750 --> 09:13:39,524
And we're going to call this a very simple neural network.

5846
09:13:40,078 --> 09:13:44,356
And we'd like for this neural network to be able to learn how to calculate some function.

5847
09:13:44,558 --> 09:13:53,984
That we want some function for the neural network to learn, and the neural network is going to learn what should the values of W0, W1, and W2 be, what should the activation function be,

5848
09:13:54,190 --> 09:13:56,356
in order to get the result that we would expect.

5849
09:13:57,262 --> 09:13:59,168
So we can actually take a look at an example of this.

5850
09:13:59,406 --> 09:14:01,796
What then is a very simple function that we might calculate?

5851
09:14:02,382 --> 09:14:10,768
Well, if we recall back from when we were looking at propositional logic, one of the simplest functions we looked at was something like the OR function that takes two inputs,

5852
09:14:11,246 --> 09:14:19,408
x and y, and outputs one, otherwise known as true, if either one of the inputs or both of them are one, and outputs a zero

5853
09:14:19,630 --> 09:14:21,732
if both of the inputs are zero or false.

5854
09:14:22,286 --> 09:14:25,572
So this then is the OR function, and this was the truth table for the OR function.

5855
09:14:25,838 --> 09:14:33,860
That as long as either of the inputs are one, the output of the function is one, and the only case where the output is zero is where both of the inputs are zero.

5856
09:14:34,478 --> 09:14:40,368
So the question is, how could we take this and train a neural network to be able to learn this particular function?

5857
09:14:40,590 --> 09:14:41,924
What would those weights look like?

5858
09:14:42,414 --> 09:14:43,652
Well, we could do something like this.

5859
09:14:44,302 --> 09:14:45,188
Here's our neural network,

5860
09:14:45,902 --> 09:14:52,164
and I'll propose that in order to calculate the OR function, we're going to use a value of one for each of the weights,

5861
09:14:52,846 --> 09:14:59,044
and we'll use a bias of negative one, and then we'll just use this step function as our activation function.

5862
09:14:59,502 --> 09:15:00,368
How then does this work?

5863
09:15:00,686 --> 09:15:08,448
Well, if I wanted to calculate something like zero or zero, which we know to be zero, because false or false is false, then what are we going to do?

5864
09:15:08,718 --> 09:15:12,848
Well, our output unit is going to calculate this input multiplied by the weight.

5865
09:15:13,008 --> 09:15:14,308
Zero times one, that's zero.

5866
09:15:14,734 --> 09:15:16,932
Same thing here, zero times one, that's zero.

5867
09:15:17,422 --> 09:15:20,848
And we'll add to that the bias, minus one.

5868
09:15:21,422 --> 09:15:23,328
So that'll give us a result of negative one.

5869
09:15:23,758 --> 09:15:32,100
If we plot that on our activation function, negative one is here, it's before the threshold, which means either zero or one, it's only one after the threshold.

5870
09:15:32,430 --> 09:15:37,924
Since negative one is before the threshold, the output that this unit provides is going to be zero.

5871
09:15:38,414 --> 09:15:42,660
And that's what we would expect it to be, that zero or zero should be zero.

5872
09:15:43,502 --> 09:15:46,928
What if instead we had had one or zero, where this is the number one?

5873
09:15:47,374 --> 09:15:53,008
Well, in this case, in order to calculate what the output is going to be, we again have to do this weighted sum.

5874
09:15:53,248 --> 09:15:54,948
One times one, that's one.

5875
09:15:55,662 --> 09:15:57,168
Zero times one, that's zero.

5876
09:15:57,518 --> 09:15:58,820
Sum of that so far is one.

5877
09:15:59,470 --> 09:16:02,128
Add negative one to that, well then the output is zero.

5878
09:16:02,414 --> 09:16:08,928
And if we plot zero on the step function, zero ends up being here, it's just at the threshold, and so the output here is going to be one.

5879
09:16:09,198 --> 09:16:11,888
Because the output of one or zero, that's one.

5880
09:16:12,302 --> 09:16:13,636
So that's what we would expect as well.

5881
09:16:13,966 --> 09:16:17,568
And just for one more example, if I had one or one, what would the result be?

5882
09:16:17,808 --> 09:16:21,568
Well, one times one is one, one times one is one, the sum of those is two.

5883
09:16:22,126 --> 09:16:24,288
I add the bias term to that, I get the number one.

5884
09:16:24,750 --> 09:16:30,820
One plotted on this graph is way over there, that's well beyond the threshold, and so this output is going to be one as well.

5885
09:16:31,054 --> 09:16:35,236
The output is always zero or one, depending on whether or not we're past the threshold.

5886
09:16:35,534 --> 09:16:38,628
And this neural network then models the OR function.

5887
09:16:38,848 --> 09:16:42,448
A very simple function, definitely, but it still is able to model it correctly.

5888
09:16:42,542 --> 09:16:47,492
If I give it the inputs, it will tell me what x1 or x2 happens to be.

5889
09:16:48,078 --> 09:16:53,316
And you could imagine trying to do this for other functions as well. A function like the and function, for instance,

5890
09:16:54,094 --> 09:16:59,012
that takes two inputs and calculates whether both x and y are true.

5891
09:16:59,406 --> 09:17:03,728
So if x is one and y is one, then the output of x and y is one.

5892
09:17:04,110 --> 09:17:06,596
But in all the other cases, the output is zero.

5893
09:17:07,150 --> 09:17:09,924
How could we model that inside of a neural network as well?

5894
09:17:10,414 --> 09:17:19,396
Well, it turns out we could do it in the same way, except instead of negative one as the bias, we can use negative two as the bias instead.

5895
09:17:20,014 --> 09:17:21,124
What does that end up looking like?

5896
09:17:21,390 --> 09:17:26,692
Well, if I had one and one, that should be one, because one true and true is equal to true.

5897
09:17:27,086 --> 09:17:28,688
Well, I take one times one, that's one.

5898
09:17:29,038 --> 09:17:31,940
One times one is one, I get a total sum of two so far.

5899
09:17:32,302 --> 09:17:41,796
Now I add the bias of negative two and I get the value zero. And zero, when I plot it on the activation function, is just past that threshold, and so the output is going to be one.

5900
09:17:42,542 --> 09:17:50,528
But if I add any other input, for example, like one and zero, well, the weighted sum of these is one plus zero is going to be one.

5901
09:17:51,054 --> 09:17:57,732
Minus two is going to give us negative one, and negative one is not past that threshold, and so the output is going to be zero.

5902
09:17:58,830 --> 09:18:05,488
So, those then are some very simple functions that we can model using a neural network that has two inputs and one output,

5903
09:18:05,742 --> 09:18:10,848
where our goal is to be able to figure out what those weights should be in order to determine what the output should be.

5904
09:18:11,086 --> 09:18:14,768
And you could imagine generalizing this to calculate more complex functions as well.

5905
09:18:14,848 --> 09:18:20,608
That maybe given the humidity and the pressure, we want to calculate what's the probability that it's going to rain, for example.

5906
09:18:20,878 --> 09:18:22,768
Or we might want to do a regression style problem.

5907
09:18:22,848 --> 09:18:30,208
We're given some amount of advertising and given what month it is, maybe, we want to predict what our expected sales are going to be for that particular month.

5908
09:18:30,478 --> 09:18:33,508
So you could imagine these inputs and outputs being different as well.

5909
09:18:33,998 --> 09:18:37,604
And it turns out that in some problems, we're not just going to have two inputs.

5910
09:18:38,126 --> 09:18:46,848
And the nice thing about these neural networks is that we can compose multiple units together, make our networks more complex just by adding more units into

5911
09:18:47,150 --> 09:18:48,388
this particular neural network.

5912
09:18:48,750 --> 09:18:57,408
So the network we've been looking at has two inputs and one output, but we could just as easily say, let's go ahead and have three inputs in there, or have even more inputs,

5913
09:18:57,568 --> 09:19:07,044
where we could arbitrarily decide however many inputs there are to our problem, all going to be calculating some sort of output that we care about figuring out the value of.

5914
09:19:07,758 --> 09:19:10,180
How then does the math work for figuring out that output?

5915
09:19:10,510 --> 09:19:12,132
Well, it's going to work in a very similar way.

5916
09:19:12,494 --> 09:19:19,684
In the case of two inputs, we had two weights indicated by these edges, and we multiplied the weights by the numbers, adding this bias term.

5917
09:19:20,302 --> 09:19:27,332
And we'll do the same thing in the other cases as well. If I have three inputs, you'll imagine multiplying each of these three inputs by each of these weights.

5918
09:19:27,888 --> 09:19:30,848
if I had five inputs instead, we're going to do the same thing.

5919
09:19:31,150 --> 09:19:41,604
Here I'm saying sum up from one to five, x i multiplied by weight i. So take each of the five input variables, multiply them by their corresponding weight, and then add the bias to that.

5920
09:19:41,934 --> 09:19:45,808
So this would be a case where there are five inputs into this neural network, for example.

5921
09:19:45,998 --> 09:19:53,408
But there could be more, arbitrarily many nodes that we want inside of this neural network, where each time we're just going to sum up all of those input variables,

5922
09:19:53,486 --> 09:19:57,156
multiplied by their weight, and then add the bias term at the very end.

5923
09:19:57,710 --> 09:20:04,196
And so this allows us to be able to represent problems that have even more inputs just by growing the size of our neural network.

5924
09:20:05,294 --> 09:20:10,756
Now, the next question we might ask is a question about how it is that we train these neural networks.

5925
09:20:10,848 --> 09:20:17,408
In the case of the OR function and the AND function, they were simple enough functions that I could just tell you, like here are what the weights should be,

5926
09:20:17,488 --> 09:20:22,980
and you could probably reason through it yourself what the weights should be in order to calculate the output that you want.

5927
09:20:23,278 --> 09:20:30,404
But in general, with functions like predicting sales or predicting whether or not it's going to rain, these are much trickier functions to be able to figure out.

5928
09:20:30,608 --> 09:20:37,220
We would like the computer to have some mechanism of calculating what it is that the weights should be, how it is to set the weights,

5929
09:20:37,646 --> 09:20:42,852
so that our neural network is able to accurately model the function that we care about trying to estimate.

5930
09:20:43,534 --> 09:20:49,092
And it turns out that the strategy for doing this, inspired by the domain of calculus, is a technique called gradient descent.

5931
09:20:49,582 --> 09:20:55,492
And what gradient descent is, it is an algorithm for minimizing loss when you're training a neural network.

5932
09:20:55,918 --> 09:21:03,248
And recall that loss refers to how bad our hypothesis function happens to be, that we can define certain loss functions,

5933
09:21:03,502 --> 09:21:11,248
and we saw some examples of loss functions last time that just give us a number for any particular hypothesis saying how poorly does it model the data?

5934
09:21:11,408 --> 09:21:18,468
How many examples does it get wrong, how much how are they worse or less bad as compared to other hypothesis functions that we might define.

5935
09:21:19,054 --> 09:21:22,244
And this loss function is just a mathematical function.

5936
09:21:22,606 --> 09:21:29,156
And when you have a mathematical function, in calculus, what you could do is calculate something known as the gradient, which you can think of as like a slope.

5937
09:21:29,328 --> 09:21:32,516
It's the direction the loss function is moving at any particular point.

5938
09:21:32,942 --> 09:21:40,324
And what it's going to tell us is in which direction should we be moving these weights in order to minimize the amount of loss.

5939
09:21:41,070 --> 09:21:47,364
And so, generally speaking, we won't get into the calculus of it, but the high-level idea for gradient descent is going to look something like this.

5940
09:21:47,822 --> 09:21:52,612
If we want to train a neural network, we'll go ahead and start just by choosing the weights randomly.

5941
09:21:52,848 --> 09:21:55,524
Just pick random weights for all of the weights in the neural network.

5942
09:21:56,142 --> 09:22:02,596
And then we'll use the input data that we have access to in order to train the network, in order to figure out what the weights should actually be.

5943
09:22:02,894 --> 09:22:04,868
So we'll repeat this process again and again.

5944
09:22:05,358 --> 09:22:09,168
The first step is we're going to calculate the gradient based on all of the data points.

5945
09:22:09,294 --> 09:22:15,108
So we'll look at all the data and figure out what the gradient is at the place where we currently are for the current setting of the weights,

5946
09:22:15,568 --> 09:22:23,716
which means in which direction should we move the weights in order to minimize the total amount of loss in order to make our solution better.

5947
09:22:24,430 --> 09:22:32,228
And once we've calculated that gradient, which direction we should move in the loss function, well then we can just update those weights according to the gradient,

5948
09:22:32,782 --> 09:22:37,568
take a small step in the direction of those weights in order to try to make our solution a little bit better.

5949
09:22:37,806 --> 09:22:42,660
And the size of the step that we take, that's going to vary, and you can choose that when you're training a particular neural network.

5950
09:22:43,022 --> 09:22:49,732
But in short, the idea is going to be take all the data points, figure out based on those data points in what direction the weights should move,

5951
09:22:49,966 --> 09:22:52,848
and then move the weights one small step in that direction.

5952
09:22:53,134 --> 09:22:59,428
And if you repeat that process over and over again, adjusting the weights a little bit at a time based on all the data points, eventually,

5953
09:22:59,726 --> 09:23:04,048
you should end up with a pretty good solution to trying to solve this sort of problem.

5954
09:23:04,270 --> 09:23:05,892
At least that's what we would hope to happen.

5955
09:23:06,734 --> 09:23:14,448
Now, as you look at this algorithm, a good question to ask anytime you're analyzing an algorithm is what is going to be the expensive part of doing the calculation?

5956
09:23:14,638 --> 09:23:19,236
What's going to take a lot of work to try to figure out, what is going to be expensive to calculate.

5957
09:23:19,630 --> 09:23:26,128
And in particular, in the case of gradient descent, the really expensive part is this all data points part right here.

5958
09:23:26,288 --> 09:23:34,404
Having to take all of the data points and using all of those data points, figure out what the gradient is at this particular setting of all of the weights.

5959
09:23:34,688 --> 09:23:41,728
Because odds are, in a big machine learning problem where you're trying to solve a big problem with a lot of data, you have a lot of data points in order to calculate.

5960
09:23:41,934 --> 09:23:47,408
And figuring out the gradient based on all of those data points is going to be expensive, and you'll have to do it many times.

5961
09:23:47,648 --> 09:23:52,996
You'll likely repeat this process again and again and again, going through all the data points, taking one small step

5962
09:23:53,328 --> 09:23:58,436
over and over as you try and figure out what the optimal setting of those weights happens to be.

5963
09:23:59,246 --> 09:24:09,508
It turns out that we would ideally like to be able to train our neural networks faster, to be able to more quickly converge to some sort of solution that is going to be a good solution to the problem.

5964
09:24:09,998 --> 09:24:14,852
So in that case, there are alternatives to just standard gradient descent, which looks at all of the data points at once.

5965
09:24:15,214 --> 09:24:23,940
We can employ a method like stochastic gradient descent, which will randomly just choose one data point at a time to calculate the gradient based on,

5966
09:24:24,128 --> 09:24:26,628
instead of calculating it based on all of the data points.

5967
09:24:27,118 --> 09:24:35,716
So the idea there is that we have some setting of the weights, we pick a data point, and based on that one data point, we figure out in which direction should we move all of the weights,

5968
09:24:35,888 --> 09:24:41,408
and move the weights in that small direction, then take another data point and do that again, and repeat this process again and again.

5969
09:24:41,568 --> 09:24:51,076
maybe looking at each of the data points multiple times, but each time only using one data point to calculate the gradient, to calculate which direction we should move in.

5970
09:24:51,662 --> 09:24:59,332
Now, just using one data point instead of all of the data points probably gives us a less accurate estimate of what the gradient actually is.

5971
09:24:59,758 --> 09:25:06,980
But on the plus side, it's going to be much faster to be able to calculate, that we can much more quickly calculate what the gradient is based on one data point

5972
09:25:07,182 --> 09:25:12,836
instead of calculating based on all of the data points and having to do all of that computational work again and again.

5973
09:25:13,422 --> 09:25:17,508
So there are trade-offs here between looking at all of the data points and just looking at one data point.

5974
09:25:18,094 --> 09:25:23,396
And it turns out that a middle ground that is also quite popular is a technique called mini batch gradient descent.

5975
09:25:23,790 --> 09:25:30,928
Where the idea there is instead of looking at all of the data versus just a single point, we instead divide our data set up into small batches,

5976
09:25:31,088 --> 09:25:34,848
groups of data points, where you can decide how big a particular batch is.

5977
09:25:35,214 --> 09:25:41,124
But in short, you're just going to look at a small number of points at any given time, hopefully getting a more accurate estimate of the gradient,

5978
09:25:41,408 --> 09:25:47,716
but also not requiring all of the computational effort needed to look at every single one of these data points.

5979
09:25:48,718 --> 09:25:57,220
So, gradient descent then is this technique that we can use in order to train these neural networks, in order to figure out what the setting of all of these weights should be,

5980
09:25:57,550 --> 09:26:06,112
if we want some way to try and get an accurate notion of how it is that this function should work, some way of modeling how to transform the inputs into

5981
09:26:06,606 --> 09:26:07,428
particular outputs.

5982
09:26:08,366 --> 09:26:13,472
Now, so far, the networks that we've taken a look at have all been structured similar to this.

5983
09:26:13,632 --> 09:26:23,236
We have some number of inputs, maybe two or three or five or more, and then we have one output that is just predicting rain or no rain, or just predicting one particular value.

5984
09:26:23,694 --> 09:26:27,524
But often in machine learning problems, we don't just care about one output.

5985
09:26:27,822 --> 09:26:38,192
We might care about an output that has multiple different values associated with it. So in the same way that we could take a neural network and add units to the input layer, we can likewise

5986
09:26:39,232 --> 09:26:41,632
add outputs to the output layer as well.

5987
09:26:41,902 --> 09:26:46,752
Instead of just one output, you could imagine we have two outputs, or we could have like four outputs, for example.

5988
09:26:47,086 --> 09:26:49,988
Where in each case, as we add more inputs or add more outputs,

5989
09:26:50,830 --> 09:26:56,068
if we want to keep this network fully connected between these two layers, we just need to add more weights.

5990
09:26:56,430 --> 09:27:06,244
That now each of these input nodes has four weights associated with each of the four outputs, and that's true for each of these various different input nodes.

5991
09:27:06,432 --> 09:27:13,508
So as we add nodes, we add more weights in order to make sure that each of the inputs can somehow be connected to each of the outputs

5992
09:27:13,902 --> 09:27:18,660
so that each output value can be calculated based on what the value of the input happens to be.

5993
09:27:19,566 --> 09:27:22,820
So what might a case be where we want multiple different output values?

5994
09:27:23,694 --> 09:27:30,532
Well, you might consider that in the case of weather predicting, for example, we might not just care whether it's raining or not raining.

5995
09:27:30,752 --> 09:27:35,204
There might be multiple different categories of weather that we would like to categorize the weather into.

5996
09:27:35,598 --> 09:27:42,672
With just a single output variable, we can do a binary classification, like rain or no rain, for instance, one or zero.

5997
09:27:42,926 --> 09:27:45,232
But it doesn't allow us to do much more than that.

5998
09:27:45,582 --> 09:27:50,352
With multiple output variables, I might be able to use each one to predict something a little different.

5999
09:27:50,512 --> 09:27:57,668
Maybe I want to categorize the weather into one of four different categories, something like is it going to be raining or sunny or cloudy or snowy?

6000
09:27:57,966 --> 09:28:07,588
And I now have four output variables that can be used to represent maybe the probability that it is rainy as opposed to sunny, as opposed to cloudy or as opposed to snowy.

6001
09:28:08,462 --> 09:28:10,020
How then would this neural network work?

6002
09:28:10,542 --> 09:28:14,820
Well, we have some input variables that represent some data that we have collected about the weather.

6003
09:28:15,214 --> 09:28:23,652
Each of those inputs gets multiplied by each of these various different weights, so we have more multiplications to do, but these are fairly quick mathematical operations to perform.

6004
09:28:24,174 --> 09:28:30,340
And then what we get is after passing them through some sort of activation function in the outputs, we end up getting some sort of number.

6005
09:28:30,702 --> 09:28:38,112
Where that number, you might imagine, you can interpret as like a probability, like a probability that it is one category as opposed to another category.

6006
09:28:38,286 --> 09:28:46,592
So here we're saying that based on the inputs, we think there is a 10% chance that it's raining, a 60% chance that it's sunny, a 20% chance that it's cloudy,

6007
09:28:46,752 --> 09:28:48,356
a 10% chance that it's snowy.

6008
09:28:48,686 --> 09:28:57,632
And given that output, if these represent like a probability distribution, well, then you could just pick, whichever one has the highest value, in this case sunny, and say that, well.

6009
09:28:57,712 --> 09:29:04,932
most likely, we think that this categorization of inputs means that the output should be snowy and that, or it should be sunny.

6010
09:29:05,072 --> 09:29:08,644
and that is what we would expect the weather to be in this particular instance.

6011
09:29:09,678 --> 09:29:17,380
And so this allows us to do these sort of multi class classifications, where instead of just having a binary classification, one or zero,

6012
09:29:17,632 --> 09:29:27,312
we can have as many different categories as we want. and we can have our neural network output, these probabilities over which categories are more likely than other categories.

6013
09:29:27,662 --> 09:29:32,420
and using that data, we're able to draw some sort of inference on what it is that we should do.

6014
09:29:33,134 --> 09:29:35,716
So this was sort of the idea of supervised machine learning.

6015
09:29:35,840 --> 09:29:44,800
I can give the neural network a whole bunch of data, a whole bunch of input data corresponding to some label, some output data, like we know that it was raining on this day,

6016
09:29:45,006 --> 09:29:46,660
we know that it was sunny on that day.

6017
09:29:46,926 --> 09:29:54,564
And using all of that data, the algorithm can use gradient descent to figure out what all of the weights should be in order to create some sort of model

6018
09:29:54,734 --> 09:29:58,564
that hopefully allows us a way to predict what we think the weather is going to be.

6019
09:29:59,246 --> 09:30:01,796
But neural networks have a lot of other applications as well.

6020
09:30:02,030 --> 09:30:07,600
You could imagine applying the same sort of idea to a reinforcement learning sort of example as well.

6021
09:30:07,854 --> 09:30:17,156
Where you remember that in reinforcement learning, what we wanted to do is train some sort of agent to learn what action to take depending on what state they currently happen to be in.

6022
09:30:17,326 --> 09:30:24,036
So depending on the current state of the world, we wanted the agent to pick from one of the available actions that is available to them.

6023
09:30:24,750 --> 09:30:34,404
And you might model that by having each of these input variables represent some information about the state, some data about what state our agent is currently in.

6024
09:30:34,894 --> 09:30:42,276
And then the output, for example, could be each of the various different actions that our agent could take. Action one, two, three, and four.

6025
09:30:42,510 --> 09:30:45,280
And you might imagine that this network would work in the same way.

6026
09:30:45,486 --> 09:30:53,840
That based on these particular inputs, we go ahead and calculate values for each of these outputs, and those outputs could model which action is better than other actions,

6027
09:30:53,920 --> 09:30:58,244
and we could just choose, based on looking at those outputs, which action we should take.

6028
09:30:59,054 --> 09:31:04,932
And so these neural networks are very broadly applicable, that all they're really doing is modeling some mathematical function.

6029
09:31:05,166 --> 09:31:14,948
So anything that we can frame as a mathematical function, something like classifying inputs into various different categories, or figuring out based on some input state, what action we should take?

6030
09:31:15,120 --> 09:31:23,600
these are all mathematical functions that we could attempt to model by taking advantage of this neural network structure, and in particular, taking advantage of this technique,

6031
09:31:23,822 --> 09:31:30,180
gradient descent, that we can use in order to figure out what the weights should be. in order to do this sort of calculation.

6032
09:31:31,278 --> 09:31:36,388
Now, how is it that you would go about training a neural network that has multiple outputs instead of just one?

6033
09:31:36,782 --> 09:31:43,940
Well, with just a single output, we could see what the output for that value should be, and then you update all of the weights that corresponded to it.

6034
09:31:44,302 --> 09:31:51,360
And when we have multiple outputs, at least in this particular case, we can really think of this as four separate neural networks.

6035
09:31:51,520 --> 09:31:59,492
That really we just have one network here that has these three inputs corresponding with these three weights corresponding to this one output value.

6036
09:31:59,982 --> 09:32:02,240
And the same thing is true for this output value.

6037
09:32:02,400 --> 09:32:10,880
This output value effectively defines yet another neural network that has these same three inputs, but a different set of weights that correspond to this output.

6038
09:32:11,086 --> 09:32:16,260
And likewise this output has its own set of weights as well, and same thing for the fourth output, too.

6039
09:32:17,006 --> 09:32:24,740
And so if you wanted to train a neural network that had four outputs instead of just one, in this case where the inputs are directly connected to the outputs,

6040
09:32:25,040 --> 09:32:28,708
you could really think of this as just training four independent neural networks.

6041
09:32:28,960 --> 09:32:37,440
We know what the outputs for each of these four should be based on our input data, and using that data, we can begin to figure out what all of these individual weights should be.

6042
09:32:37,614 --> 09:32:46,000
And maybe there's an additional step at the end to make sure that we turn these values into a probability distribution such that we can interpret which one is better than another

6043
09:32:46,080 --> 09:32:49,380
or more likely than another as a category or something like that.

6044
09:32:50,254 --> 09:32:58,212
So this then seems like it does a pretty good job of taking inputs and trying to predict what outputs should be. And we'll see some real examples of this in just a moment as well.

6045
09:32:58,766 --> 09:33:07,040
But it's important then to think about what the limitations of this sort of approach is, of just taking some linear combination of inputs and passing it into

6046
09:33:07,214 --> 09:33:08,676
some sort of activation function.

6047
09:33:09,134 --> 09:33:20,160
And it turns out that when we do this in the case of binary classification, of trying to predict like does it belong to one category or another, we can only predict things that are linearly separable.

6048
09:33:20,320 --> 09:33:29,732
Because we're taking a linear combination of inputs and using that to define some decision boundary or threshold, then what we get is a situation where if we have this set of data,

6049
09:33:30,000 --> 09:33:35,440
we can predict a line that separates linearly the red points from the blue points.

6050
09:33:36,270 --> 09:33:43,684
But a single unit that is making a binary classification, otherwise known as a perceptron, can't deal with a situation like this,

6051
09:33:44,494 --> 09:33:52,324
where we've seen this type of situation before, where there is no straight line that just goes straight through the data that will divide the red points away from the blue points.

6052
09:33:52,686 --> 09:33:54,916
It's a more complex decision boundary.

6053
09:33:55,040 --> 09:34:02,308
The decision boundary somehow needs to capture the things inside of this circle, and there isn't really a line that will allow us to deal with that.

6054
09:34:03,118 --> 09:34:08,516
So this is the limitation of the perceptron, these units that just make these binary decisions based on their inputs,

6055
09:34:08,814 --> 09:34:15,120
that a single perceptron is only capable of learning a linearly separable decision boundary.

6056
09:34:15,280 --> 09:34:17,200
All it can do is define a line.

6057
09:34:17,454 --> 09:34:26,244
And sure it can give us probabilities based on how close to that decision boundary we are, but it can only really decide based on a linear decision boundary.

6058
09:34:26,734 --> 09:34:34,800
And so this doesn't seem like it's going to generalize well to situations where real world data is involved, because real world data often isn't linearly separable.

6059
09:34:34,880 --> 09:34:40,420
It often isn't the case that we can just draw a line through the data and be able to divide it up into multiple groups.

6060
09:34:41,262 --> 09:34:42,852
So what then is the solution to this?

6061
09:34:43,310 --> 09:34:47,520
Well, what was proposed was the idea of a multi-layer neural network.

6062
09:34:47,630 --> 09:34:54,468
That so far, all of the neural networks we've seen have had a set of inputs and a set of outputs, and the inputs are connected to those outputs.

6063
09:34:55,438 --> 09:35:06,000
But in a multi-layer neural network, this is going to be an artificial neural network that has an input layer still, it has an output layer, but also has one or more hidden layers in between.

6064
09:35:06,240 --> 09:35:11,780
Other layers of artificial neurons or units that are going to calculate their own values as well.

6065
09:35:12,430 --> 09:35:20,228
So instead of a neural network that looks like this, with three inputs and one output, you might imagine in the middle here injecting a hidden layer,

6066
09:35:20,878 --> 09:35:21,280
something like this.

6067
09:35:21,454 --> 09:35:23,280
This is a hidden layer that has four nodes.

6068
09:35:23,600 --> 09:35:28,932
You could choose how many nodes or units end up going into the hidden layer. you can have multiple hidden layers as well.

6069
09:35:29,326 --> 09:35:33,284
And so now each of these inputs isn't directly connected to the output.

6070
09:35:33,646 --> 09:35:35,844
Each of the inputs is connected to this hidden layer.

6071
09:35:36,398 --> 09:35:40,196
And then all of the nodes in the hidden layer, those are connected to the one output.

6072
09:35:40,974 --> 09:35:46,276
And so this is just another step that we can take towards calculating more complex functions.

6073
09:35:46,480 --> 09:35:54,596
Each of these hidden units will calculate its output value, otherwise known as its activation, based on a linear combination of all the inputs.

6074
09:35:55,278 --> 09:36:00,480
And once we have values for all of these nodes, as opposed to this just being the output, we do the same thing again.

6075
09:36:00,720 --> 09:36:07,460
Calculate the output for this node based on multiplying each of the values for these units by their weights as well.

6076
09:36:07,982 --> 09:36:13,892
So in effect, the way this works is that we start with inputs, they get multiplied by weights in order to calculate values for the hidden nodes,

6077
09:36:14,094 --> 09:36:18,980
those get multiplied by weights in order to figure out what the ultimate output is going to be.

6078
09:36:19,758 --> 09:36:25,252
And the advantage of layering things like this is it gives us an ability to model more complex functions.

6079
09:36:25,614 --> 09:36:35,620
That instead of just having a single decision boundary, a single line dividing the red points from the blue points, each of these hidden nodes can learn a different decision boundary,

6080
09:36:35,918 --> 09:36:40,644
and we can combine those decision boundaries to figure out what the ultimate output is going to be.

6081
09:36:40,974 --> 09:36:50,084
And as we begin to imagine more complex situations, you could imagine each of these nodes learning some useful property or learning some useful feature of all of the inputs,

6082
09:36:50,510 --> 09:36:55,652
and us somehow learning how to combine those features together in order to get the output that we actually want.

6083
09:36:56,654 --> 09:37:03,908
Now, the natural question when we begin to look at this now is to ask the question of how do we train a neural network that has hidden layers inside of it?

6084
09:37:04,398 --> 09:37:12,580
And this turns out to initially be a bit of a tricky question, because the input data that we are given is we are given values for all of the inputs,

6085
09:37:13,070 --> 09:37:17,604
and we're given what the value of the output should be, what the category is, for example.

6086
09:37:18,126 --> 09:37:22,720
But the input data doesn't tell us what the values for all of these nodes should be.

6087
09:37:22,926 --> 09:37:29,476
So we don't know how far off each of these nodes actually is, because we're only given data for the inputs and the outputs.

6088
09:37:29,760 --> 09:37:38,884
The reason this is called a hidden layer is because the data that is made available to us doesn't tell us what the values for all of these intermediate nodes should actually be.

6089
09:37:39,694 --> 09:37:47,428
And so the strategy people came up with was to say that if you know what the error or the loss is on the output node,

6090
09:37:48,078 --> 09:37:58,144
well, then, based on what these weights are. if one of these weights is higher than another, you can calculate an estimate for how much the error from this node was

6091
09:37:58,464 --> 09:38:04,544
due to this part of the hidden node, or this part of the hidden layer, or this part of the hidden layer, based on the values of these weights.

6092
09:38:04,704 --> 09:38:15,344
In effect, saying that based on the error from the output, I can backpropagate the error and figure out an estimate for what the error is for each of these nodes in the hidden layer as well.

6093
09:38:15,726 --> 09:38:21,636
And there's some more calculus here that we won't get into the details of, but the idea of this algorithm is known as backpropagation.

6094
09:38:21,774 --> 09:38:25,668
It's an algorithm for training a neural network with multiple different hidden layers.

6095
09:38:26,158 --> 09:38:34,532
And the idea for this, the pseudocode for it, will again be if we want to run gradient descent with backpropagation, we'll start with a random choice of weights, as we did before.

6096
09:38:35,246 --> 09:38:38,020
And now we'll go ahead and repeat the training process again and again.

6097
09:38:38,670 --> 09:38:43,824
But what we're going to do each time is now we're going to calculate the error for the output layer first.

6098
09:38:43,984 --> 09:38:48,932
We know the output and what it should be, and we know what we calculated, so we can figure out what the error there is.

6099
09:38:49,678 --> 09:38:57,860
But then we're going to repeat for every layer, starting with the output layer, moving back into the hidden layer, then the hidden layer before that, if there are multiple hidden layers,

6100
09:38:58,224 --> 09:39:04,864
going back all the way to the very first hidden layer, assuming there are multiple, we're going to propagate the error back one layer.

6101
09:39:05,024 --> 09:39:11,140
Whatever the error was from the output, figure out what the error should be a layer before that, based on what the values of those weights are.

6102
09:39:11,758 --> 09:39:13,828
And then we can update those weights.

6103
09:39:14,830 --> 09:39:18,544
So graphically, the way you might think about this is that we first start with the output.

6104
09:39:18,704 --> 09:39:25,124
We know what the output should be, we know what output we calculated, and based on that, we can figure out, all right, how do we need to update those weights?

6105
09:39:25,424 --> 09:39:31,108
Backpropagating the error to these nodes, and using that, we can figure out how we should update these weights.

6106
09:39:31,534 --> 09:39:39,264
And you might imagine if there are multiple layers, we could repeat this process again and again to begin to figure out how all of these weights should be updated.

6107
09:39:39,566 --> 09:39:44,164
And this back propagation algorithm is really the key algorithm that makes neural networks possible.

6108
09:39:44,366 --> 09:39:52,464
It makes it possible to take these multi-level structures and be able to train those structures depending on what the values of these weights are.

6109
09:39:52,544 --> 09:40:00,516
in order to figure out how it is that we should go about updating those weights in order to create some function that is able to minimize the total amount of loss,

6110
09:40:00,624 --> 09:40:06,788
to figure out some good setting of the weights that will take the inputs and translate it into the output that we expect.

6111
09:40:07,566 --> 09:40:15,492
And this works, as we said, not just for a single hidden layer, but you can imagine multiple hidden layers, where each hidden layer we just define however many nodes we want,

6112
09:40:15,726 --> 09:40:25,220
where each of the nodes in one layer we can connect to the nodes in the next layer, defining more and more complex networks that are able to model more and more complex types of functions.

6113
09:40:26,190 --> 09:40:31,364
And so this type of network is what we might call a deep neural network, part of a larger family

6114
09:40:32,014 --> 09:40:34,464
of deep learning algorithms, if you've ever heard that term.

6115
09:40:34,624 --> 09:40:44,944
And all deep learning is about is it's using multiple layers to be able to predict and be able to model higher-level features inside of the input, to be able to figure out what the output should be.

6116
09:40:45,262 --> 09:40:52,784
And so a deep neural network is just a neural network that has multiple of these hidden layers, where we start at the input, calculate values for this layer, then this layer,

6117
09:40:52,864 --> 09:40:55,424
then this layer, and then ultimately get an output.

6118
09:40:55,598 --> 09:41:03,620
And this allows us to be able to model more and more sophisticated types of functions, that each of these layers can calculate something a little bit different,

6119
09:41:03,854 --> 09:41:07,556
and we can combine that information to figure out what the output should be.

6120
09:41:08,494 --> 09:41:18,468
Of course, as with any situation of machine learning, as we begin to make our models more and more complex, to model more and more complex functions, the risk we run is something like overfitting.

6121
09:41:18,894 --> 09:41:27,492
And we talked about overfitting last time in the context of overfitting based on when we were training our models to be able to learn some sort of decision boundary,

6122
09:41:27,694 --> 09:41:35,780
where overfitting happens when we fit too closely to the training data, and as a result, we don't generalize well to other situations as well.

6123
09:41:36,110 --> 09:41:44,384
And one of the risks we run with a far more complex neural network that has many, many different nodes, is that we might overfit based on the input data.

6124
09:41:44,464 --> 09:41:52,772
We might grow over-reliant on certain nodes to calculate things just purely based on the input data that doesn't allow us to generalize very well to the output.

6125
09:41:53,486 --> 09:41:56,292
And there are a number of strategies for dealing with overfitting.

6126
09:41:56,462 --> 09:42:00,676
But one of the most popular in the context of neural networks is a technique known as dropout.

6127
09:42:01,102 --> 09:42:10,756
And what dropout does is that when we're training the neural network, what we'll do in dropout is temporarily remove units, temporarily remove these artificial neurons from our network

6128
09:42:11,118 --> 09:42:12,036
chosen at random.

6129
09:42:12,590 --> 09:42:16,144
And the goal here is to prevent over-reliance on certain units.

6130
09:42:16,270 --> 09:42:24,544
So what generally happens in overfitting is that we begin to over rely on certain units inside the neural network to be able to tell us how to interpret the input data.

6131
09:42:24,846 --> 09:42:33,664
What dropout will do is randomly remove some of these units in order to reduce the chance that we over rely on certain units to make our neural network more robust,

6132
09:42:33,744 --> 09:42:38,500
to be able to handle the situations even when we just drop out particular neurons entirely.

6133
09:42:39,278 --> 09:42:45,668
So the way that might work is we have a network like this, and as we're training it, when we go about trying to update the weights the first time,

6134
09:42:45,934 --> 09:42:49,284
we'll just randomly pick some percentage of the nodes to drop out of the network.

6135
09:42:49,582 --> 09:42:51,216
It's as if those nodes aren't there at all.

6136
09:42:51,406 --> 09:42:55,524
It's as if the weights associated with those nodes aren't there at all, and we'll train it this way.

6137
09:42:56,078 --> 09:42:59,748
Then the next time we update the weights, we'll pick a different set and just go ahead and train that way.

6138
09:42:59,918 --> 09:43:03,908
And then again, randomly choose and train with other nodes that have been dropped out as well.

6139
09:43:04,558 --> 09:43:10,372
And the goal of that is that after the training process, if you train by dropping out random nodes inside of this neural network,

6140
09:43:10,656 --> 09:43:20,548
you hopefully end up with a network that's a little bit more robust, that doesn't rely too heavily on any one particular node, but more generally learns how to approximate a function in general.

