09:55:39,566 --> 09:55:47,076
I'll evaluate my model using model.evaluate built into TensorFlow that is just going to tell me how well do I perform on the testing data.

6290
09:55:47,406 --> 09:55:52,932
So ultimately, this is just going to give me some numbers that tell me how well we did in this particular case.

6291
09:55:54,350 --> 09:55:58,788
So now what I'm going to do is go into banknotes and go ahead and run banknotes.py.

6292
09:55:59,342 --> 09:56:02,656
And what's going to happen now is it's going to read in all of that training data.

6293
09:56:02,862 --> 09:56:09,924
It's going to generate a neural network with all my inputs, my eight hidden layers, or eight hidden units inside my layer, and then an output unit.

6294
09:56:10,350 --> 09:56:11,696
And now what it's doing is it's training.

6295
09:56:12,014 --> 09:56:17,136
It's training 20 times, and each time you can see how my accuracy is increasing on my training data.

6296
09:56:17,216 --> 09:56:26,628
It starts off the very first time, not very accurate, though better than random, something like 79% of the time it's able to accurately classify one bill from another.

6297
09:56:26,894 --> 09:56:34,724
But as I keep training, notice this accuracy value improves and improves and improves until after I've trained through all the data points 20 times,

6298
09:56:35,086 --> 09:56:39,972
it looks like my accuracy is above 99%% on the training data.

6299
09:56:40,430 --> 09:56:42,976
And here's where I tested it on a whole bunch of testing

6300
09:56:42,976 --> 09:56:47,268
data. And it looks like in this case, I was also like 99.8% accurate.

6301
09:56:48,462 --> 09:56:56,896
So just using that, I was able to generate a neural network that can detect counterfeit bills from authentic bills based on this input data 99.8% of the time,

6302
09:56:57,056 --> 09:57:02,816
at least based on this particular testing data. And I might want to test it with more data as well, just to be confident about that.

6303
09:57:02,990 --> 09:57:09,698
But this is really the value of using a machine learning library like TensorFlow, and there are others available for Python and other languages as well.

6304
09:57:09,966 --> 09:57:16,100
that all I have to do is define the structure of the network and define the data that I'm going to pass into the network.

6305
09:57:16,496 --> 09:57:25,536
And then TensorFlow runs the backpropagation algorithm for learning what all of those weights should be, for figuring out how to train this neural network to be able to accurately,

6306
09:57:25,696 --> 09:57:30,788
as accurately as possible, figure out what the output values should be there as well.

6307
09:57:31,790 --> 09:57:39,296
And so this then was a look at what it is that neural networks can do just using these sequences of layer after layer after layer.

6308
09:57:39,502 --> 09:57:42,788
And you can begin to imagine applying these to much more general problems.

6309
09:57:43,182 --> 09:57:48,740
And one big problem in computing and artificial intelligence more generally is the problem of computer vision.

6310
09:57:49,294 --> 09:57:54,148
Computer vision is all about computational methods for analyzing and understanding images.

6311
09:57:54,656 --> 09:58:02,596
You might have pictures that you want the computer to figure out how to deal with, how to process those images, and figure out how to produce some sort of useful result out of this.

6312
09:58:02,926 --> 09:58:08,096
You've seen this in the context of social media websites that are able to look at a photo that contains a whole bunch of faces,

6313
09:58:08,334 --> 09:58:12,896
and it's able to figure out what's a picture of whom and label those and tag them with appropriate people.

6314
09:58:13,216 --> 09:58:17,136
this is becoming increasingly relevant as we begin to discuss self-driving cars.

6315
09:58:17,776 --> 09:58:25,700
these cars now have cameras, and we would like for the computer to have some sort of algorithm that looks at the image and figures out what color is the light,

6316
09:58:26,096 --> 09:58:28,804
what cars are around us and in what direction, for example.

6317
09:58:29,070 --> 09:58:36,356
And so computer vision is all about taking an image and figuring out what sort of computation, what sort of calculation we can do with that image.

6318
09:58:36,846 --> 09:58:40,228
It's also relevant in the context of something like handwriting recognition.

6319
09:58:40,718 --> 09:58:43,696
This, what you're looking at, is an example of the MNIST dataset.

6320
09:58:43,776 --> 09:58:53,348
It's a big data set just of handwritten digits that we could use to ideally try and figure out how to predict, given someone's handwriting, given a photo of a digit that they have drawn,

6321
09:58:53,614 --> 09:58:57,860
can you predict whether it's a zero, one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, or nine, for example?

6322
09:58:58,350 --> 09:59:05,696
So this sort of handwriting recognition is yet another task that we might want to use, computer vision tasks and tools, to be able to apply it towards.

6323
09:59:05,870 --> 09:59:07,780
This might be a task that we might care about.

6324
09:59:08,686 --> 09:59:12,708
So, how then can we use neural networks to be able to solve a problem like this?

6325
09:59:13,070 --> 09:59:17,456
Well, neural networks rely upon some sort of input where that input is just numerical data.

6326
09:59:17,616 --> 09:59:21,476
We have a whole bunch of units where each one of them just represents some sort of number.

6327
09:59:22,030 --> 09:59:30,468
And so in the context of something like handwriting recognition, or in the context of just an image, you might imagine that an image is really just a grid of pixels,

6328
09:59:30,798 --> 09:59:33,444
grid of dots where each dot has some sort of color.

6329
09:59:34,126 --> 09:59:42,576
And in the context of something like handwriting recognition, you might imagine that if you just fill in each of these dots in a particular way, you can generate like a two or an eight, for example,

6330
09:59:42,736 --> 09:59:46,180
based on which dots happen to be shaded in and which dots are not.

6331
09:59:46,638 --> 09:59:50,212
And we can represent each of these pixel values just using numbers.

6332
09:59:50,446 --> 09:59:54,724
So for a particular pixel, for example, zero might represent entirely black.

6333
09:59:55,296 --> 10:00:03,332
depending on how you're representing color, it's often common to represent color values on a zero to 255 range, so that you can represent a color

6334
10:00:03,776 --> 10:00:08,096
using eight bits for a particular value, like how much white is in the image.

6335
10:00:08,430 --> 10:00:10,212
So zero might represent all black,

6336
10:00:11,214 --> 10:00:17,796
255 might represent entirely white as a pixel, and somewhere in between might represent some shade of gray, for example.

6337
10:00:18,336 --> 10:00:26,576
but you might imagine not just having a single slider that determines how much white is in the image, but if you had a color image, you might imagine three different numerical values, a red,

6338
10:00:26,736 --> 10:00:33,604
green, and blue value, where the red value controls how much red is in the image, we have one value for controlling how much green is in the pixel,

6339
10:00:33,742 --> 10:00:36,176
and one value for how much blue is in the pixel as well.

6340
10:00:36,398 --> 10:00:41,732
And depending on how it is that you set these values of red, green, and blue, you can get a different color.

6341
10:00:41,966 --> 10:00:49,828
And so any pixel can really be represented in this case by three numerical values: a red value, a green value, and a blue value.

6342
10:00:50,606 --> 10:00:58,416
And if you take a whole bunch of these pixels, assemble them together inside of a grid of pixels, then you really just have a whole bunch of numerical

6343
10:00:58,416 --> 10:01:02,052
values that you can use in order to perform some sort of prediction task.

6344
10:01:03,086 --> 10:01:09,924
And so what you might imagine doing is using the same techniques we talked about before, just design a neural network with a lot of inputs.

6345
10:01:10,382 --> 10:01:19,936
That for each of the pixels, we might have one or three different inputs in the case of a color image, a different input that is just connected to a deep neural network, for example.

6346
10:01:20,046 --> 10:01:26,148
And this deep neural network might take all of the pixels inside of the image of what digit a person drew,

6347
10:01:26,958 --> 10:01:35,716
and the output might be like 10 neurons that classify it as a zero or a one or a two or a three, or just tells us in some way what that digit happens to be.

6348
10:01:36,686 --> 10:01:38,532
Now there are a couple of drawbacks to this approach.

6349
10:01:39,056 --> 10:01:44,228
the first drawback to the approach is just the size of this input array, that we have a whole bunch of inputs.

6350
10:01:44,558 --> 10:01:50,980
If we have a big image that is a lot of different channels, we're looking at a lot of inputs and therefore a lot of weights that we have to calculate.

6351
10:01:51,918 --> 10:02:00,516
And a second problem is the fact that by flattening everything into just this structure of all the pixels, we've lost access to a lot of the information

6352
10:02:00,896 --> 10:02:03,136
about the structure of the image that's relevant.

6353
10:02:03,296 --> 10:02:08,016
That really, when a person looks at an image, they're looking at, particular features of that image.

6354
10:02:08,096 --> 10:02:12,816
They're looking at curves, they're looking at shapes, they're looking at what things can you identify in different regions of the image,

6355
10:02:12,896 --> 10:02:17,732
and maybe put those things together in order to get a better picture of what the overall image is about.

6356
10:02:18,158 --> 10:02:26,404
And by just turning it into pixel values for each of the pixels, sure you might be able to learn that structure, but it might be challenging in order to do so.

6357
10:02:26,496 --> 10:02:33,124
It might be helpful to take advantage of the fact that you can use properties of the image itself, the fact that it's structured in a particular way,

6358
10:02:33,454 --> 10:02:37,124
to be able to improve the way that we learn based on that image too.

6359
10:02:37,422 --> 10:02:43,616
So in order to figure out how we can train our neural networks to better be able to deal with images, we'll introduce a couple of ideas,

6360
10:02:43,776 --> 10:02:50,468
a couple of algorithms that we can apply that allow us to take the image and extract some useful information out of that image.

6361
10:02:50,830 --> 10:02:54,244
And the first idea we'll introduce is the notion of image convolution.

6362
10:02:54,670 --> 10:03:01,092
And what image convolution is all about is it's about filtering an image, sort of extracting useful or relevant features out of the image.

6363
10:03:01,550 --> 10:03:02,468
And the way we do that.

6364
10:03:03,278 --> 10:03:10,532
Is by applying a particular filter that basically adds the value for every pixel with the values for all of the neighboring pixels to it.

6365
10:03:11,008 --> 10:03:17,060
According to some sort of kernel matrix, which we'll see in a moment, is going to allow us to weight these pixels in various different ways.

6366
10:03:17,518 --> 10:03:22,820
And the goal of image convolution then is to extract some sort of interesting or useful features out of an image,

6367
10:03:23,008 --> 10:03:29,008
to be able to take a pixel and based on its neighboring pixels, maybe predict some sort of valuable information.

6368
10:03:29,198 --> 10:03:35,248
Something like taking a pixel and looking at its neighboring pixels, you might be able to predict whether or not there's some sort of curve inside the image,

6369
10:03:35,328 --> 10:03:39,088
or whether it's forming the outline of a particular line or a shape, for example.

6370
10:03:39,248 --> 10:03:40,324
And that might be useful

6371
10:03:41,038 --> 10:03:47,492
if you're trying to use all of these various different features to combine them to say something meaningful about an image as a whole.

6372
10:03:48,014 --> 10:03:49,732
So how then does image convolution work?

6373
10:03:50,062 --> 10:03:53,860
Well, we start with a kernel matrix, and the kernel matrix looks something like this.

6374
10:03:54,382 --> 10:04:02,212
And the idea of this is that given a pixel that will be the middle pixel, we're going to multiply each of the neighboring pixels by these values

6375
10:04:02,446 --> 10:04:06,372
in order to get some sort of result by summing up all the numbers together.

6376
10:04:06,862 --> 10:04:13,168
So if I take this kernel, which you can think of as like a filter that I'm going to apply to the image, and let's say that I take this image.

6377
10:04:13,328 --> 10:04:14,768
This is a four by four image.

6378
10:04:15,022 --> 10:04:19,588
We'll think of it as just a black and white image where each one is just a single pixel value.

6379
10:04:19,950 --> 10:04:22,368
So somewhere between 0 and 255, for example.

6380
10:04:22,766 --> 10:04:29,956
So we have a whole bunch of individual pixel values like this. And what I'd like to do is apply this kernel, this filter, so to speak,

6381
10:04:30,688 --> 10:04:31,968
to this image.

6382
10:04:32,398 --> 10:04:37,572
And the way I'll do that is, all right, the kernel is three by three, you can imagine a five by five kernel or a larger kernel too.

6383
10:04:38,158 --> 10:04:43,108
And I'll take it and just first apply it to the first three by three section of the image.

6384
10:04:43,694 --> 10:04:52,196
And what I'll do is I'll take each of these pixel values, multiply it by its corresponding value in the filter matrix, and add all of the results together.

6385
10:04:53,038 --> 10:05:01,008
So here, for example, I'll say 10 times 0, plus 20 times negative 1, plus 30 times 0,

6386
10:05:01,262 --> 10:05:03,408
so on and so forth, doing all of this calculation.

6387
10:05:03,726 --> 10:05:12,772
And at the end, if I take all these values, multiply them by their corresponding value in the kernel, add the results together, for this particular set of nine pixels, I get the value of 10,

6388
10:05:13,198 --> 10:05:13,764
for example.

6389
10:05:14,638 --> 10:05:15,460
And then what I'll

6390
10:05:16,302 --> 10:05:24,228
do is I'll slide this three by three grid, effectively, over, slide the kernel by one to look at the next three by three section.

6391
10:05:24,528 --> 10:05:30,848
Here I'm just sliding it over by one pixel, but you might imagine a different stride length or maybe I jump by multiple pixels at a time if you really wanted to.

6392
10:05:31,008 --> 10:05:32,048
You have different options here.

6393
10:05:32,366 --> 10:05:42,688
But here I'm just sliding over, looking at the next three by three section, and I'll do the same math: 20 times 0 plus 30 times a negative one, plus 40 times 0, plus 20 times negative one,

6394
10:05:42,848 --> 10:05:44,848
so on and so forth, plus 30 times 5.

6395
10:05:45,198 --> 10:05:46,756
And what I end up getting is the number 20.

6396
10:05:47,214 --> 10:05:55,940
Then you can imagine shifting over to this one, doing the same thing, calculating the number 40, for example, and then doing the same thing here and calculating a value there as well.

6397
10:05:56,750 --> 10:06:00,196
And so what we have now is what we'll call a feature map.

6398
10:06:00,622 --> 10:06:10,848
We have taken this kernel, applied it to each of these various different regions, and what we get is some representation of a filtered version of that image.

6399
10:06:11,214 --> 10:06:18,180
And so to give a more concrete example of why it is that this kind of thing could be useful, let's take this kernel matrix, for example, which is quite a famous one,

6400
10:06:18,446 --> 10:06:23,076
that has an eight in the middle, and then all of the neighboring pixels get a negative one.

6401
10:06:23,630 --> 10:06:32,132
And let's imagine we wanted to apply that to a three by three part of an image that looks like this, where all the values are the same, they're all 20,

6402
10:06:32,368 --> 10:06:32,580
for instance.

6403
10:06:33,518 --> 10:06:42,852
Well, in this case, if you do 20 times eight, and then subtract 20, subtract 20, subtract 20 for each of the eight neighbors, well the result of that is you just get that expression,

6404
10:06:43,278 --> 10:06:44,292
which comes out to be zero.

6405
10:06:44,686 --> 10:06:49,892
You multiplied 20 by eight, but then you subtracted 20 eight times according to that particular kernel.

6406
10:06:50,190 --> 10:06:51,812
The result of all that is just zero.

6407
10:06:52,430 --> 10:06:58,116
So the takeaway here is that when a lot of the pixels are the same value, we end up getting a value close to zero.

6408
10:06:59,246 --> 10:07:07,524
If though we had something like this, 20s along this first row, then 50s in the second row, and 50s in the third row, well then when you do this because it's the same kind of math,

6409
10:07:07,822 --> 10:07:14,916
20 times negative one, twenty times negative one, so on and so forth, then I get a higher value, a value like 90 in this particular case.

6410
10:07:15,630 --> 10:07:24,196
And so the more general idea here is that by applying this kernel, negative ones, eight in the middle, and then negative ones, what I get is

6411
10:07:24,656 --> 10:07:34,256
when this middle value is very different from the neighboring values, like 50 is greater than these twenties, then you'll end up with a value higher than zero.

6412
10:07:34,656 --> 10:07:38,016
if this number is higher than its neighbors, you end up getting a bigger output.

6413
10:07:38,286 --> 10:07:38,496
But if

6414
10:07:39,406 --> 10:07:43,108
this value is the same as all of its neighbors, then you get a lower output, something like zero.

6415
10:07:43,854 --> 10:07:49,456
And it turns out that this sort of filter can therefore be used in something like detecting edges in an image.

6416
10:07:49,646 --> 10:07:55,556
If I want to detect the boundaries between various different objects inside of an image, I might use a filter like this,

6417
10:07:55,854 --> 10:08:05,892
which is able to tell whether the value of this pixel is different from the values of the neighboring pixels, if it's greater than the values of the pixels that happen to surround it.

6418
10:08:06,766 --> 10:08:09,296
And so we can use this in terms of image filtering.

6419
10:08:09,454 --> 10:08:10,852
And so I'll show you an example of that.

6420
10:08:11,502 --> 10:08:14,468
I have here in filter.py

6421
10:08:15,214 --> 10:08:18,628
a file that uses Python's image library or p I L

6422
10:08:19,534 --> 10:08:21,216
to do some image filtering.

6423
10:08:21,376 --> 10:08:26,276
I go ahead and open an image, and then all I'm going to do is apply a kernel to that image.

6424
10:08:26,798 --> 10:08:29,700
It's going to be a three by three kernel, same kind of kernel we saw before.

6425
10:08:30,446 --> 10:08:31,492
And here is the kernel.

6426
10:08:31,918 --> 10:08:37,696
This is just a list representation of the same matrix that I showed you a moment ago. It's negative one, negative one, negative one.

6427
10:08:38,126 --> 10:08:42,532
The second row is negative one, eight, negative one, and the third row is all negative ones.

6428
10:08:43,118 --> 10:08:46,692
And then at the end, I'm going to go ahead and show the filtered image.

6429
10:08:47,854 --> 10:08:50,496
So if, for example, I go into

6430
10:08:51,886 --> 10:08:58,176
convolution directory and I open up an image like bridge.png, this is what an input image might look like.

6431
10:08:58,350 --> 10:09:01,156
Just an image of a bridge over a river.

6432
10:09:02,510 --> 10:09:06,212
Now I'm going to go ahead and run this filter program on the bridge.

6433
10:09:07,566 --> 10:09:09,776
And what I get is this image here.

6434
10:09:10,062 --> 10:09:17,376
Just by taking the original image and applying that filter to each three by three grid, I've extracted all of the boundaries,

6435
10:09:17,456 --> 10:09:21,056
all of the edges inside the image that separate one part of the image from another.

6436
10:09:21,294 --> 10:09:25,988
So here I've got a representation of boundaries between particular parts of the image.

6437
10:09:26,254 --> 10:09:32,896
And you might imagine that if a machine learning algorithm is trying to learn what an image is of, a filter like this could be pretty useful.

6438
10:09:33,056 --> 10:09:38,176
That maybe the machine learning algorithm doesn't care about all of the details of the image.

6439
10:09:38,416 --> 10:09:47,140
It just cares about certain useful features. It cares about particular shapes that are able to help it determine that based on the image, this is going to be a bridge, for example.

6440
10:09:47,662 --> 10:09:56,100
And so this type of idea of image convolution can allow us to apply filters to images that allow us to extract useful results out of those images,

6441
10:09:56,336 --> 10:09:59,076
taking an image and extracting its edges, for example.

6442
10:09:59,630 --> 10:10:05,376
And you might imagine many other filters that could be applied to an image that are able to extract particular values as well.

6443
10:10:05,550 --> 10:10:13,856
And a filter might have separate kernels for the red values, the green values, and the blue values that are all summed together at the end, such that you could have particular filters looking for.

6444
10:10:14,016 --> 10:10:17,376
Is there red in this part of the image? Are there green in other parts of the image?

6445
10:10:17,550 --> 10:10:23,300
You can begin to assemble these relevant and useful filters that are able to do these calculations as well.

6446
10:10:24,334 --> 10:10:32,324
So that then was the idea of image convolution, applying some sort of filter to an image to be able to extract some useful features out of that image.

6447
10:10:32,750 --> 10:10:35,536
But all the while, these images are still pretty big.

6448
10:10:35,856 --> 10:10:37,604
there's a lot of pixels involved in the image.

6449
10:10:37,966 --> 10:10:41,828
And realistically speaking, if you've got a really big image, that poses a couple of problems.

6450
10:10:42,190 --> 10:10:44,676
One, it means a lot of input going into the neural network.

6451
10:10:45,070 --> 10:10:47,044
But two, it also means that

6452
10:10:47,758 --> 10:10:50,336
we really have to care about what's in each particular pixel.

6453
10:10:50,542 --> 10:10:51,616
Whereas realistically,

6454
10:10:53,006 --> 10:10:59,296
if you're looking at an image, you don't care whether something is in one particular pixel versus the pixel immediately to the right of it.

6455
10:10:59,376 --> 10:11:00,676
They're pretty close together.

6456
10:11:00,974 --> 10:11:05,536
You really just care about whether there's a particular feature in some region of the image.

6457
10:11:05,646 --> 10:11:08,836
And maybe you don't care about exactly which pixel it happens to be in.

6458
10:11:09,422 --> 10:11:11,588
And so there's a technique we can use known as pooling.

6459
10:11:11,886 --> 10:11:18,308
And what pooling is, is it means reducing the size of an input by sampling from regions inside of the input.

6460
10:11:18,510 --> 10:11:22,976
So we're going to take a big image and turn it into a smaller image by using pooling.

6461
10:11:23,118 --> 10:11:26,948
And in particular, one of the most popular types of pooling is called max pooling.

6462
10:11:27,086 --> 10:11:32,772
And what max pooling does is it pools just by choosing the maximum value in a particular region.

6463
10:11:33,486 --> 10:11:38,404
So for example, let's imagine I had this four by four image, but I wanted to reduce its dimensions.

6464
10:11:38,576 --> 10:11:42,180
I wanted to make it a smaller image so that I have fewer inputs to work with.

6465
10:11:42,574 --> 10:11:52,896
Well, what I could do is I could apply a two by two max pool, where the idea would be that I'm going to first look at this two by two region and say, what is the maximum value in that region?

6466
10:11:53,166 --> 10:11:55,972
Well, it's the number 50, so we'll go ahead and just use the number 50.

6467
10:11:56,974 --> 10:11:58,340
And then we'll look at this two by two region.

6468
10:11:58,528 --> 10:11:59,808
What is the maximum value here?

6469
10:12:00,174 --> 10:12:02,048
It's 110, so that's going to be my value.

6470
10:12:02,318 --> 10:12:04,461
Likewise, here the maximum value looks like 20.

6471
10:12:04,688 --> 10:12:05,668
Go ahead and put that there.

6472
10:12:05,934 --> 10:12:10,308
Then for this last region, the maximum value was 40. so we'll go ahead and use that.

6473
10:12:10,734 --> 10:12:21,088
And what I have now is a smaller representation of this same original image that I obtained just by picking the maximum value from each of these

6474
10:12:21,088 --> 10:12:22,212
regions. So

6475
10:12:22,958 --> 10:12:30,660
again, the advantages here are now I only have to deal with a two by two input instead of a four by four, and you can imagine shrinking the size of an image even more.

6476
10:12:31,182 --> 10:12:40,048
But in addition to that, I'm now able to make my analysis independent of whether a particular value was in this pixel or this pixel.

6477
10:12:40,128 --> 10:12:47,008
Like I don't care if the 50 was here or here, as long as it was generally in this region, I'll still get access to that value.

6478
10:12:47,182 --> 10:12:50,404
So it makes our algorithms a little bit more robust as well.

6479
10:12:51,342 --> 10:12:58,884
So that then is pooling, taking the size of the image, reducing it a little bit by just sampling from particular regions inside of the image.

6480
10:12:59,502 --> 10:13:10,368
And now we can put all of these ideas together, pooling image convolution and neural networks all together into another type of neural network called a convolutional neural network, or a CNN,

6481
10:13:10,766 --> 10:13:17,088
which is a neural network that uses this convolution step, usually in the context of analyzing an image, for

6482
10:13:17,088 --> 10:13:24,164
example. And so, the way that a convolutional neural network works is that we start with some sort of input image, some grid of pixels,

6483
10:13:24,462 --> 10:13:31,044
but rather than immediately put that into the neural network layers that we've seen before, we'll start by applying a convolution step,

6484
10:13:31,214 --> 10:13:36,548
where the convolution step involves applying some number of different image filters to our original image.

6485
10:13:36,718 --> 10:13:41,728
in order to get what we call a feature map, the result of applying some filter to an image.

6486
10:13:41,966 --> 10:13:51,460
And we could do this once, but in general, we'll do this multiple times, getting a whole bunch of different feature maps, each of which might extract some different relevant feature out of the image,

6487
10:13:51,648 --> 10:13:57,508
some different important characteristic of the image that we might care about using in order to calculate what the result should be.

6488
10:13:58,190 --> 10:14:05,572
And in the same way that when we train neural networks, we can train neural networks to learn the weights between particular units inside of the neural networks.

6489
10:14:05,998 --> 10:14:15,808
We can also train neural networks to learn what those filters should be, what the values of the filters should be, in order to get the most useful, most relevant information out of the original image.

6490
10:14:16,046 --> 10:14:22,852
just by figuring out what setting of those filter values, the values inside of that kernel, results in minimizing the loss function,

6491
10:14:23,088 --> 10:14:30,628
minimizing how poorly our hypothesis actually performs in figuring out the classification of a particular image.

6492
10:14:30,928 --> 10:14:31,460
for example.

6493
10:14:31,808 --> 10:14:36,228
So we first apply this convolution step, get a whole bunch of these various different feature maps.

6494
10:14:36,782 --> 10:14:41,156
But these feature maps are quite large, right? There's a lot of pixel values that happen to be here.

6495
10:14:41,454 --> 10:14:51,568
And so a logical next step to take is a pooling step, where we reduce the size of these images by using max pooling, for example, extracting the maximum value from any particular region.

6496
10:14:51,822 --> 10:14:54,688
There are other pooling methods that exist as well, depending on the situation.

6497
10:14:54,768 --> 10:15:02,436
You could use something like average pooling, where instead of taking the maximum value from a region, you take the average value from a region, which has its uses as well.

6498
10:15:03,182 --> 10:15:11,620
But in effect, what pooling will do is it'll take these feature maps and reduce their dimensions, so that we end up with smaller grids with fewer pixels.

6499
10:15:12,014 --> 10:15:14,208
And this then is going to be easier for us to deal with.

6500
10:15:14,382 --> 10:15:16,580
It's going to mean fewer inputs that we have to worry about.

6501
10:15:16,846 --> 10:15:27,088
And it's also going to mean we're more resilient, more robust against potential movements of particular values just by one pixel when ultimately we really don't care about those one pixel differences

6502
10:15:27,406 --> 10:15:29,092
that might arise in the original image.

6503
10:15:30,126 --> 10:15:38,384
And now after we've done this pooling step, now we have a whole bunch of values that we can then flatten out and just put into a more traditional neural network.

6504
10:15:38,574 --> 10:15:46,544
So we go ahead and flatten it, and then we end up with a traditional neural network that has one input for each of these values in each of these resulting feature

6505
10:15:46,544 --> 10:15:50,304
maps after we do the convolution and after we do the pooling

6506
10:15:50,304 --> 10:15:54,624
step. And so this then is the general structure of a convolutional network.

6507
10:15:54,784 --> 10:16:03,344
We begin with the image, apply convolution, apply pooling, flatten the results, and then put that into a more traditional neural network that might itself have hidden layers.

6508
10:16:03,424 --> 10:16:13,092
You can have deep convolutional networks that have hidden layers in between this flattened layer and the eventual output to be able to calculate various different features of those values.

6509
10:16:13,358 --> 10:16:21,344
But this then can help us to be able to use convolution and pooling, to use our knowledge about the structure of an image to be able to get better results,

6510
10:16:21,454 --> 10:16:26,944
to be able to train our networks faster in order to better capture particular parts of the image.

6511
10:16:27,278 --> 10:16:30,372
And there's no reason necessarily why you can only use these steps once.

6512
10:16:30,624 --> 10:16:35,972
In fact, in practice, you'll often use convolution and pooling multiple times in multiple different steps.

6513
10:16:36,462 --> 10:16:38,724
So we what you might imagine doing is starting with an image,

6514
10:16:39,598 --> 10:16:46,948
first applying convolution to get a whole bunch of maps, then applying pooling, then applying convolution again, because these maps are still pretty big.

6515
10:16:47,950 --> 10:16:52,420
You can apply convolution to try and extract relevant features out of this result.

6516
10:16:52,846 --> 10:17:00,612
Then take those results, apply pooling in order to reduce their dimensions, and then take that and feed it into a neural network that maybe has fewer inputs.

6517
10:17:01,198 --> 10:17:03,984
So here I have two different convolution and pooling steps.

6518
10:17:04,064 --> 10:17:12,292
I do convolution and pooling once, and then I do convolution and pooling a second time, each time extracting useful features from the layer before it,

6519
10:17:12,654 --> 10:17:16,708
each time using pooling to reduce the dimensions of what you're ultimately looking at.

6520
10:17:17,230 --> 10:17:25,184
And the goal now of this sort of model is that in each of these steps, you can begin to learn different types of features of the original image.

6521
10:17:25,358 --> 10:17:28,292
That maybe in the first step you learn very low-level features.

6522
10:17:28,464 --> 10:17:35,904
Just learn and look for features like edges and curves and shapes, because based on pixels and their neighboring values, you can figure out,

6523
10:17:36,238 --> 10:17:40,068
all right, what are the edges, what are the curves, what are the various different shapes that might be present there.

6524
10:17:40,974 --> 10:17:46,244
But then, once you have a mapping, that just represents where the edges and curves and shapes happen to be,

6525
10:17:46,478 --> 10:17:53,664
you can imagine applying the same sort of process again, to begin to look for higher level features, look for objects, maybe look for people's eyes.

6526
10:17:53,664 --> 10:18:03,108
and facial recognition, for example, maybe look for more complex shapes, like the curves on a particular number. if you're trying to recognize a digit in a handwriting recognition, sort of scenario.

6527
10:18:03,566 --> 10:18:10,304
And then after all of that, now that you have these results that represent these higher level features, you can pass them into a neural network,

6528
10:18:10,384 --> 10:18:20,292
which is really just a deep neural network that looks like this, where you might imagine making a binary classification or classifying into multiple categories or performing various different tasks

6529
10:18:21,504 --> 10:18:22,788
on this sort of model.

6530
10:18:23,406 --> 10:18:28,644
So convolutional neural networks can be quite powerful and quite popular when it comes towards trying to analyze images.

6531
10:18:28,784 --> 10:18:35,424
We don't strictly need them. We could have just used a vanilla neural network that just operates with layer after layer as we've seen before.

6532
10:18:35,662 --> 10:18:41,572
But these convolutional neural networks can be quite helpful, in particular because of the way they model the way a human might look at an image.

6533
10:18:41,824 --> 10:18:50,148
That instead of a human looking at every single pixel simultaneously and trying to convolve all of them by multiplying them together, you might imagine that what convolution is really doing

6534
10:18:50,384 --> 10:18:56,964
is looking at various different regions of the image and extracting relevant information and features out of those parts of the image.

6535
10:18:57,184 --> 10:19:03,024
The same way that a human might have visual receptors that are looking at particular parts of what they see and using those,

6536
10:19:03,184 --> 10:19:08,292
combining them to figure out what meaning they can draw from all of those various different inputs.

6537
10:19:09,294 --> 10:19:13,284
And so you might imagine applying this to a situation like handwriting recognition.

