Again, this is messy. If you tried to imagine how would you write a program with just like ifs and thens to be able to do this sort of calculation, it would be tricky to do so.

6650
10:28:48,078 --> 10:28:53,392
But here I'll press classify and alright. It seems it was able to correctly classify that what I drew was the number two.

6651
10:28:54,030 --> 10:28:57,060
I'll go ahead and reset it, try it again. We'll draw like an eight, for example.

6652
10:28:57,902 --> 10:28:59,620
So here is an eight.

6653
10:29:00,462 --> 10:29:04,484
press classify, and all right, It predicts that the digit that I drew was an eight.

6654
10:29:05,038 --> 10:29:08,992
And the key here is this really begins to show the power of what the neural network is doing.

6655
10:29:09,166 --> 10:29:17,312
Somehow looking at various different features of these different pixels, figuring out what the relevant features are, and figuring out how to combine them to get a classification.

6656
10:29:17,550 --> 10:29:22,752
And this would be a difficult task to provide explicit instructions to the computer on how to do.

6657
10:29:22,992 --> 10:29:28,672
to use a whole bunch of if-thens to process all of these pixel values to figure out what the handwritten digit is.

6658
10:29:29,166 --> 10:29:33,632
everyone's going to draw their eights a little bit differently. If I drew the eight again, it would look a little bit different.

6659
10:29:33,870 --> 10:29:36,996
And yet, ideally, we want to train a network to be robust enough

6660
10:29:37,742 --> 10:29:40,432
so that it begins to learn these patterns on its own.

6661
10:29:40,672 --> 10:29:45,508
All I said was here is the structure of the network, and here is the data on which to train the network.

6662
10:29:45,838 --> 10:29:52,260
And the network learning algorithm just tries to figure out what is the optimal set of weights, what is the optimal set of filters to use

6663
10:29:52,462 --> 10:29:56,912
in order to be able to accurately classify a digit into one category or another.

6664
10:29:57,390 --> 10:30:00,996
It's going to show the power of these sorts of convolutional neural networks.

6665
10:30:02,190 --> 10:30:10,432
And so that then was a look at how we can use convolutional neural networks to begin to solve problems with regards to computer vision,

6666
10:30:10,606 --> 10:30:13,316
the ability to take an image and begin to analyze it.

6667
10:30:13,632 --> 10:30:18,596
so this is the type of analysis you might imagine that's happening in self-driving cars that are able to

6668
10:30:19,054 --> 10:30:23,792
figure out what filters to apply to an image to understand what it is that the computer is looking at,

6669
10:30:23,982 --> 10:30:31,632
or the same type of idea that might be applied to facial recognition in social media to be able to determine how to recognize faces in an image as well.

6670
10:30:31,822 --> 10:30:40,592
You can imagine a neural network that instead of classifying into one of ten different digits, could instead classify like, is this person A or is this person B,

6671
10:30:40,752 --> 10:30:43,588
trying to tell those people apart just based on convolution.

6672
10:30:44,974 --> 10:30:50,020
And so now what we'll take a look at is yet another type of neural network that can be quite popular for certain types of tasks.

6673
10:30:50,542 --> 10:30:55,428
But to do so, we'll try to generalize and think about our neural network a little bit more abstractly.

6674
10:30:55,758 --> 10:31:03,460
That here we have a sample deep neural network where we have this input layer, a whole bunch of different hidden layers that are performing certain types of calculations,

6675
10:31:04,046 --> 10:31:08,516
and then an output layer here that just generates some sort of output that we care about calculating.

6676
10:31:09,646 --> 10:31:13,632
But we could imagine representing this a little more simply, like this.

6677
10:31:14,030 --> 10:31:17,232
Here is just a more abstract representation of our neural network.

6678
10:31:17,392 --> 10:31:21,924
We have some input that might be like a vector of a whole bunch of different values as our input.

6679
10:31:22,382 --> 10:31:29,312
That gets passed into a network that performs some sort of calculation or computation, and that network produces some sort of output.

6680
10:31:29,582 --> 10:31:35,792
That output might be a single value, it might be a whole bunch of different values, but this is the general structure of the neural network that we've seen.

6681
10:31:36,046 --> 10:31:37,220
There is some sort of input

6682
10:31:37,934 --> 10:31:42,980
that gets fed into the network, and using that input, the network calculates what the output should be.

6683
10:31:43,406 --> 10:31:48,708
And this sort of model for a neural network is what we might call a feed-forward neural network.

6684
10:31:49,070 --> 10:31:52,672
Feed forward neural networks have connections only in one direction.

6685
10:31:52,878 --> 10:32:02,020
They move from one layer to the next layer to the layer after that, such that the inputs pass through various different hidden layers and then ultimately produce some sort of output.

6686
10:32:02,894 --> 10:32:08,512
So feed forward neural networks were very helpful for solving these types of classification problems that we saw before.

6687
10:32:08,672 --> 10:32:13,604
We have a whole bunch of input, we want to learn what setting of weights will allow us to calculate the output effectively.

6688
10:32:14,030 --> 10:32:17,412
But there are some limitations on feed forward neural networks that we'll see in a moment.

6689
10:32:17,646 --> 10:32:24,752
In particular, the input needs to be of a fixed shape, like a fixed number of neurons are in the input layer, and there's a fixed shape for the output,

6690
10:32:24,878 --> 10:32:27,140
like a fixed number of neurons in the output layer.

6691
10:32:27,982 --> 10:32:30,052
And that has some limitations of its own.

6692
10:32:30,574 --> 10:32:31,940
And a possible solution to this

6693
10:32:33,134 --> 10:32:40,672
And we'll see examples of the types of problems we can solve with this in just a second, is instead of just a feed-forward neural network where there are only connections in one direction,

6694
10:32:40,942 --> 10:32:46,180
from left to right, effectively, across the network, we could also imagine a recurrent neural network.

6695
10:32:46,606 --> 10:32:54,148
Where a recurrent neural network generates output that gets fed back into itself as input for future runs of that network.

6696
10:32:54,862 --> 10:33:00,900
So whereas in a traditional neural network, we have inputs that get fed into the network that get fed into the output,

6697
10:33:01,072 --> 10:33:07,396
and the only thing that determines the output is based on the original input and based on the calculation we do inside of the network itself.

6698
10:33:07,982 --> 10:33:15,712
This goes in contrast with a recurrent neural network, where in a recurrent neural network, you can imagine output from the network feeding back to

6699
10:33:15,758 --> 10:33:21,700
itself into the network again as input for the next time that you do the calculations inside of the network.

6700
10:33:22,318 --> 10:33:32,068
What this allows is it allows the network to maintain some sort of state, to store some sort of information that can be used on future runs of the network.

6701
10:33:32,910 --> 10:33:37,988
The previously, the network just defined some weights and we passed inputs through the network and it generated outputs,

6702
10:33:38,272 --> 10:33:44,932
but the network wasn't saving any information based on those inputs to be able to remember for future iterations or for future runs.

6703
10:33:45,358 --> 10:33:55,300
What a recurrent neural network will let us do is let the network store information that gets passed back in as input to the network again the next time we try and perform some sort of action.

6704
10:33:55,502 --> 10:33:59,652
And this is particularly helpful when dealing with sequences of data.

6705
10:34:00,174 --> 10:34:02,500
So we'll see a real world example of this right now, actually.

6706
10:34:02,912 --> 10:34:06,308
Microsoft has developed an AI known as the caption bot.

6707
10:34:07,086 --> 10:34:13,712
And what the caption bot does is it says, I can understand the content of any photograph and I'll try to describe it as well as any human.

6708
10:34:13,902 --> 10:34:15,908
I'll analyze your photo, but I won't store it or share it.

6709
10:34:16,238 --> 10:34:25,028
And so what Microsoft's caption bot seems to be claiming to do is it can take an image and figure out what's in the image and just give us a caption to describe it.

6710
10:34:25,646 --> 10:34:26,436
So let's try it out.

6711
10:34:26,766 --> 10:34:32,352
Here, for example, is an image of Harvard Square. It's some people walking in front of one of the buildings at Harvard Square.

6712
10:34:32,590 --> 10:34:36,868
I'll go ahead and take the URL for that image and I'll paste it into CaptionBot.

6713
10:34:37,454 --> 10:34:38,180
I'll just press go.

6714
10:34:39,022 --> 10:34:46,672
So CaptionBot is analyzing the image, and then it says, I think it's a group of people walking in front of a building, which seems amazing.

6715
10:34:46,752 --> 10:34:50,212
The AI is able to look at this image and figure out what's in the image.

6716
10:34:50,798 --> 10:34:55,012
And the important thing to recognize here is that this is no longer just a classification task.

6717
10:34:55,232 --> 10:35:03,204
We saw being able to classify images with a convolutional neural network, where the job was, you know, take the image and then figure out is it a zero or a one or a two,

6718
10:35:03,472 --> 10:35:05,476
or is it this person's face or that person's face?

6719
10:35:05,966 --> 10:35:11,908
What seems to be happening here is the input is an image, and we know how to get networks to take input of images,

6720
10:35:12,462 --> 10:35:18,788
but the output is text, like it's a sentence, it's a phrase, like a group of people walking in front of a building.

6721
10:35:19,598 --> 10:35:27,684
And this would seem to pose a challenge for our more traditional feed-forward neural networks, for the reason being that in traditional neural networks,

6722
10:35:28,352 --> 10:35:31,684
we just have a fixed size input and a fixed size output.

6723
10:35:31,872 --> 10:35:39,712
There are a certain number of neurons in the input to our neural network, and a certain number of outputs for our neural network, and then some calculation that goes on in between,

6724
10:35:39,950 --> 10:35:48,804
but the size of the inputs, the number of values in the input, and the number of values in the output, those are always going to be fixed based on the structure of the neural network.

6725
10:35:49,198 --> 10:35:55,652
And that makes it difficult to imagine how a neural network could take an image like this and say, you know, it's a group of people walking in front of the building,

6726
10:35:56,112 --> 10:35:59,712
because the output is text, like it's a sequence of

6727
10:35:59,712 --> 10:36:04,112
words. Now it might be possible for a neural network to output like one word.

6728
10:36:04,432 --> 10:36:12,580
One word you could represent is like a vector of values, and you can imagine ways of doing that. next time we'll talk a little bit more about AI as it relates to language and language processing.

6729
10:36:13,134 --> 10:36:19,632
But a sequence of words is much more challenging because depending on the image, you might imagine the output is a different number of words.

6730
10:36:19,792 --> 10:36:25,700
We could have sequences of different lengths, and somehow we still want to be able to generate the appropriate output.

6731
10:36:26,542 --> 10:36:35,748
And so the strategy here is to use a recurrent neural network, a neural network that can feed its own output back into itself as input for the next time.

6732
10:36:36,142 --> 10:36:41,412
And this allows us to do what we call a one to many relationship for inputs to outputs.

6733
10:36:41,902 --> 10:36:46,852
That in vanilla, more traditional neural networks, these are what we might consider to be one to one neural networks.

6734
10:36:47,152 --> 10:36:52,356
You pass in one set of values as input, you get one vector of values as the output.

6735
10:36:53,198 --> 10:37:03,776
But in this case, we want to pass in one value as input, the image, and we want to get a sequence, many values as output, where each value is like one of these words that gets produced by

6736
10:37:04,110 --> 10:37:04,932
this particular algorithm.

6737
10:37:05,614 --> 10:37:11,012
And so the way we might do this is we might imagine starting by providing input, the image, into our neural network.

6738
10:37:11,566 --> 10:37:15,684
And the neural network is going to generate output, but the output is not going to be the whole sequence of words,

6739
10:37:15,982 --> 10:37:20,548
because we can't represent the whole sequence of words using just a fixed set of neurons.

6740
10:37:20,896 --> 10:37:23,812
Instead, the output is just going to be the first word.

6741
10:37:24,334 --> 10:37:27,936
We're going to train the network to output what the first word of the caption should be.

6742
10:37:28,016 --> 10:37:36,416
And you could imagine that Microsoft has trained this by running a whole bunch of training samples through the AI, giving it a whole bunch of pictures and what the appropriate caption was,

6743
10:37:36,622 --> 10:37:38,660
and having the AI begin to learn from that.

6744
10:37:39,790 --> 10:37:47,620
But now, because the network generates output that can be fed back into itself, you could imagine the output of the network being fed back into the same network.

6745
10:37:48,078 --> 10:37:53,060
This here looks like a separate network, but it's really the same network that's just getting different input.

6746
10:37:53,422 --> 10:37:59,332
That this network's output gets fed back into itself, but it's going to generate another output.

6747
10:37:59,662 --> 10:38:03,364
And that other output is going to be like the second word in the caption.

6748
10:38:04,110 --> 10:38:13,252
And this recurrent neural network then this network is going to generate other output that can be fed back into itself to generate yet another word, fed back into itself to generate another word.

6749
10:38:13,646 --> 10:38:18,628
And so recurrent neural networks allow us to represent this sort of one-to-many structure.

6750
10:38:18,830 --> 10:38:27,972
You provide one image as input, and the neural network can pass data into the next run of the network, and then again and again, such that you could run the network multiple times,

6751
10:38:28,176 --> 10:38:32,964
each time generating a different output, still based on that original input.

6752
10:38:33,870 --> 10:38:39,856
And this is where recurrent neural networks become particularly useful when dealing with sequences of inputs or outputs.

6753
10:38:40,016 --> 10:38:48,868
And my output is a sequence of words, and since I can't very easily represent outputting an entire sequence of words, I'll instead output that sequence one word at a time

6754
10:38:49,166 --> 10:38:56,656
by allowing my network to pass information about what still needs to be said about the photo into the next stage of running the network.

6755
10:38:56,816 --> 10:39:02,532
So you could run the network multiple times, the same network with the same weights, just getting different input each time.

6756
10:39:02,958 --> 10:39:12,068
First, getting input from the image, and then getting input from the network itself as additional information about what additionally needs to be given in a particular caption,

6757
10:39:12,366 --> 10:39:12,740
for example.

6758
10:39:13,838 --> 10:39:17,732
So this then is a one-to-many relationship inside of a recurrent neural network.

6759
10:39:18,030 --> 10:39:26,468
But it turns out there are other models that we can use, other ways we can try and use recurrent neural networks to be able to represent data that might be stored in other forms as well.

6760
10:39:26,734 --> 10:39:35,456
We saw how we could use neural networks in order to analyze images in the context of convolutional neural networks that take an image, figure out various different properties of the image, and are

6761
10:39:35,630 --> 10:39:37,892
able to draw some sort of conclusion based on that.

6762
10:39:38,574 --> 10:39:43,812
But you might imagine that something like YouTube, they need to be able to do a lot of learning based on video.

6763
10:39:44,046 --> 10:39:53,376
They need to look through videos to detect if they're copyright violations, or they need to be able to look through videos to maybe identify what particular items are inside of the video, for example.

6764
10:39:53,614 --> 10:40:01,924
And video, you might imagine, is much more difficult to put in as input to a neural network, because whereas an image you could just treat each pixel as a different value,

6765
10:40:02,256 --> 10:40:03,536
videos are sequences;

6766
10:40:03,696 --> 10:40:11,776
they're sequences of images, and each sequence might be of different length, and so it might be challenging to represent that entire video as a single vector

6767
10:40:11,776 --> 10:40:14,372
of values that you could pass in to a neural network.

6768
10:40:15,278 --> 10:40:21,092
And so here too, recurrent neural networks can be a valuable solution for trying to solve this type of problem.

6769
10:40:21,296 --> 10:40:28,324
That instead of just passing in a single input into our neural network, we could pass in the input like one frame at a time, you might imagine.

6770
10:40:28,576 --> 10:40:35,172
First, taking the first frame of the video, passing it into the network, and then maybe not having the network output anything at all yet.

6771
10:40:35,536 --> 10:40:44,612
Let it take in another input, and this time pass it into the network, but the network gets information from the last time we provided an input into the network.

6772
10:40:45,006 --> 10:40:53,060
Then we pass in a third input and then a fourth input, where each time what the network gets is it gets the most recent input, like each frame of the video,

6773
10:40:53,614 --> 10:40:58,016
but it also gets information the network processed from all of the previous iterations.

6774
10:40:58,176 --> 10:41:05,856
So on frame number four, you end up getting the input for frame number four plus information the network has calculated from the first three

6775
10:41:05,856 --> 10:41:14,496
frames. And using all of that data combined, this recurrent neural network can begin to learn how to extract patterns from a sequence of data as well.

6776
10:41:14,862 --> 10:41:23,620
And so you might imagine if you want to classify a video into a number of different genres, like an educational video or a music video or different types of videos, that's a classification task

6777
10:41:24,046 --> 10:41:32,580
where you want to take as input each of the frames of the video, and you want to output something like what it is, what category that it happens to belong to.

6778
10:41:33,518 --> 10:41:39,524
And you can imagine doing this sort of thing, this sort of many to one learning, anytime your input is a sequence.

6779
10:41:39,822 --> 10:41:42,848
And so input is a sequence in the context of video.

6780
10:41:43,246 --> 10:41:47,648
It could be in the context of if someone has typed a message and you want to be able to categorize that message.

6781
10:41:47,854 --> 10:41:53,888
Like if you're trying to take a movie review and trying to classify it as is it a positive review or a negative review?

6782
10:41:54,062 --> 10:41:59,108
That input is a sequence of words, and the output is a classification, positive or negative.

6783
10:41:59,342 --> 10:42:06,692
There too, a recurrent neural network might be helpful for analyzing sequences of words, and they're quite popular when it comes to dealing with language.

6784
10:42:07,598 --> 10:42:09,568
Could even be used for spoken language as well.

6785
10:42:09,838 --> 10:42:17,728
That spoken language is, an audio waveform that can be segmented into distinct chunks, and each of those could be passed in as an input into a

6786
10:42:17,728 --> 10:42:21,316
recurrent neural network to be able to classify someone's voice, for instance.

6787
10:42:21,550 --> 10:42:25,284
If you want to do voice recognition to say, is this one person or is this another?

6788
10:42:25,582 --> 10:42:31,008
Here are also cases where you might want this many to one architecture for a recurrent neural

6789
10:42:31,008 --> 10:42:38,980
network. And then as one final problem, just to take a look at in terms of what we can do with these sorts of networks, imagine what Google Translate is doing.

6790
10:42:39,168 --> 10:42:47,492
So what Google Translate is doing is it's taking some text written in one language and converting it into text written in some other language, for example.

6791
10:42:47,950 --> 10:42:55,108
Where now this input is a sequence of data, it's a sequence of words, and the output is a sequence of words as well. It's also a sequence.

6792
10:42:55,630 --> 10:42:58,528
So here we want effectively like a many-to-many relationship.

6793
10:42:58,702 --> 10:43:02,288
Our input is a sequence and our output is a sequence as well.

6794
10:43:02,448 --> 10:43:09,060
And it's not quite going to work to just say take each word in the input and translate it into a word in the output.

6795
10:43:09,838 --> 10:43:16,900
Because ultimately, different languages put their words in different orders, and maybe one language uses two words for something, whereas another language only uses one.

6796
10:43:17,294 --> 10:43:18,500
So we really want some way

6797
10:43:19,534 --> 10:43:27,408
to take this information, this input, encode it somehow, and use that encoding to generate what the output ultimately should be.

6798
10:43:27,488 --> 10:43:35,728
And this has been one of the big advancements in automated translation technology is the ability to use the neural networks to do this instead of older, more traditional methods.

6799
10:43:35,808 --> 10:43:37,636
And this has improved accuracy dramatically.

6800
10:43:38,030 --> 10:43:43,908
And the way you might imagine doing this is again using a recurrent neural network with multiple inputs and multiple outputs.

6801
10:43:44,270 --> 10:43:45,728
We start by passing in all the input.

6802
10:43:45,968 --> 10:43:54,372
Input goes into the network, another input, like another word, goes into the network, and we do this multiple times, like once for each word in the input that I'm trying to translate.

6803
10:43:54,766 --> 10:44:02,788
And only after all of that is done does the network now start to generate output, like the first word of the translated sentence, and the next word of the translated sentence,

6804
10:44:03,214 --> 10:44:04,068
so on and so forth.

6805
10:44:04,302 --> 10:44:08,036
Where each time the network passes information to itself

6806
10:44:08,848 --> 10:44:17,028
by allowing for this model of giving some sort of state from one run in the network to the next run, assembling information about all the inputs,

6807
10:44:17,358 --> 10:44:22,208
and then passing in information about which part of the output in order to generate next.

6808
10:44:22,542 --> 10:44:31,204
And there are a number of different types of these sorts of recurrent neural networks. One of the most popular is known as the long short-term memory neural network, otherwise known as LSTM.

6809
10:44:31,470 --> 10:44:36,208
But in general, these types of networks can be very, very powerful whenever we're dealing with sequences,

6810
10:44:36,288 --> 10:44:42,596
whether those are sequences of images or especially sequences of words when it comes towards dealing with natural language.

6811
10:44:43,502 --> 10:44:49,700
And so that then were just some of the different types of neural networks that can be used to do all sorts of different computations.

6812
10:44:49,808 --> 10:44:53,888
And these are incredibly versatile tools that can be applied to a number of different domains.

6813
10:44:54,158 --> 10:45:02,596
We only looked at a couple of the most popular types of neural networks, more traditional feed-forward neural networks, convolutional neural networks, and recurrent neural networks.

6814
10:45:02,894 --> 10:45:09,808
But there are other types as well. There are adversarial networks where networks compete with each other to try and be able to generate new types of data,

6815
10:45:10,048 --> 10:45:15,408
as well as other networks that can solve other tasks based on what they happen to be structured and adapted for.

6816
10:45:15,630 --> 10:45:21,648
And these are very powerful tools in machine learning, for being able to very easily learn based on some set of input data,

6817
10:45:21,838 --> 10:45:30,660
and to be able to therefore figure out how to calculate some function from inputs to outputs, whether it's input to some sort of classification, like analyzing an image and getting a digit,

6818
10:45:30,798 --> 10:45:34,756
or machine translation where the input is in one language and the output is in another.

6819
10:45:34,928 --> 10:45:38,500
These tools have a lot of applications for machine learning more generally.

6820
10:45:39,278 --> 10:45:43,940
Next time, we'll look at machine learning and AI in particular in the context of natural language.

6821
10:45:44,128 --> 10:45:51,968
We talked a little bit about this today, but looking at how it is that our AI can begin to understand natural language and can begin to be able to analyze and do

6822
10:45:51,968 --> 10:45:56,676
useful tasks with regards to human language, which turns out to be a challenging and interesting task.

6823
10:45:57,070 --> 10:45:58,020
So we'll see you next time.

6824
10:46:19,342 --> 10:46:23,876
Welcome back everybody to our final class in an introduction to artificial intelligence with Python.

6825
10:46:24,270 --> 10:46:31,396
Now, so far in this class, we've been taking problems that we want to solve intelligently and framing them in ways that computers are going to be able to make sense of.

6826
10:46:31,726 --> 10:46:38,576
We've been taking problems and framing them as search problems or constraint satisfaction problems or optimization problems, for example.

6827
10:46:38,894 --> 10:46:44,548
In essence, we have been trying to communicate about problems in ways that our computer is going to be able to understand.

6828
10:46:45,134 --> 10:46:53,476
Today, the goal is going to be to get computers to understand the way you and I communicate naturally via our own natural languages, languages like English.

6829
10:46:53,774 --> 10:47:00,336
But natural language contains a lot of nuance and complexity that's going to make it challenging for computers to be able to understand.

6830
10:47:00,622 --> 10:47:07,140
So we'll need to explore some new tools and some new techniques to allow computers to make sense of natural language.

6831
10:47:07,694 --> 10:47:10,416
So, what is it exactly that we're trying to get computers to do?

6832
10:47:10,606 --> 10:47:16,964
Well, they all fall under this general heading of natural language processing, getting computers to work with natural language.

6833
10:47:17,326 --> 10:47:25,956
And these tasks include tasks like automatic summarization, given a long text, can we train the computer to be able to come up with a shorter representation of it?

6834
10:47:26,256 --> 10:47:30,692
Information extraction, getting the computer to pull out relevant facts or details out of some text.

6835
10:47:31,086 --> 10:47:36,416
Machine translation, like Google Translate, translating some text from one language into another language.

6836
10:47:36,656 --> 10:47:44,484
Question answering, if you've ever asked a question to your phone or had a conversation with an AI chatbot where you provide some text to the computer,

6837
10:47:45,070 --> 10:47:49,796
the computer is able to understand that text and then generate some text in response.

6838
10:47:50,382 --> 10:47:58,336
Text classification, where we provide some text to the computer, and the computer assigns it a label, positive or negative, inbox or spam, for example.

6839
10:47:58,574 --> 10:48:03,396
And there are several other kinds of tasks that all fall under this heading of natural language processing.

6840
10:48:03,822 --> 10:48:11,376
But before we take a look at how the computer might try to solve these kinds of tasks, it might be useful for us to think about language in general.

6841
10:48:11,536 --> 10:48:18,436
What are the kinds of challenges that we might need to deal with as we start to think about language and getting a computer to be able to understand it?

6842
10:48:18,798 --> 10:48:22,308
So one part of language that we'll need to consider is the syntax of language.

6843
10:48:22,798 --> 10:48:24,896
Syntax is all about the structure of language.

6844
10:48:25,056 --> 10:48:30,976
Language is composed of individual words, and those words are composed together in some kind of structured whole.

6845
10:48:31,246 --> 10:48:36,932
And if our computer is going to be able to understand language, it's going to need to understand something about that structure.

6846
10:48:37,390 --> 10:48:38,756
So let's take a couple of examples.

6847
10:48:39,150 --> 10:48:40,644
Here, for instance, is a sentence.

6848
10:48:40,910 --> 10:48:44,420
Just before 9 o'clock, Sherlock Holmes stepped briskly into the room.

6849
10:48:44,718 --> 10:48:49,456
That sentence is made up of words, and those words together form a structured whole.

6850
10:48:49,616 --> 10:48:51,876
This is syntactically valid as a sentence.

6851
10:48:52,494 --> 10:48:58,788
But we could take some of those same words, rearrange them, and come up with a sentence that is not syntactically valid.

6852
10:48:59,662 --> 10:49:08,896
Here, for example, just before Sherlock Holmes 9 o'clock stepped briskly the room is still composed of valid words, but they're not in any kind of logical whole.

6853
10:49:08,976 --> 10:49:12,100
This is not a syntactically well-formed sentence.

6854
10:49:12,718 --> 10:49:18,372
Another interesting challenge is that some sentences will have multiple possible valid structures.

6855
10:49:18,894 --> 10:49:23,108
Here's a sentence, for example, I saw the man on the mountain with a telescope.

6856
10:49:23,438 --> 10:49:32,100
And here, this is a valid sentence, but it actually has two different possible structures that lend themselves to two different interpretations and two different meanings.

6857
10:49:32,496 --> 10:49:38,692
Maybe I, the one doing the seeing, and the one with the telescope, or maybe the man on the mountain is the one with the telescope.

6858
10:49:39,246 --> 10:49:41,156
And so natural language is ambiguous.

6859
10:49:41,456 --> 10:49:47,076
Sometimes the same sentence can be interpreted in multiple ways, and that's something that we'll need to think about as well.

6860
10:49:47,470 --> 10:49:52,100
And this lends itself to another problem within language that we'll need to think about, which is semantics.

6861
10:49:52,526 --> 10:49:57,124
While syntax is all about the structure of language, semantics is about the meaning of language.

6862
10:49:57,376 --> 10:50:03,748
It's not enough for a computer just to know that a sentence is well structured if it doesn't know what that sentence means.

6863
10:50:04,206 --> 10:50:08,676
And so semantics is going to concern itself with the meaning of words and the meaning of sentences.

6864
10:50:09,390 --> 10:50:15,332
So if we go back to that same sentence as before, just before nine o'clock, Sherlock Holmes stepped briskly into the room,

6865
10:50:15,982 --> 10:50:17,924
I could come up with another sentence.

6866
10:50:18,318 --> 10:50:23,332
Say the sentence, a few minutes before nine, Sherlock Holmes walked quickly into the room.

6867
10:50:23,630 --> 10:50:30,948
And those are two different sentences, with some of the words the same and some of the words different, but the two sentences have essentially the same meaning.

6868
10:50:31,278 --> 10:50:38,308
And so ideally, whatever model we build will be able to understand that these two sentences, while different, mean something very similar.

6869
10:50:38,894 --> 10:50:42,176
Some syntactically well-formed sentences don't mean anything at all.

6870
10:50:42,446 --> 10:50:48,452
A famous example from linguist Noam Chomsky is the sentence, colorless green ideas sleep furiously.

6871
10:50:48,942 --> 10:50:57,856
This is a syntactically, structurally well-formed sentence. We've got adjectives, modifying a noun, ideas, we've got a verb and an adverb in the correct positions.

6872
10:50:58,094 --> 10:51:01,476
But when taken as a whole, the sentence doesn't really mean anything.

6873
10:51:01,870 --> 10:51:09,376
And so if our computers are going to be able to work with natural language and perform tasks in natural language processing, these are some concerns we'll need to think about.

6874
10:51:09,550 --> 10:51:11,044
We'll need to be thinking about syntax

6875
10:51:11,776 --> 10:51:13,700
and we'll need to be thinking about semantics.

6876
10:51:14,574 --> 10:51:20,004
So, how could we go about trying to teach a computer how to understand the structure of natural language?

6877
10:51:20,334 --> 10:51:25,136
Well, one approach we might take is by starting by thinking about the rules of natural language.

6878
10:51:25,422 --> 10:51:27,012
Our natural languages have rules.

6879
10:51:27,216 --> 10:51:32,976
In English, for example, nouns tend to come before verbs, nouns can be modified by adjectives, for example.

6880
10:51:33,262 --> 10:51:41,380
And so if only we could formalize those rules, then we could give those rules to a computer, and the computer would be able to make sense of them and understand them.

6881
10:51:41,934 --> 10:51:43,616
And so let's try to do exactly that.

6882
10:51:43,790 --> 10:51:51,696
We're going to try to define a formal grammar, where a formal grammar is some system of rules for generating sentences in a language.

6883
10:51:51,950 --> 10:51:55,876
This is going to be a rule-based approach to natural language processing.

6884
10:51:56,046 --> 10:52:03,716
We're going to give the computer some rules that we know about language and have the computer use those rules to make sense of the structure of language.

6885
10:52:04,398 --> 10:52:08,976
And there are a number of different types of formal grammars, each one of them has slightly different use cases,

6886
10:52:09,136 --> 10:52:14,180
but today we're going to focus specifically on one kind of grammar known as a context-free grammar.

6887
10:52:14,638 --> 10:52:16,324
So how does the context-free grammar work?

6888
10:52:16,496 --> 10:52:19,492
Well, here is a sentence that we might want a computer to generate.

6889
10:52:19,776 --> 10:52:21,220
She saw the city.

6890
10:52:21,550 --> 10:52:24,496
And we're going to call each of these words a terminal symbol.

6891
10:52:24,910 --> 10:52:29,376
A terminal symbol because once our computer has generated the word, there's nothing else for it to generate.

6892
10:52:29,536 --> 10:52:32,004
Once it's generated the sentence, the computer is done.

6893
10:52:32,814 --> 10:52:38,532
We're going to associate each of these terminal symbols with a non-terminal symbol that generates it.

6894
10:52:39,342 --> 10:52:43,044
So here we've got n, which stands for noun, like she or city.

6895
10:52:43,216 --> 10:52:52,548
We've got V as a non-terminal symbol, which stands for a verb, and then we have D, which stands for determiner. A determiner is a word like the or a or an in English, for example.

6896
10:52:52,846 --> 10:52:59,012
So each of these non-terminal symbols can generate the terminal symbols that we ultimately care about generating.

6897
10:52:59,630 --> 10:53:05,456
But how do we know, or how does the computer know which non-terminal symbols are associated with which terminal symbols?

6898
10:53:05,742 --> 10:53:08,096
Well, to do that, we need some kind of rule.

6899
10:53:08,398 --> 10:53:18,244
Here are some what we call rewriting rules that have a non-terminal symbol on the left-hand side of an arrow, and on the right side is what that non-terminal symbol can be replaced with.

6900
10:53:18,734 --> 10:53:26,436
So here we're saying the non-terminal symbol n, again, which stands for noun, could be replaced by any of these options, separated by vertical bars.

6901
10:53:26,766 --> 10:53:30,341
N could be replaced by she or city or car or Harry.

6902
10:53:30,830 --> 10:53:34,656
D for determiner could be replaced by the, a, or an, and so forth.

6903
10:53:34,816 --> 10:53:39,332
Each of these non-terminal symbols could be replaced by any of these words.

6904
10:53:40,174 --> 10:53:45,252
We can also have non-terminal symbols that are replaced by other non-terminal symbols.

6905
10:53:45,742 --> 10:53:47,012
Here's an interesting rule.

6906
10:53:47,296 --> 10:53:50,628
NP arrow n bar dn.

6907
10:53:50,894 --> 10:53:51,620
So what does that mean?

6908
10:53:51,982 --> 10:53:54,820
Well, np stands for a noun phrase.

6909
10:53:55,118 --> 10:53:59,748
Sometimes when we have a noun phrase in a sentence, it's not just a single word, it could be multiple words.

6910
10:54:00,174 --> 10:54:07,364
And so here we're saying a noun phrase could be just a noun, or it could be a determiner followed by a noun.

6911
10:54:07,950 --> 10:54:12,196
So we might have a noun phrase that's just a noun, like she, that's a noun phrase.

6912
10:54:12,654 --> 10:54:20,736
Or we could have a noun phrase that's multiple words, something like the city also acts as a noun phrase, but in this case it's composed of two words,

6913
10:54:20,942 --> 10:54:23,716
a determiner, the, and a noun city.

6914
10:54:24,494 --> 10:54:26,148
We could do the same for verb phrases.

6915
10:54:26,416 --> 10:54:32,580
A verb phrase, or VP, might be just a verb, or it might be a verb followed by a noun phrase.

6916
10:54:33,166 --> 10:54:41,456
So we could have a verb phrase that's just a single word, like the word walked, or we could have a verb phrase that is an entire phrase, something like saw

6917
10:54:41,678 --> 10:54:44,388
the city, as an entire verb phrase.

6918
10:54:44,974 --> 10:54:50,532
A sentence, meanwhile, we might then define as a noun phrase followed by a verb phrase.

6919
10:54:50,798 --> 10:55:01,616
And so this would allow us to generate a sentence like she saw the city, an entire sentence made up of a noun phrase, which is just the word she, and then a verb phrase, which is saw the city,

6920
10:55:01,856 --> 10:55:07,044
saw, which is a verb, and then the city, which itself is also a noun phrase.

6921
10:55:07,822 --> 10:55:14,880
And so if we could give these rules to a computer, explaining to it what non-terminal symbols could be replaced by what other symbols,

6922
10:55:15,054 --> 10:55:20,100
then a computer could take a sentence and begin to understand the structure of that sentence.

6923
10:55:20,494 --> 10:55:22,960
And so let's take a look at an example of how we might do that.

6924
10:55:23,214 --> 10:55:29,920
And to do that, we're going to use a Python library called NLTK, or the natural language toolkit, which we'll see a couple of times today.

6925
10:55:30,126 --> 10:55:36,068
It contains a lot of helpful features and functions that we can use for trying to deal with and process natural language.

6926
10:55:36,462 --> 10:55:41,764
So here we'll take a look at how we can use NLTK in order to parse a context-free grammar.

6927
10:55:42,318 --> 10:55:47,428
So let's go ahead and open up CFG0.py, CFG standing for context-free grammar.

6928
10:55:47,790 --> 10:55:52,420
And what you'll see in this file is that I first import NLTK, the natural language toolkit.

6929
10:55:53,102 --> 10:56:00,160
And the first thing I do is define a context-free grammar, saying that a sentence is a noun phrase followed by a verb phrase.

6930
10:56:00,366 --> 10:56:08,964
I'm defining what a noun phrase is, defining what a verb phrase is, and then giving some examples of what I can do with these non-terminal symbols D for determiner,

6931
10:56:09,280 --> 10:56:11,396
N for noun, and V for verb.

6932
10:56:12,238 --> 10:56:14,948
We're going to use NLTK to parse that grammar.

6933
10:56:15,374 --> 10:56:19,684
Then we'll ask the user for some input in the form of a sentence and split it into words.

6934
10:56:20,334 --> 10:56:27,780
And then we'll use this context-free grammar parser to try to parse that sentence and print out the resulting syntax tree.

6935
10:56:28,462 --> 10:56:29,828
So let's take a look at an example.

6936
10:56:30,702 --> 10:56:34,948
I'll go ahead and go into my CFG directory and we'll run cfg0.py.

6937
10:56:35,534 --> 10:56:36,960
And here I'm asked to type in a sentence.

6938
10:56:37,040 --> 10:56:39,332
Let's say I type in she walked.

6939
10:56:40,558 --> 10:56:49,028
And when I do that, I see that she walked is a valid sentence where she is a noun phrase and walked is the corresponding verb phrase.

6940
10:56:49,646 --> 10:56:52,292
I could try to do this with a more complex sentence too.

6941
10:56:52,558 --> 10:56:55,044
I could do something like she saw the city.

6942
10:56:55,854 --> 10:57:03,428
And here we see that she is the noun phrase, and then saw the city is the entire verb phrase that makes up this sentence.

6943
10:57:04,494 --> 10:57:05,956
So that was a very simple grammar.

6944
10:57:06,160 --> 10:57:08,560
Let's take a look at a slightly more complex grammar.

6945
10:57:08,878 --> 10:57:17,600
Here is CFG1.py, where a sentence is still a noun phrase followed by a verb phrase, but I've added some other possible non-terminal symbols too.

6946
10:57:17,760 --> 10:57:22,500
I have a p for adjective phrase and p p for prepositional phrase.

6947
10:57:22,702 --> 10:57:29,636
And we specified that we could have an adjective phrase before a noun phrase, or a prepositional phrase after a noun, for example.

6948
10:57:30,254 --> 10:57:37,028
So lots of additional ways that we might try to structure a sentence and interpret and parse one of those resulting sentences.

6949
10:57:37,870 --> 10:57:39,040
So let's see that one in action.

6950
10:57:39,246 --> 10:57:42,820
We'll go ahead and run CFG1.py with this new grammar.

6951
10:57:43,534 --> 10:57:47,524
And we'll try a sentence like she saw the wide street.

6952
10:57:48,430 --> 10:57:58,000
Here, Python's NLTK is able to parse that sentence and identify that she saw the wide street has this particular structure, a sentence with a noun phrase and a verb phrase,

6953
10:57:58,382 --> 10:58:06,756
where that verb phrase has a noun phrase that within it contains an adjective, and so it's able to get some sense for what the structure of this language actually is.

6954
10:58:07,758 --> 10:58:08,932
Let's try another example.

6955
10:58:09,262 --> 10:58:14,212
Let's say she saw the dog with the binoculars.

6956
10:58:14,670 --> 10:58:15,684
And we'll try that sentence.

6957
10:58:16,654 --> 10:58:19,600
And here we get one possible syntax tree.

6958
10:58:19,840 --> 10:58:21,604
She saw the dog with the binoculars.

6959
10:58:21,870 --> 10:58:26,080
But notice that this sentence is actually a little bit ambiguous in our own natural language.

6960
10:58:26,318 --> 10:58:27,360
Who has the binoculars?

6961
10:58:27,440 --> 10:58:30,960
Is it she who has the binoculars or the dog who has the binoculars?

6962
10:58:31,278 --> 10:58:35,460
And NLTK is able to identify both possible structures for the sentence.

6963
10:58:35,854 --> 10:58:45,028
In this case, the dog with the binoculars is an entire noun phrase. It's all underneath this NP here, so it's the dog that has the binoculars.

6964
10:58:45,294 --> 10:58:46,020
But we also

6965
10:58:46,702 --> 10:58:55,332
got an alternative parse tree where the dog is just the noun phrase, and with the binoculars is a prepositional phrase

6966
10:58:55,520 --> 10:58:56,836
modifying saw.

6967
10:58:57,134 --> 10:59:02,660
So she saw the dog, and she used the binoculars in order to see the dog as well.

6968
10:59:03,118 --> 10:59:08,560
So this allows us to get a sense for the structure of natural language, but it relies on us writing all of these rules.

6969
10:59:08,814 --> 10:59:15,200
And it would take a lot of effort to write all of the rules for any possible sentence that someone might write or say in the English language.

6970
10:59:15,360 --> 10:59:19,600
Language is complicated, and as a result, there are going to be some very complex rules.

6971
10:59:20,110 --> 10:59:21,316
So, what else might we try?

6972
10:59:21,646 --> 10:59:26,948
We might try to take a statistical lens towards approaching this problem of natural language processing.

6973
10:59:27,246 --> 10:59:34,724
If we were able to give the computer a lot of existing data of sentences written in the English language, what could we try to learn from that data?

6974
10:59:35,150 --> 10:59:44,832
Well, it might be difficult to try and interpret long pieces of text all at once, so instead, what we might want to do is break up that longer text into smaller pieces of information instead.

6975
10:59:45,102 --> 10:59:49,412
In particular, we might try to create n-grams out of a longer sequence of text.

6976
10:59:50,286 --> 10:59:55,428
An n-gram is just some contiguous sequence of n items from a sample of text.

6977
10:59:55,552 --> 10:59:59,392
It might be n characters in a row or n words in a row, for example.

6978
10:59:59,758 --> 11:00:04,260
So let's take a passage from Sherlock Holmes and let's look for all of the trigrams.

6979
11:00:04,512 --> 11:00:07,392
A trigram is an n-gram where n is equal to three.

6980
11:00:07,662 --> 11:00:11,152
So in this case, we're looking for sequences of three words in a row.

6981
11:00:11,502 --> 11:00:15,072
So the trigrams here would be phrases like how often have?

6982
11:00:15,232 --> 11:00:16,432
That's three words in a row.

6983
11:00:16,672 --> 11:00:18,432
Often have I is another trigram.

6984
11:00:18,752 --> 11:00:21,892
Have I said, I said to, said to you, to you that.

6985
11:00:22,112 --> 11:00:26,116
These are all trigrams, sequences of three words that appear in sequence.

6986
11:00:27,022 --> 11:00:32,932
And if we could give the computer a large corpus of text and have it pull out all of the trigrams in this case,

6987
11:00:33,232 --> 11:00:41,476
it could get a sense for what sequences of three words tend to appear next to each other in our own natural language, and as a result,

6988
11:00:41,712 --> 11:00:45,796
get some sense for what the structure of the language actually is.

6989
11:00:46,862 --> 11:00:48,352
So let's take a look at an example of that.

6990
11:00:48,558 --> 11:00:54,084
How can we use NLTK to try to get access to information about n-grams?

6991
11:00:55,214 --> 11:01:03,460
So here we're going to open up ngrams.py, and this is a Python program that's going to load a corpus of data, just some text files,

6992
11:01:03,662 --> 11:01:14,052
into our computer's memory, and then we're going to use NLTK's ngrams function, which is going to go through the corpus of text, pulling out all of the n-grams for a particular value of n.

6993
11:01:14,446 --> 11:01:16,900
And then by using Python's counter class,

6994
11:01:17,646 --> 11:01:23,428
we're going to figure out what are the most common n-grams inside of this entire corpus of text.

6995
11:01:24,334 --> 11:01:29,952
And we're going to need a data set in order to do this. And I've prepared a data set of some of the stories of Sherlock Holmes.

6996
11:01:30,032 --> 11:01:40,512
So it's just a bunch of text files, a lot of words for it to analyze, and as a result, we'll get a sense for what sequences of two words or three words tend to be most common in

6997
11:01:40,814 --> 11:01:41,412
natural language.

6998
11:01:42,574 --> 11:01:47,108
So let's give this a try. We'll go into my ngrams directory and we'll run ngrams.py.

6999
11:01:47,406 --> 11:01:55,012
We'll try an n value of two. So we're looking for sequences of two words in a row, and we'll use our corpus of stories from Sherlock Holmes.

7000
11:01:55,726 --> 11:02:02,020
And when we run this program, we get a list of the most common n-grams where n is equal to two, otherwise known as a bigram.

7001
11:02:02,446 --> 11:02:04,352
So the most common one is of the.

7002
11:02:04,718 --> 11:02:08,432
That's a sequence of two words that appears quite frequently in natural language.

7003
11:02:08,686 --> 11:02:10,592
Then in the and it was.

7004
11:02:10,752 --> 11:02:13,956
These are all common sequences of two words that appear in a row.

7005
11:02:14,734 --> 11:02:18,752
Let's instead now try running n-grams with n equal to three.

7006
11:02:18,958 --> 11:02:21,444
Let's get all of the trigrams and see what we get.

7007
11:02:21,742 --> 11:02:27,428
And now we see the most common trigrams are it was a, one of the, I think that.

7008
11:02:27,758 --> 11:02:31,012
These are all sequences of three words that appear quite frequently.

7009
11:02:31,982 --> 11:02:35,748
And we were able to do this essentially via a process known as tokenization.

7010
11:02:36,046 --> 11:02:40,032
Tokenization is the process of splitting a sequence of characters into pieces.

7011
11:02:40,238 --> 11:02:49,348
In this case, we're splitting a long sequence of text into individual words, and then looking at sequences of those words to get a sense for the structure of natural language.

7012
11:02:49,870 --> 11:02:54,672
So once we've done this, once we've done the tokenization, once we've built up our corpus of n grams.

7013
11:02:55,310 --> 11:02:56,720
What can we do with that information?

7014
11:02:57,038 --> 11:03:02,340
Well, the one thing that we might try is we could build a Markov chain, which you might recall from when we talked about probability.

7015
11:03:02,638 --> 11:03:09,700
Recall that a Markov chain is some sequence of values where we can predict one value based on the values that came before it.

7016
11:03:10,094 --> 11:03:18,148
And as a result, if we know all of the common n-grams in the English language, what words tend to be associated with what other words in sequence,

7017
11:03:18,480 --> 11:03:23,044
we can use that to predict what word might come next in a sequence of words.

7018
11:03:23,470 --> 11:03:32,320
And so we could build a Markov chain for language in order to try to generate natural language that follows the same statistical patterns as some input

7019
11:03:32,320 --> 11:03:37,200
data. So let's take a look at that and build a Markov chain for natural language.

7020
11:03:37,454 --> 11:03:41,700
And as input, I'm going to use the works of William Shakespeare.

7021
11:03:42,000 --> 11:03:45,540
So here I have a file shakespeare.txt, which is just

7022
11:03:46,382 --> 11:03:48,000
a bunch of the works of William Shakespeare.

7023
11:03:48,174 --> 11:03:51,012
It's a long text file, so plenty of data to analyze.

7024
11:03:51,374 --> 11:03:56,708
And here in generator.py, I'm using a third-party Python library in order to do this analysis.

7025
11:03:57,518 --> 11:04:03,332
We're going to read in the sample of text, and then we're going to train a Markov model based on that text.

7026
11:04:03,950 --> 11:04:07,600
And then we're going to have the Markov chain generate some sentences.

7027
11:04:07,854 --> 11:04:13,700
We're going to generate a sentence that doesn't appear in the original text, but that follows the same statistical patterns.

7028
11:04:14,094 --> 11:04:20,196
That's generating it based on the n-grams, trying to predict what word is likely to come next that we would expect

7029
11:04:21,006 --> 11:04:22,372
based on those statistical patterns.

7030
11:04:23,086 --> 11:04:24,720
So we'll go ahead and go into

7031
11:04:25,870 --> 11:04:34,320
our Markov directory, run this generator with the works of William Shakespeare's input, and what we're going to get are five new sentences

7032
11:04:34,574 --> 11:04:41,840
where these sentences are not necessarily sentences from the original input text itself, but just that follow the same statistical patterns.

7033
11:04:41,920 --> 11:04:49,920
It's predicting what word is likely to come next based on the input data that we've seen and the types of words that tend to appear in sequence there too.

7034
11:04:50,158 --> 11:04:51,876
And so we're able to generate these sentences.

7035
11:04:52,942 --> 11:04:58,724
Of course, so far, there's no guarantee that any of the sentences that are generated actually mean anything or make any sense.

7036
11:04:58,990 --> 11:05:03,652
They just happen to follow the statistical patterns that our computer is already aware of.

7037
11:05:04,014 --> 11:05:09,600
So we'll return to this issue of how to generate text in perhaps a more accurate or more meaningful way a little bit later.

7038
11:05:09,838 --> 11:05:14,820
So let's now turn our attention to a slightly different problem, and that's the problem of text classification.

7039
11:05:15,278 --> 11:05:23,684
Text classification is the problem where we have some text and we want to put that text into some kind of category. We want to apply some sort of label to that text.

7040
11:05:24,206 --> 11:05:26,960
And this kind of problem shows up in a wide variety of places.

7041
11:05:27,246 --> 11:05:29,680
A common place might be your email inbox, for example.

7042
11:05:29,840 --> 11:05:37,060
You get an email, and you want your computer to be able to identify whether the email belongs in your inbox or whether it should be filtered out into spam.

7043
11:05:37,390 --> 11:05:39,012
So we need to classify the text.

7044
11:05:39,310 --> 11:05:41,348
Is it a good email or is it spam?

7045
11:05:41,998 --> 11:05:44,548
Another common use case is sentiment analysis.

7046
11:05:44,750 --> 11:05:51,040
We might want to know whether the sentiment of some text is positive or negative. And so how might we do that?

7047
11:05:51,200 --> 11:05:56,708
This comes up in situations like product reviews, where we might have a bunch of reviews for a product on some website.

7048
11:05:57,040 --> 11:05:58,720
My grandson loved it, so much fun.

7049
11:05:58,894 --> 11:06:00,320
Product broke after a few days.

7050
11:06:00,590 --> 11:06:04,880
One of the best games I've played in a long time, and kind of cheap and flimsy, not worth it.

7051
11:06:05,040 --> 11:06:08,964
Here's some example sentences that you might see on a product review website.

7052
11:06:09,550 --> 11:06:15,840
And you and I could pretty easily look at this list of product reviews and decide which ones are positive and which ones are negative.

7053
11:06:16,000 --> 11:06:23,556
We might say the first one and the third one, those seem like positive sentiment messages, but the second one and the fourth one seem like negative sentiment messages.

7054
11:06:24,142 --> 11:06:25,120
But how did we know that?

7055
11:06:25,294 --> 11:06:28,484
And how could we train a computer to be able to figure that out as well?

7056
11:06:29,070 --> 11:06:36,320
Well, you might have clued your eye in on particular key words, where those particular words tend to mean something positive or negative.

7057
11:06:36,526 --> 11:06:42,596
So you might have identified words like loved and fun and best tend to be associated with positive messages.

7058
11:06:42,862 --> 11:06:47,428
And words like broke and cheap and flimsy tend to be associated with negative messages.

7059
11:06:48,046 --> 11:06:54,948
So if only we could train a computer to be able to learn what words tend to be associated with positive versus negative messages,

7060
11:06:55,120 --> 11:06:59,332
then maybe we could train a computer to do this kind of sentiment analysis as well.

7061
11:07:00,110 --> 11:07:01,476
So we're going to try to do just that.

7062
11:07:01,742 --> 11:07:09,316
We're going to use a model known as the bag of words model, which is a model that represents text as just an unordered collection of words.

7063
11:07:09,742 --> 11:07:15,396
For the purpose of this model, we're not going to worry about the sequence and the ordering of the words, which word came first, second or third.

7064
11:07:15,600 --> 11:07:21,360
We're just going to treat the text as a collection of words in no particular order, and we're losing information there, right?

7065
11:07:21,440 --> 11:07:24,480
The order of words is important, and we'll come back to that a little bit later.

7066
11:07:24,782 --> 11:07:31,492
But for now to simplify our model, it'll help us tremendously just to think about text as some unordered collection of words.

7067
11:07:32,302 --> 11:07:37,796
And in particular, we're going to use the bag of words model to build something known as a naive Bayes classifier.

7068
11:07:38,286 --> 11:07:40,000
So, what is a naive Bayes classifier?

7069
11:07:40,240 --> 11:07:42,720
Well, it's a tool that's going to allow us to classify text.

7070
11:07:43,214 --> 11:07:46,848
Based on Bayes' rule, again, which you might remember from when we talked about probability.

7071
11:07:47,182 --> 11:07:56,324
Bayes' rule says that the probability of B given A is equal to the probability of A given B multiplied by the probability of B

7072
11:07:56,558 --> 11:07:58,724
divided by the probability of A.

7073
11:07:59,406 --> 11:08:02,788
So how are we going to use this rule to be able to analyze text?

7074
11:08:03,534 --> 11:08:04,688
Well, what are we interested in?

7075
11:08:04,878 --> 11:08:11,236
We're interested in the probability that a message has a positive sentiment and the probability that a message has a negative sentiment,

7076
11:08:11,502 --> 11:08:17,668
which I'm here for simplicity going to represent just with these emoji, happy face and frown face, as positive and negative sentiment.

7077
11:08:18,446 --> 11:08:27,204
And so if I had a review, something like my grandson loved it, then what I'm interested in is not just the probability that a message has positive sentiment,

7078
11:08:27,630 --> 11:08:34,340
but the conditional probability that a message has positive sentiment given that this is the message, my grandson loved it.

7079
11:08:35,118 --> 11:08:42,276
But how do I go about calculating this value, the probability that the message is positive, given that the review is this sequence of words?

7080
11:08:42,830 --> 11:08:44,868
Well, here's where the bag of words model comes in.

7081
11:08:45,294 --> 11:08:52,548
Rather than treat this review as a string of a sequence of words in order, we're just going to treat it as an unordered collection of words.

7082
11:08:52,814 --> 11:09:02,128
We're going to try to calculate the probability that the review is positive, given that all of these words, my grandson loved it, are in the review in no particular order,

7083
11:09:02,382 --> 11:09:04,288
just this unordered collection of

7084
11:09:04,288 --> 11:09:10,852
words. And this is a conditional probability, which we can then apply Bayes' rule to try to make sense of.

7085
11:09:11,694 --> 11:09:15,968
So according to Bayes' rule, this conditional probability is equal to what?

7086
11:09:16,128 --> 11:09:25,188
It's equal to the probability that all of these four words are in the review, given that the review is positive, multiplied by the probability that the review is positive,

7087
11:09:25,488 --> 11:09:29,732
divided by the probability that all of these words happen to be in the review.

7088
11:09:30,606 --> 11:09:33,316
So this is the value now that we're going to try to calculate.

7089
11:09:33,870 --> 11:09:39,716
Now, one thing you might notice is that the denominator here, the probability that all of these words appear in the review,

7090
11:09:39,982 --> 11:09:45,380
doesn't actually depend on whether or not we're looking at the positive sentiment or negative sentiment case.

7091
11:09:45,902 --> 11:09:48,676
So we can actually get rid of this denominator. We don't need to calculate it.

7092
11:09:48,928 --> 11:09:52,708
We can just say that this probability is proportional to the numerator.

7093
11:09:53,230 --> 11:09:59,972
And then at the end, we're going to need to normalize the probability distribution to make sure that all of the values sum up to the value one.

7094
11:10:00,750 --> 11:10:03,140
So now, how do we calculate this value?

7095
11:10:03,470 --> 11:10:09,412
Well, this is the probability of all of these words given positive times probability of positive.

7096
11:10:09,966 --> 11:10:18,724
And that, by the definition of joint probability, is just one big joint probability, the probability that all of these things are the case, that it's a positive review,

7097
11:10:18,928 --> 11:10:21,828
and that all four of these words are in the review.

7098
11:10:22,798 --> 11:10:26,528
But still, it's not entirely obvious how we calculate that value.

7099
11:10:26,798 --> 11:10:32,036
And here is where we need to make one more assumption, and this is where the naive part of naive Bayes comes in.

7100
11:10:32,270 --> 11:10:36,548
We're going to make the assumption that all of the words are independent of each other.

7101
11:10:36,910 --> 11:10:46,048
And by that, I mean that if the word grandson is in the review, that doesn't change the probability that the word loved is in the review, or that the word it is in the review, for example.

7102
11:10:46,288 --> 11:10:48,608
And in practice, this assumption might not be true.

7103
11:10:48,688 --> 11:10:56,420
It's almost certainly the case that the probability of words do depend on each other, but it's going to simplify our analysis and still give us reasonably good results,

7104
11:10:56,768 --> 11:11:03,684
just to assume that the words are independent of each other and they only depend on whether it's positive or negative.

7105
11:11:03,888 --> 11:11:09,636
You might, for example, expect the word loved to appear more often in a positive review than in a negative review.

7106
11:11:10,542 --> 11:11:11,268
So what does that mean?

7107
11:11:11,630 --> 11:11:21,636
Well, if we make this assumption, then we can say that this value, the probability we're interested in, is not directly proportional to, but it's naively proportional to this value.

7108
11:11:22,190 --> 11:11:24,580
The probability that the review is positive

7109
11:11:25,294 --> 11:11:32,528
times the probability that my is in the review, given that it's positive, times the probability that grandson is in the review, given that it's positive,

7110
11:11:32,718 --> 11:11:35,908
and so on for the other two words that happen to be in this review.

7111
11:11:36,270 --> 11:11:41,860
And now this value, which looks a little more complex, is actually a value that we can calculate pretty easily.

7112
11:11:42,670 --> 11:11:45,968
So how are we going to estimate the probability that the review is positive?

7113
11:11:46,286 --> 11:11:52,900
Well, if we have some training data, some example data of example reviews where each one has already been labeled as positive or negative,

7114
11:11:53,198 --> 11:12:02,688
then we can estimate the probability that a review is positive just by counting the number of positive samples and dividing by the total number of samples that we have in our training data.

7115
11:12:03,502 --> 11:12:11,012
And for the conditional probabilities, the probability of loved given that it's positive, well, that's going to be the number of positive samples with loved in

7116
11:12:11,758 --> 11:12:14,692
it divided by the total number of positive samples.

7117
11:12:15,374 --> 11:12:19,268
So let's take a look at an actual example to see how we could try to calculate these values.

7118
11:12:19,790 --> 11:12:21,488
Here I've put together some sample data.

7119
11:12:21,806 --> 11:12:28,900
The way to interpret the sample data is that based on the training data, 49% of the reviews are positive, 51% are negative.

7120
11:12:29,166 --> 11:12:32,516
And then over here in this table, we have some conditional probabilities.

7121
11:12:33,422 --> 11:12:38,564
We have if the review is positive, then there's a 30% chance that my appears in it.

7122
11:12:38,702 --> 11:12:42,532
And if the review is negative, there's a 20% chance that my appears in it.

7123
11:12:42,848 --> 11:12:48,004
And based on our training data, among the positive reviews, 1% of them contain the word grandson.

7124
11:12:48,462 --> 11:12:51,620
And among the negative reviews, 2% contain the word grandson.

7125
11:12:52,398 --> 11:12:57,572
So, using this data, let's try to calculate this value, the value we're interested in.

7126
11:12:57,870 --> 11:13:01,796
And to do that, we'll need to multiply all of these values together.

7127
11:13:02,048 --> 11:13:06,688
The probability of positive, and then all of these positive conditional probabilities.

7128
11:13:06,926 --> 11:13:08,644
And when we do that, we get some value.

7129
11:13:09,358 --> 11:13:11,908
And then we can do the same thing for the negative case.

7130
11:13:12,142 --> 11:13:19,972
We're going to do the same thing, take the probability that it's negative, multiply it by all of these conditional probabilities, and we're going to get some other value.

7131
11:13:20,622 --> 11:13:27,248
And now these values don't sum to one, they're not a probability distribution yet, but I can normalize them and get some values,

7132
11:13:27,328 --> 11:13:31,076
and that tells me that we're going to predict that my grandson loved it.

7133
11:13:31,328 --> 11:13:40,928
We think there's a 68% chance, probability 0.68 that that is a positive sentiment review, and 0.32 probability that it's a negative

7134
11:13:40,928 --> 11:13:44,368
review. So what problems might we run into here?

7135
11:13:44,528 --> 11:13:51,364
What could potentially go wrong when doing this kind of analysis in order to analyze whether text has a positive or negative sentiment?

7136
11:13:51,694 --> 11:13:53,348
Well, a couple of problems might arise.

7137
11:13:53,774 --> 11:14:00,004
One problem might be: what if the word grandson never appears for any of the positive reviews?

7138
11:14:00,878 --> 11:14:08,368
If that were the case, then when we try to calculate the value, the probability that we think the review is positive, we're going to multiply all these values together,

7139
11:14:08,558 --> 11:14:14,116
and we're just going to get zero for the positive case, because we're all going to ultimately multiply by that zero value.

7140
11:14:14,382 --> 11:14:24,196
And so we're going to say that we think there is no chance that the review is positive because it contains the word grandson, and in our training data, we've never seen the word grandson appear in

7141
11:14:24,846 --> 11:14:26,564
a positive sentiment message before.

7142
11:14:26,990 --> 11:14:35,648
And that's probably not the right analysis, because in cases of rare words, it might be the case that in nowhere in our training data did we ever see the word grandson

7143
11:14:35,808 --> 11:14:38,084
appear in a message that has positive sentiment.

7144
11:14:38,350 --> 11:14:39,940
So what can we do to solve this problem?

7145
11:14:40,270 --> 11:14:48,196
Well, one thing we'll often do is some kind of additive smoothing, where we add some value alpha to each value in our distribution just to smooth out the data a little bit.

7146
11:14:48,430 --> 11:14:53,408
And a common form of this is Laplace smoothing, where we add one to each value in our distribution.

7147
11:14:53,614 --> 11:14:57,700
In essence, we pretend we've seen each value one more time than we actually have.

7148
11:14:57,968 --> 11:15:02,208
If we've never seen the word grandson for a positive review, we pretend we've seen it once.

7149
11:15:02,414 --> 11:15:11,908
If we've seen it once, we pretend we've seen it twice, just to avoid the possibility that we might multiply by zero and as a result get some results we don't want in our analysis.

7150
11:15:12,782 --> 11:15:14,212
So let's see what this looks like in practice.

7151
11:15:14,670 --> 11:15:21,348
Let's try to do some naive Bayes classification in order to classify text as either positive or negative.

7152
11:15:22,286 --> 11:15:32,100
We'll take a look at sentiment.py, and what this is going to do is load some sample data into memory, some examples of positive reviews and negative reviews,

7153
11:15:32,398 --> 11:15:37,540
and then we're going to train a naive Bayes classifier on all of this training data.

7154
11:15:38,414 --> 11:15:44,516
Training data that includes all of the words we see in positive reviews and all of the words we see in negative reviews.

7155
11:15:44,878 --> 11:15:47,364
And then we're going to try to classify some input.

7156
11:15:48,142 --> 11:15:50,532
And so we're going to do this based on a corpus of data.

7157
11:15:50,798 --> 11:15:55,716
I have some example positive reviews. Here are some positive reviews. It was great, so much fun, for example.

7158
11:15:56,046 --> 11:15:59,008
And then some negative reviews, not worth it, kind of cheap.

7159
11:15:59,088 --> 11:16:01,348
These are some examples of negative reviews.

7160
11:16:02,126 --> 11:16:08,580
So now let's try to run this classifier and see how it would classify particular text as either positive or negative.

7161
11:16:09,390 --> 11:16:10,788
We'll go ahead and run our

7162
11:16:11,886 --> 11:16:13,508
sentiment analysis on this corpus.

7163
11:16:14,318 --> 11:16:18,368
And we need to provide it with a review. So I'll say something like I enjoyed it.

7164
11:16:19,534 --> 11:16:26,532
And we see that the classifier says there is about a 0.92 probability that we think that this particular review is positive.

7165
11:16:27,118 --> 11:16:28,288
Let's try something negative.

7166
11:16:28,494 --> 11:16:30,884
We'll try kind of overpriced.

7167
11:16:31,662 --> 11:16:36,928
And we see that there is a 0.96 probability now that we think that this particular review is negative.

7168
11:16:37,230 --> 11:16:45,328
And so our naive Bayes classifier has learned what kinds of words tend to appear in positive reviews and what kinds of words tend to appear in negative reviews.

7169
11:16:45,454 --> 11:16:52,708
And as a result of that, we've been able to design a classifier that can predict whether a particular review is positive or negative.

7170
11:16:54,190 --> 11:17:00,548
And so this definitely is a useful tool that we can use to try and make some predictions, but we had to make some assumptions in order to get there.

7171
11:17:00,910 --> 11:17:08,688
So what if we want to now try to build some more sophisticated models, use some tools from machine learning to try and take better advantage of language data

7172
11:17:08,910 --> 11:17:13,060
to be able to draw more accurate conclusions and solve new kinds of tasks and new kinds of problems?

7173
11:17:13,806 --> 11:17:20,688
Well, we've seen a couple of times now that when we want to take some data and take some input, put it in a way that the computer is going to be able to make sense of,

7174
11:17:20,942 --> 11:17:24,452
it can be helpful to take that data and turn it into numbers ultimately.

7175
11:17:25,006 --> 11:17:33,248
And so, what we might want to try to do is come up with some word representation, some way to take a word and translate its meaning into numbers.

7176
11:17:33,488 --> 11:17:41,764
Because, for example, if we wanted to use a neural network to be able to process language, give our language to a neural network and have it make some predictions or perform some analysis there,

7177
11:17:42,318 --> 11:17:48,068
a neural network takes as input and produces as output a vector of values, a vector of numbers.

7178
11:17:48,462 --> 11:17:55,940
And so what we might want to do is take our data and somehow take words and convert them into some kind of numeric representation.

7179
11:17:56,782 --> 11:17:57,668
So how might we do that?

7180
11:17:57,888 --> 11:18:00,868
How might we take words and turn them into numbers?

7181
11:18:01,518 --> 11:18:02,692
Let's take a look at an example.

7182
11:18:03,278 --> 11:18:09,572
Here's a sentence, he wrote a book, and let's say I wanted to take each of those words and turn it into a vector of values.

7183
11:18:10,190 --> 11:18:11,300
Here's one way I might do that.

7184
11:18:11,566 --> 11:18:17,252
We'll say he is going to be a vector that has a one in the first position, and the rest of the values are zero.

7185
11:18:17,742 --> 11:18:21,248
Rote will have a one in the second position, and the rest of the values are zero.

7186
11:18:21,568 --> 11:18:28,420
A has a one in the third position with the rest of the value zero, and book has a one in the fourth position with the rest of the value zero.

7187
11:18:28,782 --> 11:18:32,612
So each of these words now has a distinct vector representation.

7188
11:18:33,294 --> 11:18:41,168
And this is what we often call a one hot representation, a representation of the meaning of a word as a vector with a single one

7189
11:18:41,358 --> 11:18:43,524
and all of the rest of the values are zero.

7190
11:18:43,886 --> 11:18:50,288
And so when doing this, we now have a numeric representation for every word, and we could pass in those vector representations into

7191
11:18:50,448 --> 11:18:55,012
a neural network or other models that require some kind of numeric data as input.

7192
11:18:55,822 --> 11:19:00,612
But this one-hot representation actually has a couple of problems, and it's not ideal for a few reasons.

7193
11:19:01,230 --> 11:19:07,408
One reason is here we're just looking at four words, but if you imagine a vocabulary of thousands of words or more,

7194
11:19:07,694 --> 11:19:13,888
these vectors are going to get quite long in order to have a distinct vector for every possible word in a vocabulary.

7195
11:19:14,126 --> 11:19:20,772
And as a result of that, these longer vectors are going to be more difficult to deal with, more difficult to train, and so forth, and so that might be a problem.

7196
11:19:21,550 --> 11:19:23,748
Another problem is a little bit more subtle.

7197
11:19:24,238 --> 11:19:30,608
If we want to represent a word as a vector, and in particular the meaning of a word as a vector, then ideally

7198
11:19:30,830 --> 11:19:40,324
it should be the case that words that have similar meanings should also have similar vector representations, so that they're close to each other together inside a vector space.

7199
11:19:41,038 --> 11:19:49,988
But that's not really going to be the case with these one-hot representations, because if we take some similar words, say the word wrote and the word authored, which means similar things,

7200
11:19:50,254 --> 11:19:53,124
they have entirely different vector representations.

7201
11:19:54,030 --> 11:20:02,048
Likewise, book and novel, those two words mean somewhat similar things, but they have entirely different vector representations because they each have a one

7202
11:20:02,288 --> 11:20:03,556
in some different position.

7203
11:20:04,110 --> 11:20:05,540
And so that's not ideal either.

7204
11:20:05,998 --> 11:20:10,128
So what we might be interested in instead is some kind of distributed representation.

7205
11:20:10,638 --> 11:20:19,264
A distributed representation is the representation of the meaning of a word distributed across multiple values instead of just being one hot with a one

7206
11:20:19,504 --> 11:20:20,356
in one position.

7207
11:20:20,864 --> 11:20:24,932
Here is what a distributed representation of words might be.

7208
11:20:25,104 --> 11:20:36,292
Each word is associated with some vector of values with the meaning distributed across multiple values, ideally in such a way that similar words have a similar vector representation.

7209
11:20:37,166 --> 11:20:38,944
But how are we going to come up with those values?

7210
11:20:39,118 --> 11:20:40,452
Where do those values come from?

7211
11:20:40,624 --> 11:20:44,836
How can we define the meaning of a word in this distributed sequence of numbers?

7212
11:20:45,806 --> 11:20:53,476
Well, to do that, we're going to draw inspiration from a quote from British linguist J.R. Firth, who said, You shall know a word by the company it keeps.

7213
11:20:54,254 --> 11:21:00,676
In other words, we're going to define the meaning of a word based on the words that appear around it, the context words around it.

7214
11:21:01,230 --> 11:21:04,580
Take, for example, this context, for blank he ate.

7215
11:21:05,262 --> 11:21:07,940
You might wonder what words could reasonably fill in that blank.

7216
11:21:08,782 --> 11:21:14,116
Well, it might be words like breakfast, or lunch, or dinner. All of those could reasonably fill in that blank.

7217
11:21:14,606 --> 11:21:20,292
And so what we're going to say is because the words breakfast and lunch and dinner appear in a similar context,

7218
11:21:21,038 --> 11:21:22,660
that they must have a similar meaning.

7219
11:21:23,342 --> 11:21:32,228
And that's something our computer could understand and try to learn. A computer could look at a big corpus of text, look at what words tend to appear in similar context to each other,

7220
11:21:32,464 --> 11:21:39,332
and use that to identify which words have a similar meaning, and should therefore appear close to each other inside a vector space.

7221
11:21:40,206 --> 11:21:44,144
And so one common model for doing this is known as the word2vec model.

7222
11:21:44,238 --> 11:21:52,388
It's a model for generating word vectors, a vector representation for every word, by looking at data and looking at the context in which a word appears.

7223
11:21:53,006 --> 11:21:54,144
The idea is going to be this.

7224
11:21:54,304 --> 11:22:00,324
if you start out with all of the words, just in some random position in space and train it on some training data,

7225
11:22:00,974 --> 11:22:05,668
what the word2vec model will do is start to learn what words appear in similar contexts.

7226
11:22:05,934 --> 11:22:13,796
and it will move these vectors around in such a way that hopefully, words with similar meanings, breakfast, lunch, and dinner, book, memoir, novel,

7227
11:22:14,382 --> 11:22:18,212
will hopefully appear to be near to each other as vectors as well.

7228
11:22:19,054 --> 11:22:24,036
So let's now take a look at what word2vec might look like in practice when implemented in code.

7229
11:22:24,910 --> 11:22:33,801
What I have here inside of words.text is a pre-trained model where each of these words has some vector representation trained by word2vec.

7230
11:22:34,064 --> 11:22:43,204
Each of these words has some sequence of values representing its meaning, hopefully in such a way that similar words are represented by similar vectors.

7231
11:22:43,694 --> 11:22:48,704
I also have this file vectors.py, which is going to open up the words and form them into a dictionary,

7232
11:22:48,784 --> 11:22:57,744
and we also define some useful functions like distance to get the distance between two word vectors, and closest words to find which words are nearby in terms of

7233
11:22:57,984 --> 11:22:59,492
having close vectors to each other.

7234
11:23:00,174 --> 11:23:01,316
And so let's give this a try.

7235
11:23:02,350 --> 11:23:04,964
We'll go ahead and open a Python interpreter,

7236
11:23:05,904 --> 11:23:06,224
and

7237
11:23:07,790 --> 11:23:09,508
I'm going to import these vectors.

7238
11:23:10,222 --> 11:23:14,180
And we might say, all right, what is the vector representation of the word book?

7239
11:23:15,630 --> 11:23:20,484
And we get this big long vector that represents the word book as a sequence of values.

7240
11:23:21,102 --> 11:23:29,700
And this sequence of values by itself is not all that meaningful, but it is meaningful in the context of comparing it to other vectors for other words.

7241
11:23:30,318 --> 11:23:35,300
So we could use this distance function, which is going to get us the distance between two word vectors.

7242
11:23:35,758 --> 11:23:41,604
And we might say what is the distance between the vector representation for the word book and the vector representation

7243
11:23:42,254 --> 11:23:43,172
for the word novel?

7244
11:23:44,302 --> 11:23:46,020
And we see that it's 0.34.

7245
11:23:46,304 --> 11:23:50,436
You can kind of interpret zero as being really close together and one being very far apart.

7246
11:23:51,022 --> 11:23:55,204
And so now what is the distance between book and let's say breakfast?

7247
11:23:55,822 --> 11:24:02,116
Well, book and breakfast are more different from each other than book and novel are, so I would hopefully expect the distance to be larger.

7248
11:24:02,574 --> 11:24:05,424
And in fact, it is 0.64 approximately.

7249
11:24:05,584 --> 11:24:07,620
These two words are further away from each other.

7250
11:24:08,302 --> 11:24:13,060
And what about now the distance between, let's say, lunch and breakfast?

7251
11:24:13,582 --> 11:24:14,784
Well, that's about 0.2.

7252
11:24:15,104 --> 11:24:19,076
Those are even closer together. They have a meaning that is closer to each other.

7253
11:24:19,886 --> 11:24:21,444
Another interesting thing we might

7254
11:24:22,094 --> 11:24:23,844
do is calculate the closest words.

7255
11:24:24,398 --> 11:24:31,984
We might say, what are the closest words, according to WordDevek, to the word book? And let's say let's get the ten closest words.

7256
11:24:32,144 --> 11:24:36,740
What are the ten closest vectors to the vector representation for the word book?

7257
11:24:37,614 --> 11:24:40,624
And when we perform that analysis, we get this list of words.

7258
11:24:40,910 --> 11:24:48,544
The closest one is book itself, but we also have book's plural, and then essay, memoir, essays, novella, anthology, and so on.

7259
11:24:48,784 --> 11:24:56,384
All of these words mean something similar to the word book, according to word to vec at least, because they have a similar vector representation.

7260
11:24:56,590 --> 11:25:02,948
So it seems like we've done a pretty good job of trying to capture this kind of vector representation of word meaning.

7261
11:25:03,854 --> 11:25:11,504
One other interesting side effect of Word2Vec is that it's also able to capture something about the relationships between words as well.

7262
11:25:11,918 --> 11:25:13,124
Let's take a look at an example.

7263
11:25:13,518 --> 11:25:19,908
Here, for instance, are two words, man and king, and these are each represented by Word2Vec as vectors.

7264
11:25:20,430 --> 11:25:26,500
So what might happen if I subtracted one from the other, calculated the value king minus man?

7265
11:25:27,310 --> 11:25:38,564
Well, that will be the vector that will take us from man to king, somehow represent this relationship between the vector representation of the word man and the vector representation of the word king.

7266
11:25:38,926 --> 11:25:41,668
And that's what this value, king minus man, represents.

7267
11:25:42,574 --> 11:25:50,756
So what would happen if I took the vector representation of the word woman and added that same value, king minus man, to it?

7268
11:25:51,182 --> 11:25:54,500
What would we get as the closest word to that, for example?

7269
11:25:54,958 --> 11:25:55,664
Well, we could try it.

7270
11:25:55,744 --> 11:25:59,172
Let's go ahead and go back to our Python interpreter and give this a try.

7271
11:25:59,822 --> 11:26:07,908
I could say what is the closest word to the vector representation of the word king minus the representation of the word man plus

7272
11:26:08,270 --> 11:26:10,628
the representation of the word woman.

7273
11:26:11,374 --> 11:26:13,984
And we see that the closest word is the word queen.

7274
11:26:14,286 --> 11:26:22,144
We've somehow been able to capture the relationship between king and man, and then when we apply it to the word woman, we get as the result the word

7275
11:26:22,574 --> 11:26:22,852
queen.

7276
11:26:24,174 --> 11:26:33,924
So word2vec has been able to capture not just the words and how they're similar to each other, but also something about the relationships between words and how those words are connected to each other.

7277
11:26:34,702 --> 11:26:38,304
So now that we have this vector representation of words, what can we now do with it?

7278
11:26:38,574 --> 11:26:44,676
Now we can represent words as numbers, and so we might try to pass those words as input to, say, a neural network.

7279
11:26:45,070 --> 11:26:50,304
Neural networks we've seen are very powerful tools for identifying patterns and making predictions.

7280
11:26:50,638 --> 11:27:00,004
Recall that a neural network you can think of as all of these units, but really what the neural network is doing is taking some input, passing it into the network, and then producing some output.

7281
11:27:00,334 --> 11:27:08,132
And by providing the neural network with training data, we're able to update the weights inside of the network so that the neural network can do a more accurate job

7282
11:27:08,398 --> 11:27:10,884
of translating those inputs into those outputs.

7283
11:27:11,726 --> 11:27:21,124
And now that we can represent words as numbers that could be the input or output, you could imagine passing a word in as input to a neural network and getting a word as output.

7284
11:27:21,678 --> 11:27:23,024
And so when might that be useful?

7285
11:27:23,310 --> 11:27:29,664
One common use for neural networks is in machine translation, when we want to translate text from one language into another.

7286
11:27:29,998 --> 11:27:35,780
Say translate English into French by passing English into the neural network and getting some French output.

7287
11:27:36,174 --> 11:27:42,916
You might imagine, for instance, that we could take the English word for LAMP, pass it into the neural network, get the French word for LAMP as output.

7288
11:27:43,790 --> 11:27:52,704
But in practice, when we're translating text from one language to another, we're usually not just interested in translating a single word from one language to another,

7289
11:27:52,910 --> 11:27:55,812
but a sequence, say a sentence or a paragraph of words.

7290
11:27:56,270 --> 11:28:00,228
Here, for example, is another paragraph, again taken from Sherlock Holmes, written in English.

7291
11:28:00,622 --> 11:28:09,060
And what I might want to do is take that entire sentence, pass it into the neural network, and get as output a French translation of the same sentence.

7292
11:28:10,094 --> 11:28:14,500
But recall that a neural network's input and output needs to be of some fixed size.

7293
11:28:14,862 --> 11:28:20,352
And a sentence is not a fixed size, it's variable. You might have shorter sentences and you might have longer sentences.

7294
11:28:20,686 --> 11:28:27,364
So somehow we need to solve the problem of translating a sequence into another sequence by means of a neural network.

7295
11:28:27,632 --> 11:28:31,748
And that's going to be true not only for machine translation, but also for other problems.

7296
11:28:32,112 --> 11:28:33,700
Problems like question answering.

7297
11:28:33,934 --> 11:28:38,532
If I want to pass as input a question, something like, what is the capital of Massachusetts?

7298
11:28:38,990 --> 11:28:45,508
Feed that as input into the neural network, I would hope that what I would get as output is a sentence like, the capital is Boston.

7299
11:28:45,792 --> 11:28:49,284
Again, translating some sequence into some other sequence.

7300
11:28:49,998 --> 11:28:57,232
And if you've ever had a conversation with an AI chatbot or have ever asked your phone a question, it needs to do something like this.

7301
11:28:57,392 --> 11:29:05,572
It needs to understand the sequence of words that you, the human, provided as input, and then the computer needs to generate some sequence of words as output.

7302
11:29:06,190 --> 11:29:07,108
So how can we do this?

7303
11:29:07,502 --> 11:29:15,232
Well, one tool that we can use is the recurrent neural network, which we took a look at last time, which is a way for us to provide a sequence of values to a

7304
11:29:15,232 --> 11:29:18,116
neural network by running the neural network multiple times.

7305
11:29:18,574 --> 11:29:24,644
And each time we run the neural network, what we're going to do is we're going to keep track of some hidden state.

7306
11:29:25,006 --> 11:29:32,228
And that hidden state is going to be passed from one run of the neural network to the next run of the neural network, keeping track of all of the relevant information.

7307
11:29:33,198 --> 11:29:36,192
And so let's take a look at how we could apply that to something like this.

7308
11:29:36,398 --> 11:29:45,828
And in particular, we're going to look at an architecture known as an encoder-decoder architecture, where we're going to encode this question into some kind of hidden state,

7309
11:29:46,286 --> 11:29:51,076
and then use a decoder to decode that hidden state into the output that we're interested in.

7310
11:29:51,982 --> 11:29:53,060
So what's that going to look like?

7311
11:29:53,454 --> 11:30:00,292
We'll start with the first word, the word what, that goes into our neural network, and it's going to produce some hidden state.

7312
11:30:00,686 --> 11:30:05,796
This is some information about the word what that our neural network is going to need to keep track of.

7313
11:30:06,798 --> 11:30:08,452
Then when the second word comes along,

7314
11:30:09,134 --> 11:30:15,460
we're going to feed it into that same encoder neural network, but it's going to get as input that hidden state as well.

7315
11:30:15,982 --> 11:30:23,140
So we pass in the second word, we also get the information about the hidden state, and that's going to continue for the other words in the input.

7316
11:30:23,392 --> 11:30:25,316
This is going to produce a new hidden state.

7317
11:30:25,582 --> 11:30:27,872
And so then when we get to the third word, the,

7318
11:30:28,814 --> 11:30:32,068
that goes into the encoder, it also gets access to the hidden state.

7319
11:30:32,462 --> 11:30:36,912
And then it produces a new hidden state that gets passed into the next run when we use the word capital.

7320
11:30:37,134 --> 11:30:41,380
And the same thing is going to repeat for the other words that appear in the input.

7321
11:30:41,582 --> 11:30:46,404
So of Massachusetts, that produces one final piece of hidden state.

7322
11:30:47,278 --> 11:30:49,872
Now somehow we need to signal the fact that we're done.

7323
11:30:50,032 --> 11:30:51,364
There's nothing left in the input.

7324
11:30:51,630 --> 11:31:00,068
And we typically do this by passing some kind of special token, say an end token, into the neural network, and now the decoding process is going to start.

7325
11:31:00,462 --> 11:31:02,432
We're going to generate the word the,

7326
11:31:03,310 --> 11:31:05,152
but in addition to generating the word the,

7327
11:31:06,030 --> 11:31:10,436
this decoder network is also going to generate some kind of hidden state.

7328
11:31:11,118 --> 11:31:12,772
And so what happens the next time?

7329
11:31:13,134 --> 11:31:17,956
Well, to generate the next word, it might be helpful to know what the first word was.

7330
11:31:18,542 --> 11:31:21,956
So we might pass the first word the back into the decoder network.

7331
11:31:22,766 --> 11:31:26,980
It's going to get as input this hidden state, and it's going to generate the next word capital.

7332
11:31:27,566 --> 11:31:29,636
And that's also going to generate some hidden state.

7333
11:31:29,998 --> 11:31:36,964
And we'll repeat that, passing capital into the network to generate the third word is, and then one more time in order to get the fourth word Boston.

7334
11:31:37,966 --> 11:31:39,152
And at that point we're done.

7335
11:31:39,406 --> 11:31:40,592
But how do we know we're done?

7336
11:31:40,782 --> 11:31:50,148
Usually we'll do this one more time, pass Boston into the decoder network, and get an output some end token to indicate that that is the end of our input.

7337
11:31:50,670 --> 11:32:00,804
And so this then is how we could use a recurrent neural network to take some input, encode it into some hidden state, and then use that hidden state to decode it into the output we're interested in.

7338
11:32:01,166 --> 11:32:04,512
To visualize it in a slightly different way, we have some input sequence.

7339
11:32:04,592 --> 11:32:06,372
This is just some sequence of words.

7340
11:32:06,734 --> 11:32:16,032
That input sequence goes into the encoder, which in this case is a recurrent neural network, generating these hidden states along the way, until we generate some final hidden

7341
11:32:16,032 --> 11:32:23,524
state, at which point we start the decoding process, again using a recurrent neural network, that's going to generate the output sequence as well.

7342
11:32:23,886 --> 11:32:30,564
So we've got the encoder, which is encoding the information about the input sequence into this hidden state, and then the decoder,

7343
11:32:30,798 --> 11:32:35,108
which takes that hidden state and uses it in order to generate the output sequence.

7344
11:32:36,334 --> 11:32:37,472
But there are some problems.

7345
11:32:37,552 --> 11:32:46,692
And for many years, this was the state of the art. The recurrent neural network invariants on this approach were some of the best ways we knew in order to perform tasks in natural language processing.

7346
11:32:47,022 --> 11:32:53,828
But there are some problems that we might want to try to deal with and that have been dealt with over the years to try and improve upon this kind of model.

7347
11:32:54,414 --> 11:32:57,796
And one problem you might notice happens in this encoder stage.

7348
11:32:58,222 --> 11:33:05,060
We've taken this input sequence, the sequence of words, and encoded it all into this final piece of hidden state.

7349
11:33:05,422 --> 11:33:13,700
And that final piece of hidden state needs to contain all of the information from the input sequence that we need in order to generate the output sequence.

7350
11:33:14,734 --> 11:33:20,032
And while that's possible, it becomes increasingly difficult as the sequence gets larger and larger.

7351
11:33:20,270 --> 11:33:28,912
For larger and larger input sequences, it's going to become more and more difficult to store all of the information we need about the input inside this single hidden

7352
11:33:29,134 --> 11:33:30,084
state piece of context.

7353
11:33:30,606 --> 11:33:33,472
That's a lot of information to pack into just a single value.

7354
11:33:33,712 --> 11:33:44,324
It might be useful for us when generating output to not just refer to this one value, but to all of the previous hidden values that have been generated by the encoder.

7355
11:33:45,070 --> 11:33:49,732
And so that might be useful, but how could we do that? We've got a lot of different values, we need to combine them somehow.

7356
11:33:50,126 --> 11:33:59,140
So you could imagine adding them together, taking the average of them, for example, but doing that would assume that all of these pieces of hidden state are equally important.

7357
11:33:59,694 --> 11:34:01,232
But that's not necessarily true either.

7358
11:34:01,392 --> 11:34:08,196
Some of these pieces of hidden state are going to be more important than others, depending on what word they most closely correspond to.

7359
11:34:08,494 --> 11:34:17,252
This piece of hidden state very closely corresponds to the first word of the input sequence, this one very closely corresponds to the second word of the input sequence, for example,

7360
11:34:17,742 --> 11:34:20,612
and some of those are going to be more important than others.

7361
11:34:21,262 --> 11:34:29,348
To make matters more complicated, depending on which word of the output sequence we're generating, different input words might be more or less important.

7362
11:34:29,966 --> 11:34:37,988
And so what we really want is some way to decide for ourselves which of the input values are worth paying attention to at what point in time.

7363
11:34:38,510 --> 11:34:41,764
And this is the key idea behind a mechanism known as attention.

7364
11:34:42,126 --> 11:34:50,724
Attention is all about letting us decide which values are important to pay attention to when generating, in this case, the next word in our sequence.

7365
11:34:51,822 --> 11:34:53,764
So let's take a look at an example of that.

7366
11:34:54,222 --> 11:34:54,980
Here's a sentence.

7367
11:34:55,312 --> 11:34:57,552
What is the capital of Massachusetts?

7368
11:34:57,712 --> 11:34:58,820
Same sentence as before.

7369
11:34:59,086 --> 11:35:03,940
And let's imagine that we were trying to answer that question by generating tokens of output.

7370
11:35:04,270 --> 11:35:05,604
So what would the output look like?

7371
11:35:05,838 --> 11:35:12,164
Well, it's going to look like something like the capital is, and let's say we're now trying to generate this last word here.

7372
11:35:12,558 --> 11:35:13,712
What is that last word?

7373
11:35:13,872 --> 11:35:16,068
How is the computer going to figure it out?

7374
11:35:16,654 --> 11:35:21,796
Well, what it's going to need to do is decide which values it's going to pay attention to.

7375
11:35:22,286 --> 11:35:30,592
And so the attention mechanism will allow us to calculate some attention scores for each word, some value corresponding to each word,

7376
11:35:30,832 --> 11:35:35,524
determining how relevant is it for us to pay attention to that word right now.

7377
11:35:36,238 --> 11:35:45,444
And in this case, when generating the fourth word of the output sequence, the most important words to pay attention to might be capital and Massachusetts, for example.

7378
11:35:46,158 --> 11:35:48,804
That those words are going to be particularly relevant.

7379
11:35:49,166 --> 11:35:53,472
And there are a number of different mechanisms that have been used in order to calculate these attention scores.

7380
11:35:53,712 --> 11:36:01,792
It could be something as simple as a dot product to see how similar two vectors are, or we could train an entire neural network to calculate these attention scores.

7381
11:36:01,998 --> 11:36:09,124
But the key idea is that during the training process for our neural network, we're going to learn how to calculate these attention scores.

7382
11:36:09,392 --> 11:36:16,196
Our model is going to learn what is important to pay attention to in order to decide what the next word should be.

7383
11:36:17,166 --> 11:36:24,320
So the result of all of this, calculating these attention scores, is that we can calculate some value, some value for each input word,

7384
11:36:24,526 --> 11:36:29,188
determining how important is it for us to pay attention to that particular value.

7385
11:36:29,838 --> 11:36:39,428
And recall that each of these input words is also associated with one of these hidden state context vectors, capturing information about the sentence up to that point,

7386
11:36:39,600 --> 11:36:42,820
but primarily focused on that word in particular.

7387
11:36:43,502 --> 11:36:51,972
And so what we can now do is if we have all of these vectors and we have values representing how important is it for us to pay attention to those particular vectors,

7388
11:36:52,302 --> 11:36:53,828
is we can take a weighted average.

7389
11:36:54,286 --> 11:37:03,908
We can take all of these vectors, multiply them by their attention scores, and add them up to get some new vector value, which is going to represent the context from the input,

7390
11:37:04,160 --> 11:37:08,772
but specifically paying attention to the words that we think are most important.

7391
11:37:09,486 --> 11:37:17,668
And once we've done that, that context vector can be fed into our decoder in order to say that the word should be, in this case, Boston.

7392
11:37:18,670 --> 11:37:27,844
So attention is this very powerful tool that allows any word when we're trying to decode it to decide which words from the input should we pay attention to

7393
11:37:28,046 --> 11:37:32,644
in order to determine what's important for generating the next word of the output.

7394
11:37:33,358 --> 11:37:37,600
And one of the first places this was really used was in the field of machine translation.

7395
11:37:37,902 --> 11:37:45,572
Here's an example of a diagram from the paper that introduced this idea, which was focused on trying to translate English sentences into French sentences.

7396
11:37:45,774 --> 11:37:52,196
So we have an input English sentence up along the top, and then along the left side, the output French equivalent of that same sentence.

7397
11:37:52,622 --> 11:38:00,580
And what you see in all of these squares are the attention scores visualized, where a lighter square indicates a higher attention score.

7398
11:38:01,262 --> 11:38:07,280
And what you'll notice is that there's a strong correspondence between the French word and the equivalent English word,

7399
11:38:07,440 --> 11:38:16,800
that the French word for agreement is really paying attention to the English word for agreement in order to decide what French word should be generated at that point in time.

7400
11:38:17,038 --> 11:38:19,140
And sometimes you might pay attention to multiple words.

7401
11:38:19,360 --> 11:38:29,380
If you look at the French word for economic, that's primarily paying attention to the English word for economic, but also paying attention to the English word for European in this case,

7402
11:38:29,600 --> 11:38:29,920
too.

7403
11:38:30,382 --> 11:38:37,920
And so attention scores are very easy to visualize to get a sense for what is our machine learning model really paying attention to?

7404
11:38:38,080 --> 11:38:45,860
What information is it using in order to determine what's important and what's not in order to determine what the ultimate output token should be.

7405
11:38:46,734 --> 11:38:52,720
And so when we combine the attention mechanism with a recurrent neural network, we can get very powerful and useful results

7406
11:38:52,880 --> 11:38:57,796
where we're able to generate an output sequence by paying attention to the input sequence too.

7407
11:38:58,094 --> 11:39:02,052
But there are other problems with this approach of using a recurrent neural network as well.

7408
11:39:02,286 --> 11:39:07,588
In particular, notice that every run of the neural network depends on the output of the previous step.

7409
11:39:07,886 --> 11:39:12,548
And that was important for getting a sense for the sequence of words and the ordering of those particular words,

7410
11:39:12,814 --> 11:39:21,124
but we can't run this unit of the neural network until after we've calculated the hidden state from the run before it from the previous input token.

7411
11:39:21,678 --> 11:39:25,508
And what that means is that it's very difficult to parallelize this process.

7412
11:39:25,838 --> 11:39:35,360
That as the input sequence get longer and longer, we might want to use parallelism to try and speed up this process of training the neural network and making sense of all of this language data,

7413
11:39:35,598 --> 11:39:41,860
but it's difficult to do that, and it's slow to do that with a recurrent neural network because all of it needs to be performed in sequence.

7414
11:39:42,446 --> 11:39:47,524
And that's become an increasing challenge as we've started to get larger and larger language models.

7415
11:39:47,822 --> 11:39:56,080
The more language data that we have available to us to use to train our machine learning models, the more accurate it can be, the better representation of language it can have,

7416
11:39:56,160 --> 11:39:59,840
the better understanding it can have, and the better results that we can see.

7417
11:40:00,110 --> 11:40:07,748
And so we've seen this growth of large language models that are using larger and larger data sets, but as a result, they take longer and longer to train.

7418
11:40:08,046 --> 11:40:14,564
And so this problem that recurrent neural networks are not easy to parallelize has become an increasing problem.

7419
11:40:15,182 --> 11:40:24,900
And as a result of that, that was one of the main motivations for a different architecture for thinking about how to deal with natural language, and that's known as the transformer architecture.

7420
11:40:25,184 --> 11:40:34,020
And this has been a significant milestone in the world of natural language processing for really increasing how well we can perform these kinds of natural language processing tasks,

7421
11:40:34,350 --> 11:40:39,428
as well as how quickly we can train a machine learning model to be able to produce effective results.

7422
11:40:39,982 --> 11:40:48,612
There are a number of different types of transformers in terms of how they work, but what we're going to take a look at here is the basic architecture for how one might work with a transformer

7423
11:40:48,782 --> 11:40:51,104
to get a sense for what's involved and what we're

7424
11:40:51,104 --> 11:40:58,784
doing. So let's start with the model we were looking at before, specifically at this encoder part of our encoder-decoder architecture,

7425
11:40:59,022 --> 11:41:08,772
where we used a recurrent neural network to take this input sequence and capture all of this information about the hidden state and the information we need to know about that input sequence.

7426
11:41:09,486 --> 11:41:19,904
Right now, it all needs to happen in this linear progression, but what the transformer is going to allow us to do is process each of the words independently in a way that's easy to parallelize.

7427
11:41:20,064 --> 11:41:30,852
Rather than have each word wait for some other word, each word is going to go through the same neural network and produce some kind of encoded representation of that particular input word.

7428
11:41:31,118 --> 11:41:33,156
And all of this is going to happen in parallel.

7429
11:41:33,806 --> 11:41:39,024
Now it's happening for all of the words at once, but we're really just going to focus on what's happening for one word to make it clear.

7430
11:41:39,246 --> 11:41:44,868
But know that whatever you're seeing happen for this one word is going to happen for all of the other input words too.

7431
11:41:45,710 --> 11:41:46,948
So what's going on here?

7432
11:41:47,278 --> 11:41:49,412
Well, we start with some input word.

7433
11:41:49,774 --> 11:41:57,024
That input word goes into the neural network, and the output is hopefully some encoded representation of the input word,

7434
11:41:57,230 --> 11:42:02,628
the information we need to know about the input word that's going to be relevant to us as we're generating the output.

7435
11:42:03,278 --> 11:42:07,140
And because we're doing this each word independently, it's easy to parallelize.

7436
11:42:07,264 --> 11:42:11,748
We don't have to wait for the previous word before we run this word through the neural network.

7437
11:42:12,782 --> 11:42:16,544
But what did we lose in this process by trying to parallelize this whole thing?

7438
11:42:16,782 --> 11:42:19,264
Well, we've lost all notion of word ordering.

7439
11:42:19,664 --> 11:42:21,184
The order of words is important.

7440
11:42:21,390 --> 11:42:27,172
The sentence Sherlock Holmes gave the book to Watson has a different meaning than Watson gave the book to Sherlock Holmes.

7441
11:42:27,504 --> 11:42:30,692
And so we want to keep track of that information about word position.

7442
11:42:31,406 --> 11:42:38,660
In the recurrent neural network, that happened for us automatically, because we could run each word one at a time through the neural network, get the hidden state,

7443
11:42:38,864 --> 11:42:41,348
pass it on to the next run of the neural network.

7444
11:42:41,646 --> 11:42:48,100
But that's not the case here with the transformer, where each word is being processed independent of all of the other ones.

7445
11:42:49,070 --> 11:42:51,104
So what are we going to do to try to solve that problem?

7446
11:42:51,470 --> 11:42:56,644
One thing we can do is add some kind of positional encoding to the input word.

7447
11:42:57,038 --> 11:43:02,144
The positional encoding is some vector that represents the position of the word in the sentence.

7448
11:43:02,702 --> 11:43:04,868
This is the first word, the second word, the third word, and so forth.

7449
11:43:05,230 --> 11:43:07,396
We're going to add that to the input word,

7450
11:43:08,078 --> 11:43:12,544
and the result of that is going to be a vector that captures multiple pieces of information.

7451
11:43:12,814 --> 11:43:16,708
It captures the input word itself as well as where in the sentence it appears.

7452
11:43:17,422 --> 11:43:24,704
The result of that is we can pass the output of that addition, the addition of the input word and the positional encoding into the neural network.

7453
11:43:24,942 --> 11:43:32,864
That way the neural network knows the word and where it appears in the sentence and can use both of those pieces of information to determine how best to

7454
11:43:32,864 --> 11:43:37,092
represent the meaning of that word in the encoded representation at the end of it.

7455
11:43:38,222 --> 11:43:46,660
In addition to what we have here, in addition to the positional encoding and this feed-forward neural network, we're also going to add one additional component,

7456
11:43:47,054 --> 11:43:49,348
which is going to be a self-attention step.

7457
11:43:49,934 --> 11:43:57,664
This is going to be attention where we're paying attention to the other input words, because the meaning or interpretation of an input word might

7458
11:43:57,664 --> 11:44:00,544
vary depending on the other words in the input as well.

7459
11:44:00,814 --> 11:44:10,180
And so we're going to allow each word in the input to decide what other words in the input it should pay attention to in order to decide on its encoded representation.

7460
11:44:10,766 --> 11:44:20,836
And that's going to allow us to get a better encoded representation for each word, because words are defined by their context, by the words around them and how they're used in that particular context.

7461
11:44:21,422 --> 11:44:30,212
This kind of self-attention is so valuable, in fact, that oftentimes the transformer will use multiple different self-attention layers at the same time

7462
11:44:30,510 --> 11:44:35,716
to allow for this model to be able to pay attention to multiple facets of the input at the same time.

7463
11:44:36,398 --> 11:44:41,540
And we call this multi-headed attention, where each attention head can pay attention to something different.

7464
11:44:41,838 --> 11:44:48,932
And as a result, this network can learn to pay attention to many different parts of the input for this input word all at the same time.

7465
11:44:49,582 --> 11:44:55,908
And in the spirit of deep learning, these two steps, this multi-headed self-attention layer, and this neural network layer,

7466
11:44:56,320 --> 11:45:04,036
that itself can be repeated multiple times too, in order to get a deeper representation, in order to learn deeper patterns within the input text,

7467
11:45:04,398 --> 11:45:11,840
and ultimately get a better representation of language in order to get useful, encoded representations of all of the input

7468
11:45:11,840 --> 11:45:19,780
words. And so this is the process that a transformer might use in order to take an input word and get it as encoded representation.

7469
11:45:20,078 --> 11:45:28,164
And the key idea is to really rely on this attention step in order to get information that's useful in order to determine how to encode that word.

7470
11:45:28,974 --> 11:45:33,572
And that process is going to repeat for all of the input words that are in the input sequence.

7471
11:45:33,840 --> 11:45:42,212
We're going to take all of the input words, encode them with some kind of positional encoding, feed those into these self-attention and feed forward neural networks

7472
11:45:42,480 --> 11:45:46,692
in order to ultimately get these encoded representations of the words.

7473
11:45:46,960 --> 11:45:48,480
That's the result of the encoder.

7474
11:45:48,640 --> 11:45:58,916
We get all of these encoded representations that will be useful to us when it comes time then to try to decode all of this information into the output sequence we're interested in.

7475
11:45:59,534 --> 11:46:06,320
And again, this might take place in the context of machine translation, where the output is going to be the same sentence in a different language,

7476
11:46:06,542 --> 11:46:10,560
or it might be an answer to a question in the case of an AI chatbot, for

7477
11:46:11,278 --> 11:46:14,852
example. And so now let's take a look at how that decoder is going to work.

7478
11:46:15,950 --> 11:46:18,596
Ultimately, it's going to have a very similar structure.

7479
11:46:19,022 --> 11:46:26,960
Anytime we're trying to generate the next output word, we need to know what the previous output word is, as well as its positional encoding.

7480
11:46:27,120 --> 11:46:32,068
Where in the output sequence are we? And we're going to have these same steps, self-attention,

7481
11:46:32,718 --> 11:46:41,604
because we might want an output word to be able to pay attention to other words in that same output, as well as a neural network, and that might itself repeat multiple times.

7482
11:46:42,414 --> 11:46:45,476
But in this decoder, we're going to add one additional step.

7483
11:46:45,838 --> 11:46:53,220
We're going to add an additional attention step, where instead of self-attention, where the output word is going to pay attention to other output words,

7484
11:46:53,774 --> 11:46:56,516
in this step, we're going to allow the output word

7485
11:46:57,294 --> 11:46:59,684
to pay attention to the encoded representations.

7486
11:47:00,430 --> 11:47:08,356
So recall that the encoder is taking all of the input words and transforming them into these encoded representations of all of the input words.

7487
11:47:08,814 --> 11:47:17,988
But it's going to be important for us to be able to decide which of those encoded representations we want to pay attention to when generating any particular token in the output sequence.

7488
11:47:18,606 --> 11:47:22,276
And that's what this additional attention step is going to allow us to do.

7489
11:47:22,542 --> 11:47:31,840
It's saying that every time we're generating a word of the output, we can pay attention to the other words in the output because we might want to know what are the words we've generated previously,

7490
11:47:32,078 --> 11:47:36,740
and we want to pay attention to some of them to decide what word is going to be next in the sequence.

7491
11:47:37,518 --> 11:47:40,804
But we also care about paying attention to the input words too.

7492
11:47:41,070 --> 11:47:48,960
And we want the ability to decide which of these encoded representations of the input words are going to be relevant in order for us to generate the next

7493
11:47:48,960 --> 11:47:57,316
step. And so these two pieces combine together. We have this encoder that takes all of the input words and produces this encoded representation.

7494
11:47:57,646 --> 11:48:05,700
And we have this decoder that is able to take the previous output word, pay attention to that encoded input, and then generate the next output word.

7495
11:48:06,254 --> 11:48:10,372
And this is one of the possible architectures we could use for a transformer.

7496
11:48:10,560 --> 11:48:16,004
With the key idea being these attention steps that allow words to pay attention to each other.

7497
11:48:16,334 --> 11:48:23,076
During the training process here, we can now much more easily parallelize this, because we don't have to wait for all of the words to happen in sequence.

7498
11:48:23,440 --> 11:48:26,880
And we can learn how we should perform these attention steps.

7499
11:48:27,040 --> 11:48:36,388
The model is able to learn what is important to pay attention to, what things do I need to pay attention to in order to be more accurate at predicting what the output word is.

7500
11:48:37,198 --> 11:48:46,820
And this has proved to be a tremendously effective model for conversational AI agents, for building machine translation systems, and there have been many variants proposed on this model too.

7501
11:48:47,086 --> 11:48:57,712
Some transformers only use an encoder, some only use a decoder, some use some other combination of these different particular features, but the key ideas ultimately remain the same.

7502
11:48:57,902 --> 11:49:01,188
This real focus on trying to pay attention to what is most important.

7503
11:49:01,966 --> 11:49:05,792
And the world of natural language processing is fast growing and fast evolving.

7504
11:49:06,112 --> 11:49:16,352
Year after year, we keep coming up with new models that allow us to do an even better job of performing these natural language-related tasks, all on the surface of solving the tricky problem,

7505
11:49:16,526 --> 11:49:17,828
which is our own natural language.

7506
11:49:18,032 --> 11:49:26,032
We've seen how the syntax and semantics of our language is ambiguous and it introduces all of these new challenges that we need to think about if we're going

7507
11:49:26,032 --> 11:49:30,212
to be able to design AI agents that are able to work with language effectively.

7508
11:49:30,830 --> 11:49:36,032
So as we think about where we've been in this class, all of the different types of artificial intelligence we've considered,

7509
11:49:36,206 --> 11:49:39,812
we've looked at artificial intelligence in a wide variety of different forms now.

7510
11:49:40,206 --> 11:49:48,352
We started by taking a look at search problems, where we looked at how AI can search for solutions, play games, and find the optimal decision to make.

7511
11:49:48,622 --> 11:49:56,260
We talked about knowledge, how AI can represent information that it knows and use that information to generate new knowledge as well.

7512
11:49:56,910 --> 11:50:03,952
Then we looked at what AI can do when it's less certain, when it doesn't know things for sure, and we have to represent things in terms of probability.

7513
11:50:04,334 --> 11:50:06,116
We then took a look at optimization problems.

7514
11:50:06,352 --> 11:50:12,260
We saw how a lot of problems in AI can be boiled down to trying to maximize or minimize some function.

7515
11:50:12,878 --> 11:50:17,700
And we looked at strategies that AI can use in order to do that kind of maximizing and minimizing.

7516
11:50:18,222 --> 11:50:26,032
We then looked at the world of machine learning, learning from data in order to figure out some patterns and identify how to perform a task by looking at the

7517
11:50:26,032 --> 11:50:27,620
training data that we have available to it.

7518
11:50:28,270 --> 11:50:36,272
And one of the most powerful tools there was the neural network, the sequence of units whose weights can be trained in order to allow us to really effectively

7519
11:50:36,272 --> 11:50:41,092
go from input to output and predict how to get there by learning these underlying patterns.

7520
11:50:41,742 --> 11:50:51,524
And then today we took a look at language itself, trying to understand how can we train the computer to be able to understand our natural language, to be able to understand syntax and semantics,

7521
11:50:51,792 --> 11:50:56,804
make sense of and generate natural language, which introduces a number of interesting problems too.

7522
11:50:57,038 --> 11:50:59,908
And we've really just scratched the surface of artificial intelligence.

7523
11:51:00,192 --> 11:51:07,392
There is so much interesting research and interesting new techniques and algorithms and ideas being introduced to try to solve these types of problems.

7524
11:51:07,694 --> 11:51:11,232
So I hope you enjoyed this exploration into the world of artificial intelligence.

7525
11:51:11,406 --> 11:51:15,712
A huge thanks to all of the course's teaching staff and production team for making the class possible.

7526
11:51:15,952 --> 11:51:18,884
This was an introduction to artificial intelligence with Python.

